 13: ## Collection Usage
 14: 
 15: ### .NET Upgrade Chat Mode
 16: 
 17: Discover and plan your .NET upgrade journey!
 18: 
 19: ```markdown, upgrade-analysis.prompt.md
 20: ---
 21: mode: dotnet-upgrade
 22: title: Analyze current .NET framework versions and create upgrade plan
 23: ---
 24: Analyze the repository and list each project's current TargetFramework
 25: along with the latest available LTS version from Microsoft's release schedule.
 26: Create an upgrade strategy prioritizing least-dependent projects first.
 27: ```
 28: 
 29: The upgrade chat mode automatically adapts to your repository's current .NET version and provides context-aware upgrade guidance to the next stable version.
 30: 
 31: It will help you:
 32: - Auto-detect current .NET versions across all projects
 33: - Generate optimal upgrade sequences
 34: - Identify breaking changes and modernization opportunities
 35: - Create per-project upgrade flows
 36: 
 37: ---
 38: 
 39: ### .NET Upgrade Instructions
 40: 
 41: Execute comprehensive .NET framework upgrades with structured guidance!
 42: 
 43: The instructions provide:
 44: - Sequential upgrade strategies
 45: - Dependency analysis and sequencing
 46: - Framework targeting and code adjustments
 47: - NuGet and dependency management
 48: - CI/CD pipeline updates
 49: - Testing and validation procedures
 50: 
 51: Use these instructions when implementing upgrade plans to ensure proper execution and validation.
 52: 
 53: ---
 54: 
 55: ### .NET Upgrade Prompts
 56: 
 57: Quick access to specialized upgrade analysis prompts!
 58: 
 59: The prompts collection includes ready-to-use queries for:
 60: - Project discovery and assessment
 61: - Upgrade strategy and sequencing
 62: - Framework targeting and code adjustments
 63: - Breaking change analysis
 64: - CI/CD pipeline updates
 65: - Final validation and delivery
 66: 
 67: Use these prompts for targeted analysis of specific upgrade aspects.
 68: 
 69: ---
 70: 
 71: ## Quick Start
 72: 1. Run a discovery pass to enumerate all `*.sln` and `*.csproj` files in the repository.
 73: 2. Detect the current .NET version(s) used across projects.
 74: 3. Identify the latest available stable .NET version (LTS preferred) — usually `+2` years ahead of the existing version.
 75: 4. Generate an upgrade plan to move from current → next stable version (e.g., `net6.0 → net8.0`, or `net7.0 → net9.0`).
 76: 5. Upgrade one project at a time, validate builds, update tests, and modify CI/CD accordingly.
 77: 
 78: ---
 79: 
 80: ## Auto-Detect Current .NET Version
 81: To automatically detect the current framework versions across the solution:
 82: 
 83: ```bash
 84: # 1. Check global SDKs installed
 85: dotnet --list-sdks
 86: 
 87: # 2. Detect project-level TargetFrameworks
 88: find . -name "*.csproj" -exec grep -H "<TargetFramework" {} \;
 89: 
 90: # 3. Optional: summarize unique framework versions
 91: grep -r "<TargetFramework" **/*.csproj | sed 's/.*<TargetFramework>//;s/<\/TargetFramework>//' | sort | uniq
 92: 
 93: # 4. Verify runtime environment
 94: dotnet --info | grep "Version"
 95: ```
 96: 
 97: **Chat Prompt:**
 98: > "Analyze the repository and list each project’s current TargetFramework along with the latest available LTS version from Microsoft’s release schedule."
 99: 
100: ---
101: 
102: ## Discovery & Analysis Commands
103: ```bash
104: # List all projects
105: dotnet sln list
106: 
107: # Check current target frameworks for each project
108: grep -H "TargetFramework" **/*.csproj
109: 
110: # Check outdated packages
111: dotnet list <ProjectName>.csproj package --outdated
112: 
113: # Generate dependency graph
114: dotnet msbuild <ProjectName>.csproj /t:GenerateRestoreGraphFile /p:RestoreGraphOutputPath=graph.json
115: ```
116: 
117: **Chat Prompt:**
118: > "Analyze the solution and summarize each project’s current TargetFramework and suggest the appropriate next LTS upgrade version."
119: 
120: ---
121: 
122: ## Classification Rules
123: - `TargetFramework` starts with `netcoreapp`, `net5.0+`, `net6.0+`, etc. → **Modern .NET**
124: - `netstandard*` → **.NET Standard** (migrate to current .NET version)
125: - `net4*` → **.NET Framework** (migrate via intermediate step to .NET 8+)
126: 
127: ---
128: 
129: ## Upgrade Sequence
130: 1. **Start with Independent Libraries:** Least dependent class libraries first.
131: 2. **Next:** Shared components and common utilities.
132: 3. **Then:** API, Web, or Function projects.
133: 4. **Finally:** Tests, integration points, and pipelines.
134: 
135: **Chat Prompt:**
136: > "Generate the optimal upgrade order for this repository, prioritizing least-dependent projects first."
137: 
138: ---
139: 
140: ## Per-Project Upgrade Flow
141: 1. **Create branch:** `upgrade/<project>-to-<targetVersion>`
142: 2. **Edit `<TargetFramework>`** in `.csproj` to the suggested version (e.g., `net9.0`)
143: 3. **Restore & update packages:**
144:    ```bash
145:    dotnet restore
146:    dotnet list package --outdated
147:    dotnet add package <PackageName> --version <LatestVersion>
148:    ```
149: 4. **Build & test:**
150:    ```bash
151:    dotnet build <ProjectName>.csproj
152:    dotnet test <ProjectName>.Tests.csproj
153:    ```
154: 5. **Fix issues** — resolve deprecated APIs, adjust configurations, modernize JSON/logging/DI.
155: 6. **Commit & push** PR with test evidence and checklist.
156: 
157: ---
158: 
159: ## Breaking Changes & Modernization
160: - Use `.NET Upgrade Assistant` for initial recommendations.
161: - Apply analyzers to detect obsolete APIs.
162: - Replace outdated SDKs (e.g., `Microsoft.Azure.*` → `Azure.*`).
163: - Modernize startup logic (`Startup.cs` → `Program.cs` top-level statements).
164: 
165: **Chat Prompt:**
166: > "List deprecated or incompatible APIs when upgrading from <currentVersion> to <targetVersion> for <ProjectName>."
167: 
168: ---
169: 
170: ## CI/CD Configuration Updates
171: Ensure pipelines use the detected **target version** dynamically:
172: 
173: **Azure DevOps**
174: ```yaml
175: - task: UseDotNet@2
176:   inputs:
177:     packageType: 'sdk'
178:     version: '$(TargetDotNetVersion).x'
179: ```
180: 
181: **GitHub Actions**
182: ```yaml
183: - uses: actions/setup-dotnet@v4
184:   with:
185:     dotnet-version: '${{ env.TargetDotNetVersion }}.x'
186: ```
187: 
188: ---
189: 
190: ## Validation Checklist
191: - [ ] TargetFramework upgraded to next stable version
192: - [ ] All NuGet packages compatible and updated
193: - [ ] Build and test pipelines succeed locally and in CI
194: - [ ] Integration tests pass
195: - [ ] Deployed to a lower environment and verified
196: 
197: ---
198: 
199: ## Branching & Rollback Strategy
200: - Use feature branches: `upgrade/<project>-to-<targetVersion>`
201: - Commit frequently and keep changes atomic
202: - If CI fails after merge, revert PR and isolate failing modules
203: 
204: **Chat Prompt:**
205: > "Suggest a rollback and validation plan if the .NET upgrade for <ProjectName> introduces build or runtime regressions."
206: 
207: ---
208: 
209: ## Automation & Scaling
210: - Automate upgrade detection with GitHub Actions or Azure Pipelines.
211: - Schedule nightly runs to check for new .NET releases via `dotnet --list-sdks`.
212: - Use agents to automatically raise PRs for outdated frameworks.
213: 
214: ---
215: 
216: ## Chatmode Prompt Library
217: 1. "List all projects with current and recommended .NET versions."
218: 2. "Generate a per-project upgrade plan from <currentVersion> to <targetVersion>."
219: 3. "Suggest .csproj and pipeline edits to upgrade <ProjectName>."
220: 4. "Summarize build/test results post-upgrade for <ProjectName>."
221: 5. "Create PR description and checklist for the upgrade."
