  5: 
  6: # .NET Framework Development
  7: 
  8: ## Build and Compilation Requirements
  9: - Always use `msbuild /t:rebuild` to build the solution or projects instead of `dotnet build`
 10: 
 11: ## Project File Management
 12: 
 13: ### Legacy and SDK-Style Project Structure
 14: Many .NET Framework projects use the legacy non-SDK project format, which differs significantly from modern SDK-style projects. However, SDK-style project files can also target .NET Framework, such as `net48` or `net472`. Check the `.csproj` format before applying project-file guidance:
 15: 
 16: - **Legacy non-SDK projects**: All new source files **MUST** be explicitly added to the project file (`.csproj`) using a `<Compile>` element
 17:   - Legacy non-SDK projects do not automatically include files in the directory like SDK-style projects
 18:   - Example: `<Compile Include="Path\To\NewFile.cs" />`
 19: 
 20: - **SDK-style projects**: If the project file has an `Sdk` attribute, use SDK-style conventions even when it targets .NET Framework
 21:   - Example: `<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">`
 22:   - Uses `<TargetFramework>` instead of `<TargetFrameworkVersion>`
 23:   - Example: `<TargetFramework>net48</TargetFramework>`
 24: 
 25: - **No Implicit Imports in legacy projects**: Unlike SDK-style projects, legacy non-SDK projects do not automatically import common namespaces or assemblies
 26:  
 27: - **Build Configuration in legacy projects**: Contains explicit `<PropertyGroup>` sections for Debug/Release configurations
 28: 
 29: - **Output Paths in legacy projects**: Explicit `<OutputPath>` and `<IntermediateOutputPath>` definitions
 30: 
 31: - **Target Framework in legacy projects**: Uses `<TargetFrameworkVersion>` instead of `<TargetFramework>`
 32:   - Example: `<TargetFrameworkVersion>v4.7.2</TargetFrameworkVersion>`
 33: 
 34: ## NuGet Package Management
 35: - Installing and updating NuGet packages in .NET Framework projects is a complex task requiring coordinated changes to multiple files. Therefore, **do not attempt to install or update NuGet packages** in this project.
 36: - Instead, if changes to NuGet references are required, ask the user to install or update NuGet packages using the Visual Studio NuGet Package Manager or Visual Studio package manager console.
 37: - When recommending NuGet packages, ensure they are compatible with .NET Framework or .NET Standard 2.0 (not only .NET Core or .NET 5+).
 38: 
 39: ## C# Language Version is 7.3
 40: - This project is limited to C# 7.3 features only. Please avoid using:
 41: 
 42: ### C# 8.0+ Features (NOT SUPPORTED):
 43:   - Using declarations (`using var stream = ...`)
 44:   - Await using statements (`await using var resource = ...`)
 45:   - Switch expressions (`variable switch { ... }`)
 46:   - Null-coalescing assignment (`??=`)
 47:   - Range and index operators (`array[1..^1]`, `array[^1]`)
 48:   - Default interface methods
 49:   - Readonly members in structs
 50:   - Static local functions
 51:   - Nullable reference types (`string?`, `#nullable enable`)
 52: 
 53: ### C# 9.0+ Features (NOT SUPPORTED):
 54:   - Records (`public record Person(string Name)`)
 55:   - Init-only properties (`{ get; init; }`)
 56:   - Top-level programs (program without Main method)
 57:   - Pattern matching enhancements
 58:   - Target-typed new expressions (`List<string> list = new()`)
 59: 
 60: ### C# 10+ Features (NOT SUPPORTED):
 61:   - Global using statements
 62:   - File-scoped namespaces
 63:   - Record structs
 64:   - Required members
 65: 
 66: ### Use Instead (C# 7.3 Compatible):
 67:   - Traditional using statements with braces
 68:   - Switch statements instead of switch expressions
 69:   - Explicit null checks instead of null-coalescing assignment
 70:   - Array slicing with manual indexing
 71:   - Abstract classes or interfaces instead of default interface methods
 72: 
 73: ## Environment Considerations (Windows environment)
 74: - Use Windows-style paths with backslashes (e.g., `C:\path\to\file.cs`)
 75: - Use Windows-appropriate commands when suggesting terminal operations
 76: - Consider Windows-specific behaviors when working with file system operations
 77: 
 78: ## Common .NET Framework Pitfalls and Best Practices
 79: 
 80: ### Async/Await Patterns
 81: - **ConfigureAwait(false)**: Always use `ConfigureAwait(false)` in library code to avoid deadlocks:
 82:   ```csharp
 83:   var result = await SomeAsyncMethod().ConfigureAwait(false);
 84:   ```
 85: - **Avoid sync-over-async**: Don't use `.Result` or `.Wait()` or `.GetAwaiter().GetResult()`. These sync-over-async patterns can lead to deadlocks and poor performance. Always use `await` for asynchronous calls.
 86: 
 87: ### DateTime Handling
 88: - **Use DateTimeOffset for timestamps**: Prefer `DateTimeOffset` over `DateTime` for absolute time points
 89: - **Specify DateTimeKind**: When using `DateTime`, always specify `DateTimeKind.Utc` or `DateTimeKind.Local`
 90: - **Culture-aware formatting**: Use `CultureInfo.InvariantCulture` for serialization/parsing
 91: 
 92: ### String Operations
 93: - **StringBuilder for concatenation**: Use `StringBuilder` for multiple string concatenations
 94: - **StringComparison**: Always specify `StringComparison` for string operations:
 95:   ```csharp
 96:   string.Equals(other, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
 97:   ```
 98: 
 99: ### Memory Management
100: - **Dispose pattern**: Implement `IDisposable` properly for unmanaged resources
101: - **Using statements**: Always wrap `IDisposable` objects in using statements
102: - **Avoid large object heap**: Keep objects under 85KB to avoid LOH allocation
103: 
104: ### Configuration
105: - **Use ConfigurationManager**: Access app settings through `ConfigurationManager.AppSettings`
106: - **Connection strings**: Store in `<connectionStrings>` section, not `<appSettings>`
107: - **Transformations**: Use web.config/app.config transformations for environment-specific settings
108: 
109: ### Exception Handling
110: - **Specific exceptions**: Catch specific exception types, not generic `Exception`
111: - **Don't swallow exceptions**: Always log or re-throw exceptions appropriately
112: - **Use using for disposable resources**: Ensures proper cleanup even when exceptions occur
113: 
114: ### Performance Considerations
115: - **Avoid boxing**: Be aware of boxing/unboxing with value types and generics
116: - **String interning**: Use `string.Intern()` judiciously for frequently used strings
117: - **Lazy initialization**: Use `Lazy<T>` for expensive object creation
118: - **Avoid reflection in hot paths**: Cache `MethodInfo`, `PropertyInfo` objects when possible
</file>

<file path=".github/instructions/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend--instructions-dotnet-maui-9-to-dotnet-maui-10-upgrade-instructions-md-8146e176.instructions.md">
   1: ---
   2: description: 'Instructions for upgrading .NET MAUI applications from version 9 to version 10, including breaking changes, deprecated APIs, and migration strategies for ListView to CollectionView.'
   3: applyTo: '**/*.csproj, **/*.cs, **/*.xaml'
   4: ---
   5: 
   6: # Upgrading from .NET MAUI 9 to .NET MAUI 10
   7: 
   8: This guide helps you upgrade your .NET MAUI application from .NET 9 to .NET 10 by focusing on the critical breaking changes and obsolete APIs that require code updates.
   9: 
  10: ---
  11: 
  12: ## Table of Contents
  13: 
  14: 1. [Quick Start](#quick-start)
  15: 2. [Update Target Framework](#update-target-framework)
  16: 3. [Breaking Changes (P0 - Must Fix)](#breaking-changes-p0---must-fix)
  17:    - [MessagingCenter Made Internal](#messagingcenter-made-internal)
  18:    - [ListView and TableView Deprecated](#listview-and-tableview-deprecated)
  19: 4. [Deprecated APIs (P1 - Fix Soon)](#deprecated-apis-p1---fix-soon)
  20:    - [Animation Methods](#1-animation-methods)
  21:    - [DisplayAlert and DisplayActionSheet](#2-displayalert-and-displayactionsheet)
  22:    - [Page.IsBusy](#3-pageisbusy)
  23:    - [MediaPicker APIs](#4-mediapicker-apis)
  24: 5. [Recommended Changes (P2)](#recommended-changes-p2)
  25: 6. [Bulk Migration Tools](#bulk-migration-tools)
  26: 7. [Testing Your Upgrade](#testing-your-upgrade)
  27: 8. [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting)
  28: 
  29: ---
  30: 
  31: ## Quick Start
  32: 
  33: **Five-Step Upgrade Process:**
  34: 
  35: 1. **Update TargetFramework** to `net10.0`
  36: 2. **Update CommunityToolkit.Maui** to 12.3.0+ (if you use it) - REQUIRED
  37: 3. **Fix breaking changes** - MessagingCenter (P0)
  38: 4. **Migrate ListView/TableView to CollectionView** (P0 - CRITICAL)
  39: 5. **Fix deprecated APIs** - Animation methods, DisplayAlert, IsBusy, MediaPicker (P1)
  40: 
  41: > ⚠️ **Major Breaking Changes**: 
  42: > - CommunityToolkit.Maui **must** be version 12.3.0 or later
  43: > - ListView and TableView are now obsolete (most significant migration effort)
  44: 
  45: ---
  46: 
  47: ## Update Target Framework
  48: 
  49: ### Single Platform
  50: 
  51: ```xml
  52: <Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  53:   <PropertyGroup>
  54:     <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
  55:   </PropertyGroup>
  56: </Project>
  57: ```
  58: 
  59: ### Multi-Platform
  60: 
  61: ```xml
  62: <Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  63:   <PropertyGroup>
  64:     <TargetFrameworks>net10.0-android;net10.0-ios;net10.0-maccatalyst;net10.0-windows10.0.19041.0</TargetFrameworks>
  65:   </PropertyGroup>
  66: </Project>
  67: ```
  68: 
  69: ### Optional: Linux Compatibility (GitHub Copilot, WSL, etc.)
  70: 
  71: > 💡 **For Linux Development**: If you're building on Linux (e.g., GitHub Codespaces, WSL, or using GitHub Copilot), you can make your project compile on Linux by conditionally excluding iOS/Mac Catalyst targets:
  72: 
  73: ```xml
  74: <Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  75:   <PropertyGroup>
  76:     <!-- Start with Android (always supported) -->
  77:     <TargetFrameworks>net10.0-android</TargetFrameworks>
  78:     
  79:     <!-- Add iOS/Mac Catalyst only when NOT on Linux -->
  80:     <TargetFrameworks Condition="!$([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('linux'))">$(TargetFrameworks);net10.0-ios;net10.0-maccatalyst</TargetFrameworks>
  81:     
  82:     <!-- Add Windows only when on Windows -->
  83:     <TargetFrameworks Condition="$([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('windows'))">$(TargetFrameworks);net10.0-windows10.0.19041.0</TargetFrameworks>
  84:   </PropertyGroup>
  85: </Project>
  86: ```
  87: 
  88: **Benefits:**
  89: - ✅ Compiles successfully on Linux (no iOS/Mac tools required)
  90: - ✅ Works with GitHub Codespaces and Copilot
  91: - ✅ Automatically includes correct targets based on build OS
  92: - ✅ No changes needed when switching between OS environments
  93: 
  94: **Reference:** [dotnet/maui#32186](https://github.com/dotnet/maui/pull/32186)
  95: 
  96: ### Update Required NuGet Packages
  97: 
  98: > ⚠️ **CRITICAL**: If you use CommunityToolkit.Maui, you **must** update to version 12.3.0 or later. Earlier versions are not compatible with .NET 10 and will cause compilation errors.
  99: 
 100: ```bash
 101: # Update CommunityToolkit.Maui (if you use it)
