  5: 
  6: # Kubernetes Manifests Instructions
  7: 
  8: ## Your Mission
  9: 
 10: Create production-ready Kubernetes manifests that prioritize security, reliability, and operational excellence with consistent labeling, proper resource management, and comprehensive health checks.
 11: 
 12: ## Labeling Conventions
 13: 
 14: **Required Labels** (Kubernetes recommended):
 15: - `app.kubernetes.io/name`: Application name
 16: - `app.kubernetes.io/instance`: Instance identifier
 17: - `app.kubernetes.io/version`: Version
 18: - `app.kubernetes.io/component`: Component role
 19: - `app.kubernetes.io/part-of`: Application group
 20: - `app.kubernetes.io/managed-by`: Management tool
 21: 
 22: **Additional Labels**:
 23: - `environment`: Environment name
 24: - `team`: Owning team
 25: - `cost-center`: For billing
 26: 
 27: **Useful Annotations**:
 28: - Documentation and ownership
 29: - Monitoring: `prometheus.io/scrape`, `prometheus.io/port`, `prometheus.io/path`
 30: - Change tracking: git commit, deployment date
 31: 
 32: ## SecurityContext Defaults
 33: 
 34: **Pod-level**:
 35: - `runAsNonRoot: true`
 36: - `runAsUser` and `runAsGroup`: Specific IDs
 37: - `fsGroup`: File system group
 38: - `seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault`
 39: 
 40: **Container-level**:
 41: - `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`
 42: - `readOnlyRootFilesystem: true` (with tmpfs mounts for writable dirs)
 43: - `capabilities.drop: [ALL]` (add only what's needed)
 44: 
 45: ## Pod Security Standards
 46: 
 47: Use Pod Security Admission:
 48: - **Restricted** (recommended for production): Enforces security hardening
 49: - **Baseline**: Minimal security requirements
 50: - Apply at namespace level
 51: 
 52: ## Resource Requests and Limits
 53: 
 54: **Always define**:
 55: - Requests: Guaranteed minimum (scheduling)
 56: - Limits: Maximum allowed (prevents exhaustion)
 57: 
 58: **QoS Classes**:
 59: - **Guaranteed**: requests == limits (best for critical apps)
 60: - **Burstable**: requests < limits (flexible resource use)
 61: - **BestEffort**: No resources defined (avoid in production)
 62: 
 63: ## Health Probes
 64: 
 65: **Liveness**: Restart unhealthy containers
 66: **Readiness**: Control traffic routing
 67: **Startup**: Protect slow-starting applications
 68: 
 69: Configure appropriate delays, periods, timeouts, and thresholds for each.
 70: 
 71: ## Rollout Strategies
 72: 
 73: **Deployment Strategy**:
 74: - `RollingUpdate` with `maxSurge` and `maxUnavailable`
 75: - Set `maxUnavailable: 0` for zero-downtime
 76: 
 77: **High Availability**:
 78: - Minimum 2-3 replicas
 79: - Pod Disruption Budget (PDB)
 80: - Anti-affinity rules (spread across nodes/zones)
 81: - Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) for variable load
 82: 
 83: ## Validation Commands
 84: 
 85: **Pre-deployment**:
 86: - `kubectl apply --dry-run=client -f manifest.yaml`
 87: - `kubectl apply --dry-run=server -f manifest.yaml`
 88: - `kubeconform -strict manifest.yaml` (schema validation)
 89: - `helm template ./chart | kubeconform -strict` (for Helm)
 90: 
 91: **Policy Validation**:
 92: - OPA Conftest, Kyverno, or Datree
 93: 
 94: ## Rollout & Rollback
 95: 
 96: **Deploy**:
 97: - `kubectl apply -f manifest.yaml`
 98: - `kubectl rollout status deployment/NAME`
 99: 
100: **Rollback**:
101: - `kubectl rollout undo deployment/NAME`
102: - `kubectl rollout undo deployment/NAME --to-revision=N`
103: - `kubectl rollout history deployment/NAME`
104: 
105: **Restart**:
106: - `kubectl rollout restart deployment/NAME`
107: 
108: ## Manifest Checklist
109: 
110: - [ ] Labels: Standard labels applied
111: - [ ] Annotations: Documentation and monitoring
112: - [ ] Security: runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem, dropped capabilities
113: - [ ] Resources: Requests and limits defined
114: - [ ] Probes: Liveness, readiness, startup configured
115: - [ ] Images: Specific tags (never :latest)
116: - [ ] Replicas: Minimum 2-3 for production
117: - [ ] Strategy: RollingUpdate with appropriate surge/unavailable
118: - [ ] PDB: Defined for production
119: - [ ] Anti-affinity: Configured for HA
120: - [ ] Graceful shutdown: terminationGracePeriodSeconds set
121: - [ ] Validation: Dry-run and kubeconform passed
122: - [ ] Secrets: In Secrets resource, not ConfigMaps
123: - [ ] NetworkPolicy: Least-privilege access (if applicable)
124: 
125: ## Best Practices Summary
126: 
127: 1. Use standard labels and annotations
128: 2. Always run as non-root with dropped capabilities
129: 3. Define resource requests and limits
130: 4. Implement all three probe types
131: 5. Pin image tags to specific versions
132: 6. Configure anti-affinity for HA
133: 7. Set Pod Disruption Budgets
134: 8. Use rolling updates with zero unavailability
135: 9. Validate manifests before applying
136: 10. Enable read-only root filesystem when possible
</file>

<file path=".github/instructions/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud--instructions-qa-engineering-best-practices-instructions-md-7d759f9e.instructions.md">
  1: ---
