 98: ## Scope
 99: 
100: - ✅ App configuration modification to ensure config builders are used to read app settings and connection strings from the environment variables
101: - ✅ Dockerfile creation and configuration for an ASP.NET application
102: - ✅ Specifying multiple stages in the Dockerfile to build/publish the application and copy the output to the final image
103: - ✅ Configuration of Windows container platform compatibility (Windows Server Core or Full)
104: - ✅ Proper handling of dependencies (GAC assemblies, MSIs, COM components)
105: - ❌ No infrastructure setup (assumed to be handled separately)
106: - ❌ No code changes beyond those required for containerization
107: 
108: ## Execution Process
109: 
110: 1. Review the containerization settings above to understand the containerization requirements
111: 2. Create a `progress.md` file to track changes with check marks
112: 3. Determine the .NET Framework version from the project's .csproj file by checking the `TargetFrameworkVersion` element
113: 4. Select the appropriate Windows Server container image based on:
114:    - The .NET Framework version detected from the project
115:    - The Windows Server SKU specified in containerization settings (Core or Full)
116:    - The Windows Server version specified in containerization settings (2016, 2019, or 2022)
117:    - Windows Server Core tags can be found at: https://github.com/microsoft/dotnet-framework-docker/blob/main/README.aspnet.md#full-tag-listing
118: 5. Ensure that required NuGet packages are installed. **DO NOT** install these if they are missing. If they are not installed, the user must install them manually. If they are not installed, pause executing this prompt and ask the user to install them using the Visual Studio NuGet Package Manager or Visual Studio package manager console. The following packages are required:
119:    - `Microsoft.Configuration.ConfigurationBuilders.Environment`
120: 6. Modify the `web.config` file to add configuration builders section and settings to read app settings and connection strings from environment variables:
121:    - Add ConfigBuilders section in configSections
122:    - Add configBuilders section in the root
123:    - Configure EnvironmentConfigBuilder for both appSettings and connectionStrings
124:    - Example pattern:
125:      ```xml
126:      <configSections>
127:        <section name="configBuilders" type="System.Configuration.ConfigurationBuildersSection, System.Configuration, Version=4.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=b03f5f7f11d50a3a" restartOnExternalChanges="false" requirePermission="false" />
128:      </configSections>
129:      <configBuilders>
130:        <builders>
131:          <add name="Environment" type="Microsoft.Configuration.ConfigurationBuilders.EnvironmentConfigBuilder, Microsoft.Configuration.ConfigurationBuilders.Environment" />
132:        </builders>
133:      </configBuilders>
134:      <appSettings configBuilders="Environment">
135:        <!-- existing app settings -->
136:      </appSettings>
137:      <connectionStrings configBuilders="Environment">
138:        <!-- existing connection strings -->
139:      </connectionStrings>
140:      ```
141: 7. Create a `LogMonitorConfig.json` file in the folder where the Dockerfile will be created by copying the reference `LogMonitorConfig.json` file at the end of this prompt. The file's contents **MUST NOT** not be modified and should match the reference content exactly unless instructions in containerization settings specify otherwise.
142:    - In particular, make sure the level of issues to be logged is not changed as using `Information` level for EventLog sources will cause unnecessary noise.
143: 8. Create a Dockerfile in the root of the project directory to containerize the application
144:    - The Dockerfile should use multiple stages:
145:      - Build stage: Use a Windows Server Core image to build the application
146:        - The build stage MUST use a `mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/framework/sdk` base image unless a custom base image is specified in the settings file
147:        - Copy sln, csproj, and packages.config files first
148:        - Copy NuGet.config if one exists and configure any private feeds
149:        - Restore NuGet packages       
150:        - Then, copy the rest of the source code and build and publish the application to C:\publish using MSBuild
151:      - Final stage: Use the selected Windows Server image to run the application
152:        - The final stage MUST use a `mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/framework/aspnet` base image unless a custom base image is specified in the settings file
153:        - Copy the `LogMonitorConfig.json` file to a directory in the container (e.g., C:\LogMonitor)
154:        - Download LogMonitor.exe from the Microsoft repository to the same directory
155:            - The correct LogMonitor.exe URL is: https://github.com/microsoft/windows-container-tools/releases/download/v2.1.1/LogMonitor.exe
156:        - Set the working directory to C:\inetpub\wwwroot
157:        - Copy the published output from the build stage (in C:\publish) to the final image
158:        - Set the container's entry point to run LogMonitor.exe with ServiceMonitor.exe to monitor the IIS service
159:            - `ENTRYPOINT [ "C:\\LogMonitor\\LogMonitor.exe", "C:\\ServiceMonitor.exe", "w3svc" ]`
160:    - Be sure to consider all requirements in the containerization settings:
161:      - Windows Server SKU and version
162:      - Exposed ports
163:      - User account for container
164:      - IIS settings
165:      - GAC assembly registration
166:      - MSI installation
167:      - COM component registration
168:      - Registry keys
169:      - Environment variables
170:      - Windows roles and features
171:      - File/directory copying
172:    - Model the Dockerfile after the example provided at the end of this prompt, but ensure it is customized to the specific project requirements and settings.
173:    - **IMPORTANT:** Use a Windows Server Core base image unless the user has **specifically requested** a full Windows Server image in the settings file
174: 9. Create a `.dockerignore` file in the root of the project directory to exclude unnecessary files from the Docker image. The `.dockerignore` file **MUST** include at least the following elements as well as additional patterns as specified in the containerization settings:
175:    - packages/
176:    - bin/
177:    - obj/
178:    - .dockerignore
179:    - Dockerfile
180:    - .git/
181:    - .github/
182:    - .vs/
183:    - .vscode/
184:    - **/node_modules/
185:    - *.user
186:    - *.suo
187:    - **/.DS_Store
188:    - **/Thumbs.db
189:    - Any additional patterns specified in the containerization settings
190: 10. Configure health checks if specified in the settings:
191:    - Add HEALTHCHECK instruction to Dockerfile if health check endpoint is provided
192: 11. Add the dockerfile to the project by adding the following item to the project file: `<None Include="Dockerfile" />`
193: 12. Mark tasks as completed: [ ] → [✓]
194: 13. Continue until all tasks are complete and Docker build succeeds
195: 
196: ## Build and Runtime Verification
197: 
198: confirm that Docker build succeeds once the Dockerfile is completed. Use the following command to build the Docker image:
199: 
200: ```bash
201: docker build -t aspnet-app:latest .
202: ```
203: 
204: If the build fails, review the error messages and make necessary adjustments to the Dockerfile or project configuration. Report success/failure.
