103: - ✅ Dockerfile creation and configuration for an ASP.NET Core application
104: - ✅ Specifying multiple stages in the Dockerfile to build/publish the application and copy the output to the final image
105: - ✅ Configuration of Linux container platform compatibility (Alpine, Ubuntu, Chiseled, or Azure Linux (Mariner))
106: - ✅ Proper handling of dependencies (system packages, native libraries, additional tools)
107: - ❌ No infrastructure setup (assumed to be handled separately)
108: - ❌ No code changes beyond those required for containerization
109: 
110: ## Execution Process
111: 
112: 1. Review the containerization settings above to understand the containerization requirements
113: 2. Create a `progress.md` file to track changes with check marks
114: 3. Determine the .NET version from the project's .csproj file by checking the `TargetFramework` element
115: 4. Select the appropriate Linux container image based on:
116:    - The .NET version detected from the project
117:    - The Linux distribution specified in containerization settings (Alpine, Ubuntu, Chiseled, or Azure Linux (Mariner))
118:    - If the user does not request specific base images in the containerization settings, then the base images MUST be valid mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet images with a tag as shown in the example Dockerfile, below, or in documentation
119:    - Official Microsoft .NET images for build and runtime stages:
120:       - SDK image tags (for build stage): https://github.com/dotnet/dotnet-docker/blob/main/README.sdk.md
121:       - ASP.NET Core runtime image tags: https://github.com/dotnet/dotnet-docker/blob/main/README.aspnet.md
122:       - .NET runtime image tags: https://github.com/dotnet/dotnet-docker/blob/main/README.runtime.md
123: 5. Create a Dockerfile in the root of the project directory to containerize the application
124:    - The Dockerfile should use multiple stages:
125:      - Build stage: Use a .NET SDK image to build the application
126:        - Copy csproj file(s) first
127:        - Copy NuGet.config if one exists and configure any private feeds
128:        - Restore NuGet packages
129:        - Then, copy the rest of the source code and build and publish the application to /app/publish
130:      - Final stage: Use the selected .NET runtime image to run the application
131:        - Set the working directory to /app
132:        - Set the user as directed (by default, to a non-root user (e.g., `$APP_UID`))
133:          - Unless directed otherwise in containerization settings, a new user does *not* need to be created. Use the `$APP_UID` variable to specify the user account.
134:        - Copy the published output from the build stage to the final image
135:    - Be sure to consider all requirements in the containerization settings:
136:      - .NET version and Linux distribution
137:      - Exposed ports
138:      - User account for container
139:      - ASPNETCORE_URLS configuration
140:      - System package installation
141:      - Native library dependencies
142:      - Additional .NET tools
143:      - Environment variables
144:      - File/directory copying
145:      - Volume mount points
146:      - Health check configuration
147: 6. Create a `.dockerignore` file in the root of the project directory to exclude unnecessary files from the Docker image. The `.dockerignore` file **MUST** include at least the following elements as well as additional patterns as specified in the containerization settings:
148:    - bin/
149:    - obj/
150:    - .dockerignore
151:    - Dockerfile
152:    - .git/
153:    - .github/
154:    - .vs/
155:    - .vscode/
156:    - **/node_modules/
157:    - *.user
158:    - *.suo
159:    - **/.DS_Store
160:    - **/Thumbs.db
161:    - Any additional patterns specified in the containerization settings
162: 7. Configure health checks if specified in the containerization settings:
163:    - Add HEALTHCHECK instruction to Dockerfile if health check endpoint is provided
164:    - Use curl or wget to check the health endpoint
165: 8. Mark tasks as completed: [ ] → [✓]
166: 9. Continue until all tasks are complete and Docker build succeeds
167: 
168: ## Build and Runtime Verification
169: 
170: Confirm that Docker build succeeds once the Dockerfile is completed. Use the following command to build the Docker image:
171: 
172: ```bash
173: docker build -t aspnetcore-app:latest .
174: ```
175: 
176: If the build fails, review the error messages and make necessary adjustments to the Dockerfile or project configuration. Report success/failure.
177: 
178: ## Progress Tracking
179: 
180: Maintain a `progress.md` file with the following structure:
181: ```markdown
182: # Containerization Progress
183: 
184: ## Environment Detection
185: - [ ] .NET version detection (version: ___)
186: - [ ] Linux distribution selection (distribution: ___)
187: 
188: ## Configuration Changes
189: - [ ] Application configuration verification for environment variable support
190: - [ ] NuGet package source configuration (if applicable)
191: 
192: ## Containerization
193: - [ ] Dockerfile creation
194: - [ ] .dockerignore file creation
195: - [ ] Build stage created with SDK image
196: - [ ] csproj file(s) copied for package restore
197: - [ ] NuGet.config copied if applicable
198: - [ ] Runtime stage created with runtime image
199: - [ ] Non-root user configuration
200: - [ ] Dependency handling (system packages, native libraries, tools, etc.)
201: - [ ] Health check configuration (if applicable)
202: - [ ] Special requirements implementation
203: 
204: ## Verification
205: - [ ] Review containerization settings and make sure that all requirements are met
206: - [ ] Docker build success
207: ```
208: 
209: Do not pause for confirmation between steps. Continue methodically until the application has been containerized and Docker build succeeds.
210: 
211: **YOU ARE NOT DONE UNTIL ALL CHECKBOXES ARE MARKED!** This includes building the Docker image successfully and addressing any issues that arise during the build process.
212: 
213: ## Example Dockerfile
214: 
215: An example Dockerfile for an ASP.NET Core (.NET) application using a Linux base image.
216: 
217: ```dockerfile
218: # ============================================================
219: # Stage 1: Build and publish the application
220: # ============================================================
221: 
222: # Base Image - Select the appropriate .NET SDK version and Linux distribution
223: # Possible tags include:
224: # - 8.0-bookworm-slim (Debian 12)
225: # - 8.0-noble (Ubuntu 24.04)
226: # - 8.0-alpine (Alpine Linux)
227: # - 9.0-bookworm-slim (Debian 12)
228: # - 9.0-noble (Ubuntu 24.04)
229: # - 9.0-alpine (Alpine Linux)
230: # Uses the .NET SDK image for building the application
231: FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:8.0-bookworm-slim AS build
232: ARG BUILD_CONFIGURATION=Release
233: 
234: WORKDIR /src
235: 
236: # Copy project files first for better caching
237: COPY ["YourProject/YourProject.csproj", "YourProject/"]
238: COPY ["YourOtherProject/YourOtherProject.csproj", "YourOtherProject/"]
239: 
240: # Copy NuGet configuration if it exists
241: COPY ["NuGet.config", "."]
242: 
243: # Restore NuGet packages
244: RUN dotnet restore "YourProject/YourProject.csproj"
245: 
246: # Copy source code
247: COPY . .
248: 
249: # Perform custom pre-build steps here, if needed
250: # RUN echo "Running pre-build steps..."
251: 
252: # Build and publish the application
253: WORKDIR "/src/YourProject"
254: RUN dotnet build "YourProject.csproj" -c $BUILD_CONFIGURATION -o /app/build
255: 
256: # Publish the application
257: RUN dotnet publish "YourProject.csproj" -c $BUILD_CONFIGURATION -o /app/publish /p:UseAppHost=false
258: 
259: # Perform custom post-build steps here, if needed
260: # RUN echo "Running post-build steps..."
261: 
262: # ============================================================
263: # Stage 2: Final runtime image
264: # ============================================================
265: 
266: # Base Image - Select the appropriate .NET runtime version and Linux distribution
267: # Possible tags include:
268: # - 8.0-bookworm-slim (Debian 12)
269: # - 8.0-noble (Ubuntu 24.04)
270: # - 8.0-alpine (Alpine Linux)
271: # - 8.0-noble-chiseled (Ubuntu 24.04 Chiseled)
272: # - 8.0-azurelinux3.0 (Azure Linux)
273: # - 9.0-bookworm-slim (Debian 12)
274: # - 9.0-noble (Ubuntu 24.04)
275: # - 9.0-alpine (Alpine Linux)
276: # - 9.0-noble-chiseled (Ubuntu 24.04 Chiseled)
277: # - 9.0-azurelinux3.0 (Azure Linux)
278: # Uses the .NET runtime image for running the application
279: FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:8.0-bookworm-slim AS final
280: 
281: # Install system packages if needed (uncomment and modify as needed)
282: # RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y \
283: #     curl \
284: #     wget \
285: #     ca-certificates \
286: #     libgdiplus \
287: #     && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
288: 
289: # Install additional .NET tools if needed (uncomment and modify as needed)
290: # RUN dotnet tool install --global dotnet-ef --version 8.0.0
291: # ENV PATH="$PATH:/root/.dotnet/tools"
292: 
293: WORKDIR /app
294: 
295: # Copy published application from build stage
296: COPY --from=build /app/publish .
297: 
298: # Copy additional files if needed (uncomment and modify as needed)
299: # COPY ./config/appsettings.Production.json .
300: # COPY ./certificates/ ./certificates/
301: 
302: # Set environment variables
303: ENV ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production
304: ENV ASPNETCORE_URLS=http://+:8080
305: 
306: # Add custom environment variables if needed (uncomment and modify as needed)
307: # ENV CONNECTIONSTRINGS__DEFAULTCONNECTION="your-connection-string"
308: # ENV FEATURE_FLAG_ENABLED=true
309: 
310: # Configure SSL/TLS certificates if needed (uncomment and modify as needed)
311: # ENV ASPNETCORE_Kestrel__Certificates__Default__Path=/app/certificates/app.pfx
312: # ENV ASPNETCORE_Kestrel__Certificates__Default__Password=your_password
313: 
314: # Expose the port the application listens on
315: EXPOSE 8080
316: # EXPOSE 8081  # Uncomment if using HTTPS
317: 
318: # Install curl for health checks if not already present
319: RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y curl && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
320: 
321: # Configure health check
322: HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=3s --start-period=5s --retries=3 \
323:     CMD curl -f http://localhost:8080/health || exit 1
324: 
325: # Create volumes for persistent data if needed (uncomment and modify as needed)
326: # VOLUME ["/app/data", "/app/logs"]
327: 
328: # Switch to non-root user for security
329: USER $APP_UID
330: 
331: # Set the entry point for the application
332: ENTRYPOINT ["dotnet", "YourProject.dll"]
333: ```
334: 
335: ## Adapting this Example
336: 
337: **Note:** Customize this template based on the specific requirements in containerization settings.
338: 
339: When adapting this example Dockerfile:
340: 
341: 1. Replace `YourProject.csproj`, `YourProject.dll`, etc. with your actual project names
342: 2. Adjust the .NET version and Linux distribution as needed
343: 3. Modify the dependency installation steps based on your requirements and remove any unnecessary ones
344: 4. Configure environment variables specific to your application
345: 5. Add or remove stages as needed for your specific workflow
346: 6. Update the health check endpoint to match your application's health check route
347: 
348: ## Linux Distribution Variations
349: 
350: ### Alpine Linux
351: For smaller image sizes, you can use Alpine Linux:
352: 
353: ```dockerfile
354: FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:8.0-alpine AS build
355: # ... build steps ...
356: 
357: FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:8.0-alpine AS final
358: # Install packages using apk
359: RUN apk update && apk add --no-cache curl ca-certificates
360: ```
361: 
362: ### Ubuntu Chiseled
363: For minimal attack surface, consider using chiseled images:
364: 
365: ```dockerfile
366: FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:8.0-jammy-chiseled AS final
367: # Note: Chiseled images have minimal packages, so you may need to use a different base for additional dependencies
368: ```
369: 
370: ### Azure Linux (Mariner)
371: For Azure-optimized containers:
372: 
373: ```dockerfile
374: FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:8.0-azurelinux3.0 AS final
375: # Install packages using tdnf
376: RUN tdnf update -y && tdnf install -y curl ca-certificates && tdnf clean all
377: ```
378: 
379: ## Notes on Stage Naming
380: 
381: - The `AS stage-name` syntax gives each stage a name
382: - Use `--from=stage-name` to copy files from a previous stage
383: - You can have multiple intermediate stages that aren't used in the final image
384: - The `final` stage is the one that becomes the final container image
385: 
386: ## Security Best Practices
387: 
388: - Always run as a non-root user in production
389: - Use specific image tags instead of `latest`
390: - Minimize the number of installed packages
391: - Keep base images updated
392: - Use multi-stage builds to exclude build dependencies from the final image
</file>

