  1: ---
  2: name: csharp-mstest
  3: description: 'Get best practices for MSTest 3.x/4.x unit testing, including modern assertion APIs and data-driven tests'
  4: ---
  5: 
  6: # MSTest Best Practices (MSTest 3.x/4.x)
  7: 
  8: Your goal is to help me write effective unit tests with modern MSTest, using current APIs and best practices.
  9: 
 10: ## Project Setup
 11: 
 12: - Use a separate test project with naming convention `[ProjectName].Tests`
 13: - Reference MSTest 3.x+ NuGet packages (includes analyzers)
 14: - Consider using MSTest.Sdk for simplified project setup
 15: - Run tests with `dotnet test`
 16: 
 17: ## Test Class Structure
 18: 
 19: - Use `[TestClass]` attribute for test classes
 20: - **Seal test classes by default** for performance and design clarity
 21: - Use `[TestMethod]` for test methods (prefer over `[DataTestMethod]`)
 22: - Follow Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) pattern
 23: - Name tests using pattern `MethodName_Scenario_ExpectedBehavior`
 24: 
 25: ```csharp
 26: [TestClass]
 27: public sealed class CalculatorTests
 28: {
 29:     [TestMethod]
 30:     public void Add_TwoPositiveNumbers_ReturnsSum()
 31:     {
 32:         // Arrange
 33:         var calculator = new Calculator();
 34: 
 35:         // Act
 36:         var result = calculator.Add(2, 3);
 37: 
 38:         // Assert
 39:         Assert.AreEqual(5, result);
 40:     }
 41: }
 42: ```
 43: 
 44: ## Test Lifecycle
 45: 
 46: - **Prefer constructors over `[TestInitialize]`** - enables `readonly` fields and follows standard C# patterns
 47: - Use `[TestCleanup]` for cleanup that must run even if test fails
 48: - Combine constructor with async `[TestInitialize]` when async setup is needed
 49: 
 50: ```csharp
 51: [TestClass]
 52: public sealed class ServiceTests
 53: {
 54:     private readonly MyService _service;  // readonly enabled by constructor
 55: 
 56:     public ServiceTests()
 57:     {
 58:         _service = new MyService();
 59:     }
 60: 
 61:     [TestInitialize]
 62:     public async Task InitAsync()
 63:     {
 64:         // Use for async initialization only
 65:         await _service.WarmupAsync();
 66:     }
 67: 
 68:     [TestCleanup]
 69:     public void Cleanup() => _service.Reset();
 70: }
 71: ```
 72: 
 73: ### Execution Order
 74: 
 75: 1. **Assembly Initialization** - `[AssemblyInitialize]` (once per test assembly)
 76: 2. **Class Initialization** - `[ClassInitialize]` (once per test class)
 77: 3. **Test Initialization** (for every test method):
 78:    1. Constructor
 79:    2. Set `TestContext` property
 80:    3. `[TestInitialize]`
 81: 4. **Test Execution** - test method runs
 82: 5. **Test Cleanup** (for every test method):
 83:    1. `[TestCleanup]`
 84:    2. `DisposeAsync` (if implemented)
 85:    3. `Dispose` (if implemented)
 86: 6. **Class Cleanup** - `[ClassCleanup]` (once per test class)
 87: 7. **Assembly Cleanup** - `[AssemblyCleanup]` (once per test assembly)
 88: 
 89: ## Modern Assertion APIs
 90: 
 91: MSTest provides three assertion classes: `Assert`, `StringAssert`, and `CollectionAssert`.
 92: 
 93: ### Assert Class - Core Assertions
 94: 
 95: ```csharp
 96: // Equality
 97: Assert.AreEqual(expected, actual);
 98: Assert.AreNotEqual(notExpected, actual);
 99: Assert.AreSame(expectedObject, actualObject);      // Reference equality
100: Assert.AreNotSame(notExpectedObject, actualObject);
101: 
102: // Null checks
103: Assert.IsNull(value);
104: Assert.IsNotNull(value);
105: 
106: // Boolean
107: Assert.IsTrue(condition);
108: Assert.IsFalse(condition);
109: 
110: // Fail/Inconclusive
111: Assert.Fail("Test failed due to...");
112: Assert.Inconclusive("Test cannot be completed because...");
113: ```
114: 
115: ### Exception Testing (Prefer over `[ExpectedException]`)
116: 
117: ```csharp
118: // Assert.Throws - matches TException or derived types
119: var ex = Assert.Throws<ArgumentException>(() => Method(null));
120: Assert.AreEqual("Value cannot be null.", ex.Message);
121: 
122: // Assert.ThrowsExactly - matches exact type only
123: var ex = Assert.ThrowsExactly<InvalidOperationException>(() => Method());
124: 
125: // Async versions
126: var ex = await Assert.ThrowsAsync<HttpRequestException>(async () => await client.GetAsync(url));
127: var ex = await Assert.ThrowsExactlyAsync<InvalidOperationException>(async () => await Method());
128: ```
129: 
130: ### Collection Assertions (Assert class)
131: 
132: ```csharp
133: Assert.Contains(expectedItem, collection);
134: Assert.DoesNotContain(unexpectedItem, collection);
135: Assert.ContainsSingle(collection);  // exactly one element
136: Assert.HasCount(5, collection);
137: Assert.IsEmpty(collection);
138: Assert.IsNotEmpty(collection);
139: ```
140: 
141: ### String Assertions (Assert class)
142: 
143: ```csharp
144: Assert.Contains("expected", actualString);
145: Assert.StartsWith("prefix", actualString);
146: Assert.EndsWith("suffix", actualString);
147: Assert.DoesNotStartWith("prefix", actualString);
148: Assert.DoesNotEndWith("suffix", actualString);
149: Assert.MatchesRegex(@"\d{3}-\d{4}", phoneNumber);
150: Assert.DoesNotMatchRegex(@"\d+", textOnly);
151: ```
152: 
153: ### Comparison Assertions
154: 
155: ```csharp
156: Assert.IsGreaterThan(lowerBound, actual);
157: Assert.IsGreaterThanOrEqualTo(lowerBound, actual);
158: Assert.IsLessThan(upperBound, actual);
159: Assert.IsLessThanOrEqualTo(upperBound, actual);
160: Assert.IsInRange(actual, low, high);
161: Assert.IsPositive(number);
162: Assert.IsNegative(number);
163: ```
164: 
165: ### Type Assertions
166: 
167: ```csharp
168: // MSTest 3.x - uses out parameter
169: Assert.IsInstanceOfType<MyClass>(obj, out var typed);
170: typed.DoSomething();
171: 
172: // MSTest 4.x - returns typed result directly
173: var typed = Assert.IsInstanceOfType<MyClass>(obj);
174: typed.DoSomething();
175: 
176: Assert.IsNotInstanceOfType<WrongType>(obj);
177: ```
178: 
179: ### Assert.That (MSTest 4.0+)
180: 
181: ```csharp
182: Assert.That(result.Count > 0);  // Auto-captures expression in failure message
183: ```
184: 
185: ### StringAssert Class
186: 
187: > **Note:** Prefer `Assert` class equivalents when available (e.g., `Assert.Contains("expected", actual)` over `StringAssert.Contains(actual, "expected")`).
188: 
189: ```csharp
190: StringAssert.Contains(actualString, "expected");
191: StringAssert.StartsWith(actualString, "prefix");
192: StringAssert.EndsWith(actualString, "suffix");
193: StringAssert.Matches(actualString, new Regex(@"\d{3}-\d{4}"));
194: StringAssert.DoesNotMatch(actualString, new Regex(@"\d+"));
195: ```
196: 
197: ### CollectionAssert Class
198: 
199: > **Note:** Prefer `Assert` class equivalents when available (e.g., `Assert.Contains`).
200: 
201: ```csharp
202: // Containment
203: CollectionAssert.Contains(collection, expectedItem);
204: CollectionAssert.DoesNotContain(collection, unexpectedItem);
205: 
206: // Equality (same elements, same order)
207: CollectionAssert.AreEqual(expectedCollection, actualCollection);
208: CollectionAssert.AreNotEqual(unexpectedCollection, actualCollection);
209: 
210: // Equivalence (same elements, any order)
211: CollectionAssert.AreEquivalent(expectedCollection, actualCollection);
212: CollectionAssert.AreNotEquivalent(unexpectedCollection, actualCollection);
213: 
214: // Subset checks
215: CollectionAssert.IsSubsetOf(subset, superset);
216: CollectionAssert.IsNotSubsetOf(notSubset, collection);
217: 
218: // Element validation
219: CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreInstancesOfType(collection, typeof(MyClass));
220: CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreNotNull(collection);
221: CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreUnique(collection);
222: ```
223: 
224: ## Data-Driven Tests
225: 
226: ### DataRow
227: 
228: ```csharp
229: [TestMethod]
230: [DataRow(1, 2, 3)]
231: [DataRow(0, 0, 0, DisplayName = "Zeros")]
232: [DataRow(-1, 1, 0, IgnoreMessage = "Known issue #123")]  // MSTest 3.8+
233: public void Add_ReturnsSum(int a, int b, int expected)
234: {
235:     Assert.AreEqual(expected, Calculator.Add(a, b));
236: }
237: ```
238: 
239: ### DynamicData
240: 
241: The data source can return any of the following types:
242: 
243: - `IEnumerable<(T1, T2, ...)>` (ValueTuple) - **preferred**, provides type safety (MSTest 3.7+)
244: - `IEnumerable<Tuple<T1, T2, ...>>` - provides type safety
245: - `IEnumerable<TestDataRow>` - provides type safety plus control over test metadata (display name, categories)
246: - `IEnumerable<object[]>` - **least preferred**, no type safety
247: 
248: > **Note:** When creating new test data methods, prefer `ValueTuple` or `TestDataRow` over `IEnumerable<object[]>`. The `object[]` approach provides no compile-time type checking and can lead to runtime errors from type mismatches.
249: 
250: ```csharp
251: [TestMethod]
252: [DynamicData(nameof(TestData))]
253: public void DynamicTest(int a, int b, int expected)
254: {
255:     Assert.AreEqual(expected, Calculator.Add(a, b));
256: }
257: 
258: // ValueTuple - preferred (MSTest 3.7+)
259: public static IEnumerable<(int a, int b, int expected)> TestData =>
260: [
261:     (1, 2, 3),
262:     (0, 0, 0),
263: ];
264: 
265: // TestDataRow - when you need custom display names or metadata
266: public static IEnumerable<TestDataRow<(int a, int b, int expected)>> TestDataWithMetadata =>
267: [
268:     new((1, 2, 3)) { DisplayName = "Positive numbers" },
269:     new((0, 0, 0)) { DisplayName = "Zeros" },
270:     new((-1, 1, 0)) { DisplayName = "Mixed signs", IgnoreMessage = "Known issue #123" },
271: ];
272: 
273: // IEnumerable<object[]> - avoid for new code (no type safety)
274: public static IEnumerable<object[]> LegacyTestData =>
275: [
276:     [1, 2, 3],
277:     [0, 0, 0],
278: ];
279: ```
280: 
281: ## TestContext
282: 
283: The `TestContext` class provides test run information, cancellation support, and output methods.
284: See [TestContext documentation](https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/core/testing/unit-testing-mstest-writing-tests-testcontext) for complete reference.
285: 
286: ### Accessing TestContext
287: 
288: ```csharp
289: // Property (MSTest suppresses CS8618 - don't use nullable or = null!)
290: public TestContext TestContext { get; set; }
291: 
292: // Constructor injection (MSTest 3.6+) - preferred for immutability
293: [TestClass]
294: public sealed class MyTests
295: {
296:     private readonly TestContext _testContext;
297: 
298:     public MyTests(TestContext testContext)
299:     {
300:         _testContext = testContext;
301:     }
302: }
303: 
304: // Static methods receive it as parameter
305: [ClassInitialize]
306: public static void ClassInit(TestContext context) { }
307: 
308: // Optional for cleanup methods (MSTest 3.6+)
309: [ClassCleanup]
310: public static void ClassCleanup(TestContext context) { }
311: 
312: [AssemblyCleanup]
313: public static void AssemblyCleanup(TestContext context) { }
314: ```
315: 
316: ### Cancellation Token
317: 
318: Always use `TestContext.CancellationToken` for cooperative cancellation with `[Timeout]`:
319: 
320: ```csharp
321: [TestMethod]
322: [Timeout(5000)]
323: public async Task LongRunningTest()
324: {
325:     await _httpClient.GetAsync(url, TestContext.CancellationToken);
326: }
327: ```
328: 
329: ### Test Run Properties
330: 
331: ```csharp
332: TestContext.TestName              // Current test method name
333: TestContext.TestDisplayName       // Display name (3.7+)
334: TestContext.CurrentTestOutcome    // Pass/Fail/InProgress
335: TestContext.TestData              // Parameterized test data (3.7+, in TestInitialize/Cleanup)
336: TestContext.TestException         // Exception if test failed (3.7+, in TestCleanup)
337: TestContext.DeploymentDirectory   // Directory with deployment items
338: ```
339: 
340: ### Output and Result Files
341: 
342: ```csharp
343: // Write to test output (useful for debugging)
344: TestContext.WriteLine("Processing item {0}", itemId);
345: 
346: // Attach files to test results (logs, screenshots)
347: TestContext.AddResultFile(screenshotPath);
348: 
349: // Store/retrieve data across test methods
350: TestContext.Properties["SharedKey"] = computedValue;
351: ```
352: 
353: ## Advanced Features
354: 
355: ### Retry for Flaky Tests (MSTest 3.9+)
356: 
357: ```csharp
358: [TestMethod]
359: [Retry(3)]
360: public void FlakyTest() { }
361: ```
362: 
363: ### Conditional Execution (MSTest 3.10+)
364: 
365: Skip or run tests based on OS or CI environment:
366: 
367: ```csharp
368: // OS-specific tests
369: [TestMethod]
370: [OSCondition(OperatingSystems.Windows)]
371: public void WindowsOnlyTest() { }
372: 
373: [TestMethod]
374: [OSCondition(OperatingSystems.Linux | OperatingSystems.MacOS)]
375: public void UnixOnlyTest() { }
376: 
377: [TestMethod]
378: [OSCondition(ConditionMode.Exclude, OperatingSystems.Windows)]
379: public void SkipOnWindowsTest() { }
380: 
381: // CI environment tests
382: [TestMethod]
383: [CICondition]  // Runs only in CI (default: ConditionMode.Include)
384: public void CIOnlyTest() { }
385: 
386: [TestMethod]
387: [CICondition(ConditionMode.Exclude)]  // Skips in CI, runs locally
388: public void LocalOnlyTest() { }
389: ```
390: 
391: ### Parallelization
392: 
393: ```csharp
394: // Assembly level
395: [assembly: Parallelize(Workers = 4, Scope = ExecutionScope.MethodLevel)]
396: 
397: // Disable for specific class
398: [TestClass]
399: [DoNotParallelize]
400: public sealed class SequentialTests { }
401: ```
402: 
403: ### Work Item Traceability (MSTest 3.8+)
404: 
405: Link tests to work items for traceability in test reports:
406: 
407: ```csharp
408: // Azure DevOps work items
409: [TestMethod]
410: [WorkItem(12345)]  // Links to work item #12345
411: public void Feature_Scenario_ExpectedBehavior() { }
412: 
413: // Multiple work items
414: [TestMethod]
415: [WorkItem(12345)]
416: [WorkItem(67890)]
417: public void Feature_CoversMultipleRequirements() { }
418: 
419: // GitHub issues (MSTest 3.8+)
420: [TestMethod]
421: [GitHubWorkItem("https://github.com/owner/repo/issues/42")]
422: public void BugFix_Issue42_IsResolved() { }
423: ```
424: 
425: Work item associations appear in test results and can be used for:
426: - Tracing test coverage to requirements
427: - Linking bug fixes to regression tests
428: - Generating traceability reports in CI/CD pipelines
429: 
430: ## Common Mistakes to Avoid
431: 
432: ```csharp
433: // ❌ Wrong argument order
434: Assert.AreEqual(actual, expected);
435: // ✅ Correct
436: Assert.AreEqual(expected, actual);
437: 
438: // ❌ Using ExpectedException (obsolete)
439: [ExpectedException(typeof(ArgumentException))]
440: // ✅ Use Assert.Throws
441: Assert.Throws<ArgumentException>(() => Method());
442: 
443: // ❌ Using LINQ Single() - unclear exception
444: var item = items.Single();
445: // ✅ Use ContainsSingle - better failure message
446: var item = Assert.ContainsSingle(items);
447: 
448: // ❌ Hard cast - unclear exception
449: var handler = (MyHandler)result;
450: // ✅ Type assertion - shows actual type on failure
451: var handler = Assert.IsInstanceOfType<MyHandler>(result);
452: 
453: // ❌ Ignoring cancellation token
454: await client.GetAsync(url, CancellationToken.None);
455: // ✅ Flow test cancellation
456: await client.GetAsync(url, TestContext.CancellationToken);
457: 
458: // ❌ Making TestContext nullable - leads to unnecessary null checks
459: public TestContext? TestContext { get; set; }
460: // ❌ Using null! - MSTest already suppresses CS8618 for this property
461: public TestContext TestContext { get; set; } = null!;
462: // ✅ Declare without nullable or initializer - MSTest handles the warning
463: public TestContext TestContext { get; set; }
464: ```
465: 
466: ## Test Organization
467: 
468: - Group tests by feature or component
469: - Use `[TestCategory("Category")]` for filtering
470: - Use `[TestProperty("Name", "Value")]` for custom metadata (e.g., `[TestProperty("Bug", "12345")]`)
471: - Use `[Priority(1)]` for critical tests
472: - Enable relevant MSTest analyzers (MSTEST0020 for constructor preference)
473: 
474: ## Mocking and Isolation
475: 
476: - Use Moq or NSubstitute for mocking dependencies
477: - Use interfaces to facilitate mocking
478: - Mock dependencies to isolate units under test
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/csharp-nunit/SKILL.md">
 1: ---
 2: name: csharp-nunit
 3: description: 'Get best practices for NUnit unit testing, including data-driven tests'
 4: ---
 5: 
 6: # NUnit Best Practices
 7: 
 8: Your goal is to help me write effective unit tests with NUnit, covering both standard and data-driven testing approaches.
 9: 
10: ## Project Setup
11: 
12: - Use a separate test project with naming convention `[ProjectName].Tests`
13: - Reference Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk, NUnit, and NUnit3TestAdapter packages
14: - Create test classes that match the classes being tested (e.g., `CalculatorTests` for `Calculator`)
15: - Use .NET SDK test commands: `dotnet test` for running tests
16: 
17: ## Test Structure
18: 
19: - Apply `[TestFixture]` attribute to test classes
20: - Use `[Test]` attribute for test methods
21: - Follow the Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) pattern
22: - Name tests using the pattern `MethodName_Scenario_ExpectedBehavior`
23: - Use `[SetUp]` and `[TearDown]` for per-test setup and teardown
24: - Use `[OneTimeSetUp]` and `[OneTimeTearDown]` for per-class setup and teardown
25: - Use `[SetUpFixture]` for assembly-level setup and teardown
26: 
27: ## Standard Tests
28: 
29: - Keep tests focused on a single behavior
30: - Avoid testing multiple behaviors in one test method
31: - Use clear assertions that express intent
32: - Include only the assertions needed to verify the test case
33: - Make tests independent and idempotent (can run in any order)
34: - Avoid test interdependencies
35: 
36: ## Data-Driven Tests
37: 
38: - Use `[TestCase]` for inline test data
39: - Use `[TestCaseSource]` for programmatically generated test data
40: - Use `[Values]` for simple parameter combinations
41: - Use `[ValueSource]` for property or method-based data sources
42: - Use `[Random]` for random numeric test values
43: - Use `[Range]` for sequential numeric test values
44: - Use `[Combinatorial]` or `[Pairwise]` for combining multiple parameters
45: 
46: ## Assertions
47: 
48: - Use `Assert.That` with constraint model (preferred NUnit style)
49: - Use constraints like `Is.EqualTo`, `Is.SameAs`, `Contains.Item`
50: - Use `Assert.AreEqual` for simple value equality (classic style)
51: - Use `CollectionAssert` for collection comparisons
52: - Use `StringAssert` for string-specific assertions
53: - Use `Assert.Throws<T>` or `Assert.ThrowsAsync<T>` to test exceptions
54: - Use descriptive messages in assertions for clarity on failure
55: 
56: ## Mocking and Isolation
57: 
58: - Consider using Moq or NSubstitute alongside NUnit
59: - Mock dependencies to isolate units under test
60: - Use interfaces to facilitate mocking
61: - Consider using a DI container for complex test setups
62: 
63: ## Test Organization
64: 
65: - Group tests by feature or component
66: - Use categories with `[Category("CategoryName")]`
