<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/dotnet-mcp-builder/references/client.md">
  1: # Building an MCP client in .NET
  2: 
  3: A short reference for *consuming* an MCP server from .NET — useful for testing your server, building agent harnesses, or wiring MCP into a Semantic Kernel / Microsoft.Extensions.AI pipeline.
  4: 
  5: For just *running* a server, ignore this file.
  6: 
  7: ## Packages
  8: 
  9: ```bash
 10: dotnet add package ModelContextProtocol.Core   # minimal: just client + transports
 11: # or
 12: dotnet add package ModelContextProtocol         # adds DI/hosting helpers
 13: ```
 14: 
 15: ## Connecting via STDIO (launching a server process)
 16: 
 17: ```csharp
 18: using ModelContextProtocol.Client;
 19: 
 20: var transport = new StdioClientTransport(new StdioClientTransportOptions
 21: {
 22:     Command = "dotnet",
 23:     Arguments = ["run", "--project", "../MyMcpServer"],
 24:     EnvironmentVariables = new() { ["MY_API_KEY"] = "..." },
 25:     ShutdownTimeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10),
 26:     StandardErrorLines = line => Console.Error.WriteLine($"[server] {line}")
 27: });
 28: 
 29: await using var client = await McpClient.CreateAsync(transport);
 30: ```
 31: 
 32: `StandardErrorLines` is a great debugging aid — you'll see your server's logs as they happen.
 33: 
 34: ## Connecting via HTTP (Streamable)
 35: 
 36: ```csharp
 37: using ModelContextProtocol.Client;
 38: 
 39: var transport = new HttpClientTransport(new HttpClientTransportOptions
 40: {
 41:     Endpoint = new Uri("https://my-server.example.com/mcp"),
 42:     TransportMode = HttpTransportMode.StreamableHttp,
 43:     ConnectionTimeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30),
 44:     AdditionalHeaders = new Dictionary<string, string>
 45:     {
 46:         ["Authorization"] = "Bearer ..."
 47:     }
 48: });
 49: 
 50: await using var client = await McpClient.CreateAsync(transport);
 51: ```
 52: 
 53: `TransportMode = AutoDetect` (the default) tries Streamable HTTP first and falls back to SSE — useful for older servers, but pin to `StreamableHttp` for new code so failures are loud.
 54: 
 55: ## Listing and calling tools
 56: 
 57: ```csharp
 58: IList<McpClientTool> tools = await client.ListToolsAsync();
 59: 
 60: foreach (var t in tools)
 61:     Console.WriteLine($"- {t.Name}: {t.Description}");
 62: 
 63: var echo = tools.First(t => t.Name == "Echo");
 64: CallToolResult result = await echo.CallAsync(new Dictionary<string, object?>
 65: {
 66:     ["message"] = "hello"
 67: });
 68: 
 69: if (result.IsError == true)
 70: {
 71:     var msg = result.Content.OfType<TextContentBlock>().FirstOrDefault()?.Text;
 72:     Console.Error.WriteLine($"Tool failed: {msg}");
 73:     return;
 74: }
 75: 
 76: foreach (var block in result.Content)
 77: {
 78:     switch (block)
 79:     {
 80:         case TextContentBlock text:
 81:             Console.WriteLine(text.Text);
 82:             break;
 83:         case ImageContentBlock image:
 84:             File.WriteAllBytes("out.png", image.DecodedData.ToArray());
 85:             break;
 86:     }
 87: }
 88: ```
 89: 
 90: ## Listing prompts and resources
 91: 
 92: ```csharp
 93: IList<McpClientPrompt> prompts = await client.ListPromptsAsync();
 94: GetPromptResult pr = await client.GetPromptAsync("code_review",
 95:     new Dictionary<string, object?> { ["language"] = "csharp", ["code"] = "..." });
 96: 
 97: IList<McpClientResource> resources = await client.ListResourcesAsync();
 98: ReadResourceResult rr = await client.ReadResourceAsync("config://app/settings");
 99: ```
100: 
101: ## Subscribing to server notifications
102: 
103: ```csharp
104: client.RegisterNotificationHandler(
105:     NotificationMethods.ToolListChangedNotification,
106:     async (notification, ct) =>
107:     {
108:         var updated = await client.ListToolsAsync(cancellationToken: ct);
109:         Console.WriteLine($"Tool list changed; now {updated.Count} tools.");
110:     });
111: ```
112: 
113: ## Handling server-to-client requests (sampling, elicitation, roots)
114: 
115: If your server uses these features, your client must handle them. Configure handlers when creating the client:
116: 
117: ```csharp
118: await using var client = await McpClient.CreateAsync(transport, new McpClientOptions
119: {
120:     Capabilities = new()
121:     {
122:         Sampling = new()
123:         {
124:             SamplingHandler = async (req, progress, ct) =>
125:             {
126:                 // Route req.Messages to your IChatClient and return a CreateMessageResult.
127:                 var response = await myChatClient.GetResponseAsync(/* convert */, ct);
128:                 return new CreateMessageResult { /* fill in */ };
129:             }
130:         },
131:         Elicitation = new()
132:         {
133:             ElicitationHandler = async (req, ct) =>
134:             {
135:                 // Show req.Message + req.RequestedSchema to the user; collect input.
136:                 return new ElicitResult { Action = "accept", Content = collectedValues };
137:             }
138:         },
139:         Roots = new()
140:         {
141:             RootsHandler = async (req, ct) =>
142:             {
143:                 return new ListRootsResult
144:                 {
145:                     Roots = new[] { new Root { Uri = "file:///workspace", Name = "Workspace" } }
146:                 };
147:             }
148:         }
149:     }
150: });
151: ```
152: 
153: If you don't supply a handler and the server calls the feature, the call fails with a "method not supported" error.
154: 
155: ## Using MCP tools as `IChatClient` function tools
156: 
157: If you're plugging MCP into a `Microsoft.Extensions.AI` pipeline, expose tools as `AIFunction`:
158: 
159: ```csharp
160: using Microsoft.Extensions.AI;
161: 
162: IList<McpClientTool> mcpTools = await client.ListToolsAsync();
163: 
164: var chatOptions = new ChatOptions
165: {
166:     Tools = mcpTools.Cast<AITool>().ToList()
167: };
168: 
169: var chatClient = new MyChatClient(...);   // any IChatClient
170: var response = await chatClient.GetResponseAsync(messages, chatOptions);
171: ```
172: 
173: `McpClientTool` implements `AIFunction` — function-calling middleware will invoke the right tool and feed the result back to the LLM automatically.
174: 
175: ## Resuming a session (HTTP, stateful)
176: 
177: ```csharp
178: var transport = new HttpClientTransport(new HttpClientTransportOptions
179: {
180:     Endpoint = new Uri("https://my-server.example.com/mcp"),
181:     KnownSessionId = previousSessionId
182: });
183: 
184: await using var client = await McpClient.ResumeSessionAsync(transport, new ResumeClientSessionOptions
185: {
186:     ServerCapabilities = previousServerCapabilities,
187:     ServerInfo = previousServerInfo
188: });
189: ```
190: 
191: Useful for long-lived agent processes that survive transient network drops.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/dotnet-mcp-builder/references/elicitation.md">
  1: # Elicitation
  2: 
  3: Elicitation lets a tool **ask the user for input mid-execution**, via the client. The LLM doesn't see the question; the client surfaces it directly to the user. This turns one-shot tool calls into interactive flows — collecting confirmation, missing parameters, credentials (URL mode), etc.
  4: 
  5: > **Spec version:** 2025-11-25. URL mode is the newer addition (originally 2025-06-18 had only form mode).
  6: 
  7: ## Two modes
  8: 
  9: | Mode | What it does | When to use |
 10: |---|---|---|
 11: | **Form (in-band)** | Server sends a JSON Schema; client renders a form; user submits values back through the same MCP channel. | Confirmations, missing parameters, structured choices. |
 12: | **URL (out-of-band)** | Server sends a URL; client opens it in a browser; user completes the flow there; server checks state separately. | OAuth, payments, anything the MCP channel must not see. |
 13: 
 14: ## Prerequisite: stateful transport
 15: 
 16: Elicitation requires the server to send a request *to* the client and wait for a response. That only works on:
 17: - STDIO (always).
 18: - Stateful HTTP (`options.Stateless = false`).
 19: 
 20: In stateless HTTP, `ElicitAsync` will throw — there's no transport channel back.
 21: 
 22: ## Form mode — full example
 23: 
 24: ```csharp
 25: using System.ComponentModel;
 26: using ModelContextProtocol.Protocol;
 27: using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
 28: 
 29: [McpServerToolType]
 30: public class BookingTools
 31: {
 32:     [McpServerTool, Description("Books a meeting room. Asks the user for confirmation.")]
 33:     public static async Task<string> BookRoom(
 34:         IMcpServer server,
 35:         [Description("Room name")] string room,
 36:         [Description("Start time (ISO 8601)")] DateTime start,
 37:         CancellationToken ct)
 38:     {
 39:         var elicit = await server.ElicitAsync(new ElicitRequestParams
 40:         {
 41:             Message = $"Confirm booking '{room}' at {start:HH:mm}?",
 42:             RequestedSchema = new ElicitRequestParams.RequestSchema
 43:             {
 44:                 Properties = new Dictionary<string, ElicitRequestParams.PrimitiveSchemaDefinition>
 45:                 {
 46:                     ["confirm"] = new ElicitRequestParams.BooleanSchema
 47:                     {
 48:                         Description = "Confirm the booking",
 49:                         Default = true
 50:                     },
 51:                     ["notes"] = new ElicitRequestParams.StringSchema
 52:                     {
 53:                         Description = "Optional notes for the booking"
 54:                     }
 55:                 }
 56:             }
 57:         }, ct);
 58: 
 59:         if (elicit.Action != "accept")
 60:             return "Booking cancelled by user.";
 61: 
 62:         var confirmed = elicit.Content?["confirm"].GetBoolean() ?? false;
 63:         var notes     = elicit.Content?["notes"].GetString() ?? "";
 64: 
 65:         if (!confirmed)
 66:             return "User declined to confirm.";
 67: 
 68:         // …perform the booking…
 69:         return $"Booked '{room}' at {start:O}. Notes: {notes}";
 70:     }
 71: }
 72: ```
 73: 
 74: ### Schema primitive types
 75: 
 76: You can build a `RequestedSchema` from these:
 77: 
 78: | Type | C# class | Notes |
 79: |---|---|---|
 80: | String | `StringSchema` | `Default`, `Description`. Add JSON-Schema validation at server side if you need it. |
 81: | Number | `NumberSchema` | Use for ints and floats. |
 82: | Boolean | `BooleanSchema` | Renders as a checkbox / toggle. |
 83: | Single-select enum (untitled) | `UntitledSingleSelectEnumSchema` | List of values; client renders as dropdown/radio. |
 84: | Single-select enum (titled) | `TitledSingleSelectEnumSchema` | Each value has a display title. |
 85: | Multi-select enum | `UntitledMultiSelectEnumSchema` / `TitledMultiSelectEnumSchema` | Multi-select dropdown / checkbox group. |
 86: 
 87: Each accepts `Description` and `Default`.
 88: 
 89: ### Response shape
 90: 
 91: `ElicitResult`:
 92: - `Action` — `"accept"`, `"reject"`, or `"cancel"`. Always check this first.
 93: - `Content` — `Dictionary<string, JsonElement>?` with the user's submitted values. `null` if the user rejected/cancelled.
 94: 
 95: Always handle the non-accept paths:
 96: 
 97: ```csharp
 98: if (elicit.Action == "cancel")
 99:     return "User cancelled. No changes made.";
100: if (elicit.Action == "reject")
101:     return "User declined.";
102: // Action == "accept" → safe to read elicit.Content
103: ```
104: 
105: ## URL mode — full example
106: 
107: URL mode is for flows where the user must complete something **outside** the MCP channel — typically OAuth.
108: 
109: ```csharp
110: [McpServerTool, Description("Connects the user's GitHub account.")]
111: public static async Task<string> ConnectGitHub(
112:     IMcpServer server,
113:     IOAuthService oauth,
114:     CancellationToken ct)
115: {
116:     var elicitationId = Guid.NewGuid().ToString();
117:     var authUrl = oauth.BuildAuthorizationUrl(state: elicitationId);
118: 
119:     var result = await server.ElicitAsync(new ElicitRequestParams
120:     {
121:         Mode = "url",
122:         ElicitationId = elicitationId,
123:         Url = authUrl,
124:         Message = "Please authorize access to GitHub in the browser window that just opened."
125:     }, ct);
126: 
127:     if (result.Action != "accept")
128:         return "Authorization cancelled.";
129: 
130:     // The user has come back. Look up the persisted token by elicitationId.
131:     var token = await oauth.GetTokenByStateAsync(elicitationId, ct);
132:     return token is not null ? "Connected." : "Authorization did not complete.";
133: }
134: ```
135: 
136: ### `UrlElicitationRequiredException`
137: 
138: When a tool is *blocked* on auth (rather than walking the user through it), throw `UrlElicitationRequiredException`. The client surfaces the URL to the user and the call fails cleanly. Useful for retry-after-auth patterns:
139: 
140: ```csharp
141: if (!oauth.HasValidToken)
142: {
143:     var id = Guid.NewGuid().ToString();
144:     throw new UrlElicitationRequiredException(
145:         "Authorization required",
146:         new[]
147:         {
148:             new ElicitRequestParams
149:             {
150:                 Mode = "url",
151:                 ElicitationId = id,
152:                 Url = oauth.BuildAuthorizationUrl(state: id),
153:                 Message = "Sign in to continue."
154:             }
155:         });
156: }
157: ```
158: 
159: ## When NOT to use elicitation
160: 
161: - **Trivial confirmations the LLM can ask in natural language.** If you can phrase "Should I do X?" in your tool's docstring and let the LLM ask, that's lower friction than a modal form.
162: - **Branching that the LLM should reason about.** Don't replace the LLM's judgment with a form — only elicit for things the LLM literally cannot decide (user secrets, real-time consent, picking from a list only the user knows).
163: - **Stateless deployments.** Doesn't work — see prerequisite above.
164: 
165: ## Client capability check
166: 
167: Don't blindly call `ElicitAsync`. Check first:
168: 
169: ```csharp
170: if (server.ClientCapabilities?.Elicitation is null)
171:     return "This client doesn't support elicitation; please pass the value as an argument.";
172: 
173: var elicit = await server.ElicitAsync(...);
174: ```
175: 
176: This degrades gracefully on older clients.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/dotnet-mcp-builder/references/mcp-apps.md">
  1: # MCP Apps (interactive UI)
  2: 
  3: [MCP Apps](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/extensions/apps/overview) is the official extension that lets a tool return an **interactive UI** rendered in a sandboxed iframe inside the host (Claude, Claude Desktop, VS Code Copilot, Goose, Postman, MCPJam). Typical use cases: charts, dashboards, multi-step forms, 3D viewers, real-time monitors, PDF/video viewers.
  4: 
  5: > **Important:** as of early 2026, the C# SDK does **not** ship a typed convenience layer for MCP Apps (tracked in [csharp-sdk#1431](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/csharp-sdk/issues/1431)). You implement the spec by hand: serve a `ui://` resource and emit the right `_meta` on the tool. It's not hard — just untyped. This page shows you the pattern.
  6: 
  7: ## How it works (short version)
  8: 
  9: 1. You register a **resource** at a `ui://` URI returning an HTML bundle.
 10: 2. You register a **tool** whose definition includes `_meta.ui.resourceUri` pointing to that URI.
 11: 3. When the LLM calls the tool, the host fetches the UI resource and renders it in a sandboxed iframe in the chat.
 12: 4. The HTML talks to the host over `postMessage` JSON-RPC (use `@modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps` from the bundle, or hand-roll it).
 13: 5. The app can call back into your MCP server (any tool), update the model context, etc.
 14: 
 15: The full protocol spec is at [`@modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps).
 16: 
 17: ## Step 1: Serve the UI resource
 18: 
 19: Bundle your HTML/JS/CSS into a single string (or load from `wwwroot`). Serve it at a `ui://` URI.
 20: 
 21: ```csharp
 22: using System.ComponentModel;
 23: using System.IO;
 24: using System.Reflection;
 25: using ModelContextProtocol.Protocol;
 26: using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
 27: 
 28: [McpServerResourceType]
 29: public static class ChartUiResource
 30: {
 31:     [McpServerResource(
 32:         UriTemplate = "ui://charts/interactive",
 33:         Name = "Interactive chart",
 34:         MimeType = "text/html+skybridge")]   // see "MIME type" note below
 35:     [Description("UI bundle for the interactive chart MCP App.")]
 36:     public static TextResourceContents GetUi()
 37:     {
 38:         // Load a bundled HTML/JS file from embedded resources or wwwroot.
 39:         var html = LoadEmbeddedString("MyMcpServer.AppUi.chart.html");
 40: 
 41:         return new TextResourceContents
 42:         {
 43:             Uri = "ui://charts/interactive",
 44:             MimeType = "text/html+skybridge",
 45:             Text = html
 46:         };
 47:     }
 48: 
 49:     private static string LoadEmbeddedString(string resourceName)
 50:     {
 51:         var asm = Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly();
 52:         using var stream = asm.GetManifestResourceStream(resourceName)
 53:             ?? throw new InvalidOperationException($"Missing embedded resource {resourceName}");
 54:         using var reader = new StreamReader(stream);
 55:         return reader.ReadToEnd();
 56:     }
 57: }
 58: ```
 59: 
 60: **MIME type note:** the spec uses `text/html+skybridge` for app HTML so hosts can distinguish UI bundles from regular `text/html` previews. Use that, even though plain `text/html` may work today on lenient hosts.
 61: 
 62: ## Step 2: Emit `_meta` on the tool
 63: 
 64: The C# SDK's `[McpServerTool]` doesn't expose `_meta` in the attribute today, so set it via the lower-level `Tool` definition. Do this once at startup:
 65: 
 66: ```csharp
 67: using ModelContextProtocol.Protocol;
 68: using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
 69: using System.Text.Json;
 70: using System.Text.Json.Nodes;
 71: 
 72: builder.Services.Configure<McpServerOptions>(options =>
 73: {
 74:     options.Capabilities ??= new();
 75:     options.Capabilities.Tools ??= new();
 76: 
 77:     // Define the tool manually so we can attach _meta.
 78:     var visualizeTool = new Tool
 79:     {
 80:         Name = "visualize_data",
 81:         Description = "Visualize the user's data as an interactive chart.",
 82:         InputSchema = JsonDocument.Parse("""
 83:             {
 84:               "type": "object",
 85:               "properties": {
 86:                 "datasetId": { "type": "string", "description": "Dataset to visualize." }
 87:               },
 88:               "required": ["datasetId"]
 89:             }
 90:             """).RootElement,
 91:         Meta = new JsonObject
 92:         {
 93:             ["ui"] = new JsonObject
 94:             {
 95:                 ["resourceUri"] = "ui://charts/interactive"
 96:                 // Optionally:
 97:                 // ["csp"] = new JsonObject { ["default-src"] = "'self' https://cdn.example.com" },
 98:                 // ["permissions"] = new JsonArray("clipboard-write")
 99:             }
100:         }
101:     };
102: 
103:     // Implement the call handler that returns the data the UI will render.
104:     options.Capabilities.Tools.ToolCollection ??= new();
105:     options.Capabilities.Tools.ToolCollection.Add(McpServerTool.Create(
106:         async (CallToolRequestParams req, CancellationToken ct) =>
107:         {
108:             var args = req.Arguments ?? new();
109:             var datasetId = args["datasetId"]!.GetValue<string>();
110:             var data = await LoadDataset(datasetId, ct);
111:             return new CallToolResult
112:             {
113:                 Content = [new TextContentBlock { Text = JsonSerializer.Serialize(data) }],
114:                 StructuredContent = JsonSerializer.SerializeToNode(data)
115:             };
116:         },
117:         visualizeTool));
118: });
119: ```
120: 
121: If you don't need full structured content, the tool can return *just* JSON in a text block — the UI fetches it via `app.callServerTool(...)` after rendering.
122: 
123: ### Backwards compatibility key
124: 
125: Some older hosts expect `_meta["ui/resourceUri"]` instead of `_meta.ui.resourceUri`. Set both for safety:
126: 
127: ```csharp
128: Meta = new JsonObject
129: {
130:     ["ui"] = new JsonObject { ["resourceUri"] = "ui://charts/interactive" },
131:     ["ui/resourceUri"] = "ui://charts/interactive"   // legacy
132: }
133: ```
134: 
135: ## Step 3: The HTML bundle
136: 
137: A minimum viable bundle: vanilla JS using `@modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps`. The simplest build is a single self-contained HTML file.
138: 
139: ```html
140: <!doctype html>
141: <html>
142:   <head>
143:     <meta charset="utf-8" />
144:     <title>Chart</title>
145:     <style>body { font-family: system-ui; margin: 0; }</style>
146:   </head>
147:   <body>
148:     <div id="root">Loading…</div>
149:     <script type="module">
150:       import { App } from "https://esm.sh/@modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps@1";
151: 
152:       const app = new App();
153:       await app.connect();
154: 
155:       // Fetch the data we need from the server.
156:       const resp = await app.callServerTool({
157:         name: "visualize_data",
158:         arguments: { datasetId: "default" }
159:       });
160: 
161:       const data = JSON.parse(resp.content[0].text);
162:       document.getElementById("root").textContent =
163:         `Loaded ${data.points.length} data points.`;
164: 
165:       // Tell the model what just happened (becomes part of its context).
166:       await app.updateModelContext({
167:         content: [{ type: "text", text: "User opened the chart UI." }]
168:       });
169:     </script>
170:   </body>
171: </html>
172: ```
173: 
174: **Tip:** for non-trivial UIs, build with Vite (React/Vue/Svelte/Solid — any of the [official starter templates](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps/tree/main/examples)) and have the build emit a single inlined HTML you embed as a project resource.
175: 
176: ## Project layout
177: 
178: A pragmatic layout for an MCP App in .NET:
179: 
180: ```
181: MyMcpServer/
182: ├── Program.cs
183: ├── Tools/
184: │   └── VisualizeDataTool.cs       # (or registered via Configure as above)
185: ├── Resources/
186: │   └── ChartUiResource.cs         # serves the ui:// resource
187: ├── AppUi/
188: │   ├── chart.html                 # bundled UI (Embedded Resource)
189: │   └── package.json + src/...     # if you build with Vite, output to chart.html
190: └── MyMcpServer.csproj
191: ```
192: 
193: In the csproj:
194: 
195: ```xml
196: <ItemGroup>
197:   <EmbeddedResource Include="AppUi\chart.html" />
198: </ItemGroup>
199: ```
200: 
201: Read it via `Assembly.GetManifestResourceStream("MyMcpServer.AppUi.chart.html")`.
202: 
203: ## Testing locally
204: 
205: 1. Run your MCP server (STDIO or HTTP).
206: 2. Use a host that supports MCP Apps — Claude Desktop or VS Code Copilot Chat are the easiest.
207: 3. Trigger the tool via the LLM. The UI renders inline.
208: 
209: For pure-UI iteration, [MCP Inspector](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/inspector) shows resource contents but does not fully render apps; for that, point Claude Desktop at your dev server.
210: 
211: ## Pitfalls
212: 
213: - **Wrong MIME type.** Use `text/html+skybridge`. Plain `text/html` may still work but isn't future-proof.
214: - **CSP too tight or too loose.** If your UI loads from a CDN, declare it in `Meta["ui"]["csp"]` on the `Tool` definition (this serialises to `_meta.ui.csp` on the wire). Otherwise the iframe sandbox blocks it.
215: - **Forgetting `Tool.Meta` on the tool.** Without the `Meta` property containing the `ui.resourceUri` entry, the host treats your tool as a regular text-returning tool. The UI never appears.
216: - **Trying to use browser APIs outside the sandbox.** No cookies, no localStorage from the parent. Use `app.updateModelContext` and tool calls for state.
217: 
218: ## Future-proofing
219: 
220: When the C# SDK ships its typed MCP Apps helpers (issue [#1431](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/csharp-sdk/issues/1431)), you'll likely be able to replace the manual `Configure` block with an attribute or fluent builder. The serving of `ui://` resources won't change. Keep your UI HTML as embedded resources so the migration is mechanical.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/dotnet-mcp-builder/references/packages.md">
 1: # NuGet packages and target frameworks
 2: 
 3: ## The three official packages
 4: 
 5: All packages live under the [`ModelContextProtocol` NuGet profile](https://www.nuget.org/profiles/ModelContextProtocol). The official C# SDK repo is [`modelcontextprotocol/csharp-sdk`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/csharp-sdk), maintained jointly by the MCP project and Microsoft.
 6: 
 7: | Package | When to use it | Brings in |
 8: |---|---|---|
 9: | **`ModelContextProtocol`** | Default for STDIO servers and most projects | `Core` + `Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting` integration, attribute discovery (`AddMcpServer`, `WithToolsFromAssembly`, etc.) |
10: | **`ModelContextProtocol.AspNetCore`** | HTTP (Streamable) servers hosted in ASP.NET Core | The above + `WithHttpTransport` and `MapMcp` |
11: | **`ModelContextProtocol.Core`** | Pure clients, custom hosts, low-level scenarios where you don't want the `Microsoft.Extensions.*` dependencies | Just the protocol + transports + low-level `McpServer.Create` / `McpClient.CreateAsync` |
12: 
13: **Rule of thumb:**
14: - New STDIO server → `ModelContextProtocol` + `Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting`.
15: - New HTTP server → `ModelContextProtocol.AspNetCore` only (it transitively pulls in everything you need).
16: - Pure client app → `ModelContextProtocol.Core` (or `ModelContextProtocol` if you also want hosting/DI for the client).
17: 
18: ## Versions
19: 
20: As of 2026, the stable line is **1.x** (`1.2.0` is current at time of writing). The `0.x` line was preview and has breaking differences — if you find docs or blog posts referencing `0.4`/`0.6`, treat them as out of date.
21: 
22: To check the latest:
23: 
24: ```bash
25: dotnet search ModelContextProtocol --prerelease
26: ```
27: 
28: ## Target frameworks
29: 
30: The SDK targets **`.NET 8.0`** and **`netstandard2.0`**. That means it runs on:
31: - .NET 8 (LTS)
32: - .NET 9
33: - .NET 10 (current LTS — recommended for new projects)
34: - .NET Framework 4.6.2+ via netstandard2.0 (rare; only for legacy hosts)
35: 
36: For HTTP servers you specifically need a TFM that supports ASP.NET Core (so .NET 8/9/10).
37: 
38: ## Project setup commands
39: 
40: ### STDIO server
41: 
42: ```bash
43: dotnet new console -n MyMcpServer -f net10.0
44: cd MyMcpServer
45: dotnet add package ModelContextProtocol
46: dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting
47: ```
48: 
49: ### HTTP (Streamable) server
50: 
51: ```bash
52: dotnet new web -n MyMcpServer -f net10.0
53: cd MyMcpServer
54: dotnet add package ModelContextProtocol.AspNetCore
55: ```
56: 
57: (`dotnet new web` gives you a minimal ASP.NET Core project — exactly what `MapMcp` needs.)
58: 
59: ### Client
60: 
61: ```bash
62: dotnet new console -n MyMcpClient -f net10.0
63: cd MyMcpClient
64: dotnet add package ModelContextProtocol.Core
65: ```
66: 
67: ## Optional but commonly useful
68: 
69: | Package | Why |
70: |---|---|
71: | `Microsoft.Extensions.AI` | Provides `IChatClient`, `ChatMessage`, `ChatRole`, `ChatOptions` — the abstractions used by `AsSamplingChatClient()` and by prompt return types. |
72: | `Microsoft.Extensions.AI.Abstractions` | Pulled in transitively but worth knowing about for types like `DataContent`, `TextContent`. |
73: | `OpenTelemetry.Extensions.Hosting` | The SDK emits OTel traces and metrics for tool calls — wire them up if the user has an observability story. |
74: 
75: ## What about `dnx`?
76: 
77: Newer Microsoft examples sometimes show launching servers via `dnx PackageName --version 1.2.3`. That's a valid distribution model: publish your server as a NuGet package and let users run it without cloning. It's orthogonal to how the server itself is built — keep your code identical and just change the launch command.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/dotnet-mcp-builder/references/prompt-primitive.md">
  1: # Prompts
  2: 
  3: Prompts are reusable, parameterised message templates that the user (not the LLM) typically picks from a list — think "slash commands" in a chat client. The server defines them; the host renders them as menus.
  4: 
  5: ## Anatomy of a prompt
  6: 
  7: ```csharp
  8: using System.ComponentModel;
  9: using Microsoft.Extensions.AI;
 10: using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
 11: 
 12: [McpServerPromptType]
 13: public class CodePrompts
 14: {
 15:     [McpServerPrompt, Description("Generates a code review prompt.")]
 16:     public static IEnumerable<ChatMessage> CodeReview(
 17:         [Description("The programming language")] string language,
 18:         [Description("The code to review")] string code) =>
 19:         [
 20:             new(ChatRole.User,
 21:                 $"Please review the following {language} code:\n\n```{language}\n{code}\n```"),
 22:             new(ChatRole.Assistant,
 23:                 "I'll review the code for correctness, style, and potential improvements.")
 24:         ];
 25: }
 26: ```
 27: 
 28: Register it:
 29: 
 30: ```csharp
 31: .WithPrompts<CodePrompts>()
 32: // or
 33: .WithPromptsFromAssembly()
 34: ```
 35: 
 36: ## Return types
 37: 
 38: | Return type | Result |
 39: |---|---|
 40: | `ChatMessage` | Single message. |
 41: | `IEnumerable<ChatMessage>` | Conversation seed. |
 42: | `PromptMessage` / `IEnumerable<PromptMessage>` | Lower-level — use when you need full control over content blocks (embedded resources, multiple typed blocks per message). |
 43: | `GetPromptResult` | Full control — set `Messages` and `Description`. |
 44: 
 45: `ChatMessage`/`ChatRole` come from `Microsoft.Extensions.AI`. They're the high-level shape and what you should use 90% of the time. Drop down to `PromptMessage`/`ContentBlock` only when you need embedded resources or fine-grained content typing.
 46: 
 47: ## Arguments
 48: 
 49: Every parameter (after the special ones the SDK strips out — `IMcpServer`, `CancellationToken`, etc.) becomes a prompt argument visible to the user when they pick the prompt. Use `[Description]` on each to explain what the user should supply.
 50: 
 51: To mark an argument optional, give it a default value:
 52: 
 53: ```csharp
 54: [McpServerPrompt, Description("…")]
 55: public static ChatMessage Greeting(
 56:     [Description("Their preferred greeting style")] string style = "casual")
 57:     => new(ChatRole.User, $"Greet me in a {style} style.");
 58: ```
 59: 
 60: ## Image and file content
 61: 
 62: For prompts that include images:
 63: 
 64: ```csharp
 65: [McpServerPrompt, Description("Asks the model to analyze an image.")]
 66: public static IEnumerable<ChatMessage> AnalyzeImage(
 67:     [Description("Instructions for the analysis")] string instructions)
 68: {
 69:     byte[] imageBytes = LoadSampleImage();
 70:     return new[]
 71:     {
 72:         new ChatMessage(ChatRole.User, new AIContent[]
 73:         {
 74:             new TextContent($"Please analyze this image: {instructions}"),
 75:             new DataContent(imageBytes, "image/png")
 76:         })
 77:     };
 78: }
 79: ```
 80: 
 81: For embedded text resources (e.g. seeding the conversation with a document the user picked):
 82: 
 83: ```csharp
 84: [McpServerPrompt, Description("Reviews a referenced document.")]
 85: public static IEnumerable<PromptMessage> ReviewDocument(
 86:     [Description("The document ID to review")] string documentId)
 87: {
 88:     string content = LoadDocument(documentId);
 89:     return new[]
 90:     {
 91:         new PromptMessage
 92:         {
 93:             Role = Role.User,
 94:             Content = new TextContentBlock { Text = "Please review the following document:" }
 95:         },
 96:         new PromptMessage
 97:         {
 98:             Role = Role.User,
 99:             Content = new EmbeddedResourceBlock
100:             {
101:                 Resource = new TextResourceContents
102:                 {
103:                     Uri = $"docs://documents/{documentId}",
104:                     MimeType = "text/plain",
105:                     Text = content
106:                 }
107:             }
108:         }
109:     };
110: }
111: ```
112: 
113: ## Async prompts
114: 
115: Prompts can be async — useful when you need to look up data to build the messages:
116: 
117: ```csharp
118: [McpServerPrompt, Description("Drafts a release-notes prompt.")]
119: public static async Task<IEnumerable<ChatMessage>> ReleaseNotes(
120:     string repo,
121:     string fromTag,
122:     string toTag,
123:     IGitHubClient github,
124:     CancellationToken ct)
125: {
126:     var commits = await github.GetCommitsBetweenAsync(repo, fromTag, toTag, ct);
127:     var summary = string.Join("\n", commits.Select(c => $"- {c.Message}"));
128:     return new[]
129:     {
130:         new ChatMessage(ChatRole.User,
131:             $"Draft release notes for {repo} {fromTag}→{toTag} from these commits:\n{summary}")
132:     };
133: }
134: ```
135: 
136: ## Notifying clients of prompt changes
137: 
138: ```csharp
139: await server.SendNotificationAsync(
140:     NotificationMethods.PromptListChangedNotification,
141:     new PromptListChangedNotificationParams(),
142:     cancellationToken);
143: ```
144: 
145: ## When to use prompts vs. tools
146: 
147: - **Prompt:** the *user* triggers it from a menu, supplying any required arguments. The output is messages, not data. Good for "/summarize", "/code-review", "/draft-email".
148: - **Tool:** the *LLM* triggers it (often without explicit user action) to fetch or change data. Good for "get_weather", "create_issue".
149: 
150: If both apply (the user wants a slash command that triggers the same logic the LLM could call), expose both — the same DTO/service can back both.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/dotnet-mcp-builder/references/resource-primitive.md">
  1: # Resources
  2: 
  3: Resources are server-exposed "things" identified by a URI. Hosts list them so the user can pick which ones to attach to the conversation; tools and prompts can also reference them via `EmbeddedResourceBlock`. Think files, database rows, API objects, settings — anything addressable.
  4: 
  5: Two flavours:
  6: - **Static resource** — a fixed URI (`config://app/settings`). Useful for singletons.
  7: - **Resource template** — a URI with placeholders (`docs://articles/{id}`). The host (or LLM) substitutes parameters; your method receives them.
  8: 
  9: ## Static resource
 10: 
 11: ```csharp
 12: using System.ComponentModel;
 13: using System.Text.Json;
 14: using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
 15: 
 16: [McpServerResourceType]
 17: public class AppResources
 18: {
 19:     [McpServerResource(
 20:         UriTemplate = "config://app/settings",
 21:         Name = "App Settings",
 22:         MimeType = "application/json")]
 23:     [Description("Returns application configuration settings.")]
 24:     public static string GetSettings() =>
 25:         JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { theme = "dark", language = "en" });
 26: }
 27: ```
 28: 
 29: Register:
 30: 
 31: ```csharp
 32: .WithResources<AppResources>()
 33: // or
 34: .WithResourcesFromAssembly()
 35: ```
 36: 
 37: ## Templated resource
 38: 
 39: The placeholders in `UriTemplate` map by name to method parameters. Anything not a placeholder follows the same DI rules as tools (`IMcpServer`, `CancellationToken`, services).
 40: 
 41: ```csharp
 42: [McpServerResourceType]
 43: public class DocumentResources
 44: {
 45:     [McpServerResource(
 46:         UriTemplate = "docs://articles/{id}",
 47:         Name = "Article",
 48:         MimeType = "text/markdown")]
 49:     [Description("Returns an article by its ID.")]
 50:     public static ResourceContents GetArticle(string id)
 51:     {
 52:         string? content = LoadArticle(id);
 53:         if (content is null)
 54:             throw new McpException($"Article not found: {id}");
 55: 
 56:         return new TextResourceContents
 57:         {
 58:             Uri = $"docs://articles/{id}",
 59:             MimeType = "text/markdown",
 60:             Text = content
 61:         };
 62:     }
 63: }
 64: ```
 65: 
 66: ## Return types
 67: 
 68: | Return | Result |
 69: |---|---|
 70: | `string` | Wrapped in `TextResourceContents` with the URI from the template and the declared `MimeType`. |
 71: | `byte[]` | Wrapped in `BlobResourceContents`. |
 72: | `TextResourceContents` | Returned as-is — set `Uri`, `MimeType`, `Text`. |
 73: | `BlobResourceContents` | Returned as-is — use `BlobResourceContents.FromBytes(...)`. |
 74: | `IEnumerable<ResourceContents>` | Multi-part resource. |
 75: 
 76: ### Binary resource
 77: 
 78: ```csharp
 79: [McpServerResource(
 80:     UriTemplate = "images://photos/{id}",
 81:     Name = "Photo",
 82:     MimeType = "image/png")]
 83: public static BlobResourceContents GetPhoto(int id)
 84: {
 85:     byte[] data = LoadPhoto(id);
 86:     return BlobResourceContents.FromBytes(data, $"images://photos/{id}", "image/png");
 87: }
 88: ```
 89: 
 90: ### Pointing at the file system
 91: 
 92: A common pattern is exposing files from disk. Be careful about path traversal — never trust the URI verbatim.
 93: 
 94: ```csharp
 95: [McpServerResource(
 96:     UriTemplate = "file://workspace/{*relativePath}",
 97:     Name = "Workspace file")]
 98: public static TextResourceContents ReadFile(string relativePath, IOptions<WorkspaceOptions> opts)
 99: {
100:     var root = opts.Value.RootPath;
101:     var fullPath = Path.GetFullPath(Path.Combine(root, relativePath));
102:     if (!fullPath.StartsWith(root, StringComparison.Ordinal))
103:         throw new McpException("Path traversal blocked.");
104: 
105:     return new TextResourceContents
106:     {
107:         Uri = $"file://workspace/{relativePath.Replace("\\", "/")}",
108:         MimeType = "text/plain",
109:         Text = File.ReadAllText(fullPath)
110:     };
111: }
112: ```
113: 
114: ## Listing dynamic resources
115: 
116: Attribute-based discovery covers the common case (one method per template). When you need to **enumerate** resources that don't fit a template — say, "list every file in the workspace" — implement a low-level handler in `McpServerOptions.Capabilities.Resources`:
117: 
118: ```csharp
119: builder.Services.Configure<McpServerOptions>(options =>
120: {
121:     options.Capabilities ??= new();
122:     options.Capabilities.Resources ??= new();
123: 
124:     options.Capabilities.Resources.ListResourcesHandler = (ctx, ct) =>
125:     {
126:         var resources = Directory
127:             .EnumerateFiles(WorkspaceRoot, "*.*", SearchOption.AllDirectories)
128:             .Select(path => new Resource
129:             {
130:                 Uri = "file://workspace/" + Path.GetRelativePath(WorkspaceRoot, path).Replace('\\', '/'),
131:                 Name = Path.GetFileName(path),
132:                 MimeType = "text/plain"
133:             })
134:             .ToList();
135: 
136:         return ValueTask.FromResult(new ListResourcesResult { Resources = resources });
137:     };
138: });
139: ```
140: 
141: You can mix attribute-based and handler-based — the SDK merges both.
142: 
143: ## Resource subscriptions (server-pushed updates)
144: 
145: If a client subscribes to a resource and it changes, push a notification:
146: 
147: ```csharp
148: await server.SendNotificationAsync(
149:     NotificationMethods.ResourceUpdatedNotification,
150:     new ResourceUpdatedNotificationParams { Uri = "docs://articles/42" },
151:     cancellationToken);
152: ```
153: 
154: For wholesale list changes:
155: 
156: ```csharp
157: await server.SendNotificationAsync(
158:     NotificationMethods.ResourceListChangedNotification,
159:     new ResourceListChangedNotificationParams(),
160:     cancellationToken);
161: ```
162: 
163: Both require a stateful transport.
164: 
165: ## Reading resources from a client
166: 
167: ```csharp
168: ReadResourceResult result = await client.ReadResourceAsync("config://app/settings");
169: foreach (var content in result.Contents)
170: {
171:     if (content is TextResourceContents text)
172:         Console.WriteLine($"[{text.MimeType}] {text.Text}");
173:     else if (content is BlobResourceContents blob)
174:         File.WriteAllBytes("out.bin", blob.DecodedData.ToArray());
175: }
176: ```
177: 
178: ## Resources vs. tools — when to pick which
179: 
180: - **Resource:** the user (or LLM) wants to *attach context* to the conversation. Read-only, addressable, listable. The host controls when/whether to load it. Ideal for documents, configs, schemas.
181: - **Tool:** the LLM wants to *do something* (which may include reading data). Side-effects, actions, parameters that don't fit a URI.
182: 
183: If you have something the LLM might want to *search* over, expose both: a `search_articles` tool and `docs://articles/{id}` resource template. The tool returns a list of URIs; the host fetches the content via the resource.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/dotnet-mcp-builder/references/roots.md">
  1: # Roots
  2: 
  3: Roots are filesystem (or URI) locations the **client** advertises to the server, scoping what the server is allowed to look at. Think "open workspace folders" in an IDE — the user has implicitly approved the server reading from these places. The server pulls the list when it needs it.
  4: 
  5: ## When you'd use roots
  6: 
  7: - Building a tool that scans/edits the user's project. Use roots to know which directories are in scope.
  8: - Resolving relative paths in a way that respects the user's open workspace.
  9: - Restricting file access to the advertised roots (defence in depth).
 10: 
 11: ## Prerequisite
 12: 
 13: Same as sampling/elicitation: server-to-client request → needs STDIO or stateful HTTP. Plus the client must advertise the `roots` capability.
 14: 
 15: ## Reading roots from a tool
 16: 
 17: ```csharp
 18: using System.ComponentModel;
 19: using System.Text;
 20: using ModelContextProtocol.Protocol;
 21: using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
 22: 
 23: [McpServerToolType]
 24: public class WorkspaceTools
 25: {
 26:     [McpServerTool, Description("Lists the user's project roots.")]
 27:     public static async Task<string> ListProjectRoots(
 28:         IMcpServer server,
 29:         CancellationToken cancellationToken)
 30:     {
 31:         if (server.ClientCapabilities?.Roots is null)
 32:             return "Client does not support roots.";
 33: 
 34:         var result = await server.RequestRootsAsync(
 35:             new ListRootsRequestParams(),
 36:             cancellationToken);
 37: 
 38:         var sb = new StringBuilder();
 39:         foreach (var root in result.Roots)
 40:             sb.AppendLine($"- {root.Name ?? root.Uri}: {root.Uri}");
 41: 
 42:         return sb.ToString();
 43:     }
 44: }
 45: ```
 46: 
 47: `Root` has `Uri` (string, often a `file://...`) and optional `Name` (display label).
 48: 
 49: ## Reacting to root changes
 50: 
 51: Clients send `notifications/roots/list_changed` when the user opens or closes a workspace folder. Subscribe:
 52: 
 53: ```csharp
 54: builder.Services.Configure<McpServerOptions>(options =>
 55: {
 56:     options.Capabilities ??= new();
 57: 
 58:     // The client tells us its roots changed; refresh whatever cache we have.
 59:     options.Capabilities.NotificationHandlers ??= [];
 60:     options.Capabilities.NotificationHandlers[NotificationMethods.RootsListChangedNotification] =
 61:         async (notification, ct) =>
 62:         {
 63:             // Trigger your refresh — typically pull RequestRootsAsync again.
 64:         };
 65: });
 66: ```
 67: 
 68: ## A useful pattern: cache + refresh
 69: 
 70: Roots don't change often, but refetching on every tool call is wasteful. Cache them per session and refresh on `roots/list_changed`:
 71: 
 72: ```csharp
 73: public class RootsCache
 74: {
 75:     private IReadOnlyList<Root> _roots = Array.Empty<Root>();
 76: 
 77:     public IReadOnlyList<Root> Current => _roots;
 78: 
 79:     public async Task RefreshAsync(IMcpServer server, CancellationToken ct)
 80:     {
 81:         if (server.ClientCapabilities?.Roots is null) return;
 82:         var result = await server.RequestRootsAsync(new ListRootsRequestParams(), ct);
 83:         _roots = result.Roots;
 84:     }
 85: }
 86: ```
 87: 
 88: Register as singleton (per-session in stateful HTTP, naturally singleton in STDIO).
 89: 
 90: ## Validating paths against roots
 91: 
 92: Defence in depth: even if a tool argument *looks* like a path under a root, validate.
 93: 
 94: ```csharp
 95: public static bool IsUnderAnyRoot(string absolutePath, IReadOnlyList<Root> roots)
 96: {
 97:     foreach (var root in roots)
 98:     {
 99:         if (!Uri.TryCreate(root.Uri, UriKind.Absolute, out var uri)) continue;
100:         if (!uri.IsFile) continue;
101:         var rootPath = Path.GetFullPath(uri.LocalPath);
102:         if (absolutePath.StartsWith(rootPath, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
103:             return true;
104:     }
105:     return false;
106: }
107: ```
108: 
109: If a tool receives a path outside the advertised roots, refuse with a clear message — don't silently expand scope.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/dotnet-mcp-builder/references/sampling.md">
  1: # Sampling
  2: 
  3: Sampling lets a tool **call the LLM through the client** instead of bringing its own model. The server says "summarise this for me" and the client routes the request to whatever model the user has configured (Claude, GPT, local model, anything). Costs and rate limits live with the client, not the server.
  4: 
  5: ## When to use sampling
  6: 
  7: - The tool needs an LLM step (summarise, classify, draft, extract) and you don't want to ship/configure your own model in the server.
  8: - You want to respect the user's model choice, key, and cost preferences.
  9: - You're building a "meta" tool that orchestrates LLM work as part of its job (e.g. multi-step agents).
 10: 
 11: If you already have a deterministic algorithm, don't add a sampling call "for flavour" — it adds latency and cost.
 12: 
 13: ## Prerequisite: stateful transport
 14: 
 15: Like elicitation, sampling needs the server to call back to the client. STDIO works always; HTTP needs `options.Stateless = false`.
 16: 
 17: ## Recommended: `IChatClient` adapter
 18: 
 19: The cleanest API wraps the sampling channel as `Microsoft.Extensions.AI.IChatClient`, so you write code that looks like normal LLM-calling .NET:
 20: 
 21: ```csharp
 22: using System.ComponentModel;
 23: using Microsoft.Extensions.AI;
 24: using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
 25: 
 26: [McpServerToolType]
 27: public class SummaryTools
 28: {
 29:     [McpServerTool(Name = "SummarizeContent"), Description("Summarises arbitrary text using the client's LLM.")]
 30:     public static async Task<string> Summarize(
 31:         IMcpServer server,
 32:         [Description("The text to summarize")] string text,
 33:         CancellationToken cancellationToken)
 34:     {
 35:         ChatMessage[] messages =
 36:         [
 37:             new(ChatRole.User, "Briefly summarize the following content:"),
 38:             new(ChatRole.User, text),
 39:         ];
 40: 
 41:         var options = new ChatOptions
 42:         {
 43:             MaxOutputTokens = 256,
 44:             Temperature = 0.3f,
 45:         };
 46: 
 47:         var response = await server.AsSamplingChatClient()
 48:             .GetResponseAsync(messages, options, cancellationToken);
 49: 
 50:         return $"Summary: {response}";
 51:     }
 52: }
 53: ```
 54: 
 55: Why this is nice:
 56: - Same `IChatClient` API the rest of the .NET AI ecosystem uses.
 57: - Works with `Microsoft.Extensions.AI` middleware (rate limiting, retries, telemetry, function calling).
 58: - You can swap to a direct provider in tests by injecting a different `IChatClient`.
 59: 
 60: ## Lower-level: `SampleAsync`
 61: 
 62: When you need full control over the request shape:
 63: 
 64: ```csharp
 65: using ModelContextProtocol.Protocol;
 66: 
 67: CreateMessageResult result = await server.SampleAsync(
 68:     new CreateMessageRequestParams
 69:     {
 70:         Messages =
 71:         [
 72:             new SamplingMessage
 73:             {
 74:                 Role = Role.User,
 75:                 Content = [new TextContentBlock { Text = "What is 2 + 2?" }]
 76:             }
 77:         ],
 78:         MaxTokens = 100,
 79:         Temperature = 0.0f,
 80:         SystemPrompt = "You are a precise calculator.",
 81:         // ModelPreferences, StopSequences, IncludeContext...
 82:     },
 83:     cancellationToken);
 84: 
 85: string answer = result.Content
 86:     .OfType<TextContentBlock>()
 87:     .FirstOrDefault()?.Text ?? string.Empty;
 88: ```
 89: 
 90: `ModelPreferences` lets you hint at model selection (cost vs. speed vs. intelligence priority); the *client* decides the actual model.
 91: 
 92: ```csharp
 93: ModelPreferences = new ModelPreferences
 94: {
 95:     Hints = [new ModelHint { Name = "claude" }],   // soft preference
 96:     CostPriority = 0.2,        // 0..1
 97:     SpeedPriority = 0.4,
 98:     IntelligencePriority = 0.9,
 99: }
100: ```
101: 
102: ## `IncludeContext`
103: 
104: Sampling requests can ask the client to include context from the current conversation:
105: 
106: ```csharp
107: IncludeContext = ContextInclusion.ThisServer   // include this server's prior messages
108: // or AllServers, or None (default)
109: ```
110: 
111: Useful when you need the LLM to consider what's happened in the chat so far without you re-supplying it.
112: 
113: ## Capability check
114: 
115: Always confirm the client supports sampling — many do not:
116: 
117: ```csharp
118: if (server.ClientCapabilities?.Sampling is null)
119:     throw new McpException(
120:         "This client does not support sampling. " +
121:         "Configure a model in the host or use a different MCP client.");
122: ```
123: 
124: ## Performance notes
125: 
126: - Sampling calls are network round-trips (client → its provider → back). Expect 100ms–multiple seconds. Don't loop tightly.
127: - Token costs are paid by the *user* (their API key/quota). Be conservative with `MaxTokens`.
128: - Cancellation propagates: if the user kills the tool call, the sampling request is cancelled too.
129: 
130: ## Sampling vs. doing it server-side
131: 
132: | Sampling (via client) | Direct LLM call (server-side) |
133: |---|---|
134: | Uses the user's model + key | Uses your service's key |
135: | Respects user's policy/quota | Your responsibility to bill/track |
136: | Works in any host the user has | Locked to the model you ship with |
137: | Higher latency (extra hop) | Lower latency, direct |
138: | No secrets to manage | You manage the API key |
139: 
140: For "smart" servers shipped to many users, prefer sampling. For internal corporate servers where you want consistent behaviour and you're already paying for the model, direct is fine.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/dotnet-mcp-builder/references/server-features.md">
  1: # Other server features (completions, logging, progress, filters)
  2: 
  3: A quick reference for the smaller MCP server features beyond the core primitives. Each section is short — load this file when one of these comes up.
  4: 
  5: ## Argument completions
  6: 
  7: Completions let the host autocomplete prompt arguments and resource template parameters. The user starts typing; the client asks the server "what are valid values?".
  8: 
  9: Implement via the low-level handler (no high-level attribute exists yet):
 10: 
 11: ```csharp
 12: builder.Services.Configure<McpServerOptions>(options =>
 13: {
 14:     options.Capabilities ??= new();
 15:     options.Capabilities.Completions ??= new();
 16: 
 17:     options.Capabilities.Completions.CompleteHandler = async (ctx, ct) =>
 18:     {
 19:         // ctx.Params.Ref tells us what they're completing (a prompt or resource).
 20:         // ctx.Params.Argument has the partial value typed so far.
 21:         var partial = ctx.Params.Argument.Value ?? "";
 22:         var matches = MyDataSource
 23:             .Where(x => x.StartsWith(partial, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
 24:             .Take(100)
 25:             .ToArray();
 26: 
 27:         return new CompleteResult
 28:         {
 29:             Completion = new()
 30:             {
 31:                 Values = matches,
 32:                 HasMore = false,
 33:                 Total = matches.Length
 34:             }
 35:         };
 36:     };
 37: });
 38: ```
 39: 
 40: Useful for: project IDs, file names, enum values that depend on dynamic data.
 41: 
 42: ## Logging
 43: 
 44: Servers can emit log messages that hosts surface in their UI (and the LLM can sometimes see). Use the standard `ILogger<T>` injected via DI — the SDK plumbs it through.
 45: 
 46: ```csharp
 47: public class WeatherTools
 48: {
 49:     private readonly ILogger<WeatherTools> _log;
 50:     public WeatherTools(ILogger<WeatherTools> log) => _log = log;
 51: 
 52:     [McpServerTool, Description("…")]
 53:     public string GetWeather(string city)
 54:     {
 55:         _log.LogInformation("Looking up weather for {City}", city);
 56:         return "...";
 57:     }
 58: }
 59: ```
 60: 
 61: For STDIO servers, remember: console logging **must** go to stderr (`LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace`) — otherwise it corrupts the JSON-RPC stream. See [`transport-stdio.md`](./transport-stdio.md).
 62: 
 63: To send a log specifically over the MCP channel (so the *host UI* sees it, not just your container logs):
 64: 
 65: ```csharp
 66: await server.SendNotificationAsync(
 67:     NotificationMethods.LoggingMessageNotification,
 68:     new LoggingMessageNotificationParams
 69:     {
 70:         Level = LoggingLevel.Info,
 71:         Logger = "weather",
 72:         Data = JsonSerializer.SerializeToElement(new { city, latency_ms = 123 })
 73:     },
 74:     ct);
 75: ```
 76: 
 77: The client may have set a `setLevel` filter — don't spam levels below it.
 78: 
 79: ## Progress notifications
 80: 
 81: For long-running tools, send progress updates so the host can display a spinner with text:
 82: 
 83: ```csharp
 84: [McpServerTool, Description("Processes a large dataset.")]
 85: public static async Task<string> Process(
 86:     IMcpServer server,
 87:     RequestContext<CallToolRequestParams> ctx,
 88:     string datasetId,
 89:     CancellationToken ct)
 90: {
 91:     var progressToken = ctx.Params.Meta?.ProgressToken;
 92: 
 93:     for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
 94:     {
 95:         await Task.Delay(50, ct);
 96: 
 97:         if (progressToken is not null)
 98:         {
 99:             await server.SendNotificationAsync(
100:                 NotificationMethods.ProgressNotification,
101:                 new ProgressNotificationParams
102:                 {
103:                     ProgressToken = progressToken,
104:                     Progress = i + 1,
105:                     Total = 100,
106:                     Message = $"Processing item {i + 1} of 100"
107:                 },
108:                 ct);
109:         }
110:     }
111: 
112:     return "Done.";
113: }
114: ```
115: 
116: Only send progress if the client passed a `progressToken` in the request meta — otherwise the host isn't listening.
117: 
118: ## Notification handlers (server-side)
119: 
120: Servers can react to notifications the client sends:
121: 
122: ```csharp
123: options.Capabilities ??= new();
124: options.Capabilities.NotificationHandlers ??= [];
125: 
126: options.Capabilities.NotificationHandlers[NotificationMethods.RootsListChangedNotification] =
127:     async (notification, ct) =>
128:     {
129:         // Refresh root cache, etc.
130:     };
131: 
132: options.Capabilities.NotificationHandlers[NotificationMethods.CancelledNotification] =
133:     async (notification, ct) =>
134:     {
135:         // The client cancelled a request; if you have side-effects in flight, abort them.
136:     };
137: ```
138: 
139: ## Filters / middleware
140: 
141: The SDK supports filters that wrap tool calls (think ASP.NET Core middleware for MCP). Use them for cross-cutting concerns: auth checks, telemetry, rate limiting, audit logging.
142: 
143: ```csharp
144: builder.Services
145:     .AddMcpServer()
146:     .WithStdioServerTransport()
147:     .WithToolsFromAssembly()
148:     .WithCallToolFilter(async (ctx, next) =>
149:     {
150:         var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();
151:         try
152:         {
153:             return await next(ctx);
154:         }
155:         finally
156:         {
157:             sw.Stop();
158:             ctx.Server.Services?
159:                 .GetRequiredService<ILogger<Program>>()
160:                 .LogInformation("Tool {Tool} took {Ms}ms",
161:                     ctx.Params.Name, sw.ElapsedMilliseconds);
162:         }
163:     });
164: ```
165: 
166: Similar `With*Filter` helpers exist for resources, prompts, and other capabilities — check the SDK API reference for the current set.
167: 
168: ## Server instructions (system-prompt-ish)
169: 
170: You can supply instructions sent to the client at initialise time. Hosts may include them in the LLM's system prompt.
171: 
172: ```csharp
173: builder.Services.AddMcpServer(options =>
174: {
175:     options.ServerInstructions =
176:         "Use the booking tools to schedule meetings. " +
177:         "Always confirm with the user before booking via elicitation.";
178: });
179: ```
180: 
181: Keep this short — every token here costs the user.
182: 
183: ## Capabilities advertising
184: 
185: If you want to *not* advertise a capability you happen to have code for, you can mute it:
186: 
187: ```csharp
188: builder.Services.AddMcpServer(options =>
189: {
190:     options.Capabilities = new()
191:     {
192:         Tools = new(),       // advertise tools
193:         Prompts = new(),     // advertise prompts
194:         Resources = null,    // do NOT advertise resources, even if some are registered
195:         Logging = new()
196:     };
197: });
198: ```
199: 
200: By default, the SDK advertises everything you've registered — usually the right behaviour.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/dotnet-mcp-builder/references/testing.md">
  1: # Testing and local debugging
  2: 
  3: Three workflows: interactive testing with MCP Inspector, in-process integration tests, and CI-friendly unit tests.
  4: 
  5: ## MCP Inspector (interactive)
  6: 
  7: [MCP Inspector](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/inspector) is the go-to tool for trying out a server by hand. It launches your server, connects via STDIO or HTTP, and gives you a UI to list/call tools, view resources, fire elicitations, see logs, and inspect raw JSON-RPC frames.
  8: 
  9: ### STDIO
 10: 
 11: ```bash
 12: npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector dotnet run --project ./MyMcpServer
 13: ```
 14: 
 15: Pass env vars or args after `--`:
 16: 
 17: ```bash
 18: npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector \
 19:   dotnet run --project ./MyMcpServer -- \
 20:   --some-flag value
 21: ```
 22: 
 23: ### HTTP
 24: 
 25: Start the server normally (`dotnet run`), then in Inspector pick "Streamable HTTP" and enter the URL (e.g. `http://localhost:3001`).
 26: 
 27: ### Use it for
 28: 
 29: - Verifying tool descriptions are clear (Inspector renders them like the LLM would consume them).
 30: - Walking through elicitation flows without a real LLM.
 31: - Capturing the exact JSON-RPC payloads when filing bug reports.
 32: 
 33: ## In-process integration tests (recommended)
 34: 
 35: The cleanest test setup uses `InMemoryTransport` (or the lower-level `StreamServerTransport` / `StreamClientTransport`) to wire a real server and a real client together in the same process. No subprocesses, no network.
 36: 
 37: ```csharp
 38: using System.IO.Pipelines;
 39: using ModelContextProtocol;
 40: using ModelContextProtocol.Client;
 41: using ModelContextProtocol.Protocol;
 42: using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
 43: using Xunit;
 44: 
 45: public class WeatherToolsTests
 46: {
 47:     [Fact]
 48:     public async Task GetWeather_returns_text()
 49:     {
 50:         var clientToServer = new Pipe();
 51:         var serverToClient = new Pipe();
 52: 
 53:         await using var server = McpServer.Create(
 54:             new StreamServerTransport(
 55:                 clientToServer.Reader.AsStream(),
 56:                 serverToClient.Writer.AsStream()),
 57:             new McpServerOptions
 58:             {
 59:                 ToolCollection =
 60:                 [
 61:                     McpServerTool.Create(
 62:                         (string city) => $"{city}: 18°C",
 63:                         new() { Name = "GetWeather" })
 64:                 ]
 65:             });
 66: 
 67:         var serverTask = server.RunAsync();
 68: 
 69:         await using var client = await McpClient.CreateAsync(
 70:             new StreamClientTransport(
 71:                 clientToServer.Writer.AsStream(),
 72:                 serverToClient.Reader.AsStream()));
 73: 
 74:         var tools = await client.ListToolsAsync();
 75:         var tool = tools.Single(t => t.Name == "GetWeather");
 76: 
 77:         var result = await tool.CallAsync(new Dictionary<string, object?>
 78:         {
 79:             ["city"] = "Brussels"
 80:         });
 81: 
 82:         Assert.False(result.IsError);
 83:         var text = result.Content.OfType<TextContentBlock>().Single().Text;
 84:         Assert.Equal("Brussels: 18°C", text);
 85:     }
 86: }
 87: ```
 88: 
 89: This style lets you assert on the *exposed* behaviour (what a real client sees), not internal details.
 90: 
 91: ## Testing tools that use sampling/elicitation/roots
 92: 
 93: Inject the MCP server, but supply mock client capabilities. With the in-memory pattern above, register handlers on the client:
 94: 
 95: ```csharp
 96: await using var client = await McpClient.CreateAsync(clientTransport, new McpClientOptions
 97: {
 98:     Capabilities = new()
 99:     {
100:         Sampling = new()
101:         {
102:             SamplingHandler = (req, progress, ct) =>
103:                 Task.FromResult(new CreateMessageResult
104:                 {
105:                     Content = [new TextContentBlock { Text = "MOCK SUMMARY" }]
106:                 })
107:         },
108:         Elicitation = new()
109:         {
110:             ElicitationHandler = (req, ct) =>
111:                 Task.FromResult(new ElicitResult
112:                 {
113:                     Action = "accept",
114:                     Content = JsonSerializer.SerializeToNode(new { confirm = true })
115:                                 .AsObject().ToDictionary(kv => kv.Key, kv => JsonDocument.Parse(kv.Value!.ToJsonString()).RootElement)
116:                 })
117:         }
118:     }
119: });
120: ```
121: 
122: Now your tool's `server.SampleAsync` / `server.ElicitAsync` calls hit deterministic mocks.
123: 
124: ## Unit tests at the DI layer
125: 
126: For pure logic with no MCP-specific behaviour, just test the class:
127: 
128: ```csharp
129: [Fact]
130: public void Echo_prepends_hello()
131: {
132:     Assert.Equal("hello world", EchoTool.Echo("world"));
133: }
134: ```
135: 
136: The `[McpServerTool]` attribute doesn't affect runtime behaviour outside MCP wiring — your methods are just methods.
137: 
138: ## Running it from Claude Desktop / VS Code during development
139: 
140: For end-to-end "feels-like-the-real-thing" testing:
141: 
142: 1. Run `dotnet publish -c Release` (or just `dotnet build` and use `dotnet run`).
143: 2. Point Claude Desktop / VS Code at the binary or `dotnet run --project ...`. See [`transport-stdio.md`](./transport-stdio.md) for the config snippets.
144: 3. Restart the host.
145: 4. Trigger the tool from chat.
146: 
147: When iterating, set up `dotnet watch run --project ...` so the server restarts on edit; the host typically reconnects on the next tool call.
148: 
149: ## CI
150: 
151: A typical CI pipeline:
152: 
153: ```yaml
154: - run: dotnet restore
155: - run: dotnet build --no-restore
156: - run: dotnet test --no-build --logger "trx;LogFileName=test-results.trx"
157: ```
158: 
159: Nothing MCP-specific. The in-memory transport tests run anywhere `dotnet test` runs — no Node, no Docker.
160: 
161: ## Common diagnostic tricks
162: 
163: - **"Tool isn't showing up":** call `client.ListToolsAsync()` in a quick test and dump the names. If your tool isn't there, the registration is wrong.
164: - **"LLM keeps misusing the tool":** open Inspector and look at the schema/description as the LLM sees it. Most "the model is dumb" issues are actually missing `[Description]`.
165: - **"Sampling/elicitation throws 'method not supported'":** the client doesn't advertise the capability. Either you're testing against a host that doesn't support it (Inspector supports both), or your in-memory client is missing the handler.
166: - **"HTTP returns 404 for /":** check `app.MapMcp()` is called *and* you're hitting the right path. `MapMcp("/mcp")` means the URL is `http://host/mcp`, not `http://host/`.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/dotnet-mcp-builder/references/tool-primitive.md">
  1: # Tools
  2: 
  3: Tools are functions the LLM can call. In the C# SDK they're plain methods on a class marked `[McpServerToolType]`, with each method marked `[McpServerTool]`. The SDK generates the JSON Schema from the method signature and `[Description]` attributes.
  4: 
  5: ## Anatomy of a tool
  6: 
  7: ```csharp
  8: using System.ComponentModel;
  9: using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
 10: 
 11: [McpServerToolType]
 12: public class WeatherTools
 13: {
 14:     // Static or instance — both work. Instance methods get DI for the containing class.
 15:     [McpServerTool, Description("Returns the current weather for a city.")]
 16:     public static string GetWeather(
 17:         [Description("City name, e.g. 'Brussels'")] string city,
 18:         [Description("Units: 'celsius' or 'fahrenheit'")] string units = "celsius")
 19:     {
 20:         return $"{city}: 18°{units[0]}";
 21:     }
 22: }
 23: ```
 24: 
 25: Register it (one of):
 26: 
 27: ```csharp
 28: .WithToolsFromAssembly()        // discovers all [McpServerToolType] in the calling assembly
 29: .WithTools<WeatherTools>()      // explicit, single class
 30: ```
 31: 
 32: The tool name shown to the LLM is `GetWeather` (PascalCase converted to snake_case is **not** automatic — what you see is what you get unless you set `Name` explicitly).
 33: 
 34: ## Attribute options
 35: 
 36: ```csharp
 37: [McpServerTool(
 38:     Name = "get_weather",                 // override the tool name
 39:     Title = "Get current weather",        // human-readable display name
 40:     Destructive = false,                  // hint: tool modifies state irreversibly
 41:     Idempotent = true,                    // hint: same args ⇒ same result
 42:     OpenWorld = true,                     // hint: interacts with external systems
 43:     ReadOnly = true                       // hint: doesn't mutate any state
 44: )]
 45: [Description("Returns the current weather for a city.")]
 46: public static string GetWeather(...) { ... }
 47: ```
 48: 
 49: The behaviour hints (`Destructive`, `Idempotent`, `OpenWorld`, `ReadOnly`) are advisory — clients use them to decide things like auto-approval. They don't change runtime behaviour.
 50: 
 51: ## Async, cancellation, DI
 52: 
 53: ```csharp
 54: [McpServerTool, Description("Fetches the latest commits for a repo.")]
 55: public async Task<IEnumerable<Commit>> GetCommits(
 56:     string owner,
 57:     string repo,
 58:     IGitHubClient github,                         // injected from DI
 59:     CancellationToken cancellationToken)          // injected by the SDK
 60: {
 61:     return await github.GetCommitsAsync(owner, repo, cancellationToken);
 62: }
 63: ```
 64: 
 65: The SDK recognises and special-cases these parameter types — they don't appear in the tool schema:
 66: - `IMcpServer` / `McpServer` — the current server (used for `ElicitAsync`, `SampleAsync`, `RequestRootsAsync`, sending notifications).
 67: - `CancellationToken` — propagated from the JSON-RPC request.
 68: - `RequestContext<CallToolRequestParams>` — full request context if you need it.
 69: - `IServiceProvider` — request-scoped service provider.
 70: - Anything resolvable from DI that the SDK can recognise as not a primitive payload.
 71: 
 72: Everything else is treated as a JSON-RPC argument and goes into the schema.
 73: 
 74: ## Return types
 75: 
 76: The SDK serialises whatever you return into the appropriate content blocks. Practical guidance:
 77: 
 78: | Return type | What the LLM sees |
 79: |---|---|
 80: | `string` | Single text content block. |
 81: | `int`, `bool`, `double`, etc. | Stringified into a text content block. |
 82: | Any DTO (record/class) | Serialized to JSON in a text content block, plus structured content for clients that support it. |
 83: | `IEnumerable<T>` of DTOs | JSON array. |
 84: | `ContentBlock` / `ImageContentBlock` / `AudioContentBlock` / `EmbeddedResourceBlock` | That single block, untouched. |
 85: | `IEnumerable<ContentBlock>` | Multiple blocks in order. |
 86: | `CallToolResult` | Full control — set `Content`, `StructuredContent`, `IsError`. |
 87: 
 88: ### Returning structured data the LLM can act on
 89: 
 90: ```csharp
 91: public record Forecast(string City, double TempC, string Conditions);
 92: 
 93: [McpServerTool, Description("Returns a 3-day forecast.")]
 94: public static Forecast[] GetForecast(string city) =>
 95:     new[]
 96:     {
 97:         new Forecast(city, 18.0, "sunny"),
 98:         new Forecast(city, 16.5, "cloudy"),
 99:         new Forecast(city, 14.2, "rain"),
100:     };
101: ```
102: 
103: The SDK emits the array as both a JSON text block (for older clients) and `structuredContent` (for newer ones), and infers an output schema from `Forecast`.
104: 
105: ### Returning images / audio
106: 
107: ```csharp
108: [McpServerTool, Description("Generates a chart and returns it as a PNG.")]
109: public static ImageContentBlock RenderChart(string title)
110: {
111:     byte[] png = Renderer.Render(title);
112:     return ImageContentBlock.FromBytes(png, "image/png");
113: }
114: 
115: [McpServerTool, Description("Synthesises speech.")]
116: public static AudioContentBlock Speak(string text)
117: {
118:     byte[] wav = Tts.Synthesize(text);
119:     return AudioContentBlock.FromBytes(wav, "audio/wav");
120: }
121: ```
122: 
123: ### Mixing content blocks
124: 
125: ```csharp
126: [McpServerTool, Description("Returns the chart and a caption.")]
127: public static IEnumerable<ContentBlock> RenderAnnotatedChart(string title)
128: {
129:     byte[] png = Renderer.Render(title);
130:     return new ContentBlock[]
131:     {
132:         new TextContentBlock { Text = $"Chart for: {title}" },
133:         ImageContentBlock.FromBytes(png, "image/png"),
134:         new TextContentBlock { Text = "Generated at " + DateTime.UtcNow.ToString("u") }
135:     };
136: }
137: ```
138: 
139: ### Returning an embedded resource
140: 
141: Useful when the tool result *is* a document the user might want to reuse:
142: 
143: ```csharp
144: [McpServerTool, Description("Looks up a contract.")]
145: public static EmbeddedResourceBlock GetContract(string id)
146: {
147:     return new EmbeddedResourceBlock
148:     {
149:         Resource = new TextResourceContents
150:         {
151:             Uri = $"contracts://{id}",
152:             MimeType = "text/markdown",
153:             Text = LoadContract(id)
154:         }
155:     };
156: }
157: ```
158: 
159: ## Errors
160: 
161: There are two flavours of error a tool can produce:
162: 
163: ### Tool-level errors (the LLM can read and recover from these)
164: 
165: Throw any exception — the SDK catches it and returns a `CallToolResult` with `IsError = true` and the exception message in a text block:
166: 
167: ```csharp
168: [McpServerTool, Description("Divides a by b.")]
169: public static double Divide(double a, double b)
170: {
171:     if (b == 0)
172:         throw new ArgumentException("Cannot divide by zero.");
173:     return a / b;
174: }
175: ```
176: 
177: You can also build the result explicitly:
178: 
179: ```csharp
180: [McpServerTool, Description("…")]
181: public static CallToolResult Foo(...)
182: {
183:     return new CallToolResult
184:     {
185:         IsError = true,
186:         Content = [new TextContentBlock { Text = "Detailed error explanation for the LLM." }]
187:     };
188: }
189: ```
190: 
191: ### Protocol-level errors (the call is rejected before the LLM sees a result)
192: 
193: Use `McpException` (or `McpProtocolException` with an explicit error code) for things like bad arguments:
194: 
195: ```csharp
196: [McpServerTool, Description("…")]
197: public static string Process(string input)
198: {
199:     if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(input))
200:         throw new McpProtocolException("Missing required input", McpErrorCode.InvalidParams);
201:     return $"Processed: {input}";
202: }
203: ```
204: 
205: **Heuristic:** if the LLM should *try again* with different arguments, throw a regular exception so it gets a tool error. If the call is malformed in a way the LLM can't fix, throw `McpProtocolException`.
206: 
207: ## Notifying clients of tool list changes
208: 
209: If your tools come and go at runtime (e.g. plugin loaded, user logged in), notify the client:
210: 
211: ```csharp
212: await server.SendNotificationAsync(
213:     NotificationMethods.ToolListChangedNotification,
214:     new ToolListChangedNotificationParams(),
215:     cancellationToken);
216: ```
217: 
218: Requires a stateful transport (STDIO or stateful HTTP).
219: 
220: ## Common pitfalls
221: 
222: - **Forgetting `[McpServerToolType]` on the class.** The method-level `[McpServerTool]` alone won't be discovered by `WithToolsFromAssembly`.
223: - **Vague descriptions.** `[Description("Gets data")]` makes the LLM guess. Spend a sentence describing what the tool does, when to call it, and what it returns.
224: - **Big payloads.** Tools that return megabytes of JSON eat the model's context. Trim or paginate. For binary blobs, return an `EmbeddedResourceBlock` so the host can decide how to render it.
225: - **Hiding errors.** Returning `"failed"` as a string looks like success to the SDK. Throw the exception or set `IsError = true`.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/dotnet-mcp-builder/references/transport-http.md">
  1: # Streamable HTTP transport (ASP.NET Core)
  2: 
  3: Streamable HTTP is the modern remote transport. A single endpoint accepts JSON-RPC over HTTP POST and (optionally) streams responses back as Server-Sent Events when the server has more than one message to send.
  4: 
  5: > **SSE-only is deprecated.** The legacy "HTTP+SSE" transport (separate POST endpoint + GET SSE endpoint) is gone from new clients. Use Streamable HTTP. Only enable legacy SSE (`EnableLegacySse = true`) if you must support a known-old client, and document why.
  6: 
  7: ## When to choose HTTP
  8: 
  9: - Multi-tenant or remote-hosted server.
 10: - Auth via OAuth / API gateway in front.
 11: - Horizontally scaled deployments (with `Stateless = true`).
 12: - Containers, Azure Container Apps, Kubernetes, etc.
 13: 
 14: For local single-user scenarios, [STDIO](./transport-stdio.md) is simpler.
 15: 
 16: ## Minimal server
 17: 
 18: ```bash
 19: dotnet new web -n MyHttpServer -f net10.0
 20: cd MyHttpServer
 21: dotnet add package ModelContextProtocol.AspNetCore
 22: ```
 23: 
 24: ```csharp
 25: // Program.cs
 26: using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
 27: using System.ComponentModel;
 28: 
 29: var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
 30: 
 31: builder.Services
 32:     .AddMcpServer()
 33:     .WithHttpTransport(options =>
 34:     {
 35:         // Stateless = true: each request is independent, no Mcp-Session-Id tracking.
 36:         // Required for horizontal scaling without sticky sessions.
 37:         // Disables server-to-client features (sampling, elicitation, roots, unsolicited notifications).
 38:         options.Stateless = true;
 39:     })
 40:     .WithToolsFromAssembly();
 41: 
 42: var app = builder.Build();
 43: 
 44: app.MapMcp();              // mounts the MCP endpoints at "/"
 45: // app.MapMcp("/mcp");     // or under a path prefix
 46: 
 47: app.Run("http://localhost:3001");
 48: 
 49: [McpServerToolType]
 50: public static class EchoTool
 51: {
 52:     [McpServerTool, Description("Echoes the message back to the client.")]
 53:     public static string Echo(string message) => $"hello {message}";
 54: }
 55: ```
 56: 
 57: ## Stateless vs. stateful — the most important decision
 58: 
 59: | Mode | `options.Stateless` | Behaviour | Use when |
 60: |---|---|---|---|
 61: | **Stateless** | `true` | No `Mcp-Session-Id`. Each POST is independent. | Horizontal scaling, simple tool servers, no server-initiated traffic. |
 62: | **Stateful** | `false` (default) | Server assigns and tracks `Mcp-Session-Id`. Long-lived session. | You need elicitation, sampling, roots, log notifications, or anything that pushes from server to client. Requires session affinity at the load balancer. |
 63: 
 64: **Rule:** if the user wants any of `ElicitAsync`, `SampleAsync`, `RequestRootsAsync`, or to push log/notification messages, **do not** set `Stateless = true`. The calls will fail at runtime with no transport to deliver them on.
 65: 
 66: ## Endpoint shape
 67: 
 68: `MapMcp(pattern = "")` creates a route group at `pattern` and maps:
 69: - **POST** — accepts JSON-RPC requests/responses/notifications. Returns either a JSON response or an SSE stream depending on `Accept` header and whether multiple messages need to flow back.
 70: - **GET** — used by stateful sessions for the server-to-client SSE channel.
 71: - **DELETE** — terminates a stateful session.
 72: 
 73: Default pattern is the root (`/`). To put MCP under `/mcp/v1`:
 74: 
 75: ```csharp
 76: app.MapMcp("/mcp/v1");
 77: ```
 78: 
 79: Match this on the client side (`Endpoint = new Uri("https://host/mcp/v1")`).
 80: 
 81: ## Per-session configuration (HttpContext access)
 82: 
 83: When you need to vary server behaviour per HTTP request (auth, tenant, headers), use the `ConfigureSessionOptions` callback:
 84: 
 85: ```csharp
 86: builder.Services
 87:     .AddMcpServer()
 88:     .WithHttpTransport(options =>
 89:     {
 90:         options.ConfigureSessionOptions = async (httpContext, mcpOptions, ct) =>
 91:         {
 92:             var tenantId = httpContext.Request.Headers["X-Tenant"].ToString();
 93:             mcpOptions.ServerInstructions = $"Tenant: {tenantId}";
 94:             // mutate any McpServerOptions fields per-session
 95:         };
 96:     });
 97: ```
 98: 
 99: Inside a tool, you can also inject `IHttpContextAccessor` if `AddHttpContextAccessor()` is registered. See the [`AspNetCoreMcpPerSessionTools` sample](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/csharp-sdk/tree/main/samples/AspNetCoreMcpPerSessionTools).
100: 
101: ## Authentication
102: 
103: The MCP endpoint is just an ASP.NET Core endpoint — apply standard middleware:
104: 
105: ```csharp
106: builder.Services
107:     .AddAuthentication("Bearer")
108:     .AddJwtBearer(/* configure */);
109: builder.Services.AddAuthorization();
110: 
111: var app = builder.Build();
112: 
113: app.UseAuthentication();
114: app.UseAuthorization();
115: 
116: app.MapMcp().RequireAuthorization();   // protect the endpoint
117: ```
118: 
119: For OAuth flows where the *MCP server* is the resource server, follow the [MCP authorization spec](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/basic/authorization). The [`ProtectedMcpServer` sample](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/csharp-sdk/tree/main/samples/ProtectedMcpServer) shows a working setup with discovery endpoints.
120: 
121: For machine-to-machine, an API key middleware is fine:
122: 
123: ```csharp
124: app.Use(async (ctx, next) =>
125: {
126:     if (ctx.Request.Headers["X-Api-Key"] != Configuration["ApiKey"])
127:     {
128:         ctx.Response.StatusCode = StatusCodes.Status401Unauthorized;
129:         return;
130:     }
131:     await next();
132: });
133: ```
134: 
135: ## CORS (when the client is in-browser)
136: 
137: ```csharp
138: builder.Services.AddCors(o => o.AddDefaultPolicy(p =>
139:     p.WithOrigins("https://my-host.example.com")
140:      .AllowAnyHeader()
141:      .AllowAnyMethod()
142:      .AllowCredentials()));
143: // ...
144: app.UseCors();
145: app.MapMcp();
146: ```
147: 
148: ## Health checks and observability
149: 
150: Add the standard ASP.NET Core probes; the MCP endpoint shouldn't be the liveness check.
151: 
152: ```csharp
153: builder.Services.AddHealthChecks();
154: // ...
155: app.MapHealthChecks("/healthz");
156: ```
157: 
158: The SDK emits OpenTelemetry traces (`Activity` per tool call) and metrics. Wire them up if the user has an OTel pipeline:
159: 
160: ```csharp
161: builder.Services
162:     .AddOpenTelemetry()
163:     .WithTracing(t => t.AddSource("ModelContextProtocol").AddOtlpExporter())
164:     .WithMetrics(m => m.AddMeter("ModelContextProtocol").AddOtlpExporter());
165: ```
166: 
167: ## Deployment notes
168: 
169: - **Containerise normally.** No special MCP-specific Dockerfile — it's just an ASP.NET Core app.
170: - **Behind a reverse proxy** (nginx, Azure Front Door, AWS ALB), make sure SSE buffering is **disabled** for the MCP path. nginx: `proxy_buffering off;`. Without this, streaming responses are batched into one slow blob.
171: - **Timeouts.** The client may keep an SSE connection open for a long time. Set proxy idle timeout high (e.g. 5+ minutes) for stateful deployments; less critical for stateless.
172: - **Azure Container Apps / App Service** work out of the box; both support long-lived HTTP responses.
173: 
174: ## Enabling legacy SSE (compatibility only)
175: 
176: ```csharp
177: builder.Services
178:     .AddMcpServer()
179:     .WithHttpTransport(options =>
180:     {
181:         options.EnableLegacySse = true;
182: #pragma warning disable MCP9004
183:         options.Stateless = false; // SSE requires stateful mode
184: #pragma warning restore MCP9004
185:     })
186:     .WithToolsFromAssembly();
187: ```
188: 
189: Only do this if the user has a documented client that hasn't migrated. New deployments should not enable it.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/dotnet-mcp-builder/references/transport-stdio.md">
  1: # STDIO transport
  2: 
  3: STDIO is the right choice when the server runs as a child process of the client (Claude Desktop, VS Code, MCP Inspector, a custom CLI). The client launches your executable; you read JSON-RPC frames from stdin and write them to stdout.
  4: 
  5: ## When to choose STDIO
  6: 
  7: - Local-first server (file-system access, dev tools, CLI integrations).
  8: - Distributing as a single executable or a `dnx`-runnable NuGet package.
  9: - You want the simplest possible deployment story (no network, no auth).
 10: - You need server-to-client features (sampling, elicitation, roots) — STDIO always supports them, no `Stateless` flag to worry about.
 11: 
 12: If the user wants a remote/multi-tenant server, use [HTTP Streamable](./transport-http.md) instead.
 13: 
 14: ## Minimal server
 15: 
 16: ```bash
 17: dotnet new console -n MyStdioServer -f net10.0
 18: cd MyStdioServer
 19: dotnet add package ModelContextProtocol
 20: dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting
 21: ```
 22: 
 23: ```csharp
 24: // Program.cs
 25: using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
 26: using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
 27: using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
 28: using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
 29: using System.ComponentModel;
 30: 
 31: var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);
 32: 
 33: // CRITICAL: stdout is the JSON-RPC channel. Send all logs to stderr.
 34: builder.Logging.AddConsole(o => o.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);
 35: 
 36: builder.Services
 37:     .AddMcpServer()
 38:     .WithStdioServerTransport()
 39:     .WithToolsFromAssembly();
 40: 
 41: await builder.Build().RunAsync();
 42: 
 43: [McpServerToolType]
 44: public static class EchoTool
 45: {
 46:     [McpServerTool, Description("Echoes the message back to the client.")]
 47:     public static string Echo(string message) => $"hello {message}";
 48: }
 49: ```
 50: 
 51: ## The stdout/stderr trap
 52: 
 53: The single most common bug in STDIO servers is something writing to stdout that isn't a JSON-RPC frame. The client will then drop the connection with a parse error.
 54: 
 55: **Things that silently break STDIO:**
 56: - `Console.WriteLine(...)` anywhere in your code.
 57: - A logger configured with the default console sink (writes to stdout).
 58: - `Trace.WriteLine(...)` if a default trace listener is attached.
 59: - Third-party libraries that print banners on startup.
 60: 
 61: **Defensive checklist:**
 62: 1. Configure logging to stderr **before** anything else (the snippet above does this).
 63: 2. Don't `Console.Write*` from tools or startup code. Use `ILogger` injected into the tool class.
 64: 3. If a dependency is noisy, redirect its logs through `ILogger` or suppress them at startup.
 65: 
 66: ## Server identity
 67: 
 68: The SDK sends `serverInfo` (name + version) in the `initialize` response. By default it derives them from your assembly. To override:
 69: 
 70: ```csharp
 71: builder.Services
 72:     .AddMcpServer(options =>
 73:     {
 74:         options.ServerInfo = new()
 75:         {
 76:             Name = "my-stdio-server",
 77:             Version = "1.0.0",
 78:             Title = "My STDIO MCP Server"   // optional human-readable name
 79:         };
 80:     })
 81:     .WithStdioServerTransport()
 82:     .WithToolsFromAssembly();
 83: ```
 84: 
 85: ## Reading args/env from the client
 86: 
 87: Clients (e.g. Claude Desktop config) typically launch your server with arguments and environment variables. Read them like any other .NET app:
 88: 
 89: ```csharp
 90: string apiKey = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("MY_API_KEY")
 91:     ?? throw new InvalidOperationException("MY_API_KEY not set");
 92: 
 93: string configPath = args.ElementAtOrDefault(0)
 94:     ?? Path.Combine(Environment.CurrentDirectory, "config.json");
 95: ```
 96: 
 97: Document the expected vars/args in the README so users know what to put in their client config.
 98: 
 99: ## Wiring to Claude Desktop
100: 
101: In `claude_desktop_config.json`:
102: 
103: ```json
104: {
105:   "mcpServers": {
106:     "my-server": {
107:       "command": "dotnet",
108:       "args": ["run", "--project", "C:/path/to/MyStdioServer"],
109:       "env": {
110:         "MY_API_KEY": "..."
111:       }
112:     }
113:   }
114: }
115: ```
116: 
117: For a published self-contained executable, replace `command`/`args` with the executable path. For a NuGet-distributed server using `dnx`:
118: 
119: ```json
120: "command": "dnx",
121: "args": ["MyMcpServer", "--version", "1.2.3"]
122: ```
123: 
124: ## Wiring to VS Code (GitHub Copilot Chat)
125: 
126: In `.vscode/mcp.json`:
127: 
128: ```json
129: {
130:   "servers": {
131:     "my-server": {
132:       "type": "stdio",
133:       "command": "dotnet",
134:       "args": ["run", "--project", "${workspaceFolder}/src/MyMcpServer"]
135:     }
136:   }
137: }
138: ```
139: 
140: ## Local debugging
141: 
142: The cleanest workflow is [MCP Inspector](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/inspector):
143: 
144: ```bash
145: npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector dotnet run --project ./MyStdioServer
146: ```
147: 
148: Inspector launches your server, opens a UI, and lets you call tools / list resources / fire elicitations interactively. See [`testing.md`](./testing.md) for more.
149: 
150: ## Graceful shutdown
151: 
152: `builder.Build().RunAsync()` already handles SIGINT/SIGTERM. If you have background work to flush, use `IHostApplicationLifetime`:
153: 
154: ```csharp
155: var host = builder.Build();
156: var lifetime = host.Services.GetRequiredService<IHostApplicationLifetime>();
157: lifetime.ApplicationStopping.Register(() =>
158: {
159:     // flush, close handles, etc. — keep it fast (<5s) so the client doesn't hang.
160: });
161: await host.RunAsync();
162: ```
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/dotnet-mcp-builder/SKILL.md">
 1: ---
 2: name: dotnet-mcp-builder
 3: description: 'Build Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers in C#/.NET against the current ModelContextProtocol 1.x NuGet packages. Especially helps with cases the model often gets wrong without guidance — stale preview versions (it tends to pick 0.3 or 0.4 preview), MCP Apps (interactive UI rendered in the host), elicitation URL mode, per-session HTTP wiring, OAuth and reverse-proxy deploy specifics, and debugging concrete MapMcp / STDIO / Streamable-HTTP errors. Also covers the routine work — STDIO and Streamable HTTP transports (SSE is deprecated), tools, prompts, resources, sampling, roots, completions, logging — and a basic .NET MCP client. Trigger when the user says or implies any .NET MCP server work: ModelContextProtocol, McpServerTool, MapMcp, WithStdioServerTransport, "MCP server in C#", "MCP tool in dotnet", "expose this as MCP", or names a primitive (prompt/resource/elicitation/MCP App) in a .NET context. Skip for MCP work in other languages.'
 4: ---
 5: 
 6: # Building MCP servers in .NET
 7: 
 8: This skill helps you write production-quality MCP servers and basic clients in C#/.NET against the **official** [`ModelContextProtocol`](https://www.nuget.org/profiles/ModelContextProtocol) NuGet packages, maintained by Microsoft and the MCP project. It targets the **stable 1.x** line and the current spec (2025-11-25).
 9: 
10: ## When this skill earns its keep
11: 
12: The .NET MCP SDK had years of preview packages (`0.x-preview`) before reaching `1.0`. Without help, the model tends to:
13: - Pin a stale preview version that won't compile against current samples.
14: - Miss recent spec features (elicitation URL mode, MCP Apps, structured content blocks).
15: - Get HTTP transport details wrong (stateful/stateless, proxy buffering, OAuth wiring).
16: - Forget the STDIO stdout/stderr trap.
17: 
18: If the task is one of those, *load the matching reference* and follow it. If it's truly trivial (e.g. "rename this tool method"), you don't need to read everything — the cardinal rules below are the minimum.
19: 
20: ## Mental model in 30 seconds
21: 
22: A .NET MCP server is an ordinary `Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting` (or `WebApplication`) app that wires an MCP server through DI:
23: 
24: ```csharp
25: builder.Services
26:     .AddMcpServer()
27:     .WithStdioServerTransport()      // OR .WithHttpTransport(...)
28:     .WithToolsFromAssembly()         // discover [McpServerToolType] classes
29:     .WithPrompts<MyPrompts>()        // optional
30:     .WithResources<MyResources>();   // optional
31: ```
32: 
33: Primitives are plain C# methods on classes marked with attributes (`[McpServerToolType]` + `[McpServerTool]`, `[McpServerPromptType]` + `[McpServerPrompt]`, `[McpServerResourceType]` + `[McpServerResource]`). Parameters bind from JSON-RPC; the SDK builds the JSON Schema from the signature plus `[Description]` attributes.
34: 
35: Server-to-client features (sampling, elicitation, roots, log/progress notifications) are methods on the injected `IMcpServer`.
36: 
37: ## Decision tree → which references to load
38: 
39: Always load `references/packages.md` if you're creating a new project or unsure of the current package version.
40: 
41: | Task | Load |
42: |---|---|
43: | New STDIO server | `references/transport-stdio.md` |
44: | New HTTP (Streamable) server | `references/transport-http.md` |
45: | Add/modify a tool | `references/tool-primitive.md` |
46: | Add/modify a prompt | `references/prompt-primitive.md` |
47: | Add/modify a resource | `references/resource-primitive.md` |
48: | Ask the user a question mid-tool | `references/elicitation.md` |
49: | Call the client's LLM from a tool | `references/sampling.md` |
50: | Read the user's project roots | `references/roots.md` |
51: | Return an interactive UI | `references/mcp-apps.md` |
52: | Argument completions, log/progress notifications, filters, server instructions | `references/server-features.md` |
53: | Write a .NET program that **consumes** an MCP server | `references/client.md` |
54: | MCP Inspector, in-memory tests, mocks, CI | `references/testing.md` |
55: 
56: For multi-primitive tasks, load several at once. For trivial edits in an existing file, you usually don't need any.
57: 
58: ## Cardinal rules (apply always; these prevent the highest-frequency breakages)
59: 
60: 1. **Pin the current stable package, not a preview.** Use `ModelContextProtocol` / `ModelContextProtocol.AspNetCore` / `ModelContextProtocol.Core` at the latest **1.x**. If you find yourself writing `0.3-preview` or `0.4-preview`, stop and check NuGet — preview APIs have breaking differences.
61: 2. **STDIO servers must not write to stdout.** Stdout is the JSON-RPC channel. Configure `LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace` before anything else and never `Console.WriteLine` from a tool.
62: 3. **HTTP defaults to stateful.** For horizontally-scaled deployments without server-initiated traffic, set `options.Stateless = true`. Server-to-client features (sampling, elicitation, roots, unsolicited notifications) require stateful HTTP **or** STDIO — `Stateless = true` will break them at runtime.
63: 4. **SSE-only is deprecated.** Use Streamable HTTP. Only enable legacy SSE (`EnableLegacySse = true`) for an old client you must support, and call it out.
64: 5. **Always `[Description]` tools and parameters.** This is what the LLM sees when picking and shaping calls. Vague descriptions are the #1 reason tools don't get used.
65: 6. **Show the registration line every time you add a primitive.** A new `[McpServerPromptType]` class without `.WithPrompts<...>()` (or `.WithPromptsFromAssembly()`) is invisible.
66: 7. **Don't invent APIs.** If you're unsure a method exists, say so and check the [API reference](https://csharp.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/api/ModelContextProtocol.html) — wrong method names cause silent failures.
67: 
68: ## Working style
69: 
70: - **Make minimal, additive changes.** Add a method to the existing tool class rather than restructuring the project.
71: - **For non-trivial setups, run `dotnet build`.** Catches missing usings, attribute typos, and TFM mismatches before the user sees them.
72: - **Confirm transport + .NET version + primitives before scaffolding** if context doesn't already make them obvious. Default to **.NET 10** for new projects.
73: 
74: ## When the user is stuck
75: 
76: Walk this checklist before guessing:
77: 1. **STDIO:** something is writing to stdout (logger sink, `Console.WriteLine`, library banner).
78: 2. **HTTP 404:** path mismatch — `app.MapMcp()` is root, `app.MapMcp("/mcp")` puts it under `/mcp`.
79: 3. **Tool not appearing:** missing `[McpServerToolType]` on the class, or no `.WithToolsFromAssembly()` / `.WithTools<T>()` registered.
80: 4. **Args not bound:** parameter names must match the JSON-RPC `arguments` keys; complex types bind via `System.Text.Json`.
81: 5. **Sampling/elicitation/roots failing:** transport is stateless HTTP, or the client doesn't advertise the capability.
82: 
83: Still stuck? Point the user at the [`EverythingServer`](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/csharp-sdk/tree/main/samples/EverythingServer) sample — it exercises every feature.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/dotnet-timezone/references/code-patterns.md">
  1: # .NET Timezone Code Patterns
  2: 
  3: ## Pattern 1: Basic TimeZoneInfo
  4: 
  5: Use this only when the application is Windows-only and Windows timezone IDs are acceptable.
  6: 
  7: ```csharp
  8: DateTime utcNow = DateTime.UtcNow;
  9: TimeZoneInfo sriLankaTz = TimeZoneInfo.FindSystemTimeZoneById("Sri Lanka Standard Time");
 10: DateTime localTime = TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTimeFromUtc(utcNow, sriLankaTz);
 11: 
 12: DateTime backToUtc = TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTimeToUtc(localTime, sriLankaTz);
 13: 
 14: TimeZoneInfo tokyoTz = TimeZoneInfo.FindSystemTimeZoneById("Tokyo Standard Time");
 15: DateTime tokyoTime = TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime(localTime, sriLankaTz, tokyoTz);
 16: ```
 17: 
 18: Use `TimeZoneConverter` or `NodaTime` instead for Linux, containers, or mixed environments.
 19: 
 20: ## Pattern 2: Cross-Platform With TimeZoneConverter
 21: 
 22: Recommended default for most .NET apps that run across Windows and Linux.
 23: 
 24: ```xml
 25: <PackageReference Include="TimeZoneConverter" Version="6.*" />
 26: ```
 27: 
 28: ```csharp
 29: using TimeZoneConverter;
 30: 
 31: TimeZoneInfo tz = TZConvert.GetTimeZoneInfo("Asia/Colombo");
 32: DateTime converted = TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTimeFromUtc(DateTime.UtcNow, tz);
 33: ```
 34: 
 35: This also accepts Windows IDs:
 36: 
 37: ```csharp
 38: TimeZoneInfo tz = TZConvert.GetTimeZoneInfo("Sri Lanka Standard Time");
 39: ```
 40: 
 41: ## Pattern 3: NodaTime
 42: 
 43: Use this for strict timezone arithmetic, recurring schedules, or DST edge cases where correctness matters more than minimal dependencies.
 44: 
 45: ```xml
 46: <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" Version="3.*" />
 47: ```
 48: 
 49: ```csharp
 50: using NodaTime;
 51: 
 52: DateTimeZone colomboZone = DateTimeZoneProviders.Tzdb["Asia/Colombo"];
 53: Instant now = SystemClock.Instance.GetCurrentInstant();
 54: ZonedDateTime colomboTime = now.InZone(colomboZone);
 55: 
 56: DateTimeZone tokyoZone = DateTimeZoneProviders.Tzdb["Asia/Tokyo"];
 57: ZonedDateTime tokyoTime = colomboTime.WithZone(tokyoZone);
 58: 
 59: LocalDateTime localDt = new LocalDateTime(2024, 6, 15, 14, 30, 0);
 60: ZonedDateTime zoned = colomboZone.AtStrictly(localDt);
 61: Instant utcInstant = zoned.ToInstant();
 62: ```
 63: 
 64: ## Pattern 4: DateTimeOffset For APIs
 65: 
 66: Prefer `DateTimeOffset` for values crossing service or process boundaries.
 67: 
 68: ```csharp
 69: using TimeZoneConverter;
 70: 
 71: DateTimeOffset utcNow = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;
 72: TimeZoneInfo tz = TZConvert.GetTimeZoneInfo("Asia/Colombo");
 73: DateTimeOffset colomboTime = TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime(utcNow, tz);
 74: ```
 75: 
 76: ## Pattern 5: ASP.NET Core Persistence And Presentation
 77: 
 78: Store UTC, convert at the edges.
 79: 
 80: ```csharp
 81: using TimeZoneConverter;
 82: 
 83: entity.CreatedAtUtc = DateTime.UtcNow;
 84: 
 85: public DateTimeOffset ToUserTime(DateTime utc, string userIanaTimezone)
 86: {
 87:     var tz = TZConvert.GetTimeZoneInfo(userIanaTimezone);
 88:     return TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTimeFromUtc(utc, tz);
 89: }
 90: ```
 91: 
 92: ## Pattern 6: Scheduling And Recurring Jobs
 93: 
 94: Translate a user-facing local time to UTC before scheduling.
 95: 
 96: ```csharp
 97: using TimeZoneConverter;
 98: 
 99: TimeZoneInfo tz = TZConvert.GetTimeZoneInfo("Asia/Colombo");
100: DateTime scheduledLocal = new DateTime(2024, 12, 1, 9, 0, 0, DateTimeKind.Unspecified);
101: DateTime scheduledUtc = TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTimeToUtc(scheduledLocal, tz);
102: ```
103: 
104: With Hangfire:
105: 
106: ```csharp
107: RecurringJob.AddOrUpdate(
108:     "morning-job",
109:     () => DoWork(),
110:     "0 9 * * *",
111:     new RecurringJobOptions { TimeZone = tz });
112: ```
113: 
114: ## Pattern 7: Ambiguous And Invalid DST Times
115: 
116: Check for repeated or skipped local timestamps when the timezone observes daylight saving time.
117: 
118: ```csharp
119: using TimeZoneConverter;
120: 
121: TimeZoneInfo tz = TZConvert.GetTimeZoneInfo("America/New_York");
122: DateTime localTime = new DateTime(2024, 11, 3, 1, 30, 0);
123: 
124: if (tz.IsAmbiguousTime(localTime))
125: {
126:     var offsets = tz.GetAmbiguousTimeOffsets(localTime);
127:     var standardOffset = offsets.Min();
128:     var dto = new DateTimeOffset(localTime, standardOffset);
129: }
130: 
131: if (tz.IsInvalidTime(localTime))
132: {
133:     localTime = localTime.AddHours(1);
134: }
135: ```
136: 
137: ## Common Mistakes
138: 
139: | Wrong | Better |
140: | --- | --- |
141: | `DateTime.Now` in server code | `DateTime.UtcNow` |
142: | Storing local timestamps in the database | Store UTC and convert for display |
143: | Hardcoding offsets such as `+05:30` | Use timezone IDs |
144: | Using `FindSystemTimeZoneById("Asia/Colombo")` on Windows | Use `TZConvert.GetTimeZoneInfo("Asia/Colombo")` |
145: | Comparing local `DateTime` values from different zones | Compare UTC or use `DateTimeOffset` |
146: | Creating `DateTime` without intentional kind semantics | Use `Utc`, `Local`, or deliberate `Unspecified` |
147: 
148: ## Decision Guide
149: 
150: - Use `TimeZoneInfo` only for Windows-only code with Windows IDs.
151: - Use `TimeZoneConverter` for most cross-platform applications.
152: - Use `NodaTime` when DST arithmetic or calendaring accuracy is central.
153: - Use `DateTimeOffset` for APIs and serialized timestamps.
</file>

