 1: # Python Official References and Best Practices
 2: 
 3: Use these official Python resources before finalizing module architecture or implementation details.
 4: 
 5: ## Official References
 6: 
 7: - Python home: <https://www.python.org/>
 8: - Python documentation portal: <https://docs.python.org/3/>
 9: - Python tutorial: <https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/>
10: - Python language reference: <https://docs.python.org/3/reference/>
11: - Python standard library reference: <https://docs.python.org/3/library/>
12: - Python HOWTOs: <https://docs.python.org/3/howto/>
13: - Installing modules: <https://docs.python.org/3/installing/>
14: - Distributing modules: <https://docs.python.org/3/distributing/>
15: - PEP index: <https://peps.python.org/>
16: - PyPA packaging guide: <https://packaging.python.org/>
17: 
18: ## Coding Best Practices
19: 
20: - Target and pin an explicit Python major/minor runtime for each deployment.
21: - Prefer explicit, readable code paths over clever compact logic.
22: - Use type hints for public interfaces and critical data transformations.
23: - Keep module responsibilities focused; separate protocol, business logic, and transport.
24: - Validate and sanitize external inputs at boundaries.
25: - Use structured exceptions with actionable error messages.
26: - Log with enough context for incident triage (correlation id, module id, message id).
27: 
28: ## Reliability and Performance Best Practices
29: 
30: - Avoid blocking operations in high-frequency message paths.
31: - Enforce timeouts and bounded retries with exponential backoff and jitter.
32: - Design idempotent handlers for replay and duplicate deliveries.
33: - Use resource limits and monitor memory growth to prevent edge instability.
34: - Define graceful shutdown behavior to flush buffered state safely.
35: 
36: ## Dependency and Supply Chain Best Practices
37: 
38: - Pin dependencies and document upgrade cadence.
39: - Prefer actively maintained libraries with clear release history.
40: - Track vulnerabilities and update dependencies regularly.
41: - Keep container images minimal and patched.
42: 
43: ## Testing Best Practices
44: 
45: - Unit test parsing, validation, and routing logic.
46: - Add integration tests for module I/O boundaries.
47: - Add chaos tests for network loss, slow upstream, and restart scenarios.
48: - Verify rollback behavior and state recovery in deployment tests.
</file>

