  1: # Release Governance — Branching, Protection, OIDC, and Access Control
  2: 
  3: ## Table of Contents
  4: 1. [Branch Strategy](#1-branch-strategy)
  5: 2. [Branch Protection Rules](#2-branch-protection-rules)
  6: 3. [Tag-Based Release Model](#3-tag-based-release-model)
  7: 4. [Role-Based Access Control](#4-role-based-access-control)
  8: 5. [Secure Publishing with OIDC (Trusted Publishing)](#5-secure-publishing-with-oidc-trusted-publishing)
  9: 6. [Validate Tag Author in CI](#6-validate-tag-author-in-ci)
 10: 7. [Prevent Invalid Release Tags](#7-prevent-invalid-release-tags)
 11: 8. [Full `publish.yml` with Governance Gates](#8-full-publishyml-with-governance-gates)
 12: 
 13: ---
 14: 
 15: ## 1. Branch Strategy
 16: 
 17: Use a clear branch hierarchy to separate development work from releasable code.
 18: 
 19: ```
 20: main          ← stable; only receives PRs from develop or hotfix/*
 21: develop       ← integration branch; all feature PRs merge here first
 22: feature/*     ← new capabilities (e.g., feature/add-redis-backend)
 23: fix/*         ← bug fixes (e.g., fix/memory-leak-on-close)
 24: hotfix/*      ← urgent production fixes; PR directly to main + cherry-pick to develop
 25: release/*     ← (optional) release preparation (e.g., release/v2.0.0)
 26: ```
 27: 
 28: ### Rules
 29: 
 30: | Rule | Why |
 31: |---|---|
 32: | No direct push to `main` | Prevent accidental breakage of the stable branch |
 33: | All changes via PR | Enforces review + CI before merge |
 34: | At least one approval required | Second pair of eyes on all changes |
 35: | CI must pass | Never merge broken code |
 36: | Only tags trigger releases | No ad-hoc publish from branch pushes |
 37: 
 38: ---
 39: 
 40: ## 2. Branch Protection Rules
 41: 
 42: Configure these in **GitHub → Settings → Branches → Add rule** for `main` and `develop`.
 43: 
 44: ### For `main`
 45: 
 46: ```yaml
 47: # Equivalent GitHub branch protection config (for documentation)
 48: branch: main
 49: rules:
 50:   - require_pull_request_reviews:
 51:       required_approving_review_count: 1
 52:       dismiss_stale_reviews: true
 53:   - require_status_checks_to_pass:
 54:       contexts:
 55:         - "Lint, Format & Type Check"
 56:         - "Test (Python 3.11)"   # at minimum; add all matrix versions
 57:       strict: true               # branch must be up-to-date before merge
 58:   - restrict_pushes:
 59:       allowed_actors: []         # nobody — only PR merges
 60:   - require_linear_history: true # prevents merge commits on main
 61: ```
 62: 
 63: ### For `develop`
 64: 
 65: ```yaml
 66: branch: develop
 67: rules:
 68:   - require_pull_request_reviews:
 69:       required_approving_review_count: 1
 70:   - require_status_checks_to_pass:
 71:       contexts: ["CI"]
 72:       strict: false   # less strict for the integration branch
 73: ```
 74: 
 75: ### Via GitHub CLI
 76: 
 77: ```bash
 78: # Protect main (requires gh CLI and admin rights)
 79: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/branches/main/protection \
 80:   --method PUT \
 81:   --input - <<'EOF'
 82: {
 83:   "required_status_checks": {
 84:     "strict": true,
 85:     "contexts": ["Lint, Format & Type Check", "Test (Python 3.11)"]
 86:   },
 87:   "enforce_admins": false,
 88:   "required_pull_request_reviews": {
 89:     "required_approving_review_count": 1,
 90:     "dismiss_stale_reviews": true
 91:   },
 92:   "restrictions": null
 93: }
 94: EOF
 95: ```
 96: 
 97: ---
 98: 
 99: ## 3. Tag-Based Release Model
100: 
101: **Only annotated tags on `main` trigger a release.** Branch pushes and PR merges never publish.
102: 
103: ### Tag Naming Convention
104: 
105: ```
106: vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH           # Stable:         v1.2.3
107: vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCHaN         # Alpha:           v2.0.0a1
108: vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCHbN         # Beta:            v2.0.0b1
109: vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCHrcN        # Release Candidate: v2.0.0rc1
110: ```
111: 
112: ### Release Workflow
113: 
114: ```bash
115: # 1. Merge develop → main via PR (reviewed, CI green)
116: 
117: # 2. Update CHANGELOG.md on main
118: #    Move [Unreleased] entries to [vX.Y.Z] - YYYY-MM-DD
119: 
120: # 3. Commit the changelog
121: git checkout main
122: git pull origin main
123: git add CHANGELOG.md
124: git commit -m "chore: release v1.2.3"
125: 
126: # 4. Create and push an annotated tag
127: git tag -a v1.2.3 -m "Release v1.2.3"
128: git push origin v1.2.3          # ← ONLY the tag; not --tags (avoids pushing all tags)
129: 
130: # 5. Confirm: GitHub Actions publish.yml triggers automatically
131: #    Monitor: Actions tab → publish workflow
132: #    Verify:  https://pypi.org/project/your-package/
133: ```
134: 
135: ### Why annotated tags?
136: 
137: Annotated tags (`git tag -a`) carry a tagger identity, date, and message — lightweight tags do
138: not. `setuptools_scm` works with both, but annotated tags are safer for release governance because
139: they record *who* created the tag.
140: 
141: ---
142: 
143: ## 4. Role-Based Access Control
144: 
145: | Role | What they can do |
146: |---|---|
147: | **Maintainer** | Create release tags, approve PRs, manage branch protection |
148: | **Contributor** | Open PRs to `develop`; cannot push to `main` or create release tags |
149: | **CI (GitHub Actions)** | Publish to PyPI via OIDC; cannot push code or create tags |
150: 
151: ### Implement via GitHub Teams
152: 
153: ```bash
154: # Create a Maintainers team and restrict tag creation to that team
155: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/tags/protection \
156:   --method POST \
157:   --field pattern="v*"
158: # Then set allowed actors to the Maintainers team only
159: ```
160: 
161: ---
162: 
163: ## 5. Secure Publishing with OIDC (Trusted Publishing)
164: 
165: **Never store a PyPI API token as a GitHub secret.** Use Trusted Publishing (OIDC) instead.
166: The PyPI project authorises a specific GitHub repository + workflow + environment — no long-lived
167: secret is exchanged.
168: 
169: ### One-time PyPI Setup
170: 
171: 1. Go to https://pypi.org/manage/project/your-package/settings/publishing/
172: 2. Click **Add a new publisher**
173: 3. Fill in:
174:    - **Owner:** your-github-username
175:    - **Repository:** your-repo-name
176:    - **Workflow name:** `publish.yml`
177:    - **Environment name:** `release` (must match the `environment:` key in the workflow)
178: 4. Save. No token required.
179: 
180: ### GitHub Environment Setup
181: 
182: 1. Go to **GitHub → Settings → Environments → New environment** → name it `release`
183: 2. Add a protection rule: **Required reviewers** (optional but recommended for extra safety)
184: 3. Add a deployment branch rule: **Only tags matching `v*`**
185: 
186: ### Minimal `publish.yml` using OIDC
187: 
188: ```yaml
189: # .github/workflows/publish.yml
190: name: Publish to PyPI
191: 
192: on:
193:   push:
194:     tags:
195:       - "v[0-9]+.[0-9]+.[0-9]+*"   # Matches v1.0.0, v2.0.0a1, v1.2.3rc1
196: 
197: jobs:
198:   publish:
199:     name: Build and publish
200:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
201:     environment: release       # Must match the PyPI Trusted Publisher environment name
202:     permissions:
203:       id-token: write          # Required for OIDC — grants a short-lived token to PyPI
204:       contents: read
205: 
206:     steps:
207:       - uses: actions/checkout@v4
208:         with:
209:           fetch-depth: 0       # REQUIRED for setuptools_scm
210: 
211:       - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
212:         with:
213:           python-version: "3.11"
214: 
215:       - name: Install build
216:         run: pip install build
217: 
218:       - name: Build distributions
219:         run: python -m build
220: 
221:       - name: Validate distributions
222:         run: pip install twine ; twine check dist/*
223: 
224:       - name: Publish to PyPI
225:         uses: pypa/gh-action-pypi-publish@release/v1
226:         # No `password:` or `user:` needed — OIDC handles authentication
227: ```
228: 
229: ---
230: 
231: ## 6. Validate Tag Author in CI
232: 
233: Restrict who can trigger a release by checking `GITHUB_ACTOR` against an allowlist.
234: Add this as the **first step** in your publish job to fail fast.
235: 
236: ```yaml
237: - name: Validate tag author
238:   run: |
239:     ALLOWED_USERS=("your-github-username" "co-maintainer-username")
240:     if [[ ! " ${ALLOWED_USERS[*]} " =~ " ${GITHUB_ACTOR} " ]]; then
241:       echo "::error::Release blocked: ${GITHUB_ACTOR} is not an authorised releaser."
242:       exit 1
243:     fi
244:     echo "Release authorised for ${GITHUB_ACTOR}."
245: ```
246: 
247: ### Notes
248: 
249: - `GITHUB_ACTOR` is the GitHub username of the person who pushed the tag.
250: - Store the allowlist in a separate file (e.g., `.github/MAINTAINERS`) for maintainability.
251: - For teams: replace the username check with a GitHub API call to verify team membership.
252: 
253: ---
254: 
255: ## 7. Prevent Invalid Release Tags
256: 
257: Reject workflow runs triggered by tags that do not follow your versioning convention.
258: This stops accidental publishes from tags like `test`, `backup-old`, or `v1`.
259: 
260: ```yaml
261: - name: Validate release tag format
262:   run: |
263:     # Accepts: v1.0.0  v1.0.0a1  v1.0.0b2  v1.0.0rc1  v1.0.0.post1
264:     if [[ ! "${GITHUB_REF}" =~ ^refs/tags/v[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+(a|b|rc|\.post)[0-9]*$ ]] && \
265:        [[ ! "${GITHUB_REF}" =~ ^refs/tags/v[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$ ]]; then
266:       echo "::error::Tag '${GITHUB_REF}' does not match the required format v<MAJOR>.<MINOR>.<PATCH>[pre]."
267:       exit 1
268:     fi
269:     echo "Tag format valid: ${GITHUB_REF}"
270: ```
271: 
272: ### Regex explained
273: 
274: | Pattern | Matches |
275: |---|---|
276: | `v[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+` | `v1.0.0`, `v12.3.4` |
277: | `(a\|b\|rc)[0-9]*` | `v1.0.0a1`, `v2.0.0rc2` |
278: | `\.post[0-9]*` | `v1.0.0.post1` |
279: 
280: ---
281: 
282: ## 8. Full `publish.yml` with Governance Gates
283: 
284: Complete workflow combining tag validation, author check, TestPyPI gate, and production publish.
285: 
286: ```yaml
287: # .github/workflows/publish.yml
288: name: Publish to PyPI
289: 
290: on:
291:   push:
292:     tags:
293:       - "v[0-9]+.[0-9]+.[0-9]+*"
294: 
295: jobs:
296:   publish:
297:     name: Build, validate, and publish
298:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
299:     environment: release
300:     permissions:
301:       id-token: write
302:       contents: read
303: 
304:     steps:
305:       - name: Validate release tag format
306:         run: |
307:           if [[ ! "${GITHUB_REF}" =~ ^refs/tags/v[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+(a[0-9]*|b[0-9]*|rc[0-9]*|\.post[0-9]*)?$ ]]; then
308:             echo "::error::Invalid tag format: ${GITHUB_REF}"
309:             exit 1
310:           fi
311: 
312:       - name: Validate tag author
313:         run: |
314:           ALLOWED_USERS=("your-github-username")
315:           if [[ ! " ${ALLOWED_USERS[*]} " =~ " ${GITHUB_ACTOR} " ]]; then
316:             echo "::error::${GITHUB_ACTOR} is not authorised to release."
317:             exit 1
318:           fi
319: 
320:       - uses: actions/checkout@v4
321:         with:
322:           fetch-depth: 0
323: 
324:       - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
325:         with:
326:           python-version: "3.11"
327: 
328:       - name: Install build tooling
329:         run: pip install build twine
330: 
331:       - name: Build
332:         run: python -m build
333: 
334:       - name: Validate distributions
335:         run: twine check dist/*
336: 
337:       - name: Publish to TestPyPI
338:         uses: pypa/gh-action-pypi-publish@release/v1
339:         with:
340:           repository-url: https://test.pypi.org/legacy/
341:         continue-on-error: true   # Non-fatal; remove if you always want this to pass
342: 
343:       - name: Publish to PyPI
344:         uses: pypa/gh-action-pypi-publish@release/v1
345: ```
346: 
347: ### Security checklist
348: 
349: - [ ] PyPI Trusted Publishing configured (no API token stored in GitHub)
350: - [ ] GitHub `release` environment has branch protection: tags matching `v*` only
351: - [ ] Tag format validation step is the first step in the job
352: - [ ] Allowed-users list is maintained and reviewed regularly
353: - [ ] No secrets printed in logs (check all `echo` and `run` steps)
354: - [ ] `permissions:` is scoped to `id-token: write` only — no `write-all`
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/python-pypi-package-builder/references/testing-quality.md">
  1: # Testing and Code Quality
  2: 
  3: ## Table of Contents
  4: 1. [conftest.py](#1-conftestpy)
  5: 2. [Unit tests](#2-unit-tests)
  6: 3. [Backend unit tests](#3-backend-unit-tests)
  7: 4. [Running tests](#4-running-tests)
  8: 5. [Code quality tools](#5-code-quality-tools)
  9: 6. [Pre-commit hooks](#6-pre-commit-hooks)
 10: 
 11: ---
 12: 
 13: ## 1. `conftest.py`
 14: 
 15: Use `conftest.py` to define shared fixtures. Keep fixtures focused — one fixture per concern.
 16: For async tests, use `pytest-asyncio` with `asyncio_mode = "auto"` in `pyproject.toml`.
 17: 
 18: ```python
 19: # tests/conftest.py
 20: import pytest
 21: from your_package.core import YourClient
 22: from your_package.backends.memory import MemoryBackend
 23: 
 24: 
 25: @pytest.fixture
 26: def memory_backend() -> MemoryBackend:
 27:     return MemoryBackend()
 28: 
 29: 
 30: @pytest.fixture
 31: def client(memory_backend: MemoryBackend) -> YourClient:
 32:     return YourClient(
 33:         api_key="test-key",
 34:         backend=memory_backend,
 35:     )
 36: ```
 37: 
 38: ---
 39: 
 40: ## 2. Unit Tests
 41: 
 42: Test both the happy path and the edge cases (e.g. invalid inputs, error conditions).
 43: 
 44: ```python
 45: # tests/test_core.py
 46: import pytest
 47: from your_package import YourClient
 48: from your_package.exceptions import YourPackageError
 49: 
 50: 
 51: def test_client_creates_with_valid_key():
 52:     client = YourClient(api_key="sk-test")
 53:     assert client is not None
 54: 
 55: 
 56: def test_client_raises_on_empty_key():
 57:     with pytest.raises(ValueError, match="api_key"):
 58:         YourClient(api_key="")
 59: 
 60: 
 61: def test_client_raises_on_invalid_timeout():
 62:     with pytest.raises(ValueError, match="timeout"):
 63:         YourClient(api_key="sk-test", timeout=-1)
 64: 
 65: 
 66: @pytest.mark.asyncio
 67: async def test_process_returns_expected_result(client: YourClient):
 68:     result = await client.process({"input": "value"})
 69:     assert "output" in result
 70: 
 71: 
 72: @pytest.mark.asyncio
 73: async def test_process_raises_on_invalid_input(client: YourClient):
 74:     with pytest.raises(YourPackageError):
 75:         await client.process({})  # empty input should fail
 76: ```
 77: 
 78: ---
 79: 
 80: ## 3. Backend Unit Tests
 81: 
 82: Test each backend independently, in isolation from the rest of the library. This makes failures
 83: easier to diagnose and ensures your abstract interface is actually implemented correctly.
 84: 
 85: ```python
 86: # tests/test_backends.py
 87: import pytest
 88: from your_package.backends.memory import MemoryBackend
 89: 
 90: 
 91: @pytest.mark.asyncio
 92: async def test_set_and_get():
 93:     backend = MemoryBackend()
 94:     await backend.set("key1", "value1")
 95:     result = await backend.get("key1")
 96:     assert result == "value1"
 97: 
 98: 
 99: @pytest.mark.asyncio
100: async def test_get_missing_key_returns_none():
101:     backend = MemoryBackend()
102:     result = await backend.get("nonexistent")
103:     assert result is None
104: 
105: 
106: @pytest.mark.asyncio
107: async def test_delete_removes_key():
108:     backend = MemoryBackend()
109:     await backend.set("key1", "value1")
110:     await backend.delete("key1")
111:     result = await backend.get("key1")
112:     assert result is None
113: 
114: 
115: @pytest.mark.asyncio
116: async def test_ttl_expires_entry():
117:     import asyncio
118:     backend = MemoryBackend()
119:     await backend.set("key1", "value1", ttl=1)
120:     await asyncio.sleep(1.1)
121:     result = await backend.get("key1")
122:     assert result is None
123: 
124: 
125: @pytest.mark.asyncio
126: async def test_different_keys_are_independent():
127:     backend = MemoryBackend()
128:     await backend.set("key1", "a")
129:     await backend.set("key2", "b")
130:     assert await backend.get("key1") == "a"
131:     assert await backend.get("key2") == "b"
132:     await backend.delete("key1")
133:     assert await backend.get("key2") == "b"
134: ```
135: 
136: ---
137: 
138: ## 4. Running Tests
139: 
140: ```bash
141: pip install -e ".[dev]"
142: pytest                           # All tests
143: pytest --cov --cov-report=html   # With HTML coverage report (opens in browser)
144: pytest -k "test_middleware"      # Filter by name
145: pytest -x                        # Stop on first failure
146: pytest -v                        # Verbose output
147: ```
148: 
149: Coverage config in `pyproject.toml` enforces a minimum threshold (`fail_under = 80`). CI will
150: fail if you drop below it, which catches coverage regressions automatically.
151: 
152: ---
153: 
154: ## 5. Code Quality Tools
155: 
156: ### Ruff (linting — replaces flake8, pylint, many others)
157: 
158: ```bash
159: pip install ruff
160: ruff check .           # Check for issues
161: ruff check . --fix     # Auto-fix safe issues
162: ```
163: 
164: Ruff is extremely fast and replaces most of the Python linting ecosystem. Configure it in
165: `pyproject.toml` — see `references/pyproject-toml.md` for the full config.
166: 
167: ### Black (formatting)
168: 
169: ```bash
170: pip install black
171: black .                # Format all files
172: black . --check        # CI mode — reports issues without modifying files
173: ```
174: 
175: ### isort (import sorting)
176: 
177: ```bash
178: pip install isort
179: isort .                # Sort imports
180: isort . --check-only   # CI mode
181: ```
182: 
183: Always set `profile = "black"` in `[tool.isort]` — otherwise black and isort conflict.
184: 
185: ### mypy (static type checking)
186: 
187: ```bash
188: pip install mypy
189: mypy your_package/   # Type-check your package source only
190: ```
191: 
192: Common fixes:
193: 
194: - `ignore_missing_imports = true` — ignore untyped third-party deps
195: - `from __future__ import annotations` — enables PEP 563 deferred evaluation (Python 3.9 compat)
196: - `pip install types-redis` — type stubs for the redis library
197: 
198: ### Run all at once
199: 
200: ```bash
201: ruff check . && black . --check && isort . --check-only && mypy your_package/
202: ```
203: 
204: ---
205: 
206: ## 6. Pre-commit Hooks
207: 
208: Pre-commit runs all quality tools automatically before each commit, so issues never reach CI.
209: Install once per clone with `pre-commit install`.
210: 
211: ```yaml
212: # .pre-commit-config.yaml
213: repos:
214:   - repo: https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff-pre-commit
215:     rev: v0.4.4
216:     hooks:
217:       - id: ruff
218:         args: [--fix]
219:       - id: ruff-format
220: 
221:   - repo: https://github.com/psf/black
222:     rev: 24.4.2
223:     hooks:
224:       - id: black
225: 
226:   - repo: https://github.com/pycqa/isort
227:     rev: 5.13.2
228:     hooks:
229:       - id: isort
230: 
231:   - repo: https://github.com/pre-commit/mirrors-mypy
232:     rev: v1.10.0
233:     hooks:
234:       - id: mypy
235:         additional_dependencies: [types-redis]  # Add stubs for typed dependencies
236: 
237:   - repo: https://github.com/pre-commit/pre-commit-hooks
238:     rev: v4.6.0
239:     hooks:
240:       - id: trailing-whitespace
241:       - id: end-of-file-fixer
242:       - id: check-yaml
243:       - id: check-toml
244:       - id: check-merge-conflict
245:       - id: debug-statements
246:       - id: no-commit-to-branch
247:         args: [--branch, master, --branch, main]
248: ```
249: 
250: ```bash
251: pip install pre-commit
252: pre-commit install           # Install once per clone
253: pre-commit run --all-files   # Run all hooks manually (useful before the first install)
254: ```
255: 
256: The `no-commit-to-branch` hook prevents accidentally committing directly to `main`/`master`,
257: which would bypass CI checks. Always work on a feature branch.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-backend/skills/python-pypi-package-builder/references/tooling-ruff.md">
  1: # Tooling — Ruff-Only Setup and Code Quality
  2: 
  3: ## Table of Contents
  4: 1. [Use Only Ruff (Replaces black, isort, flake8)](#1-use-only-ruff-replaces-black-isort-flake8)
  5: 2. [Ruff Configuration in pyproject.toml](#2-ruff-configuration-in-pyprojecttoml)
  6: 3. [mypy Configuration](#3-mypy-configuration)
  7: 4. [pre-commit Configuration](#4-pre-commit-configuration)
  8: 5. [pytest and Coverage Configuration](#5-pytest-and-coverage-configuration)
  9: 6. [Dev Dependencies in pyproject.toml](#6-dev-dependencies-in-pyprojecttoml)
 10: 7. [CI Lint Job — Ruff Only](#7-ci-lint-job--ruff-only)
 11: 8. [Migration Guide — Removing black and isort](#8-migration-guide--removing-black-and-isort)
 12: 
 13: ---
 14: 
 15: ## 1. Use Only Ruff (Replaces black, isort, flake8)
 16: 
 17: **Decision:** Use `ruff` as the single linting and formatting tool. Remove `black` and `isort`.
 18: 
 19: | Old (avoid) | New (use) | What it does |
 20: |---|---|---|
 21: | `black` | `ruff format` | Code formatting |
 22: | `isort` | `ruff check --select I` | Import sorting |
 23: | `flake8` | `ruff check` | Style and error linting |
 24: | `pyupgrade` | `ruff check --select UP` | Upgrade syntax to modern Python |
 25: | `bandit` | `ruff check --select S` | Security linting |
 26: | All of the above | `ruff` | One tool, one config section |
 27: 
 28: **Why ruff?**
 29: - 10–100× faster than the tools it replaces (written in Rust).
 30: - Single config section in `pyproject.toml` — no `.flake8`, `.isort.cfg`, `pyproject.toml[tool.black]` sprawl.
 31: - Actively maintained by Astral; follows the same rules as the tools it replaces.
 32: - `ruff format` is black-compatible — existing black-formatted code passes without changes.
 33: 
 34: ---
 35: 
 36: ## 2. Ruff Configuration in pyproject.toml
 37: 
 38: ```toml
 39: [tool.ruff]
 40: target-version = "py310"        # Minimum supported Python version
 41: line-length    = 88             # black-compatible default
 42: src            = ["src", "tests"]
 43: 
 44: [tool.ruff.lint]
 45: select = [
 46:     "E",   # pycodestyle errors
 47:     "W",   # pycodestyle warnings
 48:     "F",   # pyflakes
 49:     "I",   # isort
 50:     "B",   # flake8-bugbear (opinionated but very useful)
 51:     "C4",  # flake8-comprehensions
 52:     "UP",  # pyupgrade (modernise syntax)
 53:     "SIM", # flake8-simplify
 54:     "TCH", # flake8-type-checking (move imports to TYPE_CHECKING block)
 55:     "ANN", # flake8-annotations (enforce type hints — remove if too strict)
 56:     "S",   # flake8-bandit (security)
 57:     "N",   # pep8-naming
 58: ]
 59: ignore = [
 60:     "ANN101",  # Missing type annotation for `self`
 61:     "ANN102",  # Missing type annotation for `cls`
 62:     "S101",    # Use of `assert` — necessary in tests
 63:     "S603",    # subprocess without shell=True — often intentional
 64:     "B008",    # Do not perform function calls in default arguments (false positives in FastAPI/Typer)
 65: ]
 66: 
 67: [tool.ruff.lint.isort]
 68: known-first-party = ["your_package"]
 69: 
 70: [tool.ruff.lint.per-file-ignores]
 71: "tests/**" = ["S101", "ANN", "D"]   # Allow assert and skip annotations/docstrings in tests
 72: 
 73: [tool.ruff.format]
 74: quote-style              = "double"   # black-compatible
 75: indent-style             = "space"
 76: skip-magic-trailing-comma = false
 77: line-ending              = "auto"
 78: ```
 79: 
 80: ### Useful ruff commands
 81: 
 82: ```bash
 83: # Check for lint issues (no changes)
 84: ruff check .
 85: 
 86: # Auto-fix fixable issues
 87: ruff check --fix .
 88: 
 89: # Format code (replaces black)
 90: ruff format .
 91: 
 92: # Check formatting without changing files (CI mode)
 93: ruff format --check .
 94: 
 95: # Run both lint and format check in one command (for CI)
 96: ruff check . && ruff format --check .
 97: ```
 98: 
 99: ---
100: 
101: ## 3. mypy Configuration
102: 
103: ```toml
104: [tool.mypy]
105: python_version          = "3.10"
106: strict                  = true
107: warn_return_any         = true
108: warn_unused_ignores     = true
109: warn_redundant_casts    = true
110: disallow_untyped_defs   = true
111: disallow_incomplete_defs = true
112: check_untyped_defs      = true
113: no_implicit_optional    = true
114: show_error_codes        = true
115: 
116: # Ignore missing stubs for third-party packages that don't ship types
117: [[tool.mypy.overrides]]
118: module = ["redis.*", "pydantic_settings.*"]
119: ignore_missing_imports = true
120: ```
121: 
122: ### Running mypy — handle both src and flat layouts
123: 
124: ```bash
125: # src layout:
126: mypy src/your_package/
127: 
128: # flat layout:
129: mypy your_package/
130: ```
131: 
132: In CI, detect layout dynamically:
133: 
134: ```yaml
135: - name: Run mypy
136:   run: |
137:     if [ -d "src" ]; then
138:         mypy src/
139:     else
140:         mypy your_package/
141:     fi
142: ```
143: 
144: ---
145: 
146: ## 4. pre-commit Configuration
147: 
148: ```yaml
149: # .pre-commit-config.yaml
150: repos:
151:   - repo: https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff-pre-commit
152:     rev: v0.4.4    # Pin to a specific release; update periodically with `pre-commit autoupdate`
153:     hooks:
154:       - id: ruff
155:         args: [--fix]       # Auto-fix what can be fixed
156:       - id: ruff-format     # Format (replaces black hook)
157: 
158:   - repo: https://github.com/pre-commit/mirrors-mypy
159:     rev: v1.10.0
160:     hooks:
161:       - id: mypy
162:         additional_dependencies:
163:           - types-requests
164:           - types-redis
165:           # Add stubs for any typed dependency used in your package
166: 
167:   - repo: https://github.com/pre-commit/pre-commit-hooks
168:     rev: v4.6.0
169:     hooks:
170:       - id: trailing-whitespace
171:       - id: end-of-file-fixer
172:       - id: check-toml
173:       - id: check-yaml
174:       - id: check-merge-conflict
175:       - id: check-added-large-files
176:         args: ["--maxkb=500"]
177: ```
178: 
179: ### ❌ Remove these hooks (replaced by ruff)
180: 
181: ```yaml
182: # DELETE or never add:
183: - repo: https://github.com/psf/black           # replaced by ruff-format
184: - repo: https://github.com/PyCQA/isort          # replaced by ruff lint I rules
185: - repo: https://github.com/PyCQA/flake8         # replaced by ruff check
186: - repo: https://github.com/PyCQA/autoflake      # replaced by ruff check F401
187: ```
188: 
189: ### Setup
190: 
191: ```bash
192: pip install pre-commit
193: pre-commit install     # Installs git hook — runs on every commit
194: pre-commit run --all-files  # Run manually on all files
195: pre-commit autoupdate  # Update all hooks to latest pinned versions
196: ```
197: 
198: ---
199: 
200: ## 5. pytest and Coverage Configuration
201: 
202: ```toml
203: [tool.pytest.ini_options]
204: testpaths    = ["tests"]
205: addopts      = "-ra -q --strict-markers --cov=your_package --cov-report=term-missing"
206: asyncio_mode = "auto"    # Enables async tests without @pytest.mark.asyncio decorator
207: 
208: [tool.coverage.run]
209: source   = ["your_package"]
210: branch   = true
211: omit     = ["**/__main__.py", "**/cli.py"]  # omit entry points from coverage
212: 
213: [tool.coverage.report]
214: show_missing   = true
215: skip_covered   = false
216: fail_under     = 85        # Fail CI if coverage drops below 85%
217: exclude_lines  = [
218:     "pragma: no cover",
219:     "if TYPE_CHECKING:",
220:     "raise NotImplementedError",
221:     "@abstractmethod",
222: ]
223: ```
224: 
225: ### asyncio_mode = "auto" — remove @pytest.mark.asyncio
226: 
227: With `asyncio_mode = "auto"` set in `pyproject.toml`, **do not** add `@pytest.mark.asyncio`
228: to test functions. The decorator is redundant and will raise a warning in modern pytest-asyncio.
229: 
230: ```python
231: # WRONG — the decorator is deprecated when asyncio_mode = "auto":
232: @pytest.mark.asyncio
233: async def test_async_operation():
234:     result = await my_async_func()
235:     assert result == expected
236: 
237: # CORRECT — just use async def:
238: async def test_async_operation():
239:     result = await my_async_func()
240:     assert result == expected
241: ```
242: 
243: ---
244: 
245: ## 6. Dev Dependencies in pyproject.toml
246: 
247: Declare all dev/test tools in an `[extras]` group named `dev`.
248: 
249: ```toml
250: [project.optional-dependencies]
251: dev = [
252:     "pytest>=8",
253:     "pytest-asyncio>=0.23",
254:     "pytest-cov>=5",
255:     "ruff>=0.4",
256:     "mypy>=1.10",
257:     "pre-commit>=3.7",
258:     "httpx>=0.27",       # If testing HTTP transport
259:     "respx>=0.21",       # If mocking httpx in tests
260: ]
261: redis = [
262:     "redis>=5",
263: ]
264: docs = [
265:     "mkdocs-material>=9",
266:     "mkdocstrings[python]>=0.25",
267: ]
268: ```
269: 
270: Install dev dependencies:
271: 
272: ```bash
273: pip install -e ".[dev]"
274: pip install -e ".[dev,redis]"   # Include optional extras
275: ```
276: 
277: ---
278: 
279: ## 7. CI Lint Job — Ruff Only
280: 
281: Replace the separate `black`, `isort`, and `flake8` steps with a single `ruff` step.
282: 
283: ```yaml
284: # .github/workflows/ci.yml  — lint job
285: lint:
286:   name: Lint & Type Check
287:   runs-on: ubuntu-latest
288:   steps:
289:     - uses: actions/checkout@v4
290: 
291:     - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
292:       with:
293:         python-version: "3.11"
294: 
295:     - name: Install dev dependencies
296:       run: pip install -e ".[dev]"
297: 
298:     # Single step: ruff replaces black + isort + flake8
299:     - name: ruff lint
300:       run: ruff check .
301: 
302:     - name: ruff format check
303:       run: ruff format --check .
304: 
305:     - name: mypy
306:       run: |
307:         if [ -d "src" ]; then
308:             mypy src/
309:         else
310:             mypy $(basename $(ls -d */))/ 2>/dev/null || mypy .
311:         fi
312: ```
313: 
314: ---
315: 
316: ## 8. Migration Guide — Removing black and isort
317: 
318: If you are converting an existing project that used `black` and `isort`:
319: 
320: ```bash
321: # 1. Remove black and isort from dev dependencies
322: pip uninstall black isort
323: 
324: # 2. Remove black and isort config sections from pyproject.toml
325: # [tool.black]  ← delete this section
326: # [tool.isort]  ← delete this section
327: 
328: # 3. Add ruff to dev dependencies (see Section 2 for config)
329: 
330: # 4. Run ruff format to confirm existing code is already compatible
331: ruff format --check .
332: # ruff format is black-compatible; output should be identical
333: 
334: # 5. Update .pre-commit-config.yaml (see Section 4)
335: # Remove black and isort hooks; add ruff and ruff-format hooks
336: 
337: # 6. Update CI (see Section 7)
338: # Remove black, isort, flake8 steps; add ruff check + ruff format --check
339: 
340: # 7. Reinstall pre-commit hooks
341: pre-commit uninstall
342: pre-commit install
343: pre-commit run --all-files   # Verify clean
344: ```
</file>

