<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/aws-cdk-python-setup/SKILL.md">
  1: ---
  2: name: aws-cdk-python-setup
  3: description: Setup and initialization guide for developing AWS CDK (Cloud Development Kit) applications in Python. This skill enables users to configure environment prerequisites, create new CDK projects, manage dependencies, and deploy to AWS.
  4: ---
  5: # AWS CDK Python Setup Instructions
  6: 
  7: This skill provides setup guidance for working with **AWS CDK (Cloud Development Kit)** projects using **Python**.
  8: 
  9: ---
 10: 
 11: ## Prerequisites
 12: 
 13: Before starting, ensure the following tools are installed:
 14: 
 15: - **Node.js** ≥ 14.15.0 — Required for the AWS CDK CLI
 16: - **Python** ≥ 3.7 — Used for writing CDK code
 17: - **AWS CLI** — Manages credentials and resources
 18: - **Git** — Version control and project management
 19: 
 20: ---
 21: 
 22: ## Installation Steps
 23: 
 24: ### 1. Install AWS CDK CLI
 25: ```bash
 26: npm install -g aws-cdk
 27: cdk --version
 28: ```
 29: 
 30: ### 2. Configure AWS Credentials
 31: ```bash
 32: # Install AWS CLI (if not installed)
 33: brew install awscli
 34: 
 35: # Configure credentials
 36: aws configure
 37: ```
 38: Enter your AWS Access Key, Secret Access Key, default region, and output format when prompted.
 39: 
 40: ### 3. Create a New CDK Project
 41: ```bash
 42: mkdir my-cdk-project
 43: cd my-cdk-project
 44: cdk init app --language python
 45: ```
 46: 
 47: Your project will include:
 48: - `app.py` — Main application entry point
 49: - `my_cdk_project/` — CDK stack definitions
 50: - `requirements.txt` — Python dependencies
 51: - `cdk.json` — Configuration file
 52: 
 53: ### 4. Set Up Python Virtual Environment
 54: ```bash
 55: # macOS/Linux
 56: source .venv/bin/activate
 57: 
 58: # Windows
 59: .venv\Scripts\activate
 60: ```
 61: 
 62: ### 5. Install Python Dependencies
 63: ```bash
 64: pip install -r requirements.txt
 65: ```
 66: Primary dependencies:
 67: - `aws-cdk-lib` — Core CDK constructs
 68: - `constructs` — Base construct library
 69: 
 70: ---
 71: 
 72: ## Development Workflow
 73: 
 74: ### Synthesize CloudFormation Templates
 75: ```bash
 76: cdk synth
 77: ```
 78: Generates `cdk.out/` containing CloudFormation templates.
 79: 
 80: ### Deploy Stacks to AWS
 81: ```bash
 82: cdk deploy
 83: ```
 84: Reviews and confirms deployment to the configured AWS account.
 85: 
 86: ### Bootstrap (First Deployment Only)
 87: ```bash
 88: cdk bootstrap
 89: ```
 90: Prepares environment resources like S3 buckets for asset storage.
 91: 
 92: ---
 93: 
 94: ## Best Practices
 95: 
 96: - Always activate the virtual environment before working.
 97: - Run `cdk diff` before deployment to preview changes.
 98: - Use development accounts for testing.
 99: - Follow Pythonic naming and directory conventions.
100: - Keep `requirements.txt` pinned for consistent builds.
101: 
102: ---
103: 
104: ## Troubleshooting Tips
105: 
106: If issues occur, check:
107: 
108: - AWS credentials are correctly configured.
109: - Default region is set properly.
110: - Node.js and Python versions meet minimum requirements.
111: - Run `cdk doctor` to diagnose environment issues.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/aws-cloudwatch-investigation/SKILL.md">
  1: ---
  2: name: aws-cloudwatch-investigation
  3: description: >
  4:   Reusable investigation patterns for AWS CloudWatch: Logs Insights query templates,
  5:   alarm-to-deployment correlation, blast-radius narrowing decision tree, and
  6:   PromQL-style metric query patterns for structured incident triage.
  7: ---
  8: 
  9: # AWS CloudWatch Investigation Skill
 10: 
 11: Reusable patterns for investigating production incidents using CloudWatch Logs, Metrics, and Alarms. These patterns are designed to be composed together during incident triage.
 12: 
 13: ---
 14: 
 15: ## Pattern 1: Logs Insights Query Templates
 16: 
 17: ### Error Spike Detection
 18: 
 19: Find the top errors in a time window, grouped by error type:
 20: 
 21: ```
 22: fields @timestamp, @message, @logStream
 23: | filter @message like /(?i)(error|exception|fatal|critical)/
 24: | stats count(*) as errorCount by bin(5m), @logStream
 25: | sort errorCount desc
 26: | limit 20
 27: ```
 28: 
 29: ### P99 Latency Breakdown by Operation
 30: 
 31: Identify which operations are driving latency spikes:
 32: 
 33: ```
 34: fields @timestamp, @duration, operation
 35: | filter ispresent(@duration)
 36: | stats avg(@duration) as avgMs,
 37:         pct(@duration, 50) as p50Ms,
 38:         pct(@duration, 95) as p95Ms,
 39:         pct(@duration, 99) as p99Ms,
 40:         count(*) as invocations
 41:   by operation
 42: | sort p99Ms desc
 43: | limit 15
 44: ```
 45: 
 46: ### Lambda Cold Start Detection
 47: 
 48: Quantify cold start impact during an incident:
 49: 
 50: ```
 51: fields @timestamp, @duration, @initDuration, @memorySize, @maxMemoryUsed
 52: | filter ispresent(@initDuration)
 53: | stats count(*) as coldStarts,
 54:         avg(@initDuration) as avgInitMs,
 55:         max(@initDuration) as maxInitMs,
 56:         avg(@duration) as avgDurationMs
 57:   by bin(5m)
 58: | sort @timestamp desc
 59: ```
 60: 
 61: ### Out-of-Memory (OOM) Detection
 62: 
 63: Find Lambda functions or containers killed by memory pressure:
 64: 
 65: ```
 66: fields @timestamp, @message, @logStream, @memorySize, @maxMemoryUsed
 67: | filter @message like /Runtime exited|out of memory|OOMKilled|Cannot allocate memory|MemoryError/
 68: | stats count(*) as oomEvents by @logStream, bin(10m)
 69: | sort oomEvents desc
 70: | limit 10
 71: ```
 72: 
 73: For memory utilization trending before OOM:
 74: 
 75: ```
 76: fields @timestamp, @maxMemoryUsed, @memorySize
 77: | filter ispresent(@maxMemoryUsed)
 78: | stats max(@maxMemoryUsed / @memorySize * 100) as peakMemPct,
 79:         avg(@maxMemoryUsed / @memorySize * 100) as avgMemPct
 80:   by bin(5m)
 81: | sort @timestamp desc
 82: ```
 83: 
 84: ### Timeout Detection
 85: 
 86: Find invocations that hit the configured timeout:
 87: 
 88: ```
 89: fields @timestamp, @duration, @logStream, @requestId
 90: | filter @message like /Task timed out/ or @duration > 28000
 91: | stats count(*) as timeouts by @logStream, bin(5m)
 92: | sort timeouts desc
 93: ```
 94: 
 95: ---
 96: 
 97: ## Pattern 2: Alarm History to Deploy-Event Correlation
 98: 
 99: ### Process
100: 
101: 1. **Get alarm transition time** — note the exact timestamp when the alarm entered ALARM state.
102: 2. **Query CloudTrail** for deployment-related events in a window of [alarm_time - 30min, alarm_time]:
103: 
104: ```
105: # CloudTrail Lake query for deployment events
106: SELECT eventTime, eventName, userIdentity.arn, requestParameters
107: FROM <event-data-store-id>
108: WHERE eventTime > '<alarm_time_minus_30m>'
109:   AND eventTime < '<alarm_time>'
110:   AND eventName IN (
111:     'UpdateFunctionCode', 'UpdateFunctionConfiguration',
112:     'UpdateService', 'CreateDeployment', 'RegisterTaskDefinition',
113:     'CreateChangeSet', 'ExecuteChangeSet',
114:     'StartPipelineExecution', 'PutImage'
115:   )
116: ORDER BY eventTime DESC
117: ```
118: 
119: 3. **Correlation criteria** — a deploy is "correlated" if:
120:    - It targets the same service/resource as the alarm
121:    - It completed within 15 minutes before the alarm transition
122:    - The deployer identity matches a CI/CD role (not a human applying a hotfix)
123: 
124: 4. **Strengthening the correlation:**
125:    - Check if the same alarm was healthy in the previous deployment cycle
126:    - Verify no other environmental changes (scaling events, config changes) in the same window
127:    - Look for canary/synthetic monitor failures that started at the same time
128: 
129: ### Output Format
130: 
131: ```
132: Deploy Correlation:
133:   Event: UpdateFunctionCode
134:   Time: 2024-03-15T14:23:07Z (12 min before alarm)
135:   Actor: arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/github-actions-deploy/session
136:   Resource: arn:aws:lambda:us-east-1:123456789012:function:payment-processor
137:   Correlation: STRONG — same resource, CI/CD actor, alarm was OK prior cycle
138: ```
139: 
140: ---
141: 
142: ## Pattern 3: Narrow the Blast Radius Decision Tree
143: 
144: Use this tree to systematically scope an incident from broadest to most specific:
145: 
146: ```
147: START
148:   |
149:   v
150: [1] ACCOUNT — Which account(s) show the alarm?
151:   |  - Check: Are alarms firing in multiple accounts?
152:   |  - If yes → suspect shared service (SSO, networking, shared deployment pipeline)
153:   |  - If no → proceed to Region
154:   v
155: [2] REGION — Which region(s) are affected?
156:   |  - Check: Same alarm in other regions?
157:   |  - If multi-region → suspect global service (IAM, Route53, S3 global)
158:   |  - If single-region → proceed to Service
159:   v
160: [3] SERVICE — Which service namespace shows degradation?
161:   |  - Check CloudWatch namespace: AWS/Lambda, AWS/ECS, AWS/ApiGateway, etc.
162:   |  - If multiple services → suspect shared dependency (VPC, NAT, DNS, IAM)
163:   |  - If single service → proceed to Operation
164:   v
165: [4] OPERATION — Which API action or function is failing?
166:   |  - For Lambda: which function name?
167:   |  - For ECS: which service/task definition?
168:   |  - For API GW: which stage/resource/method?
169:   |  - If all operations → suspect service-level issue (throttling, quota)
170:   |  - If specific operation → proceed to Resource
171:   v
172: [5] RESOURCE — Which specific resource instance?
173:      - Function ARN, Task ID, DB instance identifier
174:      - This is your investigation target
175:      - Proceed to log and trace analysis scoped to this resource
176: ```
177: 
178: ### Shared Dependency Investigation
179: 
180: When blast radius spans multiple services, investigate in this order:
181: 
182: 1. **VPC/Networking** — NAT Gateway ErrorPortAllocation, packet drops, DNS resolution failures
183: 2. **IAM/STS** — ThrottlingException on AssumeRole, token vending latency
184: 3. **Downstream dependency** — shared database, cache, or external API
185: 4. **Deployment pipeline** — simultaneous deploys across services from same pipeline run
186: 5. **AWS service event** — check AWS Health Dashboard and Service Health for the region
187: 
188: ---
189: 
190: ## Pattern 4: PromQL-Style Metric Query Patterns
191: 
192: These patterns use CloudWatch metric math and GetMetricData to build composite signals. Express them as metric queries for dashboards or programmatic retrieval.
193: 
194: ### Error Rate as Percentage
195: 
196: ```
197: MetricDataQueries:
198:   - Id: errors
199:     MetricStat:
200:       Metric:
201:         Namespace: AWS/Lambda
202:         MetricName: Errors
203:         Dimensions: [{Name: FunctionName, Value: TARGET}]
204:       Period: 60
205:       Stat: Sum
206:   - Id: invocations
207:     MetricStat:
208:       Metric:
209:         Namespace: AWS/Lambda
210:         MetricName: Invocations
211:         Dimensions: [{Name: FunctionName, Value: TARGET}]
212:       Period: 60
213:       Stat: Sum
214:   - Id: error_rate
215:     Expression: "errors / invocations * 100"
216:     Label: "Error Rate %"
217: ```
218: 
219: ### Latency Anomaly Detection (Compare to Baseline)
220: 
221: ```
222: MetricDataQueries:
223:   - Id: current_p99
224:     MetricStat:
225:       Metric:
226:         Namespace: AWS/Lambda
227:         MetricName: Duration
228:         Dimensions: [{Name: FunctionName, Value: TARGET}]
229:       Period: 300
230:       Stat: p99
231:   - Id: baseline_p99
232:     MetricStat:
233:       Metric:
234:         Namespace: AWS/Lambda
235:         MetricName: Duration
236:         Dimensions: [{Name: FunctionName, Value: TARGET}]
237:       Period: 300
238:       Stat: p99
239:     # Use StartTime/EndTime set to same window last week
240:   - Id: anomaly_ratio
241:     Expression: "current_p99 / baseline_p99"
242:     Label: "Latency vs Baseline (ratio > 2 = anomaly)"
243: ```
244: 
245: ### Throttling Pressure Score
246: 
247: Combine multiple throttling signals into a single pressure metric:
248: 
249: ```
250: MetricDataQueries:
251:   - Id: lambda_throttles
252:     MetricStat:
253:       Metric: {Namespace: AWS/Lambda, MetricName: Throttles}
254:       Period: 60
255:       Stat: Sum
256:   - Id: api_gw_429s
257:     MetricStat:
258:       Metric: {Namespace: AWS/ApiGateway, MetricName: 4XXError, Dimensions: [{Name: ApiName, Value: TARGET}]}
259:       Period: 60
260:       Stat: Sum
261:   - Id: dynamo_throttles
262:     MetricStat:
263:       Metric: {Namespace: AWS/DynamoDB, MetricName: ThrottledRequests, Dimensions: [{Name: TableName, Value: TARGET}]}
264:       Period: 60
265:       Stat: Sum
266:   - Id: throttle_pressure
267:     Expression: "lambda_throttles + api_gw_429s + dynamo_throttles"
268:     Label: "Combined Throttle Pressure"
269: ```
270: 
271: ### Concurrent Execution Headroom
272: 
273: ```
274: MetricDataQueries:
275:   - Id: concurrent
276:     MetricStat:
277:       Metric: {Namespace: AWS/Lambda, MetricName: ConcurrentExecutions}
278:       Period: 60
279:       Stat: Maximum
280:   - Id: headroom
281:     Expression: "1000 - concurrent"
282:     Label: "Remaining Concurrency (account limit 1000)"
283: ```
284: 
285: ---
286: 
287: ## Pattern 5: Incident Timeline Reconstruction
288: 
289: ### Process
290: 
291: Reconstruct a precise timeline by merging data from multiple sources:
292: 
293: 1. **Collect timestamps:**
294: 
295: | Source | Query | Yields |
296: |--------|-------|--------|
297: | CloudWatch Alarms | Alarm history API | State transition times |
298: | CloudWatch Metrics | GetMetricData with 1-min period | First anomaly point |
299: | CloudWatch Logs | Logs Insights with `earliest(@timestamp)` | First error occurrence |
300: | CloudTrail | LookupEvents filtered by time | Deployment/change events |
301: | AWS Health | DescribeEvents | AWS-side incidents |
302: 
303: 2. **Build the timeline:**
304: 
305: ```
306: fields @timestamp, @message
307: | filter @message like /ERROR|WARN|timeout|refused|denied/
308: | stats earliest(@timestamp) as firstSeen, latest(@timestamp) as lastSeen, count(*) as occurrences
309:   by @message
310: | sort firstSeen asc
311: | limit 20
312: ```
313: 
314: 3. **Identify the sequence:**
315: 
316: ```
317: Timeline:
318:   T-15m: CloudTrail — UpdateFunctionCode by CI/CD role
319:   T-12m: Logs — first error "Connection refused to payments-api.internal"
320:   T-10m: Metrics — Error count crosses 5/min threshold
321:   T-8m:  Alarm — PaymentProcessorErrors enters ALARM
322:   T-5m:  Metrics — p99 latency spikes to 28s (timeout)
323:   T-0:   Current — error rate at 45%, alarm still firing
324: ```
325: 
326: 4. **Determine root event** — the earliest change that preceded all symptoms. Walk backward from the first symptom to the most recent mutation (deploy, config change, scaling event, or external dependency shift).
327: 
328: ### Gotchas
329: 
330: - CloudWatch metric timestamps are end-of-period. A 1-minute datapoint at 14:05 covers 14:04-14:05.
331: - CloudTrail events can have up to 15-minute delivery delay. Use `eventTime`, not ingestion time.
332: - Log group timestamps depend on the agent/SDK flush interval. Allow for 30-60s of clock skew.
333: - Alarm state changes have a built-in evaluation delay (periods x evaluation periods). The actual anomaly started earlier.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/aws-cost-optimize/SKILL.md">
  1: ---
  2: name: aws-cost-optimize
  3: description: 'Analyze AWS resources used in the app (IaC files and/or resources in a target account/region) and optimize costs - creating GitHub issues for identified optimizations.'
  4: ---
  5: 
  6: # AWS Cost Optimize
  7: 
  8: This workflow analyzes Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC) files and AWS resources to generate cost optimization recommendations. It creates individual GitHub issues for each optimization opportunity plus one EPIC issue to coordinate implementation, enabling efficient tracking and execution of cost savings initiatives.
  9: 
 10: ## Prerequisites
 11: - AWS CLI configured and authenticated (`aws sts get-caller-identity` succeeds)
 12: - GitHub MCP server configured and authenticated
 13: - Target GitHub repository identified
 14: - AWS resources deployed (IaC files optional but helpful)
 15: 
 16: ## Workflow Steps
 17: 
 18: ### Step 1: Get AWS Cost Optimization Best Practices
 19: **Action**: Retrieve cost optimization best practices before analysis
 20: **Tools**: `fetch` to retrieve AWS documentation
 21: **Process**:
 22: 1. **Load Best Practices**:
 23:    - Fetch `https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cost-management/latest/userguide/cost-optimization-best-practices.html`
 24:    - Fetch the AWS Well-Architected Cost Optimization pillar summary
 25:    - Use these practices to inform subsequent analysis and recommendations
 26: 
 27: ### Step 2: Discover AWS Infrastructure
 28: **Action**: Dynamically discover and analyze AWS resources and configurations
 29: **Tools**: AWS CLI + Local file system access
 30: **Process**:
 31: 1. **Account & Region Discovery**:
 32:    - Execute `aws sts get-caller-identity` to confirm account
 33:    - Execute `aws configure get region` to determine default region
 34: 
 35: 2. **Resource Discovery** (per region):
 36:    - EC2 instances: `aws ec2 describe-instances --query 'Reservations[].Instances[].[InstanceId,InstanceType,State.Name,Tags]'`
 37:    - RDS instances: `aws rds describe-db-instances --query 'DBInstances[].[DBInstanceIdentifier,DBInstanceClass,Engine,MultiAZ]'`
 38:    - Lambda functions: `aws lambda list-functions --query 'Functions[].[FunctionName,Runtime,MemorySize,Architectures]'`
 39:    - ECS clusters/services: `aws ecs list-clusters` then `aws ecs describe-services`
 40:    - S3 buckets: `aws s3api list-buckets --query 'Buckets[].Name'`
 41:    - ElastiCache clusters: `aws elasticache describe-cache-clusters`
 42:    - NAT Gateways: `aws ec2 describe-nat-gateways`
 43:    - Load Balancers: `aws elbv2 describe-load-balancers`
 44: 
 45: 3. **IaC Detection**:
 46:    - Scan for IaC files: `**/*.tf`, `**/*.yaml` (CloudFormation/SAM), `**/*.json` (CloudFormation), `**/cdk.json`, `lib/**/*.ts` (CDK)
 47:    - Parse resource definitions to understand intended configurations
 48:    - Do NOT use application code files — only IaC files as the source of truth
 49:    - If no IaC files found: STOP and report to user
 50: 
 51: ### Step 3: Collect Usage Metrics & Validate Current Costs
 52: **Action**: Gather utilization data and verify actual resource costs
 53: **Tools**: AWS CLI (CloudWatch, Cost Explorer)
 54: **Process**:
 55: 1. **CloudWatch Metrics** (last 7 days):
 56:    ```bash
 57:    # EC2 CPU utilization
 58:    aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics \
 59:      --namespace AWS/EC2 --metric-name CPUUtilization \
 60:      --dimensions Name=InstanceId,Value=<id> \
 61:      --start-time $(date -u -d '7 days ago' +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ) \
 62:      --end-time $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ) \
 63:      --period 3600 --statistics Average
 64: 
 65:    # Lambda duration
 66:    aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics \
 67:      --namespace AWS/Lambda --metric-name Duration \
 68:      --dimensions Name=FunctionName,Value=<name> \
 69:      --start-time $(date -u -d '7 days ago' +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ) \
 70:      --end-time $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ) \
 71:      --period 86400 --statistics Average,Maximum
 72:    ```
 73: 
 74: 2. **AWS Cost Explorer**:
 75:    ```bash
 76:    aws ce get-cost-and-usage \
 77:      --time-period Start=$(date -u -d '30 days ago' +%Y-%m-%d),End=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%d) \
 78:      --granularity MONTHLY --metrics BlendedCost \
 79:      --group-by Type=DIMENSION,Key=SERVICE
 80:    ```
 81: 
 82: 3. **Calculate Baseline Metrics**: CPU/Memory averages, Lambda invocation rates, data transfer patterns, and a realistic current monthly total.
 83: 
 84: ### Step 4: Generate Cost Optimization Recommendations
 85: **Action**: Analyze resources to identify optimization opportunities
 86: **Process**:
 87: 1. **Apply Optimization Patterns**:
 88: 
 89:    **Compute**:
 90:    - EC2: Right-size based on CPU/memory (<20% average → downsize), convert On-Demand to Savings Plans, migrate to Graviton/ARM (up to 40% cheaper)
 91:    - Lambda: Reduce memory for idle functions, switch to `arm64` (20% cheaper)
 92:    - ECS/EKS: Use Fargate Spot for dev/batch workloads
 93: 
 94:    **Database**:
 95:    - RDS: Right-size instance class, convert single-AZ for dev, use Aurora Serverless v2 for variable load
 96:    - DynamoDB: Switch Provisioned → On-Demand for unpredictable traffic
 97:    - ElastiCache: Right-size node type based on memory utilization
 98: 
 99:    **Storage**:
100:    - S3: Lifecycle policies (Standard → Standard-IA after 30d → Glacier after 90d), enable Intelligent-Tiering
101:    - EBS: Delete unattached volumes, convert gp2 → gp3 (same performance, 20% cheaper)
102: 
103:    **Network**:
104:    - Consolidate NAT Gateways for non-production environments
105:    - Use VPC endpoints for S3/DynamoDB to avoid NAT Gateway charges
106: 
107: 2. **Calculate Priority Score**:
108:    ```
109:    Priority Score = (Value Score × Monthly Savings) / (Risk Score × Implementation Days)
110:    High: Score > 20 | Medium: Score 5-20 | Low: Score < 5
111:    ```
112: 
113: ### Step 5: User Confirmation
114: **Action**: Present summary and get approval before creating GitHub issues
115: 
116: ```
117: 🎯 AWS Cost Optimization Summary
118: 
119: 📊 Analysis Results:
120: • Total Resources Analyzed: X
121: • Current Monthly Cost: $X
122: • Potential Monthly Savings: $Y
123: • Optimization Opportunities: Z
124: • High Priority Items: N
125: 
126: 🏆 Recommendations:
127: 1. [Resource]: [Current] → [Target] = $X/month savings - [Risk] | [Effort]
128: ...
129: 
130: 💡 This will create Y individual GitHub issues + 1 EPIC issue.
131: 
132: ❓ Proceed with creating GitHub issues? (y/n)
133: ```
134: 
135: Wait for user confirmation before proceeding.
136: 
137: ### Step 6: Create Individual Optimization Issues
138: **Action**: Create separate GitHub issues for each optimization. Label with "cost-optimization" (green) and "aws" (orange).
139: 
140: **Title**: `[COST-OPT] [Resource Type] - [Brief Description] - $X/month savings`
141: 
142: **Body**:
143: ```markdown
144: ## 💰 Cost Optimization: [Brief Title]
145: 
146: **Monthly Savings**: $X | **Risk Level**: [Low/Medium/High] | **Effort**: X days
147: 
148: ### 📋 Description
149: [Clear explanation of the optimization and why it's needed]
150: 
151: ### 🔧 Implementation
152: 
153: **IaC Files Detected**: [Yes/No]
154: 
155: ```bash
156: # IaC modification (preferred) or AWS CLI fallback
157: ```
158: 
159: ### 📊 Evidence
160: - Current Configuration: [details]
161: - Usage Pattern: [evidence from CloudWatch]
162: - Cost Impact: $X/month → $Y/month
163: 
164: ### ✅ Validation Steps
165: - [ ] Test in non-production environment
166: - [ ] Verify no performance degradation via CloudWatch
167: - [ ] Confirm cost reduction in AWS Cost Explorer
168: 
169: ### ⚠️ Risks & Considerations
170: - [Risk and mitigation]
171: 
172: **Priority Score**: X | **Value**: X/10 | **Risk**: X/10
173: ```
174: 
175: ### Step 7: Create EPIC Coordinating Issue
176: **Action**: Create master tracking issue. Label with "cost-optimization" (green), "aws" (orange), "epic" (purple).
177: 
178: **Title**: `[EPIC] AWS Cost Optimization Initiative - $X/month potential savings`
179: 
180: **Body**: Executive summary with account/region details, Mermaid architecture diagram of current resources, prioritized checklist linking all individual issues (High → Medium → Low), progress tracking, and success criteria (>80% of estimated savings realized, no performance degradation).
181: 
182: ## Error Handling
183: - **AWS Authentication Failure**: Guide through `aws configure`
184: - **No Resources Found**: Create informational issue about AWS resource deployment
185: - **Insufficient Permissions**: List required IAM read-only permissions
186: - **GitHub Creation Failure**: Output formatted recommendations to console
187: - **Cost Explorer Not Enabled**: Guide user to enable in AWS Console
188: 
189: ## Success Criteria
190: - ✅ All cost estimates verified against actual configurations and AWS pricing
191: - ✅ Individual GitHub issues created for each optimization
192: - ✅ EPIC issue provides comprehensive coordination and tracking
193: - ✅ All recommendations include specific AWS CLI or IaC commands
194: - ✅ User confirmation obtained before creating issues
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/aws-resource-health-diagnose/SKILL.md">
  1: ---
  2: name: aws-resource-health-diagnose
  3: description: 'Analyze AWS resource health, diagnose issues from CloudWatch logs and metrics, and create a remediation plan for identified problems.'
  4: ---
  5: 
  6: # AWS Resource Health & Issue Diagnosis
  7: 
  8: This workflow analyzes a specific AWS resource to assess its health status, diagnose potential issues using CloudWatch logs and metrics, and develop a comprehensive remediation plan for any problems discovered.
  9: 
 10: ## Prerequisites
 11: - AWS CLI configured and authenticated
 12: - Target AWS resource identified (name, type, and optionally region/account)
 13: - CloudWatch logging and metrics enabled on the target resource
 14: 
 15: ## Workflow Steps
 16: 
 17: ### Step 1: Get AWS Diagnostic Best Practices
 18: Fetch `https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/` for monitoring and troubleshooting guidance to inform the diagnostic approach.
 19: 
 20: ### Step 2: Resource Discovery & Identification
 21: Locate the target resource using the appropriate AWS CLI command for its type:
 22: 
 23: ```bash
 24: # EC2
 25: aws ec2 describe-instances --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=<name>"
 26: # Lambda
 27: aws lambda get-function --function-name <name>
 28: # RDS
 29: aws rds describe-db-instances --db-instance-identifier <name>
 30: # ECS
 31: aws ecs describe-services --cluster <cluster> --services <name>
 32: # ALB
 33: aws elbv2 describe-load-balancers --names <name>
 34: # DynamoDB
 35: aws dynamodb describe-table --table-name <name>
 36: # SQS
 37: aws sqs get-queue-attributes --queue-url <url> --attribute-names All
 38: # API Gateway
 39: aws apigatewayv2 get-apis
 40: ```
 41: 
 42: If multiple matches are found, prompt the user to specify region/account.
 43: 
 44: ### Step 3: Health Status Assessment
 45: Run service-specific health checks:
 46: 
 47: ```bash
 48: # EC2
 49: aws ec2 describe-instance-status --instance-ids <id>
 50: 
 51: # RDS
 52: aws rds describe-db-instances --db-instance-identifier <name> \
 53:   --query 'DBInstances[0].DBInstanceStatus'
 54: 
 55: # Lambda - error rate over 24h
 56: aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace AWS/Lambda \
 57:   --metric-name Errors --dimensions Name=FunctionName,Value=<name> \
 58:   --start-time $(date -u -d '24 hours ago' +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ) \
 59:   --end-time $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ) \
 60:   --period 3600 --statistics Sum
 61: 
 62: # ECS
 63: aws ecs describe-services --cluster <cluster> --services <name> \
 64:   --query 'services[0].[status,runningCount,desiredCount,pendingCount]'
 65: ```
 66: 
 67: Key health indicators by service type:
 68: - **Lambda**: Error rate, throttle rate, duration P99, concurrent executions
 69: - **RDS**: CPU utilization, FreeStorageSpace, DatabaseConnections, ReadLatency/WriteLatency
 70: - **ECS**: Running vs desired task count, task stop reason
 71: - **ALB**: TargetResponseTime, HTTPCode_ELB_5XX_Count, UnHealthyHostCount
 72: - **SQS**: ApproximateNumberOfMessagesNotVisible, ApproximateAgeOfOldestMessage
 73: - **DynamoDB**: ConsumedReadCapacityUnits, ThrottledRequests, SuccessfulRequestLatency
 74: 
 75: ### Step 4: Log & Metrics Analysis
 76: Find log groups and run CloudWatch Logs Insights queries:
 77: 
 78: ```bash
 79: # Find log groups
 80: aws logs describe-log-groups --log-group-name-prefix /aws/<service>/<name>
 81: 
 82: # Start a query (last 24h errors)
 83: aws logs start-query \
 84:   --log-group-name /aws/lambda/<name> \
 85:   --start-time $(date -u -d '24 hours ago' +%s) \
 86:   --end-time $(date -u +%s) \
 87:   --query-string 'filter @message like /ERROR/ | stats count(*) as errorCount by bin(1h)'
 88: 
 89: # Get results
 90: aws logs get-query-results --query-id <id>
 91: 
 92: # Lambda cold starts
 93: aws logs start-query \
 94:   --log-group-name /aws/lambda/<name> \
 95:   --start-time $(date -u -d '24 hours ago' +%s) \
 96:   --end-time $(date -u +%s) \
 97:   --query-string 'filter @type = "REPORT" | filter @initDuration > 0 | stats count() as coldStarts by bin(1h)'
 98: 
 99: # RDS Performance Insights (if enabled)
100: aws pi get-resource-metrics \
101:   --service-type RDS --identifier db:<identifier> \
102:   --metric-queries '[{"Metric":"db.load.avg"}]' \
103:   --start-time $(date -u -d '24 hours ago' +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ) \
104:   --end-time $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ) \
105:   --period-in-seconds 3600
106: ```
107: 
108: Identify: recurring error patterns, correlation with deployments (CloudTrail), performance trends, dependency failures.
109: 
110: ### Step 5: Issue Classification & Root Cause Analysis
111: **Severity**:
112: - **Critical**: Service unavailable, data loss, security incidents
113: - **High**: Performance degradation, error rates >5%, intermittent failures
114: - **Medium**: Warnings, suboptimal configuration, minor performance issues
115: - **Low**: Informational alerts, optimization opportunities
116: 
117: **Root Cause Categories**:
118: - Configuration Issues: wrong settings, missing env vars, IAM permission denials
119: - Resource Constraints: CPU/memory/disk limits, Lambda throttling, RDS connection exhaustion
120: - Network Issues: security group rules, VPC routing, DNS, NACLs
121: - Application Issues: code bugs, memory leaks, unhandled exceptions, slow queries
122: - Dependency Issues: downstream timeouts, SQS/SNS failures, external API limits
123: - Security Issues: KMS key issues, certificate expiration
124: 
125: ### Step 6: Generate Remediation Plan
126: 
127: **Immediate Actions** (Critical):
128: ```bash
129: # Lambda throttling — increase reserved concurrency
130: aws lambda put-reserved-concurrency \
131:   --function-name <name> --reserved-concurrent-executions 100
132: 
133: # RDS connection exhaustion — reboot to reset connections
134: aws rds reboot-db-instance --db-instance-identifier <name>
135: ```
136: 
137: **Short-term Fixes** (High/Medium): Configuration adjustments, right-sizing, CloudWatch alarm improvements, IAM corrections.
138: 
139: **Long-term Improvements**: Architectural changes for resilience, preventive monitoring, enable AWS Health Dashboard notifications via EventBridge.
140: 
141: ### Step 7: Report & User Confirmation
142: 
143: Present findings:
144: ```
145: 🏥 AWS Resource Health Assessment
146: 
147: 📊 Resource Overview:
148: • Resource: [Name] ([Type])
149: • Status: [Healthy/Warning/Critical]
150: • Region: [Region] | Account: [Account ID]
151: 
152: 🚨 Issues Identified:
153: • Critical: X | High: Y | Medium: Z | Low: N
154: 
155: 🔍 Top Issues:
156: 1. [Issue]: [Description] — Impact: [High/Medium/Low]
157: 2. [Issue]: [Description] — Impact: [High/Medium/Low]
158: 
159: 🛠️ Remediation: X immediate, Y short-term, Z long-term actions
160: 
161: ❓ Proceed with detailed remediation plan? (y/n)
162: ```
163: 
164: Then generate a full markdown report covering: health metrics, issues with root cause analysis, phased remediation steps with AWS CLI commands, CloudWatch alarm recommendations, and validation checklist.
165: 
166: ## Error Handling
167: - **Resource Not Found**: Ask user to clarify name/region
168: - **Authentication Issues**: Guide through `aws configure`
169: - **Insufficient Permissions**: List required IAM actions (`logs:*`, `cloudwatch:*`, `pi:*`)
170: - **No Logs Available**: Suggest enabling CloudWatch logging for the resource type
171: - **Query Timeouts**: Use shorter time windows
172: 
173: ## Success Criteria
174: - ✅ Resource health accurately assessed across all key metrics
175: - ✅ All significant issues identified and classified by severity
176: - ✅ Root cause analysis completed for major problems
177: - ✅ Actionable remediation plan with AWS CLI commands
178: - ✅ CloudWatch monitoring recommendations included
