572:   --time-period Start=$(date -u +%Y-%m-01),End=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%d) \
573:   --granularity MONTHLY --metrics BlendedCost \
574:   --query 'ResultsByTime[].[TimePeriod.Start,Total.BlendedCost.Amount,Total.BlendedCost.Unit]' \
575:   --output table
576: 
577: # "cost by service" / "spending breakdown"
578: aws ce get-cost-and-usage \
579:   --time-period Start=$(date -u -d '30 days ago' +%Y-%m-%d),End=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%d) \
580:   --granularity MONTHLY --metrics BlendedCost \
581:   --group-by Type=DIMENSION,Key=SERVICE --output table
582: 
583: # "AWS Budgets"
584: aws budgets describe-budgets \
585:   --account-id $(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text) \
586:   --query 'Budgets[].[BudgetName,BudgetType,BudgetLimit.Amount,CalculatedSpend.ActualSpend.Amount]' \
587:   --output table
588: 
589: # "Trusted Advisor recommendations"
590: aws support describe-trusted-advisor-checks --language en \
591:   --query 'checks[].[id,name,category]' --output table
592: ```
593: 
594: ---
595: 
596: ### CROSS-SERVICE QUERIES
597: 
598: ```bash
599: # "resources tagged Environment=production" / "all production resources"
600: aws resourcegroupstaggingapi get-resources \
601:   --tag-filters Key=Environment,Values=production \
602:   --query 'ResourceTagMappingList[].[ResourceARN]' --output table
603: 
604: # "all resources tagged <key>=<value>"
605: aws resourcegroupstaggingapi get-resources \
606:   --tag-filters Key=<key>,Values=<value> \
607:   --query 'ResourceTagMappingList[].[ResourceARN,Tags]' --output table
608: 
609: # "inventory of all resources" (AWS Config)
610: aws configservice list-discovered-resources --resource-type <type> \
611:   --query 'resourceIdentifiers[].[resourceType,resourceId,resourceName]' --output table
612: ```
613: 
614: ---
615: 
616: ## Output Formatting Rules
617: 
618: 1. Always use `--output table` for list results; use `--output json` only when deep detail is explicitly requested
619: 2. Always use `--query` to extract only relevant fields — never dump raw JSON
620: 3. For large result sets (>20 items), show a count first, then offer to filter
621: 4. When a command returns nothing, explain why (wrong region, no resources, insufficient permissions)
622: 5. Offer to drill into a specific resource: "Found 47 EC2 instances. Filter by state, type, or tag?"
623: 
624: ## Error Handling
625: 
626: | Error | Response |
627: |---|---|
628: | `AccessDenied` | "You don't have permission to list [resource]. Required: `<service>:<Action>`." |
629: | `NoCredentialProviders` | "Run `aws configure` or set `AWS_PROFILE`." |
630: | Empty result | "No [resources] found in [region]. Check another region?" |
631: | Invalid identifier | "Could not find '[name]'. Check the name or provide the resource ID." |
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/aws-well-architected-review/SKILL.md">
  1: ---
  2: name: aws-well-architected-review
  3: description: 'Perform an AWS Well-Architected Framework review of the current workload IaC and architecture, generating findings and GitHub issues for improvements.'
  4: ---
  5: 
  6: # AWS Well-Architected Review
  7: 
  8: This workflow performs a structured AWS Well-Architected Framework (WAF) review against your workload's IaC files and deployed infrastructure. It identifies risks across all 6 WAF pillars and creates GitHub issues to track remediation.
  9: 
 10: ## Prerequisites
 11: - AWS CLI configured and authenticated
 12: - IaC files present in the repository (Terraform, CloudFormation, CDK, or SAM)
 13: - GitHub MCP server configured and authenticated
 14: 
 15: ## Workflow Steps
 16: 
 17: ### Step 1: Load Well-Architected Framework Reference
 18: Fetch current AWS WAF best practices:
 19: - `https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/framework/welcome.html`
 20: - Pillar-specific lenses relevant to the workload type (Serverless, SaaS, etc.)
 21: 
 22: ### Step 2: Discover IaC & Architecture
 23: Scan the repository for IaC files:
 24: - Terraform: `**/*.tf`
 25: - CloudFormation/SAM: `**/*.yaml`, `**/*.json` (CFn templates)
 26: - CDK: `lib/**/*.ts`, `bin/**/*.ts`, `cdk.json`
 27: 
 28: Identify key AWS services in use (compute, data, networking, security, observability) and generate a Mermaid architecture diagram.
 29: 
 30: ### Step 3: Pillar-by-Pillar Review
 31: 
 32: #### Pillar 1: Operational Excellence
 33: - [ ] All infrastructure defined as IaC (no manual console changes)
 34: - [ ] Consistent tagging strategy applied across all resources
 35: - [ ] CloudWatch alarms defined for key metrics
 36: - [ ] Automated deployment pipeline present (no manual deployments)
 37: - [ ] CloudTrail enabled for audit logging
 38: - [ ] Runbooks or operational documentation present
 39: 
 40: #### Pillar 2: Security
 41: - [ ] IAM roles use least-privilege policies (no `*` actions without justification)
 42: - [ ] No hardcoded credentials in IaC or code
 43: - [ ] Secrets managed via Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store
 44: - [ ] S3 buckets have public access blocked and server-side encryption enabled
 45: - [ ] Sensitive resources placed in private subnets
 46: - [ ] Security groups restrict inbound to minimum required ports/CIDRs
 47: - [ ] KMS encryption enabled for sensitive data stores (RDS, EBS, S3, SQS, DynamoDB)
 48: - [ ] SSL/TLS enforced on all endpoints (`enforceSSL: true`)
 49: - [ ] GuardDuty enabled (`aws guardduty list-detectors`)
 50: - [ ] AWS WAF configured on public-facing APIs and CloudFront distributions
 51: - [ ] MFA delete enabled on critical S3 buckets
 52: 
 53: #### Pillar 3: Reliability
 54: - [ ] Multi-AZ deployments for production databases (RDS Multi-AZ, DynamoDB Global Tables)
 55: - [ ] Auto Scaling configured with appropriate policies for EC2/ECS
 56: - [ ] S3 versioning and lifecycle policies configured
 57: - [ ] RDS automated backups enabled with appropriate retention period
 58: - [ ] DynamoDB Point-in-Time Recovery (PITR) enabled
 59: - [ ] Dead Letter Queues (DLQ) configured for Lambda, SQS, SNS
 60: - [ ] Route 53 health checks configured for DNS failover
 61: - [ ] Lambda reserved concurrency set to prevent noisy-neighbor throttling
 62: 
 63: #### Pillar 4: Performance Efficiency
 64: - [ ] Right-sized instance types (Lambda memory, EC2 type, RDS class)
 65: - [ ] Graviton/ARM instances used where available (Lambda `arm64`, EC2 Graviton)
 66: - [ ] Caching implemented (ElastiCache, DAX, CloudFront, API Gateway caching)
 67: - [ ] CloudFront used for global static content delivery
 68: - [ ] Aurora Serverless or DynamoDB On-Demand for variable load patterns
 69: - [ ] Lambda Provisioned Concurrency for latency-critical synchronous paths
 70: 
 71: #### Pillar 5: Cost Optimization
 72: - [ ] EC2 Reserved Instances or Savings Plans for steady-state workloads
 73: - [ ] S3 lifecycle policies moving data to cheaper storage tiers
 74: - [ ] Lambda `arm64` architecture adopted (20% cost reduction)
 75: - [ ] VPC Endpoints for S3/DynamoDB to avoid NAT Gateway charges
 76: - [ ] gp2 EBS volumes migrated to gp3 (same performance, 20% cheaper)
 77: - [ ] Development/test environments have auto-shutdown schedules
 78: - [ ] AWS Budgets and Cost Anomaly Detection configured
 79: - [ ] Unattached EBS volumes and idle EC2 instances identified
 80: 
 81: #### Pillar 6: Sustainability
 82: - [ ] Graviton/ARM instances selected where available
 83: - [ ] Serverless/managed services preferred over always-on EC2
 84: - [ ] S3 lifecycle policies reduce unnecessary long-term data storage
 85: - [ ] Auto Scaling configured to avoid over-provisioning
 86: - [ ] Region selection considers AWS renewable energy commitments
 87: 
 88: ### Step 4: Risk Classification
 89: For each finding, classify:
 90: - **High Risk**: Security vulnerability, single point of failure, no backup/recovery
 91: - **Medium Risk**: Suboptimal reliability, cost inefficiency, performance concern
 92: - **Low Risk**: Best practice deviation, minor optimization opportunity
 93: 
 94: ### Step 5: User Confirmation
 95: 
 96: ```
 97: 🏗️ AWS Well-Architected Review Summary
 98: 
 99: 📊 Review Results:
100: • IaC Files Analyzed: X
101: • AWS Services Identified: Y
102: • Total Findings: Z
103:   • High Risk: A (immediate action required)
104:   • Medium Risk: B (should address soon)
105:   • Low Risk: C (nice to have)
106: 
107: 🔴 Top High Risk Findings:
108: 1. [Pillar]: [Finding] — [Why it matters]
109: 2. [Pillar]: [Finding] — [Why it matters]
110: 
111: 💡 This will create Z individual GitHub issues + 1 EPIC issue.
112: 
113: ❓ Proceed with creating GitHub issues? (y/n)
114: ```
115: 
116: ### Step 6: Create Individual Finding Issues
117: Label with "well-architected" and the pillar name (e.g., "security", "reliability").
118: 
119: **Title**: `[WAF-<PILLAR>] [Brief Finding] — [Risk Level]`
120: 
121: **Body**:
122: ````markdown
123: ## 🏗️ Well-Architected Finding: [Brief Title]
124: 
125: **Pillar**: [Name] | **Risk Level**: [High/Medium/Low] | **Effort**: [Low/Medium/High]
126: 
127: ### 📋 Description
128: [Clear explanation of the finding and why it matters]
129: 
130: ### 🔧 Remediation
131: 
132: **IaC Fix** (preferred):
133: ```hcl
134: # Terraform example
135: resource "aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration" "example" {
136:   bucket = aws_s3_bucket.example.id
137:   rule {
138:     apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
139:       sse_algorithm = "aws:kms"
140:     }
141:   }
142: }
143: ```
144: 
145: **AWS CLI fallback**:
146: ```bash
147: aws s3api put-bucket-encryption --bucket <name> \
148:   --server-side-encryption-configuration '{"Rules":[{"ApplyServerSideEncryptionByDefault":{"SSEAlgorithm":"aws:kms"}}]}'
149: ```
150: 
151: ### 📚 AWS Reference
152: - [WAF Best Practice Link]
153: - [AWS Documentation Link]
154: 
155: ### ✅ Validation
156: - [ ] Change implemented in IaC and deployed
157: - [ ] AWS Config rule passes (if applicable)
158: - [ ] Security Hub finding resolved (if applicable)
159: 
160: **Well-Architected Question**: [WAF question this maps to]
161: ````
162: 
163: ### Step 7: Create EPIC Tracking Issue
164: Label with "well-architected" and "epic".
165: 
166: **Title**: `[EPIC] AWS Well-Architected Review — X findings across 6 pillars`
167: 
168: **Body**: Executive summary with pillar breakdown table (finding counts by pillar and risk level), Mermaid architecture diagram, prioritized checklist linking all individual issues (High → Medium → Low), and success criteria:
169: - All High-risk findings resolved
170: - Medium findings have accepted mitigation plans
171: - No regression in existing CloudWatch alarms or Config rules
172: 
173: ## Error Handling
174: - **No IaC Files Found**: Limit review to live resource discovery via AWS CLI and note the gap
175: - **Insufficient AWS Permissions**: List required read-only permissions for the review
176: - **GitHub Creation Failure**: Output all findings as formatted markdown to console
177: 
178: ## Success Criteria
179: - ✅ All 6 WAF pillars reviewed against IaC and live infrastructure
180: - ✅ All findings classified by risk level and pillar
181: - ✅ Actionable remediation steps with IaC examples for each finding
182: - ✅ GitHub issues created for team tracking
183: - ✅ Architecture diagram generated for EPIC context
184: - ✅ AWS documentation references included
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/az-cost-optimize/SKILL.md">
  1: ---
  2: name: az-cost-optimize
  3: description: 'Analyze Azure resources used in the app (IaC files and/or resources in a target rg) and optimize costs - creating GitHub issues for identified optimizations.'
  4: ---
  5: 
  6: # Azure Cost Optimize
  7: 
  8: This workflow analyzes Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC) files and Azure resources to generate cost optimization recommendations. It creates individual GitHub issues for each optimization opportunity plus one EPIC issue to coordinate implementation, enabling efficient tracking and execution of cost savings initiatives.
  9: 
 10: ## Prerequisites
 11: - Azure MCP server configured and authenticated
 12: - GitHub MCP server configured and authenticated  
 13: - Target GitHub repository identified
 14: - Azure resources deployed (IaC files optional but helpful)
 15: - Prefer Azure MCP tools (`azmcp-*`) over direct Azure CLI when available
 16: 
 17: ## Workflow Steps
 18: 
 19: ### Step 1: Get Azure Best Practices
 20: **Action**: Retrieve cost optimization best practices before analysis
 21: **Tools**: Azure MCP best practices tool
 22: **Process**:
 23: 1. **Load Best Practices**:
 24:    - Execute `azmcp-bestpractices-get` to get some of the latest Azure optimization guidelines. This may not cover all scenarios but provides a foundation.
 25:    - Use these practices to inform subsequent analysis and recommendations as much as possible
 26:    - Reference best practices in optimization recommendations, either from the MCP tool output or general Azure documentation
 27: 
 28: ### Step 2: Discover Azure Infrastructure
 29: **Action**: Dynamically discover and analyze Azure resources and configurations
 30: **Tools**: Azure MCP tools + Azure CLI fallback + Local file system access
 31: **Process**:
 32: 1. **Resource Discovery**:
 33:    - Execute `azmcp-subscription-list` to find available subscriptions
 34:    - Execute `azmcp-group-list --subscription <subscription-id>` to find resource groups
 35:    - Get a list of all resources in the relevant group(s):
 36:      - Use `az resource list --subscription <id> --resource-group <name>`
 37:    - For each resource type, use MCP tools first if possible, then CLI fallback:
 38:      - `azmcp-cosmos-account-list --subscription <id>` - Cosmos DB accounts
 39:      - `azmcp-storage-account-list --subscription <id>` - Storage accounts  
 40:      - `azmcp-monitor-workspace-list --subscription <id>` - Log Analytics workspaces
 41:      - `azmcp-keyvault-key-list` - Key Vaults
 42:      - `az webapp list` - Web Apps (fallback - no MCP tool available)
 43:      - `az appservice plan list` - App Service Plans (fallback)
 44:      - `az functionapp list` - Function Apps (fallback)
 45:      - `az sql server list` - SQL Servers (fallback)
 46:      - `az redis list` - Redis Cache (fallback)
 47:      - ... and so on for other resource types
 48: 
 49: 2. **IaC Detection**:
 50:    - Use `file_search` to scan for IaC files: "**/*.bicep", "**/*.tf", "**/main.json", "**/*template*.json"
 51:    - Parse resource definitions to understand intended configurations
 52:    - Compare against discovered resources to identify discrepancies
 53:    - Note presence of IaC files for implementation recommendations later on
 54:    - Do NOT use any other file from the repository, only IaC files. Using other files is NOT allowed as it is not a source of truth.
 55:    - If you do not find IaC files, then STOP and report no IaC files found to the user.
 56: 
 57: 3. **Configuration Analysis**:
 58:    - Extract current SKUs, tiers, and settings for each resource
 59:    - Identify resource relationships and dependencies
 60:    - Map resource utilization patterns where available
 61: 
 62: ### Step 3: Collect Usage Metrics & Validate Current Costs
 63: **Action**: Gather utilization data AND verify actual resource costs
 64: **Tools**: Azure MCP monitoring tools + Azure CLI
 65: **Process**:
 66: 1. **Find Monitoring Sources**:
 67:    - Use `azmcp-monitor-workspace-list --subscription <id>` to find Log Analytics workspaces
 68:    - Use `azmcp-monitor-table-list --subscription <id> --workspace <name> --table-type "CustomLog"` to discover available data
 69: 
 70: 2. **Execute Usage Queries**:
 71:    - Use `azmcp-monitor-log-query` with these predefined queries:
 72:      - Query: "recent" for recent activity patterns
 73:      - Query: "errors" for error-level logs indicating issues
 74:    - For custom analysis, use KQL queries:
 75:    ```kql
 76:    // CPU utilization for App Services
 77:    AppServiceAppLogs
 78:    | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
 79:    | summarize avg(CpuTime) by Resource, bin(TimeGenerated, 1h)
 80:    
 81:    // Cosmos DB RU consumption  
 82:    AzureDiagnostics
 83:    | where ResourceProvider == "MICROSOFT.DOCUMENTDB"
 84:    | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
 85:    | summarize avg(RequestCharge) by Resource
 86:    
 87:    // Storage account access patterns
 88:    StorageBlobLogs
 89:    | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
 90:    | summarize RequestCount=count() by AccountName, bin(TimeGenerated, 1d)
 91:    ```
 92: 
 93: 3. **Calculate Baseline Metrics**:
 94:    - CPU/Memory utilization averages
 95:    - Database throughput patterns
 96:    - Storage access frequency
 97:    - Function execution rates
 98: 
 99: 4. **VALIDATE CURRENT COSTS**: 
100:    - Using the SKU/tier configurations discovered in Step 2
101:    - Look up current Azure pricing at https://azure.microsoft.com/pricing/ or use `az billing` commands
102:    - Document: Resource → Current SKU → Estimated monthly cost
103:    - Calculate realistic current monthly total before proceeding to recommendations
104: 
105: ### Step 4: Generate Cost Optimization Recommendations
106: **Action**: Analyze resources to identify optimization opportunities
107: **Tools**: Local analysis using collected data
108: **Process**:
109: 1. **Apply Optimization Patterns** based on resource types found:
110:    
111:    **Compute Optimizations**:
112:    - App Service Plans: Right-size based on CPU/memory usage
113:    - Function Apps: Premium → Consumption plan for low usage
114:    - Virtual Machines: Scale down oversized instances
115:    
116:    **Database Optimizations**:
117:    - Cosmos DB: 
118:      - Provisioned → Serverless for variable workloads
119:      - Right-size RU/s based on actual usage
120:    - SQL Database: Right-size service tiers based on DTU usage
121:    
122:    **Storage Optimizations**:
123:    - Implement lifecycle policies (Hot → Cool → Archive)
124:    - Consolidate redundant storage accounts
125:    - Right-size storage tiers based on access patterns
126:    
127:    **Infrastructure Optimizations**:
128:    - Remove unused/redundant resources
129:    - Implement auto-scaling where beneficial
130:    - Schedule non-production environments
131: 
132: 2. **Calculate Evidence-Based Savings**: 
133:    - Current validated cost → Target cost = Savings
134:    - Document pricing source for both current and target configurations
135: 
136: 3. **Calculate Priority Score** for each recommendation:
137:    ```
138:    Priority Score = (Value Score × Monthly Savings) / (Risk Score × Implementation Days)
139:    
140:    High Priority: Score > 20
141:    Medium Priority: Score 5-20
142:    Low Priority: Score < 5
143:    ```
144: 
145: 4. **Validate Recommendations**:
146:    - Ensure Azure CLI commands are accurate
147:    - Verify estimated savings calculations
148:    - Assess implementation risks and prerequisites
149:    - Ensure all savings calculations have supporting evidence
150: 
151: ### Step 5: User Confirmation
152: **Action**: Present summary and get approval before creating GitHub issues
153: **Process**:
154: 1. **Display Optimization Summary**:
155:    ```
156:    🎯 Azure Cost Optimization Summary
157:    
158:    📊 Analysis Results:
159:    • Total Resources Analyzed: X
160:    • Current Monthly Cost: $X 
161:    • Potential Monthly Savings: $Y 
162:    • Optimization Opportunities: Z
163:    • High Priority Items: N
164:    
165:    🏆 Recommendations:
166:    1. [Resource]: [Current SKU] → [Target SKU] = $X/month savings - [Risk Level] | [Implementation Effort]
167:    2. [Resource]: [Current Config] → [Target Config] = $Y/month savings - [Risk Level] | [Implementation Effort]
168:    3. [Resource]: [Current Config] → [Target Config] = $Z/month savings - [Risk Level] | [Implementation Effort]
169:    ... and so on
170:    
171:    💡 This will create:
172:    • Y individual GitHub issues (one per optimization)
173:    • 1 EPIC issue to coordinate implementation
174:    
175:    ❓ Proceed with creating GitHub issues? (y/n)
176:    ```
177: 
178: 2. **Wait for User Confirmation**: Only proceed if user confirms
179: 
180: ### Step 6: Create Individual Optimization Issues
181: **Action**: Create separate GitHub issues for each optimization opportunity. Label them with "cost-optimization" (green color), "azure" (blue color).
182: **MCP Tools Required**: `create_issue` for each recommendation
183: **Process**:
184: 1. **Create Individual Issues** using this template:
185: 
186:    **Title Format**: `[COST-OPT] [Resource Type] - [Brief Description] - $X/month savings`
187:    
188:    **Body Template**:
189:    ```markdown
190:    ## 💰 Cost Optimization: [Brief Title]
191:    
192:    **Monthly Savings**: $X | **Risk Level**: [Low/Medium/High] | **Implementation Effort**: X days
193:    
194:    ### 📋 Description
195:    [Clear explanation of the optimization and why it's needed]
196:    
197:    ### 🔧 Implementation
198:    
199:    **IaC Files Detected**: [Yes/No - based on file_search results]
200:    
201:    ```bash
202:    # If IaC files found: Show IaC modifications + deployment
203:    # File: infrastructure/bicep/modules/app-service.bicep
204:    # Change: sku.name: 'S3' → 'B2'
205:    az deployment group create --resource-group [rg] --template-file infrastructure/bicep/main.bicep
206:    
207:    # If no IaC files: Direct Azure CLI commands + warning
208:    # ⚠️ No IaC files found. If they exist elsewhere, modify those instead.
209:    az appservice plan update --name [plan] --sku B2
210:    ```
211:    
212:    ### 📊 Evidence
213:    - Current Configuration: [details]
214:    - Usage Pattern: [evidence from monitoring data]
215:    - Cost Impact: $X/month → $Y/month
216:    - Best Practice Alignment: [reference to Azure best practices if applicable]
217:    
218:    ### ✅ Validation Steps
219:    - [ ] Test in non-production environment
220:    - [ ] Verify no performance degradation
221:    - [ ] Confirm cost reduction in Azure Cost Management
222:    - [ ] Update monitoring and alerts if needed
223:    
224:    ### ⚠️ Risks & Considerations
225:    - [Risk 1 and mitigation]
226:    - [Risk 2 and mitigation]
227:    
228:    **Priority Score**: X | **Value**: X/10 | **Risk**: X/10
229:    ```
230: 
231: ### Step 7: Create EPIC Coordinating Issue
232: **Action**: Create master issue to track all optimization work. Label it with "cost-optimization" (green color), "azure" (blue color), and "epic" (purple color).
233: **MCP Tools Required**: `create_issue` for EPIC
234: **Note about mermaid diagrams**: Ensure you verify mermaid syntax is correct and create the diagrams taking accessibility guidelines into account (styling, colors, etc.).
235: **Process**:
236: 1. **Create EPIC Issue**:
237: 
238:    **Title**: `[EPIC] Azure Cost Optimization Initiative - $X/month potential savings`
239:    
240:    **Body Template**:
241:    ```markdown
242:    # 🎯 Azure Cost Optimization EPIC
243:    
244:    **Total Potential Savings**: $X/month | **Implementation Timeline**: X weeks
245:    
246:    ## 📊 Executive Summary
247:    - **Resources Analyzed**: X
248:    - **Optimization Opportunities**: Y  
249:    - **Total Monthly Savings Potential**: $X
250:    - **High Priority Items**: N
251:    
252:    ## 🏗️ Current Architecture Overview
253:    
254:    ```mermaid
255:    graph TB
256:        subgraph "Resource Group: [name]"
257:            [Generated architecture diagram showing current resources and costs]
258:        end
259:    ```
260:    
261:    ## 📋 Implementation Tracking
262:    
263:    ### 🚀 High Priority (Implement First)
264:    - [ ] #[issue-number]: [Title] - $X/month savings
265:    - [ ] #[issue-number]: [Title] - $X/month savings
266:    
267:    ### ⚡ Medium Priority 
268:    - [ ] #[issue-number]: [Title] - $X/month savings
269:    - [ ] #[issue-number]: [Title] - $X/month savings
270:    
271:    ### 🔄 Low Priority (Nice to Have)
272:    - [ ] #[issue-number]: [Title] - $X/month savings
273:    
274:    ## 📈 Progress Tracking
275:    - **Completed**: 0 of Y optimizations
276:    - **Savings Realized**: $0 of $X/month
277:    - **Implementation Status**: Not Started
278:    
279:    ## 🎯 Success Criteria
280:    - [ ] All high-priority optimizations implemented
281:    - [ ] >80% of estimated savings realized
282:    - [ ] No performance degradation observed
283:    - [ ] Cost monitoring dashboard updated
284:    
285:    ## 📝 Notes
286:    - Review and update this EPIC as issues are completed
287:    - Monitor actual vs. estimated savings
288:    - Consider scheduling regular cost optimization reviews
289:    ```
290: 
291: ## Error Handling
292: - **Cost Validation**: If savings estimates lack supporting evidence or seem inconsistent with Azure pricing, re-verify configurations and pricing sources before proceeding
293: - **Azure Authentication Failure**: Provide manual Azure CLI setup steps
294: - **No Resources Found**: Create informational issue about Azure resource deployment
295: - **GitHub Creation Failure**: Output formatted recommendations to console
296: - **Insufficient Usage Data**: Note limitations and provide configuration-based recommendations only
297: 
298: ## Success Criteria
299: - ✅ All cost estimates verified against actual resource configurations and Azure pricing
300: - ✅ Individual issues created for each optimization (trackable and assignable)
301: - ✅ EPIC issue provides comprehensive coordination and tracking
302: - ✅ All recommendations include specific, executable Azure CLI commands
303: - ✅ Priority scoring enables ROI-focused implementation
304: - ✅ Architecture diagram accurately represents current state
305: - ✅ User confirmation prevents unwanted issue creation
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/azure-architecture-autopilot/references/ai-data.md">
  1: # Domain Pack: AI/Data (v1)
  2: 
