 10: ---
 11: 
 12: ## 1. Microsoft Foundry (CognitiveServices)
 13: 
 14: ### Resource Hierarchy
 15: 
 16: ```
 17: Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts (kind: 'AIServices')
 18: ├── /projects          — Foundry Project (required for portal access)
 19: └── /deployments       — Model deployments (GPT-4o, embedding, etc.)
 20: ```
 21: 
 22: ### Bicep Core Structure
 23: 
 24: ```bicep
 25: // Foundry resource
 26: resource foundry 'Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts@<fetch>' = {
 27:   name: foundryName
 28:   location: location
 29:   kind: 'AIServices'
 30:   sku: { name: '<confirm with user>' }               // ← SKU confirmed after MS Docs check in Phase 1
 31:   identity: { type: 'SystemAssigned' }
 32:   properties: {
 33:     customSubDomainName: foundryName  // ← Required, globally unique. Cannot change after creation — must delete and recreate if omitted
 34:     allowProjectManagement: true
 35:     publicNetworkAccess: 'Disabled'
 36:     networkAcls: { defaultAction: 'Deny' }
 37:   }
 38: }
 39: 
 40: // Foundry Project — Must be created as a set with Foundry
 41: resource project 'Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts/projects@<fetch>' = {
 42:   parent: foundry
 43:   name: '${foundryName}-project'
 44:   location: location
 45:   sku: { name: '<same as parent>' }
 46:   kind: 'AIServices'
 47:   identity: { type: 'SystemAssigned' }
 48:   properties: {}
 49: }
 50: 
 51: // Model deployment — At Foundry resource level
 52: resource deployment 'Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts/deployments@<fetch>' = {
 53:   parent: foundry
 54:   name: '<model-name>'                              // ← Confirmed with user in Phase 1
 55:   sku: {
 56:     name: '<deployment-type>'                        // ← GlobalStandard, Standard, etc. — MS Docs fetch
 57:     capacity: <confirm with user>                    // ← Capacity units — verify available range from MS Docs
 58:   }
 59:   properties: {
 60:     model: {
 61:       format: 'OpenAI'
 62:       name: '<model-name>'                           // ← Must verify availability (fetch)
 63:       version: '<fetch>'                             // ← Version also fetched
 64:     }
 65:   }
 66: }
 67: ```
 68: 
 69: > `@<fetch>`: Verify API version from the URLs in `azure-dynamic-sources.md`.
 70: > Model name/version/deployment type/capacity: All Dynamic — Confirmed with user after MS Docs fetch in Phase 1.
 71: 
 72: ---
 73: 
 74: ## 2. Azure AI Search
 75: 
 76: ### Bicep Core Structure
 77: 
 78: ```bicep
 79: resource search 'Microsoft.Search/searchServices@<fetch>' = {
 80:   name: searchName
 81:   location: location
 82:   sku: { name: '<confirm with user>' }
 83:   identity: { type: 'SystemAssigned' }
 84:   properties: {
 85:     hostingMode: 'default'
 86:     publicNetworkAccess: 'disabled'
 87:     semanticSearch: '<confirm with user>'    // disabled | free | standard — verify in MS Docs
 88:   }
 89: }
 90: ```
 91: 
 92: ### Design Notes
 93: 
 94: - PE support: Basic SKU or higher (verify latest constraints in MS Docs)
 95: - Semantic Ranker: Activated via `semanticSearch` property (`disabled` | `free` | `standard`) — verify per-SKU support in MS Docs
 96: - Vector search: Supported on paid SKUs (verify in MS Docs)
 97: - Commonly used together with Foundry for RAG configurations
 98: 
 99: ---
100: 
101: ## 3. ADLS Gen2 (Storage Account)
102: 
103: ### Bicep Core Structure
104: 
105: ```bicep
106: resource storage 'Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts@<fetch>' = {
107:   name: storageName        // Lowercase+numbers only, no hyphens
108:   location: location
109:   kind: 'StorageV2'
110:   sku: { name: 'Standard_LRS' }
111:   properties: {
112:     isHnsEnabled: true                 // ← Never omit this
113:     accessTier: 'Hot'
114:     allowBlobPublicAccess: false
115:     minimumTlsVersion: 'TLS1_2'
116:     publicNetworkAccess: 'Disabled'
117:     networkAcls: { defaultAction: 'Deny' }
118:   }
119: }
120: 
121: // Container
122: resource container 'Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/blobServices/containers@<fetch>' = {
123:   name: '${storage.name}/default/raw'
124: }
125: ```
126: 
127: ### Design Notes
128: 
129: - `isHnsEnabled` cannot be changed after creation → Resource must be recreated if omitted
130: - PE: May need both `blob` and `dfs` PEs depending on use case
131: - Common containers: `raw`, `processed`, `curated`
132: 
133: ---
134: 
135: ## 4. Microsoft Fabric
136: 
137: ### Bicep Core Structure
138: 
139: ```bicep
140: resource fabric 'Microsoft.Fabric/capacities@<fetch>' = {
141:   name: fabricName
142:   location: location
143:   sku: { name: '<confirm with user>', tier: 'Fabric' }
144:   properties: {
145:     administration: {
146:       members: [ '<admin-email>' ]    // ← Required, deployment fails without it
147:     }
148:   }
149: }
150: ```
151: 
152: ### Design Notes
153: 
154: - Only Capacity can be provisioned via Bicep
155: - Workspace, Lakehouse, Warehouse, etc. must be created manually in the portal
156: - Confirm admin email with the user (`ask_user`)
157: 
158: ### Required Confirmation Items When Adding in Phase 1
159: 
160: When Fabric is added during conversation, the following items must be confirmed via ask_user before updating the diagram:
161: 
162: - [ ] **SKU/Capacity**: F2, F4, F8, ... — Provide choices after fetching available SKUs from MS Docs
163: - [ ] **administration.members**: Admin email — Deployment fails without it
164: 
165: > Do not arbitrarily include sub-workloads (OneLake, data pipelines, Warehouse, etc.) that the user did not specify. Only Capacity can be provisioned via Bicep.
166: 
167: ---
168: 
169: ## 5. Azure Data Factory
170: 
171: ### Bicep Core Structure
172: 
173: ```bicep
174: resource adf 'Microsoft.DataFactory/factories@<fetch>' = {
175:   name: adfName
176:   location: location
177:   identity: { type: 'SystemAssigned' }
178:   properties: {
179:     publicNetworkAccess: 'Disabled'
180:   }
181: }
182: ```
183: 
184: ### Design Notes
185: 
186: - Self-hosted Integration Runtime requires manual setup outside Bicep
187: - Primarily used for on-premises data ingestion scenarios
188: - PE groupId: `dataFactory`
189: 
190: ---
191: 
192: ## 6. AML / AI Hub (MachineLearningServices)
193: 
194: ### When to Use
195: 
196: ```
197: Decision Rule:
198: ├─ General AI/RAG → Use Foundry (AIServices)
199: └─ ML training, open-source models needed → Consider AI Hub
200:     └─ Only when the user explicitly requests it
201: ```
202: 
203: ### Bicep Core Structure
204: 
205: ```bicep
206: resource hub 'Microsoft.MachineLearningServices/workspaces@<fetch>' = {
207:   name: hubName
208:   location: location
209:   kind: 'Hub'
210:   sku: { name: '<confirm with user>', tier: '<confirm with user>' }  // e.g., Basic/Basic — verify available SKUs in MS Docs
211:   identity: { type: 'SystemAssigned' }
212:   properties: {
213:     friendlyName: hubName
214:     storageAccount: storage.id
215:     keyVault: keyVault.id
216:     applicationInsights: appInsights.id    // Required for Hub
217:     publicNetworkAccess: 'Disabled'
218:   }
219: }
220: ```
221: 
222: ### AI Hub Dependencies
223: 
224: Additional resources needed when using Hub:
225: - Storage Account
226: - Key Vault
227: - Application Insights + Log Analytics Workspace
228: - Container Registry (optional)
229: 
230: ---
231: 
232: ## 7. Common AI/Data Architecture Combinations
233: 
234: ### RAG Chatbot
235: 
236: ```
237: Foundry (AIServices) + Project
238: ├── <chat-model> (chat)              — Confirmed after availability check in Phase 1
239: ├── <embedding-model> (embedding)    — Confirmed after availability check in Phase 1
240: ├── AI Search (vector + semantic)
241: ├── ADLS Gen2 (document store)
242: └── Key Vault (secrets)
243: + Full VNet/PE configuration
244: ```
245: 
246: ### Data Platform
247: 
248: ```
249: Fabric Capacity (analytics)
250: ├── ADLS Gen2 (data lake)
251: ├── ADF (ingestion)
252: └── Key Vault (secrets)
253: + VNet/PE configuration
254: ```
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/azure-architecture-autopilot/references/architecture-guidance-sources.md">
  1: # Architecture Guidance Sources (For Design Direction Decisions)
  2: 
  3: A source registry for using Azure official architecture guidance **only for design direction decisions**.
  4: 
  5: > **The URLs in this document are a list of sources for "where to look".**
  6: > Do not hardcode the contents of these URLs as fixed facts.
  7: > Do not use for SKU, API version, region, model availability, or PE mapping decisions — those are handled exclusively via `azure-dynamic-sources.md`.
  8: 
  9: ---
 10: 
 11: ## Purpose Separation
 12: 
 13: | Purpose | Document to Use | Decidable Items |
 14: |---------|----------------|-----------------|
 15: | **Design direction decisions** | This document (architecture-guidance-sources) | Architecture patterns, best practices, service combination direction, security boundary design |
 16: | **Deployment spec verification** | `azure-dynamic-sources.md` | API version, SKU, region, model availability, PE groupId, actual property values |
 17: 
 18: **What must NOT be decided using this document:**
 19: - API version
 20: - SKU names/pricing
 21: - Region availability
 22: - Model names/versions/deployment types
 23: - PE groupId / DNS Zone mapping
 24: - Specific values for resource properties
 25: 
 26: ---
 27: 
 28: ## Primary Sources
 29: 
 30: Targeted fetch targets for design direction decisions.
 31: 
 32: | ID | Document | URL | Purpose |
 33: |----|----------|-----|---------|
 34: | A1 | Azure Architecture Center | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/ | Hub — Entry point for finding domain-specific documents |
 35: | A2 | Well-Architected Framework | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/framework/ | Security/reliability/performance/cost/operations principles |
 36: | A3 | Cloud Adoption Framework / Landing Zone | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/ready/landing-zone/ | Enterprise governance, network topology, subscription structure |
 37: | A4 | Azure AI/ML Architecture | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/ai-ml/ | AI/ML workload reference architecture hub |
 38: | A5 | Basic Foundry Chat Reference Architecture | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/ai-ml/architecture/basic-azure-ai-foundry-chat | Basic Foundry-based chatbot structure |
 39: | A6 | Baseline AI Foundry Chat Reference Architecture | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/ai-ml/architecture/baseline-openai-e2e-chat | Foundry chatbot enterprise baseline (including network isolation) |
 40: | A7 | RAG Solution Design Guide | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/ai-ml/guide/rag/rag-solution-design-and-evaluation-guide | RAG pattern design guide |
 41: | A8 | Microsoft Fabric Overview | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/get-started/microsoft-fabric-overview | Fabric platform overview and workload understanding |
 42: | A9 | Fabric Governance / Adoption | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/guidance/fabric-adoption-roadmap-governance | Fabric governance, adoption roadmap |
 43: 
 44: ## Secondary Sources (awareness only)
 45: 
 46: Not direct fetch targets; referenced only for change awareness.
 47: 
 48: | Document | URL | Notes |
 49: |----------|-----|-------|
 50: | Azure Updates | https://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/updates/ | Service changes/new feature announcements. Not a targeted fetch target |
 51: 
 52: ---
 53: 
 54: ## Fetch Trigger — When to Query
 55: 
 56: Architecture guidance documents are **not queried on every request.** Only perform targeted fetch when the following triggers apply.
 57: 
 58: ### Trigger Conditions
 59: 
 60: 0. **When the user's workload type is identified in Phase 1 (automatic)**
 61:    - Pre-query the relevant workload's reference architecture to adjust question depth
 62:    - Triggers automatically even if the user doesn't mention "best practice" etc.
 63:    - Purpose: Reflect official architecture-based design decision points in questions, beyond SKU/region spec questions
 64: 1. **When the user requests design direction justification**
 65:    - Keywords such as "best practice", "reference architecture", "recommended structure", "baseline", "well-architected", "landing zone", "enterprise pattern"
 66: 2. **When architecture boundaries for a new service combination are ambiguous**
 67:    - Inter-service relationships that cannot be determined from existing reference files/service-gotchas
 68: 3. **When enterprise-level security/governance design is needed**
 69:    - Subscription structure, network topology, landing zone patterns
 70: 
 71: ### When Triggers Do Not Apply
 72: 
 73: - Simple resource creation (SKU/API version/region questions) → Use only `azure-dynamic-sources.md`
 74: - Service combinations already covered in domain-packs → Prioritize reference files
 75: - Bicep property value verification → `service-gotchas.md` or MS Docs Bicep reference
 76: 
 77: ---
 78: 
 79: ## Fetch Budget
 80: 
 81: | Scenario | Max Fetch Count |
 82: |----------|----------------|
 83: | Default (when trigger fires) | Architecture guidance documents **up to 2** |
 84: | Additional fetch allowed when | Conflicts between documents / core design uncertainty remains / user explicitly requests deeper justification |
 85: | Simple deployment spec questions | **0** (no architecture guidance queries) |
 86: 
 87: ---
 88: 
 89: ## Decision Rule by Question Type
 90: 
 91: | Question Type | Documents to Query | Design Decision Points to Extract | Documents NOT to Query |
 92: |--------------|-------------------|----------------------------------|----------------------|
 93: | RAG / chatbot / Foundry app | A5 or A6 + A7 | Network isolation level, authentication method (managed identity vs key), indexing strategy (push vs pull), monitoring scope | Do not traverse entire Architecture Center |
 94: | Enterprise security / governance / landing zone | A2 + A3 | Subscription structure, network topology (hub-spoke etc.), identity/governance model, security boundary | AI/ML domain documents not needed |
 95: | Fabric data platform | A8 + A9 | Capacity model (SKU selection criteria), governance level, data boundary (workspace separation etc.) | AI-related documents not needed |
 96: | Ambiguous service combination (unclear pattern) | A1 (find closest domain document from hub) + that document | Key design decision points identified from the document | Do not traverse all sub-documents |
 97: | Simple resource creation values (SKU/API/region) | No query | — | All architecture guidance |
 98: | General AI/ML architecture | A4 (hub) + closest reference architecture | Compute isolation, data boundary, model serving approach | Do not crawl entirely |
 99: 
100: ---
101: 
102: ## URL Fallback Rule
103: 
104: 1. Use `en-us` Learn URLs by default
105: 2. If a specific URL returns 404 / redirect / deprecated → Fall back to the parent hub page
106:    - Example: If A5 fails → Search for "foundry chat" keyword on A4 (AI/ML hub)
107: 3. If not found on the parent hub either → Search by title keyword on A1 (Architecture Center main)
108: 4. **Do not use the contents of a URL as fixed rules just because the URL exists**
109: 
110: ---
111: 
112: ## Full Traversal Prohibited
113: 
114: - Do not broadly traverse (crawl) Architecture Center sub-documents
115: - Only targeted fetch 1–2 related documents according to the decision rule by question type
116: - Even within fetched documents, only reference relevant sections; do not read the entire document
117: - Unlimited fetching, recursive link following, and sub-page enumeration are prohibited
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/azure-architecture-autopilot/references/azure-common-patterns.md">
  1: # Azure Common Patterns (Stable)
  2: 
  3: This file contains only **near-immutable patterns** that are repeated across Azure services.
  4: Dynamic information such as API version, SKU, and region is not included here → See `azure-dynamic-sources.md`.
  5: 
  6: ---
  7: 
  8: ## 1. Network Isolation Patterns
  9: 
 10: ### Private Endpoint 3-Component Set
 11: 
 12: All services using PE must have the 3-component set configured:
 13: 
 14: 1. **Private Endpoint** — Placed in pe-subnet
 15: 2. **Private DNS Zone** + **VNet Link** (`registrationEnabled: false`)
 16: 3. **DNS Zone Group** — Linked to PE
 17: 
 18: > If any one is missing, DNS resolution fails even with PE present, causing connection failure.
 19: 
 20: ### PE Subnet Required Settings
 21: 
 22: ```bicep
 23: resource peSubnet 'Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/subnets' = {
 24:   properties: {
 25:     addressPrefix: peSubnetPrefix              // ← CIDR as parameter — prevent existing network conflicts
 26:     privateEndpointNetworkPolicies: 'Disabled'  // ← Required. PE deployment fails without it
 27:   }
 28: }
 29: ```
 30: 
 31: ### publicNetworkAccess Pattern
 32: 
 33: Services using PE must include:
 34: ```bicep
 35: properties: {
 36:   publicNetworkAccess: 'Disabled'
 37:   networkAcls: {
 38:     defaultAction: 'Deny'
 39:   }
 40: }
 41: ```
 42: 
 43: ---
 44: 
 45: ## 2. Security Patterns
 46: 
 47: ### Key Vault
 48: 
 49: ```bicep
 50: properties: {
 51:   enableRbacAuthorization: true    // Do not use Access Policy method
 52:   enableSoftDelete: true
 53:   softDeleteRetentionInDays: 90
 54:   enablePurgeProtection: true
 55: }
 56: ```
 57: 
 58: ### Managed Identity
 59: 
 60: When AI services access other resources:
 61: ```bicep
 62: identity: {
 63:   type: 'SystemAssigned'  // or 'UserAssigned'
 64: }
 65: ```
 66: 
 67: ### Sensitive Information
 68: 
 69: - Use `@secure()` decorator
 70: - Do not store plaintext in `.bicepparam` files
 71: - Use Key Vault references
 72: 
 73: ---
 74: 
 75: ## 3. Naming Conventions (CAF-based)
 76: 
 77: ```
 78: rg-{project}-{env}          Resource Group
 79: vnet-{project}-{env}        Virtual Network
 80: st{project}{env}             Storage Account (no special characters, lowercase+numbers only)
 81: kv-{project}-{env}           Key Vault
 82: srch-{project}-{env}         AI Search
 83: foundry-{project}-{env}      Cognitive Services (Foundry)
 84: ```
 85: 
 86: > Name collision prevention: Recommend using `uniqueString(resourceGroup().id)`
 87: > ```bicep
 88: > param storageName string = 'st${uniqueString(resourceGroup().id)}'
 89: > ```
 90: 
 91: ---
 92: 
 93: ## 4. Bicep Module Structure
 94: 
 95: ```
 96: <project>/
 97: ├── main.bicep              # Orchestration — module calls + parameter passing
 98: ├── main.bicepparam         # Environment-specific values (excluding sensitive info)
 99: └── modules/
100:     ├── network.bicep           # VNet, Subnet
101:     ├── <service>.bicep         # Per-service modules
102:     ├── keyvault.bicep          # Key Vault
103:     └── private-endpoints.bicep # All PE + DNS Zone + VNet Link
104: ```
105: 
106: ### Dependency Management
107: 
108: ```bicep
109: // ✅ Correct: Implicit dependency via resource reference
110: resource project '...' = {
111:   properties: {
112:     parentId: foundry.id  // foundry reference → automatically deploys foundry first
113:   }
114: }
115: 
116: // ❌ Avoid: Explicit dependsOn (use only when necessary)
117: ```
118: 
119: ---
120: 
121: ## 5. PE Bicep Common Template
122: 
123: ```bicep
124: // ── Private Endpoint ──
125: resource pe 'Microsoft.Network/privateEndpoints@<fetch>' = {
126:   name: 'pe-${serviceName}'
127:   location: location
128:   properties: {
129:     subnet: { id: peSubnetId }
130:     privateLinkServiceConnections: [{
131:       name: 'pls-${serviceName}'
132:       properties: {
133:         privateLinkServiceId: serviceId
134:         groupIds: ['<groupId>']  // ← Varies by service. See service-gotchas.md
135:       }
136:     }]
137:   }
138: }
139: 
140: // ── Private DNS Zone ──
141: resource dnsZone 'Microsoft.Network/privateDnsZones@<fetch>' = {
142:   name: '<dnsZoneName>'  // ← Varies by service
143:   location: 'global'
144: }
145: 
146: // ── VNet Link ──
147: resource vnetLink 'Microsoft.Network/privateDnsZones/virtualNetworkLinks@<fetch>' = {
148:   parent: dnsZone
149:   name: '${dnsZone.name}-link'
150:   location: 'global'
151:   properties: {
152:     virtualNetwork: { id: vnetId }
153:     registrationEnabled: false  // ← Must be false
154:   }
155: }
156: 
157: // ── DNS Zone Group ──
158: resource dnsGroup 'Microsoft.Network/privateEndpoints/privateDnsZoneGroups@<fetch>' = {
159:   parent: pe
160:   name: 'default'
161:   properties: {
162:     privateDnsZoneConfigs: [{
163:       name: 'config'
164:       properties: { privateDnsZoneId: dnsZone.id }
165:     }]
166:   }
167: }
168: ```
169: 
170: > `@<fetch>`: Always verify the latest stable API version from MS Docs before deployment.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/azure-architecture-autopilot/references/azure-dynamic-sources.md">
 1: # Azure Dynamic Sources Registry
 2: 
 3: This file manages **only the sources (URLs) for frequently changing information**.
 4: Actual values (API version, SKU, region, etc.) are not recorded here.
 5: Always fetch the URLs below to verify the latest information before generating Bicep.
 6: 
 7: ---
 8: 
 9: ## 1. Bicep API Version (Always Must Fetch)
10: 
11: Per-service MS Docs Bicep reference. Verify the latest stable apiVersion from these URLs before use.
12: 
13: | Service | MS Docs URL |
14: |---------|-------------|
15: | CognitiveServices (Foundry/OpenAI) | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/templates/microsoft.cognitiveservices/accounts |
16: | AI Search | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/templates/microsoft.search/searchservices |
17: | Storage Account | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/templates/microsoft.storage/storageaccounts |
18: | Key Vault | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/templates/microsoft.keyvault/vaults |
19: | Virtual Network | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/templates/microsoft.network/virtualnetworks |
20: | Private Endpoints | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/templates/microsoft.network/privateendpoints |
21: | Private DNS Zones | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/templates/microsoft.network/privatednszones |
