  1: # Bicep Generator Agent
  2: 
  3: Receives the finalized architecture spec from Phase 1 and generates deployable Bicep templates.
  4: 
  5: ## Step 0: Verify Latest Specs (Required Before Bicep Generation)
  6: 
  7: Do not hardcode API versions in Bicep code.
  8: Always fetch the MS Docs Bicep reference for the services you intend to use and confirm the latest stable apiVersion before using it.
  9: 
 10: ### Verification Steps
 11: 1. Identify the list of services to be used
 12: 2. Fetch the MS Docs URL for each service (using the web_fetch tool)
 13: 3. Confirm the latest stable API version from the page
 14: 4. Write Bicep using that version
 15: 
 16: ### Model Deployment Availability Check (Required When Using Foundry/OpenAI Models)
 17: 
 18: Verify that the model name specified by the user is actually deployable in the target region **before generating Bicep**.
 19: Model availability varies by region and changes frequently — do not rely on static knowledge.
 20: 
 21: **Verification Methods (in priority order):**
 22: 1. Check the MS Docs model availability page: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/openai/concepts/models
 23: 2. Or query directly via Azure CLI:
 24:    ```powershell
 25:    az cognitiveservices account list-models --name "<FOUNDRY_NAME>" --resource-group "<RG_NAME>" -o table
 26:    ```
 27:    (When the Foundry resource already exists)
 28: 
 29: **If the model is not available in the target region:**
 30: - Inform the user and suggest available regions or alternative models
 31: - Do not substitute a different model or region without user approval
 32: 
 33: ### Per-Service MS Docs URLs
 34: 
 35: The full URL registry is in `references/azure-dynamic-sources.md`. Refer to this file when fetching.
 36: Reference files are located under the `.github/skills/azure-architecture-autopilot/` path.
 37: 
 38: > **Important**: Fetch directly from the URL using web_fetch to confirm the latest stable apiVersion. Do not blindly use hardcoded versions from reference files or previous conversations.
 39: 
 40: > **Always verify child resources too**: Check the API versions for child resources (accounts/projects, accounts/deployments, privateDnsZones/virtualNetworkLinks, privateEndpoints/privateDnsZoneGroups, etc.) from the parent resource page. Parent and child API versions may differ.
 41: 
 42: > **Same principle applies when errors/warnings occur**: If an API version–related error occurs during what-if or deployment, do not trust the version in the error message as the "latest version" and apply it directly. Always re-fetch the MS Docs URL to confirm the actual latest stable version before making corrections.
 43: 
 44: ---
 45: 
 46: ## Information Reference Principles (Stable vs Dynamic)
 47: 
 48: ### Always Fetch (Dynamic)
 49: - API version → Fetch from URLs in `azure-dynamic-sources.md`
 50: - Model availability (name, version, region) → Fetch
 51: - SKU list/pricing → Fetch
 52: - Region availability → Fetch
 53: 
 54: ### Reference First (Stable)
 55: - Required property patterns (`isHnsEnabled`, `allowProjectManagement`, etc.) → `service-gotchas.md`
 56: - PE groupId & DNS Zone mappings (major services) → `service-gotchas.md`
 57: - PE/security/naming common patterns → `azure-common-patterns.md`
 58: - AI/Data service configuration guide → `ai-data.md`
 59: 
 60: > If unsure about stable information, re-verify with MS Docs. But there is no need to fetch every time.
 61: 
 62: ---
 63: 
 64: ## Unknown Service Fallback Workflow
 65: 
 66: When the user requests a service not covered by the v1 scope (`ai-data.md`):
 67: 
 68: 1. **Notify the user**: "This service is outside the v1 default scope. It will be generated on a best-effort basis by referencing MS Docs."
 69: 2. **Fetch API version**: Construct the URL in the format `https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/templates/microsoft.{provider}/{resourceType}` and fetch
 70: 3. **Identify resource type/required properties**: Confirm the resource type and required properties from the fetched Docs
 71: 4. **Verify PE mapping**: Fetch `https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/private-link/private-endpoint-dns` to confirm groupId/DNS Zone
 72: 5. **Apply common patterns**: Apply security/network/naming patterns from `azure-common-patterns.md`
 73: 6. **Write Bicep**: Generate the module based on the above information
 74: 7. **Hand off to reviewer**: Validate compilation with `az bicep build`
 75: 
 76: ## Input Information
 77: 
 78: The following information must be finalized upon completion of Phase 1:
 79: 
 80: ```
 81: - services: [Service list + SKU]
 82: - networking: Whether private_endpoint is used
 83: - resource_group: Resource group name
 84: - location: Deployment location (confirmed with user in Phase 1)
 85: - subscription_id: Azure subscription ID
 86: ```
 87: 
 88: ## Output File Structure
 89: 
 90: ```
 91: <project-name>/
 92: ├── main.bicep              # Main orchestration — module calls and parameter passing
 93: ├── main.bicepparam         # Parameter file — environment-specific values, excluding sensitive info
 94: └── modules/
 95:     ├── network.bicep           # VNet, Subnet (including pe-subnet)
 96:     ├── ai.bicep                # AI services (configured per user requirements)
 97:     ├── storage.bicep           # ADLS Gen2 (isHnsEnabled: true required)
 98:     ├── fabric.bicep            # Microsoft Fabric Capacity (only when needed)
 99:     ├── keyvault.bicep          # Key Vault
100:     ├── monitoring.bicep        # Application Insights, Log Analytics (only needed for Hub-based configurations)
101:     └── private-endpoints.bicep # All PEs + Private DNS Zones + VNet Links + DNS Zone Groups
102: ```
103: 
104: ## Module Responsibilities
105: 
106: ### `network.bicep`
107: - VNet — CIDR received as a parameter (to avoid conflicts with existing address spaces in the customer environment)
108: - pe-subnet — `privateEndpointNetworkPolicies: 'Disabled'` required
109: - Additional subnets handled via parameters as needed
110: 
111: ### `ai.bicep`
112: - **Microsoft Foundry resource** (`Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts`, `kind: 'AIServices'`) — Top-level AI resource
113:   - `customSubDomainName: foundryName` required — **Cannot be changed after creation. If omitted, the resource must be deleted and recreated**
114:   - `identity: { type: 'SystemAssigned' }` required
115:   - `allowProjectManagement: true` required
116:   - Model deployment (`Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts/deployments`) — Performed at the Foundry resource level
117: - **⚠️ Foundry Project** (`Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts/projects`) — **Must be created as a child resource**
118:   - Resource type: `Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts/projects` (never create as a standalone `accounts` resource)
119:   - Use `parent: foundryAccount` in Bicep
120:   - Incorrect example: Creating a Project as a separate `kind: 'AIServices'` account → Not recognized in the portal
121:   - Correct example:
122:     ```bicep
123:     resource foundryProject 'Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts/projects@<apiVersion>' = {
124:       parent: foundryAccount
125:       name: 'project-${uniqueString(resourceGroup().id)}'
126:       location: location
127:       kind: 'AIServices'
128:       properties: {}
129:     }
130:     ```
131: - **Azure AI Search** — Semantic Ranking, vector search configuration
132: - Hub-based (`Microsoft.MachineLearningServices/workspaces`) should only be considered when the user explicitly requests it or when ML training/open-source models are needed. For standard AI/RAG workloads, Foundry (AIServices) is the default choice
133: 
134: **⛔ CognitiveServices Prohibited Properties:**
135: - `apiProperties.statisticsEnabled` — This property does not exist. Never use it. Causes `ApiPropertiesInvalid` error during deployment
136: - `apiProperties.qnaAzureSearchEndpointId` — QnA Maker only. Do not use with Foundry
137: - Do not arbitrarily add unvalidated properties to `properties.apiProperties`
138: 
139: ### `storage.bicep`
140: - ADLS Gen2: `isHnsEnabled: true` ← **Never omit this**
141: - Containers: raw, processed, curated (or as per requirements)
142: - `allowBlobPublicAccess: false`, `minimumTlsVersion: 'TLS1_2'`
143: 
144: ### `keyvault.bicep`
145: - `enableRbacAuthorization: true` (do not use access policy model)
146: - `enableSoftDelete: true`, `softDeleteRetentionInDays: 90`
147: - `enablePurgeProtection: true`
148: 
149: ### `monitoring.bicep`
150: - Log Analytics Workspace
151: - Application Insights (only needed for Hub-based configurations — not required for Foundry AIServices)
152: 
153: ### `private-endpoints.bicep`
154: - 3-piece set for each service:
155:   1. `Microsoft.Network/privateEndpoints` (placed in pe-subnet)
156:   2. `Microsoft.Network/privateDnsZones` + VNet Link (`registrationEnabled: false`)
157:   3. `Microsoft.Network/privateEndpoints/privateDnsZoneGroups`
158: - For per-service DNS Zone mappings, refer to `references/service-gotchas.md`
159: 
160: **⚠️ Foundry/AIServices PE DNS Rules:**
161: - PE groupId: `account`
162: - DNS Zone Group must include **2 zones**:
163:   1. `privatelink.cognitiveservices.azure.com`
164:   2. `privatelink.openai.azure.com`
165: - Including only one causes DNS resolution failure for OpenAI API calls → connection error
166: 
167: **⚠️ ADLS Gen2 (isHnsEnabled: true) PE Rules:**
168: - 2 PEs required:
169:   1. `blob` → `privatelink.blob.core.windows.net`
170:   2. `dfs` → `privatelink.dfs.core.windows.net`
171: - Without the DFS PE, Data Lake operations (file system creation, directory manipulation) will fail
172: 
173: ### `rbac.bicep` (or inline in main.bicep)
174: 
175: **⚠️ RBAC Role Assignment — Never Omit**
176: 
177: **Any service with a Managed Identity (`identity.type: 'SystemAssigned'`) must have RBAC role assignments created.**
178: Having an identity without role assignments causes inter-service authentication failures.
179: This is not optional — it is a **mandatory item**.
180: Omission will be reported as CRITICAL in Phase 3 review.
181: 
182: - Required RBAC mappings:
183: 
184: | Source Service | Target Service | Role | Role Definition ID |
185: |------------|-----------|------|-------------------|
186: | Foundry | Storage | `Storage Blob Data Contributor` | `ba92f5b4-2d11-453d-a403-e96b0029c9fe` |
187: | Foundry | AI Search | `Search Index Data Contributor` | `8ebe5a00-799e-43f5-93ac-243d3dce84a7` |
188: | Foundry | AI Search | `Search Service Contributor` | `7ca78c08-252a-4471-8644-bb5ff32d4ba0` |
189: | App Service | Key Vault | `Key Vault Secrets User` | `4633458b-17de-408a-b874-0445c86b69e6` |
190: | AKS (kubeletIdentity) | ACR | `AcrPull` | `7f951dda-4ed3-4680-a7ca-43fe172d538d` |
191: | Data Factory | Storage | `Storage Blob Data Contributor` | `ba92f5b4-2d11-453d-a403-e96b0029c9fe` |
192: | Data Factory | Key Vault | `Key Vault Secrets User` | `4633458b-17de-408a-b874-0445c86b69e6` |
193: | Databricks | Storage | `Storage Blob Data Contributor` | `ba92f5b4-2d11-453d-a403-e96b0029c9fe` |
194: 
195: > **AKS Special Rule**: AKS uses `identityProfile.kubeletidentity.objectId`, not `identity.principalId`.
196: 
197: ```bicep
198: // RBAC Example — Foundry → Storage Blob Data Contributor
199: resource foundryStorageRole 'Microsoft.Authorization/roleAssignments@2022-04-01' = {
200:   name: guid(storageAccount.id, foundry.id, 'ba92f5b4-2d11-453d-a403-e96b0029c9fe')
201:   scope: storageAccount
202:   properties: {
203:     roleDefinitionId: subscriptionResourceId('Microsoft.Authorization/roleDefinitions', 'ba92f5b4-2d11-453d-a403-e96b0029c9fe')
204:     principalId: foundry.identity.principalId
205:     principalType: 'ServicePrincipal'
206:   }
207: }
208: ```
209: 
210: ### SQL Server Rules
211: - **Password management**: Declare `@secure() param sqlAdminPassword string` in main.bicep and pass it to modules
212:   - Do not generate with `newGuid()` inside modules — the password changes on redeployment
213:   - Store as a Key Vault Secret so it can be retrieved after deployment
214: - **Authentication method**: Default to `administrators.azureADOnlyAuthentication: true`
215:   - Many organizational policies (MCAPS, etc.) block standalone SQL authentication
216:   - AAD-only authentication + Managed Identity is the most secure configuration
217: 
218: ### Network Secret Handling
219: - **VPN Gateway shared key**: `@secure() param vpnSharedKey string` — `@secure()` is mandatory
220: - Never include plaintext VPN keys in `.bicepparam` — provide at deployment time or use Key Vault reference
221: - This rule applies the same as for SQL passwords
222: - **Applies to**: VPN shared key, ExpressRoute authorization key, Wi-Fi PSK, and all other network secrets
223: - Module params must also include the `@secure()` decorator
224: 
225: ### ⚠️ Network Isolation Consistency Rules
226: - When setting `publicNetworkAccess: 'Disabled'`, you **must** also create the corresponding PE for that service
227: - Setting publicNetworkAccess to Disabled without a PE makes the service unreachable → unusable after deployment
228: - The Phase 3 reviewer must report this inconsistency as **CRITICAL**
229: - When an inconsistency is found: either add a PE module or revert publicNetworkAccess to Enabled
230: 
231: ## Mandatory Coding Principles
232: 
233: ### Naming Conventions
234: ```bicep
235: // Use uniqueString to prevent naming collisions — always required
236: param foundryName string = 'foundry-${uniqueString(resourceGroup().id)}'
237: param searchName string = 'srch-${uniqueString(resourceGroup().id)}'
238: param storageName string = 'st${uniqueString(resourceGroup().id)}'  // No special characters allowed
239: param keyVaultName string = 'kv-${uniqueString(resourceGroup().id)}'
240: ```
241: > **⚠️ Resources requiring `customSubDomainName` (Foundry, Cognitive Services, etc.) must include `uniqueString()`.**
242: > Static strings (e.g., `'my-rag-chatbot'`) may already be in use by another tenant, causing deployment failures.
243: > The same applies to Foundry Project names — `'project-${uniqueString(resourceGroup().id)}'`
244: 
245: ### Network Isolation
246: ```bicep
247: // Required for all services when using Private Endpoints
248: publicNetworkAccess: 'Disabled'
249: networkAcls: {
250:   defaultAction: 'Deny'
251:   ipRules: []
252:   virtualNetworkRules: []
253: }
254: ```
255: 
256: ### Dependency Management
257: ```bicep
258: // Use implicit dependencies via resource references instead of explicit dependsOn
259: resource aiProject '...' = {
260:   properties: {
261:     hubResourceId: aiHub.id  // Reference to aiHub → aiHub is automatically deployed first
262:   }
263: }
264: ```
265: 
266: ### Security
267: ```bicep
268: // Use Key Vault references for sensitive values — never store plaintext in parameter files
269: @secure()
270: param adminPassword string  // Do not put plaintext values in main.bicepparam
271: ```
272: 
273: ### Code Comments
274: ```bicep
275: // Microsoft Foundry resource — kind: 'AIServices'
276: // customSubDomainName: Required, globally unique. Cannot be changed after creation — if omitted, resource must be deleted and recreated
277: // allowProjectManagement: true is required or Foundry Project creation will fail
278: // Replace apiVersion with the latest version fetched in Step 0
279: resource foundry 'Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts@<version fetched in Step 0>' = {
280:   kind: 'AIServices'
281:   properties: {
282:     customSubDomainName: foundryName
283:     allowProjectManagement: true
284:     ...
285:   }
286: }
287: ```
288: 
289: ### ⚠️ Bicep Code Quality Validation (Required After Generation)
290: 
291: **Module Declaration Validation:**
292: - Verify that the `name:` property in each module block is not duplicated
293: - Correct example: `name: 'deploy-sql'`
294: - Incorrect example: `name: 'name: 'deploy-sql'` (duplicated name: → compilation error)
295: 
296: **Duplicate Property Prevention:**
297: - If the same property name appears more than once within a single resource block, it causes a compilation error
298: - Especially common in complex resources like VPN Gateway (`gatewayType`), Firewall, AKS, etc.
299: - Check for `BCP025: The property "xxx" is declared multiple times` in the `az bicep build` output
300: 
301: **`az bicep build` Must Be Run:**
302: - After generating all Bicep files, always run `az bicep build --file main.bicep`
303: - Fix errors and recompile
304: - Warnings (BCP081, etc.) can be ignored after verifying the API version in MS Docs
305: 
306: ## main.bicep Base Structure
307: 
308: ```bicep
309: // ============================================================
310: // Azure [Project Name] Infrastructure — main.bicep
311: // Generated: [Date]
312: // ============================================================
313: 
314: targetScope = 'resourceGroup'
315: 
316: // ── Common Parameters ─────────────────────────────────────
317: param location string   // Location confirmed in Phase 1 — do not hardcode
318: param projectPrefix string
319: param vnetAddressPrefix string    // ← Confirm with user. Prevent conflicts with existing networks
320: param peSubnetPrefix string       // ← PE-dedicated subnet CIDR within the VNet
321: 
322: // ── Network ───────────────────────────────────────────────
323: module network './modules/network.bicep' = {
324:   name: 'deploy-network'
325:   params: {
326:     location: location
327:     vnetAddressPrefix: vnetAddressPrefix
328:     peSubnetPrefix: peSubnetPrefix
329:   }
330: }
331: 
332: // ── AI/Data Services ──────────────────────────────────────
333: module ai './modules/ai.bicep' = {
334:   name: 'deploy-ai'
335:   params: {
336:     location: location
337:     // Add separate params if regions differ per service — verify available regions in MS Docs
338:   }
339:   dependsOn: [network]
340: }
341: 
342: // ── Storage ───────────────────────────────────────────────
343: module storage './modules/storage.bicep' = {
344:   name: 'deploy-storage'
345:   params: {
346:     location: location
347:   }
348: }
349: 
350: // ── Key Vault ─────────────────────────────────────────────
351: module keyVault './modules/keyvault.bicep' = {
352:   name: 'deploy-keyvault'
353:   params: {
354:     location: location
355:   }
356: }
357: 
358: // ── Private Endpoints (All Services) ──────────────────────
359: module privateEndpoints './modules/private-endpoints.bicep' = {
360:   name: 'deploy-private-endpoints'
361:   params: {
362:     location: location
363:     vnetId: network.outputs.vnetId
364:     peSubnetId: network.outputs.peSubnetId
365:     foundryId: ai.outputs.foundryId
366:     searchId: ai.outputs.searchId
367:     storageId: storage.outputs.storageId
368:     keyVaultId: keyVault.outputs.keyVaultId
369:   }
370: }
371: 
372: // ── Outputs ───────────────────────────────────────────────
373: output vnetId string = network.outputs.vnetId
374: output foundryEndpoint string = ai.outputs.foundryEndpoint
375: output searchEndpoint string = ai.outputs.searchEndpoint
376: ```
377: 
378: ## main.bicepparam Base Structure
379: 
380: ```bicep
381: using './main.bicep'
382: 
383: param location = '<Location confirmed in Phase 1>'
384: param projectPrefix = '<Project prefix>'
385: // Do not put sensitive values here — use Key Vault references
386: // Set regions after verifying per-service availability in MS Docs
387: ```
388: 
389: ### @secure() Parameter Handling
390: 
391: When a `.bicepparam` file contains a `using` directive, additional `--parameters` flags cannot be used with `az deployment`.
392: Therefore, `@secure()` parameters must follow these rules:
393: 
394: - **Set a default value when possible**: `@secure() param password string = newGuid()`
395: - **If user input is required for @secure() parameters**: Generate a JSON parameter file (`main.parameters.json`) alongside instead of using `.bicepparam`
396: - **Never do this**: Generate a command that uses `.bicepparam` and `--parameters key=value` simultaneously
397: 
398: ## Common Mistake Checklist
399: 
400: The full checklist is in `references/service-gotchas.md`. Key summary:
401: 
402: | Item | ❌ Incorrect | ✅ Correct |
403: |------|--------|----------|
404: | ADLS Gen2 | `isHnsEnabled` omitted | `isHnsEnabled: true` |
405: | PE Subnet | Policy not set | `privateEndpointNetworkPolicies: 'Disabled'` |
406: | PE Configuration | PE only created | PE + DNS Zone + VNet Link + DNS Zone Group |
407: | Foundry | `kind: 'OpenAI'` | `kind: 'AIServices'` + `allowProjectManagement: true` |
408: | Foundry | `customSubDomainName` omitted | `customSubDomainName: foundryName` — cannot be changed after creation |
409: | Foundry Project | Not created | Must always be created as a set with the Foundry resource |
410: | Hub Usage | Used for standard AI | Only when explicitly requested by user or ML/open-source models needed |
411: | Public Network | Not configured | `publicNetworkAccess: 'Disabled'` |
412: | Storage Name | Contains hyphens | Lowercase + digits only, `uniqueString()` recommended |
413: | API version | Copied from previous value | Fetch from MS Docs (Dynamic) |
414: | Region | Hardcoded | Parameter + verify availability in MS Docs (Dynamic) |
415: 
416: ## After Generation Is Complete
417: 
418: When Bicep generation is complete:
419: 1. Provide the user with a summary report of the generated file list and each file's role
420: 2. Immediately transition to Phase 3 (Bicep Reviewer)
421: 3. The reviewer proceeds with automated review and corrections following the `references/bicep-reviewer.md` guidelines
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/azure-architecture-autopilot/references/bicep-reviewer.md">
  1: # Bicep Reviewer Agent
  2: 
  3: Reviews generated Bicep code and automatically fixes any issues found.
  4: 
  5: ## Review Order
  6: 
  7: ### Step 1: Bicep Compilation (Run First)
  8: 
  9: Run actual Bicep compilation **before** the checklist. Do not declare "pass" based on visual inspection alone.
 10: 
 11: ```powershell
 12: az bicep build --file main.bicep 2>&1
 13: ```
 14: 
 15: Collect all WARNINGs and ERRORs from the compilation results. This is the foundational data for the review.
 16: 
 17: ### Step 2: Fix Compilation Errors/Warnings
 18: 
 19: Fix issues found in compilation results:
 20: - **ERROR** → Must fix and recompile
 21: - **WARNING** → Handle according to the criteria below
 22: 
 23: **🚨 WARNING Handling Criteria — Do Not Force Unnecessary Fixes:**
 24: 
 25: WARNINGs do not block deployment. Attempting to resolve warnings often introduces deployment errors, so use the following criteria:
 26: 
 27: | WARNING Type | Action | Reason |
 28: |---|---|---|
 29: | BCP081 (type not defined) | **Leave as-is** (if API version is the latest confirmed from MS Docs) | Local Bicep CLI type definitions are not yet updated. No impact on deployment |
 30: | BCP035 (missing property) | **Judge carefully** — Check MS Docs to verify if the property is actually required; if not, leave as-is | Adding properties can cause deployment failures due to compatibility issues (e.g., computeMode) |
 31: | BCP187 (sku/kind type unverified) | **Leave as-is** | Values confirmed from MS Docs will work correctly at deployment |
 32: | no-hardcoded-env-urls | **Leave as-is** | DNS Zone names inevitably require hardcoding |
 33: 
 34: **Never do the following:**
 35: - Downgrade API versions to resolve WARNINGs (maintain latest stable)
 36: - Add properties not confirmed in MS Docs to resolve WARNINGs
 37: - Force fixes targeting "zero warnings"
 38: 
 39: **Principle: Document WARNINGs in review results, but do not fix them if they don't block deployment.**
 40: 
 41: Common issues and responses:
 42: - BCP081 (type not defined) → API version is likely incorrect. Fetch MS Docs and update to the actual latest stable version
 43: - BCP036 (type mismatch) → Check property value casing and type, then fix
 44: - BCP037 (property not allowed) → Check MS Docs to verify if the property is supported in that API version
 45: - no-hardcoded-env-urls → Hardcoded URLs in DNS Zone names etc. are sometimes unavoidable in Bicep. Note in review results
 46: 
 47: ### Step 3: Checklist Review
 48: 
 49: Review the following items after compilation passes. See `references/service-gotchas.md` for full gotchas.
 50: 
 51: #### Critical (Must Fix)
 52: - [ ] Microsoft Foundry `customSubDomainName` setting exists — **Cannot be changed after creation; if missing, resource must be deleted and recreated**
 53: - [ ] When using Microsoft Foundry, **Foundry Project (`accounts/projects`) must exist** — Portal access unavailable without it
 54: - [ ] Microsoft Foundry `identity: { type: 'SystemAssigned' }` — Project creation fails without it
 55: - [ ] `publicNetworkAccess: 'Disabled'` — All services using PE
 56: - [ ] ADLS Gen2 `isHnsEnabled: true` — Without it, becomes regular Blob Storage
 57: - [ ] pe-subnet `privateEndpointNetworkPolicies: 'Disabled'` — PE creation fails without it
 58: - [ ] Private DNS Zone Group — Must exist for every PE
 59: - [ ] Key Vault `enablePurgeProtection: true`
 60: 
 61: #### High (Recommended Fix)
 62: - [ ] Storage `allowBlobPublicAccess: false`, `minimumTlsVersion: 'TLS1_2'`
 63: - [ ] Private DNS Zone VNet Link `registrationEnabled: false`
 64: - [ ] Resource types and kind values per service match `references/ai-data.md` or MS Docs
 65: - [ ] Model deployments: Order guaranteed (`dependsOn`)
 66: - [ ] No sensitive values in parameter files — **Remove immediately if found**
 67: 
 68: #### Medium (Recommended)
 69: - [ ] Resource name collision prevention using `uniqueString()`
 70: - [ ] Leverage implicit dependencies through resource references
 71: 
 72: ### Step 4: Hardcoding Regression Check (Prevent Dynamic Information Leakage)
 73: 
 74: Verify the following items are not hardcoded as literal values in the Bicep code:
 75: 
 76: #### Must Be Parameterized (No Hardcoding)
 77: - [ ] `location` — Literal region names (`'eastus'`, `'koreacentral'`, etc.) are not used directly; passed via `param location`
 78: - [ ] Model name/version — Not literals; use values confirmed in Phase 1 and validated for availability in Step 0
 79: - [ ] SKU — Use values confirmed with the user
 80: 
 81: #### Verify Dynamic Values Have Not Regressed Into References
 82: This is not directly within this review's scope, but if specific API versions, SKU lists, or region lists are hardcoded in code comments or parameter descriptions, remove them and replace with "Check MS Docs" guidance.
 83: 
 84: #### Decision Rule Violation Check
 85: - [ ] If `kind: 'OpenAI'` is used instead of Foundry → Change to `kind: 'AIServices'` unless the user explicitly requested it
 86: - [ ] If Hub (`MachineLearningServices`) is used for general AI/RAG → Change to Foundry unless the user explicitly requested it
 87: - [ ] If a standalone Azure OpenAI resource is used → Suggest reviewing Foundry usage unless the user explicitly requested it or Docs indicate it's necessary
 88: 
 89: ### Step 5: Recompile After Fixes
 90: 
 91: If any changes were made in Steps 2–4, run `az bicep build` again to verify no new errors were introduced.
 92: 
 93: ### Limitations of `az bicep build`
 94: 
 95: Compilation only validates syntax and types. The following items cannot be caught by compilation and are finally verified in Phase 4's `az deployment group what-if`:
 96: - Retired/unavailable SKU
 97: - Per-region service availability
 98: - Model name validity
 99: - Preview-only properties
100: - Service policy changes (quota, capacity, etc.)
101: 
102: State these limitations in the review results so the user understands the importance of the what-if step.
103: 
104: ### Step 6: Report Results
105: 
106: ```markdown
107: ## Bicep Code Review Results
108: 
109: **Compilation Result**: [PASS/WARNING N items]
110: **Checklist**: ✅ Passed X items / ⚠️ Warnings X items
111: **Hardcoding Check**: [PASS / N violations]
112: **Auto-fixed**: X items
113: 
114: ### Compilation Warnings (Remaining)
115: - [Warning content — including reason why it cannot be fixed]
116: 
117: ### Auto-fix Details
118: - [File:line number] Before → After (reason)
119: 
120: ### Hardcoding Violations (If Any)
121: - [File:line number] [Violation details] → [Fix method]
122: 
123: **Conclusion**: [Ready for deployment / Manual review required]
124: ```
125: 
126: ### Step 7: Phase 4 Transition — Reassurance Message Required
127: 
128: When asking whether to proceed to Phase 4 after passing code review, **always include a message to reassure the user**.
129: Users may feel uneasy about the word "deployment", so clearly communicate that what-if is a safe validation step.
130: 
131: ```
132: ask_user({
133:   question: "Code review passed! Ready to proceed to the next step?\n\n⚡ This does NOT deploy immediately:\n  1️⃣ What-if validation — Simulates what will be created (not a deployment, safe)\n  2️⃣ Preview diagram — Review the architecture to be deployed as a diagram\n  3️⃣ Final confirmation — Actual deployment only after you review the diagram and approve\n\nNothing will be deployed without your approval.",
134:   choices: [
135:     "Proceed to next step (what-if validation + preview diagram) (Recommended)",
136:     "Just give me the code, I'll deploy later"
137:   ]
138: })
139: ```
140: 
141: **Key points:**
142: - Always state "This does NOT deploy immediately"
143: - Explain the 3-step process: what-if → preview diagram → final confirmation
144: - Reassure with "Nothing will be deployed without your approval"
