<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/azure-devops-cli/references/long-comments-on-windows.md">
 1: # Posting Long Comments and Bodies on Windows
 2: 
 3: On Windows the `az` command resolves to `az.cmd`, a batch wrapper invoked by `cmd.exe`. The whole command line is capped at ~8191 characters, so a long `--discussion`, `--description`, or `--content` value can be silently truncated or fail. Detect the shell before composing a long argument and route accordingly. Skipping this is the most common reason the agent burns 3-5 turns falling back to raw token retrieval and REST calls.
 4: 
 5: ## Detect the shell first
 6: 
 7: | Environment | Signal | Action |
 8: |---|---|---|
 9: | PowerShell on Windows | `$IsWindows -eq $true` and `$PSVersionTable.PSVersion` is set | Use `azps.ps1` (see below) |
10: | PowerShell on macOS / Linux | `$IsWindows -eq $false` | Plain `az` is fine, no cmd.exe wrapper |
11: | bash / zsh / sh | `$BASH_VERSION` or `$ZSH_VERSION` set, or `uname` works | Plain `az` is fine, no cmd.exe wrapper |
12: | Windows `cmd.exe` | `%ComSpec%` ends in `cmd.exe`, no `$PSVersionTable` | Use `azps.ps1` if PowerShell is installed, otherwise see `az devops invoke` fallback below |
13: 
14: ## Option 1: `azps.ps1` (PowerShell on Windows)
15: 
16: `azps.ps1` ships with the Azure CLI installer and invokes the Python entry point directly. No `cmd.exe` length cap.
17: 
18: ```powershell
19: # Read the long body into a variable and pass it through. No quoting headaches.
20: $body = Get-Content -Raw .\comment.md
21: azps.ps1 boards work-item update --id 1234 --discussion $body
22: ```
23: 
24: ## Option 2: dedicated `--file-path` flag where Azure CLI offers one
25: 
26: Some commands have a native file flag and you should prefer it over any inline body:
27: 
28: - `az devops wiki page create` and `az devops wiki page update` take `--file-path` (with optional `--encoding`).
29: - Use it on any shell, including Windows.
30: 
31: ```bash
32: az devops wiki page create --path 'My page' --wiki myproject --file-path ./page.md --encoding utf-8
33: ```
34: 
35: ## Option 3: `az devops invoke` fallback
36: 
37: When no `--file-path` exists (work-item `--discussion`, PR `--description`) and you're not in PowerShell, post the body via the underlying REST API. `az devops invoke` runs inside the Python entry point, so it isn't subject to the `cmd.exe` cap either, and it takes the request body from a file with `--in-file`:
38: 
39: ```bash
40: # Post a long discussion comment to work item 1234.
41: # REST: POST /{project}/_apis/wit/workItems/{id}/comments?api-version=7.0-preview.3
42: az devops invoke \
43:   --area wit --resource comments \
44:   --route-parameters project={project} workItemId=1234 \
45:   --api-version 7.0-preview.3 \
46:   --http-method POST \
47:   --in-file ./comment.json
48: ```
49: 
50: Where `comment.json` is `{ "text": "<long markdown body>" }`. This is the universal escape hatch when neither `azps.ps1` nor `--file-path` is available. `az devops invoke` itself accepts `--in-file` natively.
51: 
52: ## Don't rely on `@<file>` for plain string args
53: 
54: The Azure CLI `@<file>` convention is documented for JSON parameters (see [the official quoting guide](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cli/azure/use-azure-cli-successfully-quoting)). It is not guaranteed to expand plain string args like `--discussion` or `--description`, so don't reach for it as a substitute for the three options above.
</file>

