<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/code-tour/references/examples.md">
  1: # Real-World CodeTour Examples
  2: 
  3: Reference this file when you want to see how real repos use CodeTour features.
  4: Each example is sourced from a public GitHub repo with a direct link to the `.tour` file.
  5: 
  6: ---
  7: 
  8: ## microsoft/codetour — Contributor orientation
  9: 
 10: **Tour file:** https://github.com/microsoft/codetour/blob/main/.tours/intro.tour
 11: **Persona:** New contributor
 12: **Steps:** ~5 · **Depth:** Standard
 13: 
 14: **What makes it good:**
 15: - Intro step with an embedded SVG architecture diagram (raw GitHub URL inside the description)
 16: - Rich markdown per step with emoji section headers (`### 🎥 Tour Player`)
 17: - Inline cross-file links inside descriptions: `[Gutter decorator](./src/player/decorator.ts)`
 18: - Uses the top-level `description` field as a subtitle for the tour itself
 19: 
 20: **Technique to copy:** Embed images and cross-links in descriptions to make them self-contained.
 21: 
 22: ```json
 23: {
 24:   "file": "src/player/index.ts",
 25:   "line": 436,
 26:   "description": "### 🎥 Tour Player\n\nThe CodeTour player ...\n\n![Architecture](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/.../overview.svg)\n\nSee also: [Gutter decorator](./src/player/decorator.ts)"
 27: }
 28: ```
 29: 
 30: ---
 31: 
 32: ## a11yproject/a11yproject.com — New contributor onboarding
 33: 
 34: **Tour file:** https://github.com/a11yproject/a11yproject.com/blob/main/.tours/code-tour.tour
 35: **Persona:** External contributor
 36: **Steps:** 26 · **Depth:** Deep
 37: 
 38: **What makes it good:**
 39: - Almost entirely `directory` steps — orients to every `src/` subdirectory without getting lost in files
 40: - Conversational, beginner-friendly tone throughout
 41: - `selection` on the opening step to highlight the exact entry in `package.json`
 42: - Closes with a genuine thank-you and call-to-action
 43: 
 44: **Technique to copy:** Use directory steps as the skeleton of an onboarding tour — they teach structure without requiring the author to explain every file.
 45: 
 46: ```json
 47: {
 48:   "directory": "src/_data",
 49:   "description": "This folder contains the **data files** for the site. Think of them as a lightweight database — YAML files that power the resource listings, posts index, and nav."
 50: }
 51: ```
 52: 
 53: ---
 54: 
 55: ## github/codespaces-codeql — The most technically complete example
 56: 
 57: **Tour file:** https://github.com/github/codespaces-codeql/blob/main/.tours/codeql-tutorial.tour
 58: **Persona:** Security engineer / concept learner
 59: **Steps:** 12 · **Depth:** Standard
 60: 
 61: **What makes it good:**
 62: - `isPrimary: true` — auto-launches when the Codespace opens
 63: - `commands` array to run real VS Code commands mid-tour: the tour literally executes `codeQL.runQuery` when the reader arrives at that step
 64: - `view` property to switch the sidebar panel (`"view": "codeQLDatabases"`)
 65: - `pattern` instead of `line` for resilient matching: `"pattern": "import tutorial.*"`
 66: - `selection` to highlight the exact `select` clause in a query file
 67: 
 68: **This is the canonical reference for `commands`, `view`, and `pattern`.**
 69: 
 70: ```json
 71: {
 72:   "file": "tutorial.ql",
 73:   "pattern": "import tutorial.*",
 74:   "view": "codeQLDatabases",
 75:   "commands": ["codeQL.setDefaultTourDatabase", "codeQL.runQuery"],
 76:   "title": "Run your first query",
 77:   "description": "Click the **▶ Run** button above. The results appear in the CodeQL Query Results panel."
 78: }
 79: ```
 80: 
 81: ---
 82: 
 83: ## github/codespaces-learn-with-me — Minimal interactive tutorial
 84: 
 85: **Tour file:** https://github.com/github/codespaces-learn-with-me/blob/main/.tours/main.tour
 86: **Persona:** Total beginner
 87: **Steps:** 4 · **Depth:** Quick
 88: 
 89: **What makes it good:**
 90: - Only 4 steps — proves that less is more for quick/vibecoder personas
 91: - `isPrimary: true` for auto-launch
 92: - Each step tells the reader to **do something** (edit a string, change a color) — not just read
 93: - Ends with a tangible outcome: "your page is live"
 94: 
 95: **Technique to copy:** For quick/vibecoder tours, cut mercilessly. Four steps that drive action beat twelve that explain everything.
 96: 
 97: ---
 98: 
 99: ## blackgirlbytes/copilot-todo-list — 28-step interactive tutorial
100: 
101: **Tour file:** https://github.com/blackgirlbytes/copilot-todo-list/blob/main/.tours/main.tour
102: **Persona:** Concept learner / hands-on tutorial
103: **Steps:** 28 · **Depth:** Deep
104: 
105: **What makes it good:**
106: - Uses **content-only checkpoint steps** (no `file` key) as progress milestones: "Check out your page! 🎉" and "Try it out!" between coding tasks
107: - Terminal inline commands in descriptions: `>> npm install uuid; npm install styled-components`
108: - Each file step shows the exact code the user should accept, in a markdown code fence, so they know the expected output
109: 
110: **Technique to copy:** Checkpoint steps (content-only, milestone title) break up long tours and give the reader a sense of progress.
111: 
112: ```json
113: {
114:   "title": "Check out your page! 🎉",
115:   "description": "Open the **Simple Browser** tab to see your to-do list. You should see all three tasks rendering from your data array.\n\nOnce you're happy with it, continue to add interactivity."
116: }
117: ```
118: 
119: ---
120: 
121: ## lucasjellema/cloudnative-on-oci-2021 — Multi-tour architecture series
122: 
123: **Tour files:**
124: - https://github.com/lucasjellema/cloudnative-on-oci-2021/blob/main/.tours/function-tweet-retriever.tour
125: - https://github.com/lucasjellema/cloudnative-on-oci-2021/blob/main/.tours/oci-and-infrastructure-as-code.tour
126: - https://github.com/lucasjellema/cloudnative-on-oci-2021/blob/main/.tours/build-and-deployment-pipeline-function-tweet-retriever.tour
127: 
128: **Persona:** Platform engineer / architect
129: **Steps:** 12 per tour · **Depth:** Standard
130: 
131: **What makes it good:**
132: - Three separate tours for three separate concerns (function code, IaC, CI/CD pipeline) — each standalone but linked via `nextTour`
133: - `selection` coordinates used heavily in Terraform files where a block (not a single line) is the point
134: - Steps include markdown links to official OCI documentation inline
135: - Designed to be browsed via `vscode.dev/github.com/...` without cloning
136: 
137: **Technique to copy:** For complex systems, write one tour per layer and chain them with `nextTour`. Don't try to cover infrastructure + application code + CI/CD in one tour.
138: 
139: ---
140: 
141: ## SeleniumHQ/selenium — Monorepo build system onboarding
142: 
143: **Tour files:**
144: - `.tours/bazel.tour` — Bazel workspace and build target orientation
145: - `.tours/building-and-testing-the-python-bindings.tour` — Python bindings BUILD.bazel walkthrough
146: 
147: **Persona:** External contributor (build system focus)
148: **Steps:** ~10 per tour
149: 
150: **What makes it good:**
151: - Targets a non-obvious entry point — not the product code but the build system
152: - Proves that "contributor onboarding" tours don't have to start with `main()` — they start with whatever is confusing about this specific repo
153: - Used in a large, mature OSS project at scale
154: 
155: ---
156: 
157: ## Technique quick-reference
158: 
159: | Feature | When to use | Real example |
160: |---------|-------------|-------------|
161: | `isPrimary: true` | Auto-launch tour when repo opens (Codespace, vscode.dev) | codespaces-learn-with-me, codespaces-codeql |
162: | `commands: [...]` | Run a VS Code command when reader arrives at this step | codespaces-codeql (`codeQL.runQuery`) |
163: | `view: "terminal"` | Switch VS Code sidebar/panel at this step | codespaces-codeql (`codeQLDatabases`) |
164: | `pattern: "regex"` | Match by line content, not number — use for volatile files | codespaces-codeql |
165: | `selection: {start, end}` | Highlight a block (function body, config section, type def) | a11yproject, oci-2021, codespaces-codeql |
166: | `directory: "path/"` | Orient to a folder without reading every file | a11yproject, codespaces-codeql |
167: | `uri: "https://..."` | Link to PR, issue, RFC, ADR, external doc | Any PR review tour |
168: | `nextTour: "Title"` | Chain tours in a series | oci-2021 (3-part series) |
169: | Checkpoint steps (content-only) | Progress milestones in long interactive tours | copilot-todo-list |
170: | `>> command` in description | Terminal inline command link in VS Code | copilot-todo-list |
171: | Embedded image in description | Architecture diagrams, screenshots | microsoft/codetour |
172: 
173: ---
174: 
175: ## Discover more real tours on GitHub
176: 
177: **Search all `.tour` files on GitHub:**
178: https://github.com/search?q=path%3A**%2F*.tour+&type=code
179: 
180: This search returns every `.tour` file committed to a public GitHub repo. Use it to:
181: - Find tours for repos in the same language/framework as the one you're working on
182: - Study how other authors handle the same personas or step types
183: - Look up how a specific field (`commands`, `selection`, `pattern`) is used in the wild
184: 
185: Filter by language or keyword to narrow results — e.g. add `language:TypeScript` or `fastapi` to the query.
186: 
187: ---
188: 
189: ## Further reading
190: 
191: - **DEV Community — "Onboard your codebase with CodeTour"**: https://dev.to/tobiastimm/onboard-your-codebase-with-codetour-2jc8
192: - **Coder Blog — "Onboard to new projects faster with CodeTour"**: https://coder.com/blog/onboard-to-new-projects-faster-with-codetour
193: - **Microsoft Tech Community — Educator Developer Blog**: https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/educatordeveloperblog/codetour-vscode-extension-allows-you-to-produce-interactive-guides-assessments-a/1274297
194: - **AMIS Technology Blog — vscode.dev + CodeTour**: https://technology.amis.nl/software-development/visual-studio-code-the-code-tours-extension-for-in-context-and-interactive-readme/
195: - **CodeTour GitHub Topics**: https://github.com/topics/codetour
