<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/code-tour/SKILL.md">
  1: ---
  2: name: code-tour
  3: description: >
  4:   Use this skill to create CodeTour .tour files — persona-targeted, step-by-step walkthroughs
  5:   that link to real files and line numbers. Trigger for: "create a tour", "make a code tour",
  6:   "generate a tour", "onboarding tour", "tour for this PR", "tour for this bug", "RCA tour",
  7:   "architecture tour", "explain how X works", "vibe check", "PR review tour",
  8:   "contributor guide", "help someone ramp up", or any request for a structured walkthrough
  9:   through code. Supports 20 developer personas (new joiner, bug fixer, architect, PR reviewer,
 10:   vibecoder, security reviewer, and more), all CodeTour step types (file/line, selection,
 11:   pattern, uri, commands, view), and tour-level fields (ref, isPrimary, nextTour).
 12:   Works with any repository in any language.
 13: ---
 14: 
 15: # Code Tour Skill
 16: 
 17: You are creating a **CodeTour** — a persona-targeted, step-by-step walkthrough of a codebase
 18: that links directly to files and line numbers. CodeTour files live in `.tours/` and work with
 19: the [VS Code CodeTour extension](https://github.com/microsoft/codetour).
 20: 
 21: Two scripts are bundled in `scripts/`:
 22: 
 23: - **`scripts/validate_tour.py`** — run after writing any tour. Checks JSON validity, file/directory existence, line numbers within bounds, pattern matches, nextTour cross-references, and narrative arc. Run it: `python ~/.agents/skills/code-tour/scripts/validate_tour.py .tours/<name>.tour --repo-root .`
 24: - **`scripts/generate_from_docs.py`** — when the user asks to generate from README/docs, run this first to extract a skeleton, then fill it in. Run it: `python ~/.agents/skills/code-tour/scripts/generate_from_docs.py --persona new-joiner --output .tours/skeleton.tour`
 25: 
 26: Two reference files are bundled:
 27: 
 28: - **`references/codetour-schema.json`** — the authoritative JSON schema. Read it to verify any field name or type. Every field you use must conform to it.
 29: - **`references/examples.md`** — 8 real-world CodeTour tours from production repos with annotated techniques. Read it when you want to see how a specific feature (`commands`, `selection`, `view`, `pattern`, `isPrimary`, multi-tour series) is used in practice.
 30: 
 31: ### Real-world `.tour` files on GitHub
 32: 
 33: These are confirmed production `.tour` files. Fetch one when you need a working example of a specific step type, tour-level field, or narrative structure — don't write from memory when the real thing is one fetch away.
 34: 
 35: Find more with the GitHub code search: https://github.com/search?q=path%3A**%2F*.tour+&type=code
 36: 
 37: #### By step type / technique demonstrated
 38: 
 39: | What to study | File URL |
 40: |---|---|
 41: | `directory` + `file+line` (contributor onboarding) | https://github.com/coder/code-server/blob/main/.tours/contributing.tour |
 42: | `selection` + `file+line` + intro content step (accessibility project) | https://github.com/a11yproject/a11yproject.com/blob/main/.tours/code-tour.tour |
 43: | Minimal tutorial — tight `file+line` narration for interactive learning | https://github.com/lostintangent/rock-paper-scissors/blob/master/main.tour |
 44: | Multi-tour repo with `nextTour` chaining (cloud native OCI walkthroughs) | https://github.com/lucasjellema/cloudnative-on-oci-2021/blob/main/.tours/introduction.tour |
 45: | `isPrimary: true` (marks the onboarding entry point) | https://github.com/nickvdyck/webbundlr/blob/main/.tours/getting-started.tour |
 46: | `pattern` instead of `line` (regex-anchored steps) | https://github.com/nickvdyck/webbundlr/blob/main/.tours/architecture.tour |
 47: 
 48: **Raw content tip:** Prefix `raw.githubusercontent.com` and drop `/blob/` for raw JSON access.
 49: 
 50: A great tour is not just annotated files. It is a **narrative** — a story told to a specific
 51: person about what matters, why it matters, and what to do next. Your goal is to write the tour
 52: that the right person would wish existed when they first opened this repo.
 53: 
 54: **CRITICAL: Only create `.tour` JSON files. Never create, modify, or scaffold any other files.**
 55: 
 56: ---
 57: 
 58: ## Step 1: Discover the repo
 59: 
 60: Before asking the user anything, explore the codebase:
 61: 
 62: - List the root directory, read the README, and check key config files
 63:   (package.json, pyproject.toml, go.mod, Cargo.toml, composer.json, etc.)
 64: - Identify the language(s), framework(s), and what the project does
 65: - Map the folder structure 1–2 levels deep
 66: - Find entry points: main files, index files, app bootstrapping
 67: - **Note which files actually exist** — every path you write in the tour must be real
 68: 
 69: If the repo is sparse or empty, say so and work with what exists.
 70: 
 71: **If the user says "generate from README" or "use the docs":** run the skeleton generator first, then fill in every `[TODO: ...]` by reading the actual files:
 72: 
 73: ```bash
 74: python skills/code-tour/scripts/generate_from_docs.py \
 75:   --persona new-joiner \
 76:   --output .tours/skeleton.tour
 77: ```
 78: 
 79: ### Entry points by language/framework
 80: 
 81: Don't read everything — start here, then follow imports.
 82: 
 83: | Stack | Entry points to read first |
 84: |-------|---------------------------|
 85: | **Node.js / TS** | `index.js/ts`, `server.js`, `app.js`, `src/main.ts`, `package.json` (scripts) |
 86: | **Python** | `main.py`, `app.py`, `__main__.py`, `manage.py` (Django), `app/__init__.py` (Flask/FastAPI) |
 87: | **Go** | `main.go`, `cmd/<name>/main.go`, `internal/` |
 88: | **Rust** | `src/main.rs`, `src/lib.rs`, `Cargo.toml` |
 89: | **Java / Kotlin** | `*Application.java`, `src/main/java/.../Main.java`, `build.gradle` |
 90: | **Ruby** | `config/application.rb`, `config/routes.rb`, `app/controllers/application_controller.rb` |
 91: | **PHP** | `index.php`, `public/index.php`, `bootstrap/app.php` (Laravel) |
 92: 
 93: ### Repo type variants — adjust focus accordingly
 94: 
 95: The same persona asks for different things depending on what kind of repo this is:
 96: 
 97: | Repo type | What to emphasize | Typical anchor files |
 98: |-----------|-------------------|----------------------|
 99: | **Service / API** | Request lifecycle, auth, error contracts | router, middleware, handler, schema |
100: | **Library / SDK** | Public API surface, extension points, versioning | index/exports, types, changelog |
101: | **CLI tool** | Command parsing, config loading, output formatting | main, commands/, config |
102: | **Monorepo** | Package boundaries, shared contracts, build graph | root package.json/pnpm-workspace, shared/, packages/ |
103: | **Framework** | Plugin system, lifecycle hooks, escape hatches | core/, plugins/, lifecycle |
104: | **Data pipeline** | Source → transform → sink, schema ownership | ingest/, transform/, schema/, dbt models |
105: | **Frontend app** | Component hierarchy, state management, routing | pages/, store/, router, api/ |
106: 
107: For **monorepos**: identify the 2–3 packages most relevant to the persona's goal. Don't try to tour everything — open the tour with a step that explains how to navigate the workspace, then stay focused.
108: 
109: ### Large repo strategy
110: 
111: For repos with 100+ files: don't try to read everything.
112: 
113: 1. Read entry points and the README first
114: 2. Build a mental model of the top 5–7 modules
115: 3. For the requested persona, identify the **2–3 modules that matter most** and read those deeply
116: 4. For modules you're not covering, mention them in the intro step as "out of scope for this tour"
117: 5. Use `directory` steps for areas you mapped but didn't read — they orient without requiring full knowledge
118: 
119: A focused 10-step tour of the right files beats a scattered 25-step tour of everything.
120: 
121: ---
122: 
123: ## Step 2: Read the intent — infer everything you can, ask only what you can't
124: 
125: **One message from the user should be enough.** Read their request and infer persona,
126: depth, and focus before asking anything.
127: 
128: ### Intent map
129: 
130: | User says | → Persona | → Depth | → Action |
131: |-----------|-----------|---------|----------|
132: | "tour for this PR" / "PR review" / "#123" | pr-reviewer | standard | Add `uri` step for the PR; use `ref` for the branch |
133: | "why did X break" / "RCA" / "incident" | rca-investigator | standard | Trace the failure causality chain |
134: | "debug X" / "bug tour" / "find the bug" | bug-fixer | standard | Entry → fault points → tests |
135: | "onboarding" / "new joiner" / "ramp up" | new-joiner | standard | Directories, setup, business context |
136: | "quick tour" / "vibe check" / "just the gist" | vibecoder | quick | 5–8 steps, fast path only |
137: | "explain how X works" / "feature tour" | feature-explainer | standard | UI → API → backend → storage |
138: | "architecture" / "tech lead" / "system design" | architect | deep | Boundaries, decisions, tradeoffs |
139: | "security" / "auth review" / "trust boundaries" | security-reviewer | standard | Auth flow, validation, sensitive sinks |
140: | "refactor" / "safe to extract?" | refactorer | standard | Seams, hidden deps, extraction order |
141: | "performance" / "bottlenecks" / "slow path" | performance-optimizer | standard | Hot path, N+1, I/O, caches |
142: | "contributor" / "open source onboarding" | external-contributor | quick | Safe areas, conventions, landmines |
143: | "concept" / "explain pattern X" | concept-learner | standard | Concept → implementation → rationale |
144: | "test coverage" / "where to add tests" | test-writer | standard | Contracts, seams, coverage gaps |
145: | "how do I call the API" | api-consumer | standard | Public surface, auth, error semantics |
146: 
147: **Infer silently:** persona, depth, focus area, whether to add `uri`/`ref`, `isPrimary`.
148: 
149: **Ask only if you genuinely can't infer:**
150: - "bug tour" but no bug described → ask for the bug description
151: - "feature tour" but no feature named → ask which feature
152: - "specific files" explicitly requested → honor them as required stops
153: 
154: Never ask about `nextTour`, `commands`, `when`, or `stepMarker` unless the user mentioned them.
155: 
156: ### PR tour recipe
157: 
158: For PR tours: set `"ref"` to the branch, open with a `uri` step for the PR, cover changed files first, then unchanged-but-critical files, close with a reviewer checklist.
159: 
160: ### User-provided customization — always honor these
161: 
162: | User says | What to do |
163: |-----------|-----------|
164: | "cover `src/auth.ts` and `config/db.yml`" | Those files are required stops |
165: | "pin to the `v2.3.0` tag" / "this commit: abc123" | Set `"ref": "v2.3.0"` |
166: | "link to PR #456" / pastes a URL | Add a `uri` step at the right narrative moment |
167: | "lead into the security tour when done" | Set `"nextTour": "Security Review"` |
168: | "make this the main onboarding tour" | Set `"isPrimary": true` |
169: | "open a terminal at this step" | Add `"commands": ["workbench.action.terminal.focus"]` |
170: | "deep" / "thorough" / "5 steps" / "quick" | Override depth accordingly |
171: 
172: ---
173: 
174: ## Step 3: Read the actual files — no exceptions
175: 
176: **Every file path and line number in the tour must be verified by reading the file.**
177: A tour pointing to the wrong file or a non-existent line is worse than no tour.
178: 
179: For every planned step:
180: 1. Read the file
181: 2. Find the exact line of the code you want to highlight
182: 3. Understand it well enough to explain it to the target persona
183: 
184: If a user-requested file doesn't exist, say so — don't silently substitute another.
185: 
186: ---
187: 
188: ## Step 4: Write the tour
189: 
190: Save to `.tours/<persona>-<focus>.tour`. Read `references/codetour-schema.json` for the
191: authoritative field list. Every field you use must appear in that schema.
192: 
193: ### Tour root
194: 
195: ```json
196: {
197:   "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
198:   "title": "Descriptive Title — Persona / Goal",
199:   "description": "One sentence: who this is for and what they'll understand after.",
200:   "ref": "main",
201:   "isPrimary": false,
202:   "nextTour": "Title of follow-up tour",
203:   "steps": []
204: }
205: ```
206: 
207: Omit any field that doesn't apply to this tour.
208: 
209: **`when`** — conditional display. A JavaScript expression evaluated at runtime. Only show this tour
210: if the condition is true. Useful for persona-specific auto-launching, or hiding advanced tours
211: until a simpler one is complete.
212: ```json
213: { "when": "workspaceFolders[0].name === 'api'" }
214: ```
215: 
216: **`stepMarker`** — embed step anchors directly in source code comments. When set, CodeTour
217: looks for `// <stepMarker>` comments in files and uses them as step positions instead of
218: (or alongside) line numbers. Useful for tours on actively changing code where line numbers
219: shift constantly. Example: set `"stepMarker": "CT"` and put `// CT` in the source file.
220: Don't suggest this unless the user asks — it requires editing source files, which is unusual.
221: 
222: ---
223: 
224: ### Step types — full reference
225: 
226: All step types: **content** (intro/closing, max 2), **directory**, **file+line** (workhorse), **selection** (code block), **pattern** (regex match), **uri** (external link), **view** (focus VS Code panel), **commands** (run VS Code commands).
227: 
228: > **Path rule:** `"file"` and `"directory"` must be relative to repo root. No absolute paths, no leading `./`.
229: 
230: ---
231: 
232: ### When to use each step type
233: 
234: | Situation | Step type |
235: |-----------|-----------|
236: | Tour intro or closing | content |
237: | "Here's what lives in this folder" | directory |
238: | One line tells the whole story | file + line |
239: | A function/class body is the point | selection |
240: | Line numbers shift, file is volatile | pattern |
241: | PR / issue / doc gives the "why" | uri |
242: | Reader should open terminal or explorer | view or commands |
243: 
244: ---
245: 
246: ### Step count calibration
247: 
248: Match steps to depth and persona. These are targets, not hard limits.
249: 
250: | Depth | Total steps | Core path steps | Notes |
251: |-------|-------------|-----------------|-------|
252: | Quick | 5–8 | 3–5 | Vibecoder, fast explorer — cut ruthlessly |
253: | Standard | 9–13 | 6–9 | Most personas — breadth + enough detail |
254: | Deep | 14–18 | 10–13 | Architect, RCA — every tradeoff surfaced |
255: 
256: Scale with repo size too. A 3-file CLI doesn't get 15 steps. A 200-file monolith shouldn't be squeezed into 5.
257: 
258: | Repo size | Recommended standard depth |
259: |-----------|---------------------------|
260: | Tiny (< 20 files) | 5–8 steps |
261: | Small (20–80 files) | 8–11 steps |
262: | Medium (80–300 files) | 10–13 steps |
263: | Large (300+ files) | 12–15 steps (scoped to relevant subsystem) |
264: 
265: ---
266: 
267: ### Writing excellent descriptions — the SMIG formula
268: 
269: Every description should answer four questions in order. You don't need four paragraphs — but every description needs all four elements, even briefly.
270: 
271: **S — Situation**: What is the reader looking at? One sentence grounding them in context.
272: **M — Mechanism**: How does this code work? What pattern, rule, or design is in play?
273: **I — Implication**: Why does this matter for *this persona's goal specifically*?
274: **G — Gotcha**: What would a smart person get wrong here? What's non-obvious, fragile, or surprising?
275: 
276: Descriptions should tell the reader something they couldn't learn by reading the file themselves. Name the pattern, explain the design decision, flag failure modes, and cross-reference related context.
277: 
278: ---
279: 
280: ## Narrative arc — every tour, every persona
281: 
282: 1. **Orientation** — **must be a `file` or `directory` step, never content-only.**
283:    Use `"file": "README.md", "line": 1` or `"directory": "src"` and put your welcome text in the description.
284:    A content-only first step (no `file`, `directory`, or `uri`) renders as a blank page in VS Code CodeTour — this is a known VS Code extension behaviour, not configurable.
285: 
286: 2. **High-level map** (1–3 directory or uri steps) — major modules and how they relate.
287:    Not every folder — just what this persona needs to know.
288: 
289: 3. **Core path** (file/line, selection, pattern, uri steps) — the specific code that matters.
290:    This is the heart of the tour. Read and narrate. Don't skim.
291: 
292: 4. **Closing** (content) — what the reader now understands, what they can do next,
293:    2–3 suggested follow-up tours. If `nextTour` is set, reference it by name here.
294: 
295: ### Closing steps
296: 
297: Don't summarize — the reader just read it. Instead, tell them what they can now *do*, what to avoid, and suggest 2-3 follow-up tours.
298: 
299: ---
300: 
301: ## The 20 personas
302: 
303: | Persona | Goal | Must cover | Avoid |
304: |---------|------|------------|-------|
305: | **Vibecoder** | Get the vibe fast | Entry point, request flow, main modules. Max 8 steps. | Deep dives, edge cases |
306: | **New joiner** | Structured ramp-up | Directories, setup, business context, service boundaries. | Advanced internals |
307: | **Bug fixer** | Root cause fast | User action → trigger → fault points. Repro hints + test locations. | Architecture tours |
308: | **RCA investigator** | Why did it fail | Causality chain, side effects, race conditions, observability. | Happy path |
309: | **Feature explainer** | One feature end-to-end | UI → API → backend → storage. Feature flags, edge cases. | Unrelated features |
310: | **PR reviewer** | Review the change correctly | Change story, invariants, risky areas, reviewer checklist. URI step for PR. | Unrelated context |
311: | **Security reviewer** | Trust boundaries | Auth flow, input validation, secret handling, sensitive sinks. | Unrelated business logic |
312: | **Refactorer** | Safe restructuring | Seams, hidden deps, coupling hotspots, safe extraction order. | Feature explanations |
313: | **External contributor** | Contribute without breaking | Safe areas, code style, architecture landmines. | Deep internals |
314: | **Tech lead / architect** | Shape and rationale | Module boundaries, design tradeoffs, risk hotspots. | Line-by-line walkthroughs |
315: 
316: ---
317: 
318: ## Designing a tour series
319: 
320: When a codebase is complex enough that one tour can't cover it well, design a series.
321: The `nextTour` field chains them: when the reader finishes one tour, VS Code offers to
322: launch the next automatically.
323: 
324: **Plan the series before writing any tour.** A good series has:
325: - A clear escalation path (broad → narrow, orientation → deep-dive)
326: - No duplicate steps between tours
327: - Each tour standalone enough to be useful on its own
328: 
329: Set `nextTour` in each tour to the `title` of the next one (must match exactly). Each tour should be standalone enough to be useful on its own.
330: 
331: ---
332: 
333: ## What CodeTour cannot do
334: 
335: If asked for any of these, say clearly that it's not supported — do not suggest a workaround that doesn't exist:
336: 
337: | Request | Reality |
338: |---|---|
339: | **Auto-advance to next step after X seconds** | Not supported. Navigation is always manual — the reader clicks Next. There is no timer, delay, or autoplay step mechanic in CodeTour. |
340: | **Embed a video or GIF in a step** | Not supported. Descriptions are Markdown text only. |
341: | **Run arbitrary shell commands** | Not supported. `commands` only executes VS Code commands (e.g. `workbench.action.terminal.focus`), not shell commands. |
342: | **Branch / conditional next step** | Not supported. Tours are linear. `when` controls whether a tour is shown, not which step follows which. |
343: | **Show a step without opening a file** | Partially — content-only steps work, but step 1 must have a `file` or `directory` anchor or VS Code shows a blank page. |
344: 
345: ---
346: 
347: ## Anti-patterns
348: 
349: | Anti-pattern | Fix |
350: |---|---|
351: | **File listing** — visiting files with "this file contains..." | Tell a story; each step should depend on the previous one |
352: | **Generic descriptions** | Name the specific pattern/gotcha unique to *this* codebase |
353: | **Line number guessing** | Never write a line number you didn't verify by reading the file |
354: | **Ignoring the persona** | Cut every step that doesn't serve their specific goal |
355: | **Hallucinated files** | If a file doesn't exist, skip the step |
356: 
357: ---
358: 
359: ## Quality checklist — verify before writing the file
360: 
361: - [ ] Every `file` path is **relative to the repo root** (no leading `/` or `./`)
362: - [ ] Every `file` path read and confirmed to exist
363: - [ ] Every `line` number verified by reading the file (not guessed)
364: - [ ] Every `directory` is **relative to the repo root** and confirmed to exist
365: - [ ] Every `pattern` regex would match a real line in the file
366: - [ ] Every `uri` is a complete, real URL (https://...)
367: - [ ] `ref` is a real branch/tag/commit if set
368: - [ ] `nextTour` exactly matches the `title` of another `.tour` file if set
369: - [ ] Only `.tour` JSON files created — no source code touched
370: - [ ] First step has a `file` or `directory` anchor (content-only first step = blank page in VS Code)
371: - [ ] Tour ends with a closing content step that tells the reader what they can *do* next
372: - [ ] Every description answers SMIG — Situation, Mechanism, Implication, Gotcha
373: - [ ] Persona's priorities drive step selection (cut everything that doesn't serve their goal)
374: - [ ] Step count matches requested depth and repo size (see calibration table)
375: - [ ] At most 2 content-only steps (intro + closing)
376: - [ ] All fields conform to `references/codetour-schema.json`
377: 
378: ---
379: 
380: ## Step 5: Validate the tour
381: 
382: **Always run the validator immediately after writing the tour file. Do not skip this step.**
383: 
384: ```bash
385: python ~/.agents/skills/code-tour/scripts/validate_tour.py .tours/<name>.tour --repo-root .
386: ```
387: 
388: The validator checks:
389: - JSON validity
390: - Every `file` path exists and every `line` is within file bounds
391: - Every `directory` exists
392: - Every `pattern` regex compiles and matches at least one line in the file
393: - Every `uri` starts with `https://`
394: - `nextTour` matches an existing tour title in `.tours/`
395: - Content-only step count (warns if > 2)
396: - Narrative arc (warns if no orientation or closing step)
397: 
398: **Fix every error before proceeding.** Re-run until the validator reports ✓ or only warnings. Warnings are advisory — use your judgment. Do not show the user the tour until validation passes.
399: 
400: **Common VS Code issues:** Content-only first step renders blank (anchor to file/directory instead). Absolute or `./`-prefixed paths silently fail. Out-of-bounds line numbers scroll nowhere.
401: 
402: If you can't run scripts, manually verify: step 1 has `file`/`directory`, all paths exist, all line numbers are in bounds, `nextTour` matches exactly.
403: 
404: **Autoplay:** `isPrimary: true` + `.vscode/settings.json` with `{ "codetour.promptForPrimaryTour": true }` prompts on repo open. Omit `ref` for tours that should appear on any branch.
405: 
406: **Share:** For public repos, users can open tours at `https://vscode.dev/github.com/<owner>/<repo>` with no install.
407: 
408: ---
409: 
410: ## Step 6: Summarize
411: 
412: After writing the tour, tell the user:
413: - File path (`.tours/<name>.tour`)
414: - One-paragraph summary of what the tour covers and who it's for
415: - The `vscode.dev` URL if the repo is public (so they can share it immediately)
416: - 2–3 suggested follow-up tours (or the next tour in the series if one was planned)
417: - Any user-requested files that didn't exist (be explicit — don't quietly substitute)
418: 
419: ---
420: 
421: ## File naming
422: 
423: `<persona>-<focus>.tour` — kebab-case, communicates both:
424: ```
425: onboarding-new-joiner.tour
426: bug-fixer-payment-flow.tour
427: architect-overview.tour
428: vibecoder-quickstart.tour
429: pr-review-auth-refactor.tour
430: security-auth-boundaries.tour
431: concept-dependency-injection.tour
432: rca-login-outage.tour
433: ```
</file>

