  5: 
  6: # ASP.NET .NET Framework Containerization Prompt
  7: 
  8: Containerize the ASP.NET (.NET Framework) project specified in the containerization settings below, focusing **exclusively** on changes required for the application to run in a Windows Docker container. Containerization should consider all settings specified here.
  9: 
 10: **REMEMBER:** This is a .NET Framework application, not .NET Core. The containerization process will be different from that of a .NET Core application.
 11: 
 12: ## Containerization Settings
 13: 
 14: This section of the prompt contains the specific settings and configurations required for containerizing the ASP.NET (.NET Framework) application. Prior to running this prompt, ensure that the settings are filled out with the necessary information. Note that in many cases, only the first few settings are required. Later settings can be left as defaults if they do not apply to the project being containerized.
 15: 
 16: Any settings that are not specified will be set to default values. The default values are provided in `[square brackets]`.
 17: 
 18: ### Basic Project Information
 19: 1. Project to containerize: 
 20:    - `[ProjectName (provide path to .csproj file)]`
 21: 
 22: 2. Windows Server SKU to use:
 23:    - `[Windows Server Core (Default) or Windows Server Full]`
 24: 
 25: 3. Windows Server version to use:
 26:    - `[2022, 2019, or 2016 (Default 2022)]`
 27: 
 28: 4. Custom base image for the build stage of the Docker image ("None" to use standard Microsoft base image):
 29:    - `[Specify base image to use for build stage (Default None)]`
 30: 
 31: 5. Custom base image for the run stage of the Docker image ("None" to use standard Microsoft base image):
 32:    - `[Specify base image to use for run stage (Default None)]`   
 33: 
 34: ### Container Configuration
 35: 1. Ports that must be exposed in the container image:
 36:    - Primary HTTP port: `[e.g., 80]`
 37:    - Additional ports: `[List any additional ports, or "None"]`
 38: 
 39: 2. User account the container should run as:
 40:    - `[User account, or default to "ContainerUser"]`
 41: 
 42: 3. IIS settings that must be configured in the container image:
 43:    - `[List any specific IIS settings, or "None"]`
 44: 
 45: ### Build configuration
 46: 1. Custom build steps that must be performed before building the container image:
 47:    - `[List any specific build steps, or "None"]`
 48: 
 49: 2. Custom build steps that must be performed after building the container image:
 50:    - `[List any specific build steps, or "None"]`
 51: 
 52: ### Dependencies
 53: 1. .NET assemblies that should be registered in the GAC in the container image:
 54:    - `[Assembly name and version, or "None"]`
 55: 
 56: 2. MSIs that must be copied to the container image and installed:
 57:    - `[MSI names and versions, or "None"]`
 58: 
 59: 3. COM components that must be registered in the container image:
 60:    - `[COM component names, or "None"]`
 61: 
 62: ### System Configuration
 63: 1. Registry keys and values that must be added to the container image:
 64:    - `[Registry paths and values, or "None"]`
 65: 
 66: 2. Environment variables that must be set in the container image:
 67:    - `[Variable names and values, or "Use defaults"]`
 68: 
 69: 3. Windows Server roles and features that must be installed in the container image:
 70:    - `[Role/feature names, or "None"]`
 71: 
 72: ### File System
 73: 1. Files/directories that need to be copied to the container image:
 74:    - `[Paths relative to project root, or "None"]`
 75:    - Target location in container: `[Container paths, or "Not applicable"]`
 76: 
 77: 2. Files/directories to exclude from containerization:
 78:    - `[Paths to exclude, or "None"]`
 79: 
 80: ### .dockerignore Configuration
 81: 1. Patterns to include in the `.dockerignore` file (.dockerignore will already have common defaults; these are additional patterns):
 82:    - Additional patterns: `[List any additional patterns, or "None"]`
 83: 
 84: ### Health Check Configuration
 85: 1. Health check endpoint:
 86:    - `[Health check URL path, or "None"]`
 87: 
 88: 2. Health check interval and timeout:
 89:    - `[Interval and timeout values, or "Use defaults"]`
 90: 
 91: ### Additional Instructions
 92: 1. Other instructions that must be followed to containerize the project:
 93:    - `[Specific requirements, or "None"]`
 94: 
 95: 2. Known issues to address:
 96:    - `[Describe any known issues, or "None"]`
 97: 
 98: ## Scope
 99: 
100: - ✅ App configuration modification to ensure config builders are used to read app settings and connection strings from the environment variables
101: - ✅ Dockerfile creation and configuration for an ASP.NET application
102: - ✅ Specifying multiple stages in the Dockerfile to build/publish the application and copy the output to the final image
103: - ✅ Configuration of Windows container platform compatibility (Windows Server Core or Full)
104: - ✅ Proper handling of dependencies (GAC assemblies, MSIs, COM components)
105: - ❌ No infrastructure setup (assumed to be handled separately)
106: - ❌ No code changes beyond those required for containerization
107: 
108: ## Execution Process
109: 
110: 1. Review the containerization settings above to understand the containerization requirements
111: 2. Create a `progress.md` file to track changes with check marks
112: 3. Determine the .NET Framework version from the project's .csproj file by checking the `TargetFrameworkVersion` element
113: 4. Select the appropriate Windows Server container image based on:
114:    - The .NET Framework version detected from the project
115:    - The Windows Server SKU specified in containerization settings (Core or Full)
116:    - The Windows Server version specified in containerization settings (2016, 2019, or 2022)
117:    - Windows Server Core tags can be found at: https://github.com/microsoft/dotnet-framework-docker/blob/main/README.aspnet.md#full-tag-listing
118: 5. Ensure that required NuGet packages are installed. **DO NOT** install these if they are missing. If they are not installed, the user must install them manually. If they are not installed, pause executing this prompt and ask the user to install them using the Visual Studio NuGet Package Manager or Visual Studio package manager console. The following packages are required:
119:    - `Microsoft.Configuration.ConfigurationBuilders.Environment`
120: 6. Modify the `web.config` file to add configuration builders section and settings to read app settings and connection strings from environment variables:
121:    - Add ConfigBuilders section in configSections
122:    - Add configBuilders section in the root
123:    - Configure EnvironmentConfigBuilder for both appSettings and connectionStrings
124:    - Example pattern:
125:      ```xml
126:      <configSections>
127:        <section name="configBuilders" type="System.Configuration.ConfigurationBuildersSection, System.Configuration, Version=4.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=b03f5f7f11d50a3a" restartOnExternalChanges="false" requirePermission="false" />
128:      </configSections>
129:      <configBuilders>
130:        <builders>
131:          <add name="Environment" type="Microsoft.Configuration.ConfigurationBuilders.EnvironmentConfigBuilder, Microsoft.Configuration.ConfigurationBuilders.Environment" />
132:        </builders>
133:      </configBuilders>
134:      <appSettings configBuilders="Environment">
135:        <!-- existing app settings -->
136:      </appSettings>
137:      <connectionStrings configBuilders="Environment">
138:        <!-- existing connection strings -->
139:      </connectionStrings>
140:      ```
141: 7. Create a `LogMonitorConfig.json` file in the folder where the Dockerfile will be created by copying the reference `LogMonitorConfig.json` file at the end of this prompt. The file's contents **MUST NOT** not be modified and should match the reference content exactly unless instructions in containerization settings specify otherwise.
142:    - In particular, make sure the level of issues to be logged is not changed as using `Information` level for EventLog sources will cause unnecessary noise.
143: 8. Create a Dockerfile in the root of the project directory to containerize the application
144:    - The Dockerfile should use multiple stages:
145:      - Build stage: Use a Windows Server Core image to build the application
146:        - The build stage MUST use a `mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/framework/sdk` base image unless a custom base image is specified in the settings file
147:        - Copy sln, csproj, and packages.config files first
148:        - Copy NuGet.config if one exists and configure any private feeds
149:        - Restore NuGet packages       
150:        - Then, copy the rest of the source code and build and publish the application to C:\publish using MSBuild
151:      - Final stage: Use the selected Windows Server image to run the application
152:        - The final stage MUST use a `mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/framework/aspnet` base image unless a custom base image is specified in the settings file
153:        - Copy the `LogMonitorConfig.json` file to a directory in the container (e.g., C:\LogMonitor)
154:        - Download LogMonitor.exe from the Microsoft repository to the same directory
155:            - The correct LogMonitor.exe URL is: https://github.com/microsoft/windows-container-tools/releases/download/v2.1.1/LogMonitor.exe
156:        - Set the working directory to C:\inetpub\wwwroot
157:        - Copy the published output from the build stage (in C:\publish) to the final image
158:        - Set the container's entry point to run LogMonitor.exe with ServiceMonitor.exe to monitor the IIS service
159:            - `ENTRYPOINT [ "C:\\LogMonitor\\LogMonitor.exe", "C:\\ServiceMonitor.exe", "w3svc" ]`
160:    - Be sure to consider all requirements in the containerization settings:
161:      - Windows Server SKU and version
162:      - Exposed ports
163:      - User account for container
164:      - IIS settings
165:      - GAC assembly registration
166:      - MSI installation
167:      - COM component registration
168:      - Registry keys
169:      - Environment variables
170:      - Windows roles and features
171:      - File/directory copying
172:    - Model the Dockerfile after the example provided at the end of this prompt, but ensure it is customized to the specific project requirements and settings.
173:    - **IMPORTANT:** Use a Windows Server Core base image unless the user has **specifically requested** a full Windows Server image in the settings file
174: 9. Create a `.dockerignore` file in the root of the project directory to exclude unnecessary files from the Docker image. The `.dockerignore` file **MUST** include at least the following elements as well as additional patterns as specified in the containerization settings:
175:    - packages/
176:    - bin/
177:    - obj/
178:    - .dockerignore
179:    - Dockerfile
180:    - .git/
181:    - .github/
182:    - .vs/
183:    - .vscode/
184:    - **/node_modules/
185:    - *.user
186:    - *.suo
187:    - **/.DS_Store
188:    - **/Thumbs.db
189:    - Any additional patterns specified in the containerization settings
190: 10. Configure health checks if specified in the settings:
191:    - Add HEALTHCHECK instruction to Dockerfile if health check endpoint is provided
192: 11. Add the dockerfile to the project by adding the following item to the project file: `<None Include="Dockerfile" />`
193: 12. Mark tasks as completed: [ ] → [✓]
194: 13. Continue until all tasks are complete and Docker build succeeds
195: 
196: ## Build and Runtime Verification
197: 
198: confirm that Docker build succeeds once the Dockerfile is completed. Use the following command to build the Docker image:
199: 
200: ```bash
201: docker build -t aspnet-app:latest .
202: ```
203: 
204: If the build fails, review the error messages and make necessary adjustments to the Dockerfile or project configuration. Report success/failure.
205: 
206: ## Progress Tracking
207: 
208: Maintain a `progress.md` file with the following structure:
209: ```markdown
210: # Containerization Progress
211: 
212: ## Environment Detection
213: - [ ] .NET Framework version detection (version: ___)
214: - [ ] Windows Server SKU selection (SKU: ___)
215: - [ ] Windows Server version selection (Version: ___)
216: 
217: ## Configuration Changes
218: - [ ] Web.config modifications for configuration builders
219: - [ ] NuGet package source configuration (if applicable)
220: - [ ] Copy LogMonitorConfig.json and adjust if required by settings
221: 
222: ## Containerization
223: - [ ] Dockerfile creation
224: - [ ] .dockerignore file creation
225: - [ ] Build stage created with SDK image
226: - [ ] sln, csproj, packages.config, and (if applicable) NuGet.config copied for package restore
227: - [ ] Runtime stage created with runtime image
228: - [ ] Non-root user configuration
229: - [ ] Dependency handling (GAC, MSI, COM, registry, additional files, etc.)
230: - [ ] Health check configuration (if applicable)
231: - [ ] Special requirements implementation
232: 
233: ## Verification
234: - [ ] Review containerization settings and make sure that all requirements are met
235: - [ ] Docker build success
236: ```
237: 
238: Do not pause for confirmation between steps. Continue methodically until the application has been containerized and Docker build succeeds.
239: 
240: **YOU ARE NOT DONE UNTIL ALL CHECKBOXES ARE MARKED!** This includes building the Docker image successfully and addressing any issues that arise during the build process.
241: 
242: ## Reference Materials
243: 
244: ### Example Dockerfile
245: 
246: An example Dockerfile for an ASP.NET (.NET Framework) application using a Windows Server Core base image.
247: 
248: ```dockerfile
249: # escape=`
250: # The escape directive changes the escape character from \ to `
251: # This is especially useful in Windows Dockerfiles where \ is the path separator
252: 
253: # ============================================================
254: # Stage 1: Build and publish the application
255: # ============================================================
256: 
257: # Base Image - Select the appropriate .NET Framework version and Windows Server Core version
258: # Possible tags include:
259: # - 4.8.1-windowsservercore-ltsc2025 (Windows Server 2025)
260: # - 4.8-windowsservercore-ltsc2022 (Windows Server 2022)
261: # - 4.8-windowsservercore-ltsc2019 (Windows Server 2019)
262: # - 4.8-windowsservercore-ltsc2016 (Windows Server 2016)
263: # - 4.7.2-windowsservercore-ltsc2019 (Windows Server 2019)
264: # - 4.7.2-windowsservercore-ltsc2016 (Windows Server 2016)
265: # - 4.7.1-windowsservercore-ltsc2016 (Windows Server 2016)
266: # - 4.7-windowsservercore-ltsc2016 (Windows Server 2016)
267: # - 4.6.2-windowsservercore-ltsc2016 (Windows Server 2016)
268: # - 3.5-windowsservercore-ltsc2025 (Windows Server 2025)
269: # - 3.5-windowsservercore-ltsc2022 (Windows Server 2022)
270: # - 3.5-windowsservercore-ltsc2019 (Windows Server 2019)
271: # - 3.5-windowsservercore-ltsc2019 (Windows Server 2016)
272: # Uses the .NET Framework SDK image for building the application
273: FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/framework/sdk:4.8-windowsservercore-ltsc2022 AS build
274: ARG BUILD_CONFIGURATION=Release
275: 
276: # Set the default shell to PowerShell
277: SHELL ["powershell", "-command"]
278: 
279: WORKDIR /app
280: 
281: # Copy the solution and project files
282: COPY YourSolution.sln .
283: COPY YourProject/*.csproj ./YourProject/
284: COPY YourOtherProject/*.csproj ./YourOtherProject/
285: 
286: # Copy packages.config files
287: COPY YourProject/packages.config ./YourProject/
288: COPY YourOtherProject/packages.config ./YourOtherProject/
289: 
290: # Restore NuGet packages
291: RUN nuget restore YourSolution.sln
292: 
293: # Copy source code
294: COPY . .
295: 
296: # Perform custom pre-build steps here, if needed
297: 
298: # Build and publish the application to C:\publish
299: RUN msbuild /p:Configuration=$BUILD_CONFIGURATION `
300:             /p:WebPublishMethod=FileSystem `
301:             /p:PublishUrl=C:\publish `
302:             /p:DeployDefaultTarget=WebPublish
303: 
304: # Perform custom post-build steps here, if needed
305: 
306: # ============================================================
307: # Stage 2: Final runtime image
308: # ============================================================
309: 
310: # Base Image - Select the appropriate .NET Framework version and Windows Server Core version
311: # Possible tags include:
312: # - 4.8.1-windowsservercore-ltsc2025 (Windows Server 2025)
313: # - 4.8-windowsservercore-ltsc2022 (Windows Server 2022)
314: # - 4.8-windowsservercore-ltsc2019 (Windows Server 2019)
315: # - 4.8-windowsservercore-ltsc2016 (Windows Server 2016)
316: # - 4.7.2-windowsservercore-ltsc2019 (Windows Server 2019)
317: # - 4.7.2-windowsservercore-ltsc2016 (Windows Server 2016)
318: # - 4.7.1-windowsservercore-ltsc2016 (Windows Server 2016)
