294: ## Enabling Libraries in VS Code
295: 
296: Libraries are enabled inside the draw.io editor (which VS Code embeds):
297: 
298: 1. Open any `.drawio` or `.drawio.svg` file in VS Code
299: 2. Click the `+` icon in the shape panel (left sidebar) →`Search Shapes` or `More Shapes`
300: 3. Check the library you want to activate
301: 4. Shapes appear in the panel for drag-and-drop
302: 
303: Libraries are stored per-user in draw.io settings (not per-project).
304: 
305: ---
306: 
307: ## Custom Shape Library Creation
308: 
309: A custom library is an XML file with `.xml` extension loaded via `File > Open Library`:
310: 
311: ```xml
312: <mxlibrary>
313:   [
314:     {
315:       "xml": "&lt;mxCell value=\"Component\" style=\"rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#dae8fc;\" vertex=\"1\"&gt;&lt;mxGeometry width=\"120\" height=\"60\" as=\"geometry\" /&gt;&lt;/mxCell&gt;",
316:       "w": 120,
317:       "h": 60,
318:       "aspect": "fixed",
319:       "title": "My Component"
320:     }
321:   ]
322: </mxlibrary>
323: ```
324: 
325: Each shape entry contains:
326: - `xml`: XML-escaped cell definition
327: - `w` / `h`: Default width/height
328: - `aspect`: `"fixed"` to lock ratio
329: - `title`: Name shown in panel
330: 
331:     }
332:   ]
333: </mxlibrary>
334: ```
</file>

