<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-actions-hardening/references/injection.md">
 1: # Script Injection
 2: 
 3: `${{ <expr> }}` is substituted into the script **as text, before the shell runs**. Any expression
 4: that resolves to data an outside contributor controls is therefore a command-injection sink.
 5: 
 6: ## Attacker-Controllable Contexts
 7: 
 8: These can be set by anyone who can open an issue, PR, or comment:
 9: 
10: | Context | Set by |
11: | --- | --- |
12: | `github.event.issue.title` / `.body` | Issue author |
13: | `github.event.pull_request.title` / `.body` | PR author |
14: | `github.event.pull_request.head.ref` / `.head.label` | PR author (branch name) |
15: | `github.head_ref` | PR author (branch name) |
16: | `github.event.comment.body` | Commenter |
17: | `github.event.review.body` / `.review_comment.body` | Reviewer |
18: | `github.event.commits.*.message` / `head_commit.message` | Commit author |
19: | `github.event.commits.*.author.email` / `.name` | Commit author |
20: | `github.event.pages.*.page_name` | Wiki editor |
21: 
22: A branch named `$(<attacker-command>)` or an issue titled `"; <attacker-command> #` becomes shell
23: when interpolated into a `run:` step.
24: 
25: ## The Vulnerable Pattern
26: 
27: ```yaml
28: # VULNERABLE
29: - run: |
30:     echo "Reviewing PR: ${{ github.event.pull_request.title }}"
31:     git checkout ${{ github.head_ref }}
32: ```
33: 
34: ## The Safe Pattern — Pass Through `env:`
35: 
36: Bind the untrusted value to an environment variable, then reference the *shell* variable (quoted).
37: The shell variable is data, never re-parsed as workflow syntax:
38: 
39: ```yaml
40: # SAFE
41: - env:
42:     PR_TITLE: ${{ github.event.pull_request.title }}
43:     HEAD_REF: ${{ github.head_ref }}
44:   run: |
45:     echo "Reviewing PR: $PR_TITLE"
46:     git checkout "$HEAD_REF"
47: ```
48: 
49: `${{ }}` now appears only on the `env:` side, where it is assigned as a value rather than spliced
50: into a command. Always quote the shell variable (`"$PR_TITLE"`) to prevent word-splitting and
51: globbing.
52: 
53: ## `actions/github-script`
54: 
55: The same rule applies. Do not interpolate `${{ }}` into the `script:` body — pass it through the
56: environment and read `process.env`:
57: 
58: ```yaml
59: # VULNERABLE
60: - uses: actions/github-script@<sha>
61:   with:
62:     script: console.log("${{ github.event.issue.title }}")
63: 
64: # SAFE
65: - uses: actions/github-script@<sha>
66:   env:
67:     TITLE: ${{ github.event.issue.title }}
68:   with:
69:     script: console.log(process.env.TITLE)
70: ```
71: 
72: ## Custom Action Inputs
73: 
74: Passing untrusted `${{ }}` into a composite or JS action's `with:` inputs can be safe or not
75: depending on whether the action itself interpolates the input into a shell. When in doubt, pass via
76: `env:` and have the action read the environment, or sanitize/validate first (e.g. a branch name
77: should match `^[A-Za-z0-9._/-]+$`).
78: 
79: ## Quick Audit Checklist
80: 
81: 1. Grep every `run:` and `script:` for `${{`.
82: 2. For each, resolve what the expression points to.
83: 3. If it can be set by a non-collaborator → rewrite via `env:` with a quoted shell variable.
84: 4. `github.actor`, `github.repository`, `github.sha`, `github.ref` (for branch protection contexts)
85:    and similar server-controlled values are not attacker-set, but a defense-in-depth `env:` rewrite
86:    costs nothing.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-actions-hardening/references/permissions-and-tokens.md">
 1: # Permissions and Tokens
 2: 
 3: Every workflow run gets an automatic `GITHUB_TOKEN`. Its scope is the blast radius if a step is
 4: compromised, so scope it to the minimum.
 5: 
 6: ## The Default Is Too Broad
 7: 
 8: If a workflow has no `permissions:` block, it inherits the repository/organization default. On
 9: older or permissive repos that default is **read/write to most scopes**. A single injected command
10: or malicious dependency then runs with the ability to push code, publish releases, or approve PRs.
11: 
12: ## Least-Privilege Recipe
13: 
14: Set a restrictive default at the top level, then elevate per job only where needed.
15: 
16: ```yaml
17: # Deny by default
18: permissions: {}
19: 
20: jobs:
21:   build:
22:     permissions:
23:       contents: read          # checkout only
24:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
25:     steps: [...]
26: 
27:   comment:
28:     permissions:
29:       contents: read
30:       pull-requests: write    # this job posts a comment; nothing else
31:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
32:     steps: [...]
33: ```
34: 
35: Common scopes: `contents`, `pull-requests`, `issues`, `actions`, `packages`, `id-token`,
36: `deployments`, `checks`, `statuses`. Each is `read`, `write`, or `none`.
37: 
38: ## Findings to Flag
39: 
40: * No `permissions:` block anywhere → MEDIUM (inherits possibly-broad default).
41: * `permissions: write-all` → HIGH.
42: * A `write` scope the job's steps never use → HIGH (drop it).
43: * Top-level `write` that should live on one job → MEDIUM (move it down).
44: 
45: ## OIDC Instead of Long-Lived Cloud Secrets
46: 
47: Storing static cloud keys (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, etc.) as repo secrets means a leak is permanent
48: until manually rotated. Prefer OpenID Connect: the workflow requests a short-lived token the cloud
49: provider trusts, scoped to that repo/branch, expiring in minutes.
50: 
51: ```yaml
52: permissions:
53:   id-token: write     # required to request the OIDC token
54:   contents: read
55: jobs:
56:   deploy:
57:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
58:     steps:
59:       - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@<sha>
60:         with:
61:           role-to-assume: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/my-ci-role
62:           aws-region: us-east-1
63:       # no AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY secrets needed
64: ```
65: 
66: The same pattern exists for Azure (`azure/login`), GCP (`google-github-actions/auth`), HashiCorp
67: Vault, and others. On the cloud side, scope the trust policy to the specific repo and ideally a
68: specific branch/environment so a fork or another repo cannot assume the role.
69: 
70: ## Secret Hygiene
71: 
72: * Reference secrets only in the jobs that need them.
73: * Never `echo` a secret or enable shell tracing (`set -x`) in a step that handles one.
74: * Don't pass secrets into third-party actions you haven't pinned and reviewed.
75: * Remember fork `pull_request` runs get no secrets — don't try to "fix" that by switching to
76:   `pull_request_target` (see `triggers-and-privilege.md`).
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-actions-hardening/references/report-format.md">
 1: # Report Format
 2: 
 3: Use this structure for every workflow hardening review.
 4: 
 5: ## 1. Summary Table (always first)
 6: 
 7: ```
 8: GitHub Actions Hardening — <workflow file(s) reviewed>
 9: 
10: | Severity   | Count |
11: | ---------- | ----- |
12: | 🔴 CRITICAL | 1     |
13: | 🟠 HIGH     | 2     |
14: | 🟡 MEDIUM   | 1     |
15: | 🔵 LOW      | 1     |
16: | ⚪ INFO     | 0     |
17: ```
18: 
19: If nothing was found: `No issues found. Checked: triggers, injection sinks, permissions, action
20: pinning, secret handling.`
21: 
22: ## 2. Findings (grouped by issue type, not by file)
23: 
24: For each finding use a card:
25: 
26: ```
27: ### 🔴 CRITICAL — Script injection via PR title on a privileged trigger
28: 
29: File: .github/workflows/triage.yml  (line 14)
30: Trigger: pull_request_target
31: 
32: Offending code:
33:     - run: echo "New PR: ${{ github.event.pull_request.title }}"
34: 
35: Risk: pull_request_target runs with a read/write token and repository secrets, and any
36: contributor can open a PR with a title like  "; <attacker-command> #  which is executed as shell.
37: This allows secret exfiltration and pushes with the workflow token.
38: 
39: Fix:
40:     - env:
41:         PR_TITLE: ${{ github.event.pull_request.title }}
42:       run: echo "New PR: $PR_TITLE"
43: 
44: Confidence: High
45: ```
46: 
47: ## 3. Remediation Blocks
48: 
49: Every CRITICAL and HIGH finding includes a concrete before/after. Preserve the author's
50: indentation, step names, and surrounding structure — change only what fixes the issue, and add a
51: one-line comment explaining the change where it isn't obvious.
52: 
53: ## 4. Closing Note
54: 
55: End with the explicit line:
56: 
57: > Review each change before committing. Nothing has been modified.
58: 
59: ## Style Rules
60: 
61: * Quote the exact offending line and give its location.
62: * Explain risk in plain English — what an attacker actually does, not just the rule name.
63: * Per-finding confidence: High / Medium / Low.
64: * Don't inflate severity: a fork `pull_request` (read-only token, no secrets) running untrusted
65:   code is not CRITICAL on its own.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-actions-hardening/references/supply-chain.md">
 1: # Supply Chain
 2: 
 3: A workflow runs other people's code every time it `uses:` an action. Those actions execute with
 4: your token and (on privileged triggers) your secrets, so their integrity is your integrity.
 5: 
 6: ## Pin Third-Party Actions to a Commit SHA
 7: 
 8: Tags (`@v4`) and branches (`@main`) are **mutable** — the upstream owner (or anyone who compromises
 9: them) can repoint them to new code without you changing a line. A full 40-character commit SHA is
10: immutable.
11: 
12: ```yaml
13: # Mutable — the tag can be moved to malicious code
14: - uses: some-org/some-action@v3
15: 
16: # Pinned — this exact tree, forever
17: - uses: some-org/some-action@3f1e0a9c8b7d6e5f4a3b2c1d0e9f8a7b6c5d4e3f # v3.2.1
18: ```
19: 
20: Rules:
21: 
22: * Third-party actions (anything not `actions/*` or `github/*`) → **MUST** be SHA-pinned. Flag tags
23:   and branches as HIGH.
24: * `@main` / `@master` → HIGH regardless of publisher; that is unversioned "latest".
25: * First-party `actions/*` → SHA-pinning is the hardened recommendation (LOW if only tag-pinned).
26: * Keep a trailing `# vX.Y.Z` comment so humans and Dependabot can read the intended version.
27: 
28: This is not theoretical: real incidents have seen popular actions' tags repointed to code that
29: exfiltrated secrets from every workflow that referenced the mutable tag.
30: 
31: ## Let Dependabot Update the Pins
32: 
33: SHA pins go stale. Enable Dependabot for the `github-actions` ecosystem so updates arrive as
34: reviewable PRs (it understands the `# vX.Y.Z` comment and bumps the SHA):
35: 
36: ```yaml
37: # .github/dependabot.yml
38: version: 2
39: updates:
40:   - package-ecosystem: github-actions
41:     directory: /
42:     schedule:
43:       interval: weekly
44: ```
45: 
46: ## Artifact and Cache Poisoning
47: 
48: * An artifact uploaded by an untrusted `pull_request` build is **untrusted data**. A privileged
49:   `workflow_run` may download it, but must treat it as data only — never execute it, and validate
50:   paths when extracting (a crafted artifact can contain `../` path-traversal entries).
51: * Caches are keyed and can be populated by less-privileged runs; do not trust cached build outputs
52:   to be untampered in a privileged context.
53: 
54: ## Self-Hosted Runners on Public Repos
55: 
56: Default (GitHub-hosted) runners are ephemeral — a fresh VM per job, destroyed after. **Self-hosted
57: runners persist**, so untrusted fork PR code running on one can:
58: 
59: * Leave behind tools/backdoors for the next job,
60: * Read other repositories' checkouts or credentials on the same machine,
61: * Pivot into your network.
62: 
63: Never use self-hosted runners for workflows that public forks can trigger. If you must, use
64: ephemeral, isolated, single-use runners and never expose secrets to fork-triggered jobs.
65: 
66: ## `checkout` Credential Persistence
67: 
68: `actions/checkout` writes the token into `.git/config` by default so later `git` steps can push.
69: If the job subsequently runs untrusted code, that code can read the token. Set
70: `persist-credentials: false` when you don't need to push, especially before running build/test of
71: untrusted code.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-actions-hardening/references/triggers-and-privilege.md">
 1: # Triggers and Privilege
 2: 
 3: The single most important question for workflow security is: **can an outside contributor trigger
 4: this workflow, and if so, what token and secrets does it get?** GitHub answers this differently per
 5: trigger.
 6: 
 7: ## Trust Matrix
 8: 
 9: | Trigger | Who can fire it | `GITHUB_TOKEN` | Secrets available | Risk |
10: | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
11: | `push` | Repo collaborators | read/write | yes | Low — trusted authors |
12: | `pull_request` (same-repo branch) | Collaborators | read/write | yes | Low |
13: | `pull_request` (from a fork) | **Anyone** | **read-only** | **no** | Low by design — even malicious code can't steal anything |
14: | `pull_request_target` | **Anyone with a fork** | **read/write** | **yes** | **High** — runs in base-repo context |
15: | `workflow_run` | Fires after another workflow | **read/write** | **yes** | **High** |
16: | `issue_comment`, `issues` | **Anyone** | **read/write** | **yes** | **High** |
17: 
18: The trap: `pull_request` from a fork is *safe* because GitHub deliberately strips the token down
19: and withholds secrets. Maintainers who find that "the secrets don't work on fork PRs" often switch
20: to `pull_request_target` to get them back — and in doing so hand a write token and every secret to
21: arbitrary contributors.
22: 
23: ## Why `pull_request_target` Is Dangerous
24: 
25: `pull_request_target` checks out the **base** repository's workflow definition (so a fork can't
26: change what runs), but it runs with full privileges. The danger is when the workflow then
27: explicitly checks out the **fork's** code and executes it:
28: 
29: ```yaml
30: # DANGEROUS — RCE with a write token + secrets
31: on: pull_request_target
32: jobs:
33:   build:
34:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
35:     steps:
36:       - uses: actions/checkout@<sha>
37:         with:
38:           ref: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}   # fork's code
39:       - run: npm install && npm test                        # runs the fork's code + scripts
40: ```
41: 
42: `npm install` alone runs arbitrary lifecycle scripts from the PR. With `pull_request_target` those
43: scripts can read `secrets.*` and push commits with the write token.
44: 
45: ## The Safe Two-Workflow Pattern
46: 
47: Split responsibilities. An **unprivileged** workflow runs the untrusted code; a **privileged**
48: workflow consumes only the trusted *output*.
49: 
50: ```yaml
51: # 1) Unprivileged: runs untrusted code, no secrets, read-only token
52: name: PR Build
53: on: pull_request
54: permissions:
55:   contents: read
56: jobs:
57:   build:
58:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
59:     steps:
60:       - uses: actions/checkout@<sha>
61:       - run: npm ci && npm run build
62:       - uses: actions/upload-artifact@<sha>
63:         with: { name: pr, path: dist/ }
64: ```
65: 
66: ```yaml
67: # 2) Privileged: triggered by the first, never runs fork code
68: name: PR Comment
69: on:
70:   workflow_run:
71:     workflows: ["PR Build"]
72:     types: [completed]
73: permissions:
74:   pull-requests: write
75: jobs:
76:   comment:
77:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
78:     steps:
79:       - uses: actions/download-artifact@<sha>   # data only, not executed
80:       # post results, using the trusted token — but never execute the artifact
81: ```
82: 
83: ## Rules
84: 
85: * Treat `pull_request_target`, `workflow_run`, `issue_comment`, and `issues` as privileged.
86: * In a privileged workflow, **never** check out and execute PR/fork code.
87: * If you only need to label, comment, or triage based on metadata, that is fine — just don't run
88:   the contributor's code.
89: * Prefer `pull_request` (with its safe read-only/no-secrets defaults) whenever possible.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-actions-hardening/SKILL.md">
  1: ---
  2: name: github-actions-hardening
  3: description: Security hardening reviewer for GitHub Actions workflow files (.github/workflows/*.yml). Reasons about the Actions threat model that pattern matchers and general code linters miss — untrusted-input script injection, privileged triggers running fork code, mutable action references, and over-scoped tokens. Use this skill when asked to review, audit, harden, or secure a GitHub Actions workflow, when writing a new workflow, or for any request like "is this workflow safe?", "review my CI for security issues", "why is pull_request_target dangerous here?", "pin my actions", or "lock down GITHUB_TOKEN permissions". Covers script injection via ${{ }} interpolation, pull_request_target / workflow_run privilege escalation, SHA-pinning of third-party actions, least-privilege permissions, GITHUB_ENV/GITHUB_OUTPUT injection, secret exposure, OIDC over long-lived credentials, and self-hosted runner exposure on public repositories.
  4: ---
  5: 
  6: # GitHub Actions Hardening
  7: 
  8: A focused security reviewer for GitHub Actions workflows. It reasons about the *Actions-specific*
  9: threat model — where trust boundaries live in trigger types, token scopes, and string
 10: interpolation — rather than the application-code vulnerabilities a general security scanner looks
 11: for. Most workflow risks are invisible to language linters because the dangerous code is the YAML
 12: itself and the way GitHub expands `${{ }}` expressions into a shell before your script runs.
 13: 
 14: ## When to Use This Skill
 15: 
 16: Use this skill when the request involves:
 17: 
 18: * Reviewing, auditing, or hardening any file under `.github/workflows/`
 19: * Authoring a new workflow and wanting it secure by default
 20: * A workflow that uses `pull_request_target`, `workflow_run`, or `issue_comment` triggers
 21: * Questions about `GITHUB_TOKEN` permissions or the `permissions:` key
 22: * Pinning actions to commit SHAs vs tags vs branches
 23: * Handling untrusted input (issue titles, PR bodies, branch names, commit messages) in `run:` steps
 24: * OIDC / cloud authentication from Actions, or secret handling in CI
 25: * Self-hosted runners on public repositories
 26: * Any request like "is this workflow safe?", "secure my CI", or "review this GitHub Action"
 27: 
 28: ## The Core Insight
 29: 
 30: In a workflow, **`${{ <expr> }}` is expanded by the runner into the script *before* the shell
 31: executes it.** So a step like:
 32: 
 33: ```yaml
 34: - run: echo "Title: ${{ github.event.issue.title }}"
 35: ```
 36: 
 37: is not passing a variable — it is *pasting attacker-controlled text directly into your shell
 38: command*. An issue titled `"; <attacker-command> #` is concatenated into the script and executed.
 39: This single mechanism is the most common real-world Actions vulnerability, and models routinely
 40: generate it. Treat every
 41: `${{ }}` that contains data an outside contributor can influence as a code-injection sink.
 42: 
 43: ## Execution Workflow
 44: 
 45: Follow these steps **in order** for every workflow reviewed.
 46: 
 47: ### Step 1 — Map the Triggers and Trust Level
 48: 
 49: Read every `on:` trigger and classify the workflow's privilege:
 50: 
 51: * `push`, `pull_request` (from same repo) → runs with the contributor's own trust
 52: * `pull_request` from a **fork** → runs with a **read-only** token, **no secrets** (safe by design)
 53: * `pull_request_target`, `workflow_run`, `issue_comment`, `issues` → run in the context of the
 54:   **base repository** with a **read/write token and full access to secrets**, but can be
 55:   **triggered by outside contributors**. These are the dangerous triggers.
 56: 
 57: Read `references/triggers-and-privilege.md` for the full trust matrix.
 58: 
 59: ### Step 2 — Hunt for Script Injection
 60: 
 61: For every `run:` block, every `script:` in `actions/github-script`, and every input to a custom
 62: action, list the `${{ }}` expressions and check whether any resolve to attacker-controllable data.
 63: High-risk contexts include:
 64: 
 65: * `github.event.issue.title`, `github.event.issue.body`
 66: * `github.event.pull_request.title`, `github.event.pull_request.body`, `.head.ref`, `.head.label`
 67: * `github.event.comment.body`, `github.event.review.body`
 68: * `github.event.pages.*.page_name`, `github.event.commits.*.message`, `github.event.head_commit.*`
 69: * `github.head_ref` and any `github.event.*` field a fork author can set
 70: 
 71: Read `references/injection.md` for the complete sink list and the safe-pattern fixes.
 72: 
 73: ### Step 3 — Check Privileged Triggers Don't Execute Untrusted Code
 74: 
 75: If a `pull_request_target` or `workflow_run` workflow checks out PR/fork code
 76: (`ref: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}`) **and then runs it** (build, test, install
 77: scripts, `npm install` with lifecycle scripts, etc.), that is remote code execution against a
 78: privileged token. Flag it as CRITICAL. The safe pattern is to split into two workflows: an
 79: unprivileged `pull_request` workflow that runs the untrusted code, and a privileged
 80: `workflow_run` workflow that only consumes its results.
 81: 
 82: ### Step 4 — Audit `permissions:`
 83: 
 84: * If there is **no** `permissions:` block, the workflow inherits the repository default, which may
 85:   be read/write to everything. Flag it.
 86: * Recommend a top-level `permissions: {}` (deny-all) or `contents: read`, then grant the minimum
 87:   per job (e.g. `pull-requests: write` only on the job that comments).
 88: * Flag any `permissions: write-all` or broad `write` scopes that the steps don't actually need.
 89: 
 90: Read `references/permissions-and-tokens.md` for the per-scope guidance and OIDC setup.
 91: 
 92: ### Step 5 — Audit Action References (Supply Chain)
 93: 
 94: For every `uses:`:
 95: 
 96: * **Third-party actions** (not `actions/*` or `github/*`) MUST be pinned to a full 40-character
 97:   commit SHA, not a tag or branch. Tags and branches are mutable; a compromised upstream action
 98:   can rewrite `v1` to malicious code that runs with your token and secrets.
 99: * First-party `actions/*` are lower risk but SHA-pinning is still the hardened recommendation.
100: * Flag `@main`, `@master`, or any branch reference as HIGH — that is "latest" and can change under
101:   you at any time.
102: * Note the human-readable version in a trailing comment: `uses: foo/bar@<sha> # v2.1.0`.
103: 
104: Read `references/supply-chain.md` for pinning, Dependabot for actions, and artifact/cache risks.
105: 
106: ### Step 6 — Check Secret and Output Handling
107: 
108: * No secrets echoed, printed, or written to logs; no `set -x` / `bash -x` in steps that touch
109:   secrets.
110: * Secrets must not be passed to steps that run untrusted code or to untrusted third-party actions.
111: * Untrusted multiline data written to `$GITHUB_ENV` or `$GITHUB_OUTPUT` can inject environment
112:   variables or step outputs — use the random-delimiter heredoc form and never write raw user input.
113: * `actions/checkout` leaves a token on disk by default; set `persist-credentials: false` when the
114:   job later runs untrusted code.
115: 
116: ### Step 7 — Produce the Report
117: 
118: Output findings using the format in `references/report-format.md`: a severity summary table first,
119: then grouped findings with file, the exact offending YAML, the risk in plain English, and a
120: concrete before/after fix. Never auto-apply changes — present them for review.
121: 
122: ## Severity Guide
123: 
124: | Severity | Meaning | Example |
125: | --- | --- | --- |
126: | 🔴 CRITICAL | Token/secret theft or RCE reachable by an outside contributor | `pull_request_target` checking out and running fork code; `${{ github.event.* }}` in a `run:` on a privileged trigger |
127: | 🟠 HIGH | Exploitable supply-chain or scope problem | Third-party action on a mutable tag/branch; `write-all` permissions; injection sink on `issue_comment` |
128: | 🟡 MEDIUM | Risk under conditions or chaining | Missing `permissions:` block; secret reachable by a non-fork PR author |
129: | 🔵 LOW | Hardening gap, low direct risk | First-party action not SHA-pinned; `persist-credentials` left default on a non-privileged job |
130: | ⚪ INFO | Observation, not a vulnerability | Version comment missing next to a pinned SHA |
131: 
132: ## Output Rules
133: 
134: * **Always** show a findings summary table (counts by severity) first.
135: * **Group by issue type**, not by file.
136: * **Be exact** — quote the offending line and give the line location.
137: * **Always** pair every CRITICAL/HIGH with a concrete corrected YAML snippet.
138: * **Never** claim a fork `pull_request` is dangerous just because it runs untrusted code — it has
139:   no secrets and a read-only token. Reserve CRITICAL for the privileged triggers.
140: * If the workflow is already hardened, say so and list what was checked.
141: 
142: ## Reference Files
143: 
144: Load these as needed:
145: 
146: * `references/triggers-and-privilege.md` — Trust matrix for every trigger, why `pull_request_target`
147:   and `workflow_run` are privileged, and the two-workflow safe pattern.
148:   + Search patterns: `pull_request_target`, `workflow_run`, `issue_comment`, `fork`, `secrets`, `read-only token`, `trust boundary`
149: * `references/injection.md` — Full list of attacker-controllable `${{ }}` contexts and the
150:   `env:`-variable safe pattern for each sink (`run`, `github-script`, action inputs).
151:   + Search patterns: `script injection`, `github.event`, `head_ref`, `issue title`, `env`, `intermediate variable`, `actions/github-script`
152: * `references/permissions-and-tokens.md` — `GITHUB_TOKEN` scopes, least-privilege `permissions:`
153:   recipes per job type, and OIDC for cloud auth instead of long-lived secrets.
154:   + Search patterns: `permissions`, `GITHUB_TOKEN`, `write-all`, `contents: read`, `id-token`, `OIDC`, `least privilege`
155: * `references/supply-chain.md` — SHA-pinning third-party actions, Dependabot for `github-actions`,
156:   artifact and cache poisoning across `workflow_run`, and self-hosted runner exposure.
157:   + Search patterns: `SHA pin`, `uses`, `mutable tag`, `Dependabot`, `download-artifact`, `cache`, `self-hosted runner`
158: * `references/report-format.md` — Output template: summary table, finding cards, and before/after
159:   remediation blocks.
160:   + Search patterns: `report`, `format`, `finding`, `summary`, `remediation`, `before`, `after`
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-actions-runtime-upgrade-conventions/SKILL.md">
 1: ---
 2: name: github-actions-runtime-upgrade-conventions
 3: description: 'Upgrade GitHub Actions to supported runtimes by selecting safe action versions, preserving workflow behavior, and validating post-upgrade execution.'
 4: ---
 5: 
 6: # GitHub Actions Runtime Upgrade Conventions
 7: 
 8: Use this skill when editing GitHub Actions workflows to address deprecation warnings about action runtimes (for example Node.js runtime migrations).
 9: 
10: ## Use This Skill When
11: 
12: - Workflow logs report an action is running on a deprecated runtime.
13: - You are upgrading action versions in `.github/workflows/*.yml` or `.github/workflows/*.yaml`.
14: - You need to keep existing workflow behavior while modernizing action dependencies.
15: 
16: ## Upgrade Rules
17: 
18: - Prefer upgrading to the latest stable **major** version of each action that is compatible with the workflow.
19: - Prefer immutable pins: resolve the target release to a full commit SHA and use that SHA in `uses:`.
20: - Do not pin to mutable tags or branches (for example `@v4` or `@main`) in final recommendations.
21: - Upgrade one action at a time per commit (or one tightly related group) so failures are easy to isolate.
22: - Keep existing workflow behavior unchanged while upgrading runtime/dependency actions.
23: 
24: ## Actions We Track in This Repo
25: 
26: Prioritize runtime review for these groups when warnings appear:
27: 
28: - Any first-party action under `actions/*`
29: - Especially setup actions under `actions/setup-*` (for example `setup-node`, `setup-python`, `setup-dotnet`)
30: - Any other action explicitly named by the runtime deprecation warning in workflow logs
31: 
32: ## Pinning Pattern
33: 
34: ```yaml
35: steps:
36:   - uses: actions/checkout@8ade135a41bc03ea155e62e844d188df1ea18608 # v4.3.1
37:   - uses: actions/setup-node@60edb5dd545a775178f52524783378180af0d1f8 # v4.0.4
38: ```
39: 
40: When recommending upgrades, identify the latest compatible release first, then use the corresponding commit SHA with an optional version comment.
41: 
42: ## Verification Checklist
43: 
44: After changing action versions:
45: 
46: 1. Ensure all edited workflows still parse and keep the same triggers/permissions unless intentionally changed.
47: 2. Run the affected workflows (or equivalent local build/test commands) and confirm the upgraded steps complete successfully.
48: 3. Confirm release/signing/artifact steps still produce expected outputs where applicable.
49: 4. Check workflow run logs for any new deprecation warnings or runtime migration notes.
50: 
51: ## PR Notes
52: 
53: Include in the PR summary:
54: 
55: - Which actions were upgraded (from -> to).
56: - Whether any action could not move to a new major and why.
57: - Which workflows were re-run to validate the change.
58: 
59: ## How This Complements Dependabot
60: 
61: Dependabot can automate many updates, but this skill still helps when:
62: 
63: - Dependabot is not enabled for workflows in a repository.
64: - Runtime warnings appear before an automated update is available.
65: - A workflow needs behavior-preserving validation after the action bump.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-codespaces-efficiency/references/codespaces.md">
 1: # Codespaces and Devcontainer Efficiency
 2: 
 3: Load this reference only when the task involves `.devcontainer/`, Codespaces sizing, prebuilds, or workspace cost.
 4: 
 5: If the repo is onboarding Codespaces for the first time, define a minimal baseline first, then optimize.
 6: 
 7: ## Audit Order
 8: 
 9: Inspect in this order:
10: 
11: 1. Check whether `.devcontainer/` exists.
12: 2. If it does not exist, gather baseline requirements first: language/toolchain, required CLIs, expected startup target, machine-size constraints, and whether prebuilds are needed.
13: 3. Review `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`.
14: 4. Ensure `.devcontainer/devcontainer-lock.json` exists; if missing, recommend adding it because many repos predate lock-file support.
15: 5. Review related Dockerfiles, features, and setup scripts.
16: 6. Review docs that recommend machine sizes, prebuilds, or startup expectations.
17: 
18: For new setups (step 2), start minimal and avoid optional tools until usage data justifies them.
19: 
20: ## Common Waste Sources
21: 
22: - Oversized base images
23: - Unnecessary packages or extensions
24: - Slow post-create bootstrap steps
25: - Prebuilds enabled for too many branches
26: - Missing guidance on machine sizing or idle timeout discipline
27: 
28: ## Preferred Fix Order
29: 
30: 1. Remove unnecessary packages, features, and extensions
31: 2. Reduce startup commands and post-create installs
32: 3. Recommend the smallest machine size that preserves throughput
33: 4. Narrow prebuild scope to sustained-usage branches (default branch, active release branches, and any branch with more than five Codespaces per week)
34: 5. Add or tighten idle-timeout and cleanup guidance
35: 
36: ## Safe-Change Rules
37: 
38: - Do not optimize startup by removing tools the team actually needs every day.
39: - Distinguish repo changes from org or user settings.
40: - Prefer documentation when the effective control lives outside the repo.
41: - Avoid turning the devcontainer into a production-like image unless the team explicitly needs that.
42: 
43: ## Reporting Focus
44: 
45: When reporting Codespaces improvements, separate:
46: 
47: - Faster startup
48: - Lower steady-state workspace cost
49: - Lower prebuild spend
50: - Guidance-only recommendations that still need org or user action
</file>

