 4: ---
 5: 
 6: # GitHub Codespaces Efficiency
 7: 
 8: Use this skill as a lean entrypoint for GitHub Codespaces efficiency work. Inspect the repo, identify waste, and load only needed references.
 9: 
10: If no `.devcontainer/` exists yet, load [`references/codespaces.md`](./references/codespaces.md) and define a baseline before proceeding with the steps below.
11: 
12: ## Use This Skill When
13: 
14: - The user wants faster Codespaces startup or lower Codespaces spend.
15: - The repo has a `.devcontainer/` or explicit Codespaces configuration questions.
16: - The user asks for devcontainer optimization, machine sizing, prebuild strategy, or idle-timeout guidance.
17: - The user is setting up Codespaces for the first time or needs help creating a new `.devcontainer/` from scratch.
18: 
19: ## Load Only What You Need
20: 
21: - [`references/codespaces.md`](./references/codespaces.md) — devcontainer, machine-sizing, prebuild, idle-timeout guidance, and reporting.
22: - [`references/review-rubric.md`](./references/review-rubric.md) — load only for review passes.
23: 
24: ## Core Workflow
25: 
26: ### 1. Measure first
27: 
28: ```bash
29: find .devcontainer -maxdepth 2 -type f
30: gh codespace list
31: repo=$(gh repo view --json nameWithOwner --jq .nameWithOwner)
32: gh api "/repos/$repo/codespaces/machines"
33: ```
34: 
35: If `gh` auth fails or the user lacks repo admin scope, proceed with static analysis of `.devcontainer/` files; mark machine-type and prebuild recommendations as unverified.
36: 
37: Look for: devcontainer image >2 GB or more than 10 features, machine type larger than usage data supports, missing `devcontainer-lock.json` (recommend adding — many repos predate lock-file support), prebuilds scoped too broadly, and idle timeout mismatched to usage patterns.
38: 
39: ### 2. Apply guardrails
40: 
41: Check each proposed fix against these rules before recommending it:
42: 
43: 1. Does not remove tools the team uses every day — drop any fix that strips required development tools or extensions.
44: 2. Does not assume smaller is always better — balance machine cost against developer experience and throughput.
45: 3. Does not turn the devcontainer into a production image — drop any fix that adds production-only dependencies unless the team explicitly requires it.
46: 4. Incremental changes preferred — a greenfield baseline is appropriate only when no `.devcontainer/` exists; flag (do not drop) changes that restructure an existing config.
47: 5. Repo changes stay separate from org settings — split any fix that mixes repo-editable files with org-level or user-level Codespaces settings into two distinct recommendations.
48: 
49: ### 3. Select the top 3 fixes
50: 
51: From the six candidates below, keep only those supported by audit evidence from step 1 *and* passing all guardrails from step 2. Rank survivors by estimated monthly cost savings (USD). Select all candidates that meet both criteria, up to a maximum of 3.
52: 
53: 1. Trim devcontainer — remove features, packages, or extensions not needed for everyday development work; target image <2 GB and fewer than 10 features
54: 2. Right-size machine type — match to observed usage patterns; if data is unavailable, state assumptions explicitly
55: 3. Scope prebuilds — enable for the default branch, `release/*` branches active in the last 14 days, and branches with more than 5 Codespaces per week; disable for all others
56: 4. Tune idle timeout — 30 min default; 15 min if most sessions end before 30 min; 60 min if most sessions run longer
57: 5. Remove unused extensions or port-forwarding rules
58: 6. Reduce devcontainer image size and improve layer caching
59: 
60: ### 4. Verify
61: 
62: - Start a test Codespace to confirm devcontainer changes build and start as expected.
63: - Validate machine sizing against observed usage when telemetry is available; otherwise mark as unverified.
64: - Treat unexpected build or startup failures as real bugs even when the configuration looks correct.
65: 
66: ## Required Output
67: 
68: **Waste sources:** [top cost or startup-time drivers]
69: 
70: **Proposed fixes:** [top 3 changes supported by audit evidence and passing guardrails]
71: 
72: **Validation:** [proven live / static-only / remaining risk]
73: 
74: **Impact:**
75: - Startup time: [expected] / [measured if available]
76: - Monthly spend: [expected] / [measured if available]
77: - Resource utilization: [expected] / [measured if available]
78: 
79: ## References
80: 
81: - [`references/codespaces.md`](./references/codespaces.md)
82: - [`references/review-rubric.md`](./references/review-rubric.md) — load when reviewing completed efficiency work
</file>

