382:     distribution: "temurin"
383: - name: Build with Maven
384:   run: mvn compile
385: - name: Run tests
386:   run: mvn test
387: ```
388: 
389: **AVOID in workflows:**
390: - ❌ Complex configuration setups
391: - ❌ Multiple environment configurations
392: - ❌ Advanced tooling setup
393: - ❌ Custom scripts or complex logic
394: - ❌ Multiple package managers
395: - ❌ Database setup or external services
396: 
397: **INCLUDE only:**
398: - ✅ Language/runtime setup
399: - ✅ Basic dependency installation
400: - ✅ Simple linting (if standard)
401: - ✅ Basic test running
402: - ✅ Standard build commands
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-issues/references/dependencies.md">
 1: # Issue Dependencies (Blocked By / Blocking)
 2: 
 3: Dependencies let you mark that an issue is blocked by another issue. This creates a formal dependency relationship visible in the UI and trackable via API. No MCP tools exist for dependencies; use REST or GraphQL directly.
 4: 
 5: ## Using REST API
 6: 
 7: **List issues blocking this issue:**
 8: ```
 9: GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{issue_number}/dependencies/blocked_by
10: ```
11: 
12: **Add a blocking dependency:**
13: ```
14: POST /repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{issue_number}/dependencies/blocked_by
15: Body: { "issue_id": 12345 }
16: ```
17: 
18: The `issue_id` is the numeric issue **ID** (not the issue number).
19: 
20: **Remove a blocking dependency:**
21: ```
22: DELETE /repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{issue_number}/dependencies/blocked_by/{issue_id}
23: ```
24: 
25: ## Using GraphQL
26: 
27: **Read dependencies:**
28: ```graphql
29: {
30:   repository(owner: "OWNER", name: "REPO") {
31:     issue(number: 123) {
32:       blockedBy(first: 10) { nodes { number title state } }
33:       blocking(first: 10) { nodes { number title state } }
34:       issueDependenciesSummary { blockedBy blocking totalBlockedBy totalBlocking }
35:     }
36:   }
37: }
38: ```
39: 
40: **Add a dependency:**
41: ```graphql
42: mutation {
43:   addBlockedBy(input: {
44:     issueId: "BLOCKED_ISSUE_NODE_ID"
45:     blockingIssueId: "BLOCKING_ISSUE_NODE_ID"
46:   }) {
47:     blockingIssue { number title }
48:   }
49: }
50: ```
51: 
52: **Remove a dependency:**
53: ```graphql
54: mutation {
55:   removeBlockedBy(input: {
56:     issueId: "BLOCKED_ISSUE_NODE_ID"
57:     blockingIssueId: "BLOCKING_ISSUE_NODE_ID"
58:   }) {
59:     blockingIssue { number title }
60:   }
61: }
62: ```
63: 
64: ## Tracked issues (read-only)
65: 
66: Task-list tracking relationships are available via GraphQL as read-only fields:
67: 
68: - `trackedIssues(first: N)` - issues tracked in this issue's task list
69: - `trackedInIssues(first: N)` - issues whose task lists reference this issue
70: 
71: These are set automatically when issues are referenced in task lists (`- [ ] #123`). There are no mutations to manage them.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-issues/references/images.md">
  1: # Images in Issues and Comments
  2: 
  3: How to embed images in GitHub issue bodies and comments programmatically via the CLI.
  4: 
  5: ## Methods (ranked by reliability)
  6: 
  7: ### 1. GitHub Contents API (recommended for private repos)
  8: 
  9: Push image files to a branch in the same repo, then reference them with a URL that works for authenticated viewers.
 10: 
 11: **Step 1: Create a branch**
 12: 
 13: ```bash
 14: # Get the SHA of the default branch
 15: SHA=$(gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/git/ref/heads/main --jq '.object.sha')
 16: 
 17: # Create a new branch
 18: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/git/refs -X POST \
 19:   -f ref="refs/heads/{username}/images" \
 20:   -f sha="$SHA"
 21: ```
 22: 
 23: **Step 2: Upload images via Contents API**
 24: 
 25: ```bash
 26: # Base64-encode the image and upload
 27: BASE64=$(base64 -i /path/to/image.png)
 28: 
 29: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/contents/docs/images/my-image.png \
 30:   -X PUT \
 31:   -f message="Add image" \
 32:   -f content="$BASE64" \
 33:   -f branch="{username}/images" \
 34:   --jq '.content.path'
 35: ```
 36: 
 37: Repeat for each image. The Contents API creates a commit per file.
 38: 
 39: **Step 3: Reference in markdown**
 40: 
 41: ```markdown
 42: ![Description](https://github.com/{owner}/{repo}/raw/{username}/images/docs/images/my-image.png)
 43: ```
 44: 
 45: > **Important:** Use `github.com/{owner}/{repo}/raw/{branch}/{path}` format, NOT `raw.githubusercontent.com`. The `raw.githubusercontent.com` URLs return 404 for private repos. The `github.com/.../raw/...` format works because the browser sends auth cookies when the viewer is logged in and has repo access.
 46: 
 47: **Pros:** Works for any repo the viewer has access to, images live in version control, no expiration.
 48: **Cons:** Creates commits, viewers must be authenticated, images won't render in email notifications or for users without repo access.
 49: 
 50: ### 2. Gist hosting (public images only)
 51: 
 52: Upload images as files in a gist. Only works for images you're comfortable making public.
 53: 
 54: ```bash
 55: # Create a gist with a placeholder file
 56: gh gist create --public -f description.md <<< "Image hosting gist"
 57: 
 58: # Note: gh gist edit does NOT support binary files.
 59: # You must use the API to add binary content to gists.
 60: ```
 61: 
 62: > **Limitation:** Gists don't support binary file uploads via the CLI. You'd need to base64-encode and store as text, which won't render as images. Not recommended.
 63: 
 64: ### 3. Browser upload (most reliable rendering)
 65: 
 66: The most reliable way to get permanent image URLs is through the GitHub web UI:
 67: 
 68: 1. Open the issue/comment in a browser
 69: 2. Drag-drop or paste the image into the comment editor
 70: 3. GitHub generates a permanent `https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/{UUID}` URL
 71: 4. These URLs work for anyone, even without repo access, and render in email notifications
 72: 
 73: > **Why the API can't do this:** GitHub's `upload/policies/assets` endpoint requires a browser session (CSRF token + cookies). It returns an HTML error page when called with API tokens. There is no public API for generating `user-attachments` URLs.
 74: 
 75: ## Taking screenshots programmatically
 76: 
 77: Use `puppeteer-core` with local Chrome to screenshot HTML mockups:
 78: 
 79: ```javascript
 80: const puppeteer = require('puppeteer-core');
 81: 
 82: const browser = await puppeteer.launch({
 83:   executablePath: '/Applications/Google Chrome.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome',
 84:   defaultViewport: { width: 900, height: 600, deviceScaleFactor: 2 }
 85: });
 86: 
 87: const page = await browser.newPage();
 88: await page.setContent(htmlString);
 89: 
 90: // Screenshot specific elements
 91: const elements = await page.$$('.section');
 92: for (let i = 0; i < elements.length; i++) {
 93:   await elements[i].screenshot({ path: `mockup-${i + 1}.png` });
 94: }
 95: 
 96: await browser.close();
 97: ```
 98: 
 99: > **Note:** MCP Playwright may not connect to localhost due to network isolation. Use puppeteer-core with a local Chrome installation instead.
100: 
101: ## Quick reference
102: 
103: | Method | Private repos | Permanent | No auth needed | API-only |
104: |--------|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|
105: | Contents API + `github.com/raw/` | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ |
106: | Browser drag-drop (`user-attachments`) | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ |
107: | `raw.githubusercontent.com` | ❌ (404) | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ |
108: | Gist | Public only | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ (no binary) |
109: 
110: ## Common pitfalls
111: 
112: - **`raw.githubusercontent.com` returns 404 for private repos** even with a valid token in the URL. GitHub's CDN does not pass auth headers through.
113: - **API download URLs are temporary.** URLs returned by `gh api repos/.../contents/...` with `download_url` include a token that expires.
114: - **`upload/policies/assets` requires a browser session.** Do not attempt to call this endpoint from the CLI.
115: - **Base64 encoding for large files** can hit API payload limits. The Contents API has a ~100MB file size limit but practical limits are lower for base64-encoded payloads.
116: - **Email notifications** will not render images that require authentication. If email readability matters, use the browser upload method.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-issues/references/issue-fields.md">
  1: # Issue Fields
  2: 
  3: Issue fields are custom metadata (dates, text, numbers, single-select) defined at the organization level and set per-issue. They are separate from labels, milestones, and assignees. Common examples: Start Date, Target Date, Priority, Impact, Effort.
  4: 
  5: **Prefer issue fields over project fields.** When you need to set metadata like dates, priority, or status on an issue, use issue fields (which live on the issue itself) rather than project fields (which live on a project item). Issue fields travel with the issue across projects and views, while project fields are scoped to a single project. Only use project fields when issue fields are not available or when the field is project-specific (e.g., sprint iterations).
  6: 
  7: ## REST API (recommended)
  8: 
  9: The REST API is the simplest way to discover fields and set values.
 10: 
 11: ### Discovering available fields
 12: 
 13: ```bash
 14: gh api orgs/{org}/issue-fields --jq '.[] | {id, name, options: [.options[]? | {id, name}]}'
 15: ```
 16: 
 17: ### Reading field values on an issue
 18: 
 19: ```bash
 20: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{number}/issue-field-values
 21: ```
 22: 
 23: ### Setting field values
 24: 
 25: ```bash
 26: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{number}/issue-field-values \
 27:   -X POST \
 28:   --input - <<'EOF'
 29: {"issue_field_values": [{"field_id": 1, "value": "P1"}]}
 30: EOF
 31: ```
 32: 
 33: **Important:** The payload must be a JSON object with an `issue_field_values` array. Each entry has:
 34: - `field_id` (integer): the field's numeric ID from the org fields list
 35: - `value` (string): the **option name** for single-select fields (e.g., `"P1"`, `"High"`), or the literal value for text/number/date fields
 36: 
 37: Common mistakes to avoid:
 38: - Passing the option ID instead of the option name as `value` (the API expects the display name)
 39: - Sending `field_id` and `value` as top-level keys without wrapping in `issue_field_values` array
 40: - Using `-f` flags instead of `--input` with JSON body
 41: 
 42: ### Example: Set priority to P1
 43: 
 44: ```bash
 45: # 1. Find the Priority field ID and option names
 46: gh api orgs/{org}/issue-fields --jq '.[] | select(.name == "Priority")'
 47: 
 48: # 2. Set it (use the option NAME, not ID)
 49: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{number}/issue-field-values \
 50:   -X POST \
 51:   --input - <<'EOF'
 52: {"issue_field_values": [{"field_id": 1, "value": "P1"}]}
 53: EOF
 54: ```
 55: 
 56: ### Example: Set multiple fields at once
 57: 
 58: ```bash
 59: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{number}/issue-field-values \
 60:   -X POST \
 61:   --input - <<'EOF'
 62: {"issue_field_values": [
 63:   {"field_id": 1, "value": "P1"},
 64:   {"field_id": 5, "value": "2026-06-01"},
 65:   {"field_id": 7, "value": "High"}
 66: ]}
 67: EOF
 68: ```
 69: 
 70: ### Workflow for setting fields (REST)
 71: 
 72: 1. **Discover fields** - `gh api orgs/{org}/issue-fields` to get field IDs and option names
 73: 2. **Set values** - POST to `repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{number}/issue-field-values` with JSON body
 74: 3. **Batch when possible** - multiple fields can be set in a single request
 75: 
 76: ## GraphQL API (alternative)
 77: 
 78: The GraphQL API requires the `GraphQL-Features: issue_fields` HTTP header. Without it, the fields are not visible in the schema.
 79: 
 80: ### Discovering available fields (GraphQL)
 81: 
 82: ```graphql
 83: # Header: GraphQL-Features: issue_fields
 84: {
 85:   organization(login: "OWNER") {
 86:     issueFields(first: 30) {
 87:       nodes {
 88:         __typename
 89:         ... on IssueFieldDate { id name }
 90:         ... on IssueFieldText { id name }
 91:         ... on IssueFieldNumber { id name }
 92:         ... on IssueFieldSingleSelect { id name options { id name color } }
 93:       }
 94:     }
 95:   }
 96: }
 97: ```
 98: 
 99: Field types: `IssueFieldDate`, `IssueFieldText`, `IssueFieldNumber`, `IssueFieldSingleSelect`.
100: 
101: ### Reading field values (GraphQL)
102: 
103: ```graphql
104: # Header: GraphQL-Features: issue_fields
105: {
106:   repository(owner: "OWNER", name: "REPO") {
107:     issue(number: 123) {
108:       issueFieldValues(first: 20) {
109:         nodes {
110:           __typename
111:           ... on IssueFieldDateValue {
112:             value
113:             field { ... on IssueFieldDate { id name } }
114:           }
115:           ... on IssueFieldTextValue {
116:             value
117:             field { ... on IssueFieldText { id name } }
118:           }
119:           ... on IssueFieldNumberValue {
120:             value
121:             field { ... on IssueFieldNumber { id name } }
122:           }
123:           ... on IssueFieldSingleSelectValue {
124:             name
125:             color
126:             field { ... on IssueFieldSingleSelect { id name } }
127:           }
128:         }
129:       }
130:     }
131:   }
132: }
133: ```
134: 
135: ### Setting field values (GraphQL)
136: 
137: Use `setIssueFieldValue` to set one or more fields at once. You need the issue's node ID and the field IDs from the discovery query above.
138: 
139: ```graphql
140: # Header: GraphQL-Features: issue_fields
141: mutation {
142:   setIssueFieldValue(input: {
143:     issueId: "ISSUE_NODE_ID"
144:     issueFields: [
145:       { fieldId: "IFD_xxx", dateValue: "2026-04-15" }
146:       { fieldId: "IFT_xxx", textValue: "some text" }
147:       { fieldId: "IFN_xxx", numberValue: 3.0 }
148:       { fieldId: "IFSS_xxx", singleSelectOptionId: "OPTION_ID" }
149:     ]
150:   }) {
151:     issue { id title }
152:   }
153: }
154: ```
155: 
156: Each entry in `issueFields` takes a `fieldId` plus exactly one value parameter:
157: 
158: | Field type | Value parameter | Format |
159: |-----------|----------------|--------|
160: | Date | `dateValue` | ISO 8601 date string, e.g. `"2026-04-15"` |
161: | Text | `textValue` | String |
162: | Number | `numberValue` | Float |
163: | Single select | `singleSelectOptionId` | Node ID from the field's `options` list |
164: 
165: To clear a field value, set `delete: true` instead of a value parameter.
166: 
167: ## Searching by field values
168: 
169: ### GraphQL bulk query (recommended)
170: 
171: The most reliable way to find issues by field value is to fetch issues via GraphQL and filter by `issueFieldValues`. The search qualifier syntax (`field.name:value`) is not yet reliable across all environments.
172: 
173: ```bash
174: # Find all open P1 issues in a repo
175: gh api graphql -H "GraphQL-Features: issue_fields" -f query='
176: {
177:   repository(owner: "OWNER", name: "REPO") {
178:     issues(first: 100, states: OPEN) {
179:       nodes {
180:         number
181:         title
182:         updatedAt
183:         assignees(first: 3) { nodes { login } }
184:         issueFieldValues(first: 10) {
185:           nodes {
186:             __typename
187:             ... on IssueFieldSingleSelectValue {
188:               name
189:               field { ... on IssueFieldSingleSelect { name } }
190:             }
191:           }
192:         }
193:       }
194:     }
195:   }
196: }' --jq '
197:   [.data.repository.issues.nodes[] |
198:     select(.issueFieldValues.nodes[] |
199:       select(.field.name == "Priority" and .name == "P1")
200:     ) |
201:     {number, title, updatedAt, assignees: [.assignees.nodes[].login]}
202:   ]'
203: ```
204: 
205: **Schema notes for `IssueFieldSingleSelectValue`:**
206: - The selected option's display text is in `.name` (not `.value`)
207: - Also available: `.color`, `.description`, `.id`
208: - The parent field reference is in `.field` (use inline fragment to get the field name)
209: 
210: ### Search qualifier syntax (experimental)
211: 
212: Issue fields may also be searchable using dot notation in search queries. This requires `advanced_search=true` on REST or `ISSUE_ADVANCED` search type on GraphQL, but results are inconsistent and may return 0 results even when matching issues exist.
213: 
214: ```
215: field.priority:P0                  # Single-select equals value
216: field.target-date:>=2026-04-01     # Date comparison
217: has:field.priority                 # Has any value set
218: no:field.priority                  # Has no value set
219: ```
220: 
221: Field names use the **slug** (lowercase, hyphens for spaces). For example, "Target Date" becomes `target-date`.
222: 
223: ```bash
224: # REST API (may not return results in all environments)
225: gh api "search/issues?q=repo:owner/repo+field.priority:P0+is:open&advanced_search=true" \
226:   --jq '.items[] | "#\(.number): \(.title)"'
227: ```
228: 
229: > **Warning:** The colon notation (`field:Priority:P1`) is silently ignored. If using search qualifiers, always use dot notation (`field.priority:P1`). However, the GraphQL bulk query approach above is more reliable. See [search.md](search.md) for the full search guide.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-issues/references/issue-types.md">
 1: # Issue Types (Advanced GraphQL)
 2: 
 3: Issue types (Bug, Feature, Task, Epic, etc.) are defined at the **organization** level and inherited by repositories. They categorize issues beyond labels.
 4: 
 5: For basic usage, the MCP tools handle issue types natively. Call `mcp__github__list_issue_types` to discover types, and pass `type: "Bug"` to `mcp__github__create_issue` or `mcp__github__update_issue`. This reference covers advanced GraphQL operations.
 6: 
 7: ## GraphQL Feature Header
 8: 
 9: All GraphQL issue type operations require the `GraphQL-Features: issue_types` HTTP header.
10: 
11: ## List types (org or repo level)
12: 
13: ```graphql
14: # Header: GraphQL-Features: issue_types
15: {
16:   organization(login: "OWNER") {
17:     issueTypes(first: 20) {
18:       nodes { id name color description isEnabled }
19:     }
20:   }
21: }
22: ```
23: 
24: Types can also be listed per-repo via `repository.issueTypes` or looked up by name via `repository.issueType(name: "Bug")`.
25: 
26: ## Read an issue's type
27: 
28: ```graphql
29: # Header: GraphQL-Features: issue_types
30: {
31:   repository(owner: "OWNER", name: "REPO") {
32:     issue(number: 123) {
33:       issueType { id name color }
34:     }
35:   }
36: }
37: ```
38: 
39: ## Set type on an existing issue
40: 
41: ```graphql
42: # Header: GraphQL-Features: issue_types
43: mutation {
44:   updateIssueIssueType(input: {
45:     issueId: "ISSUE_NODE_ID"
46:     issueTypeId: "IT_xxx"
47:   }) {
48:     issue { id issueType { name } }
49:   }
50: }
51: ```
52: 
53: ## Create issue with type
54: 
55: ```graphql
56: # Header: GraphQL-Features: issue_types
57: mutation {
58:   createIssue(input: {
59:     repositoryId: "REPO_NODE_ID"
60:     title: "Fix login bug"
61:     issueTypeId: "IT_xxx"
62:   }) {
63:     issue { id number issueType { name } }
64:   }
65: }
66: ```
67: 
68: To clear the type, set `issueTypeId` to `null`.
69: 
70: ## Available colors
71: 
72: `GRAY`, `BLUE`, `GREEN`, `YELLOW`, `ORANGE`, `RED`, `PINK`, `PURPLE`
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-issues/references/projects.md">
  1: # Projects V2
  2: 
  3: GitHub Projects V2 is managed via GraphQL. The MCP server provides three tools that wrap the GraphQL API, so you typically don't need raw GraphQL.
  4: 
  5: ## Using MCP tools (preferred)
  6: 
  7: **List projects:**
  8: Call `mcp__github__projects_list` with `method: "list_projects"`, `owner`, and `owner_type` ("user" or "organization").
  9: 
 10: **List project fields:**
 11: Call `mcp__github__projects_list` with `method: "list_project_fields"` and `project_number`.
 12: 
 13: **List project items:**
 14: Call `mcp__github__projects_list` with `method: "list_project_items"` and `project_number`.
 15: 
 16: **Add an issue/PR to a project:**
 17: Call `mcp__github__projects_write` with `method: "add_project_item"`, `project_id` (node ID), and `content_id` (issue/PR node ID).
 18: 
 19: **Update a project item field value:**
 20: Call `mcp__github__projects_write` with `method: "update_project_item"`, `project_id`, `item_id`, `field_id`, and `value` (object with one of: `text`, `number`, `date`, `singleSelectOptionId`, `iterationId`).
 21: 
 22: **Delete a project item:**
 23: Call `mcp__github__projects_write` with `method: "delete_project_item"`, `project_id`, and `item_id`.
 24: 
 25: ## Workflow for project operations
 26: 
 27: 1. **Find the project** — see [Finding a project by name](#finding-a-project-by-name) below
 28: 2. **Discover fields** - use `projects_list` with `list_project_fields` to get field IDs and option IDs
 29: 3. **Find items** - use `projects_list` with `list_project_items` to get item IDs
 30: 4. **Mutate** - use `projects_write` to add, update, or delete items
 31: 
 32: ## Finding a project by name
 33: 
 34: > **⚠️ Known issue:** `projectsV2(query: "…")` does keyword search, not exact name match, and returns results sorted by recency. Common words like "issue" or "bug" return hundreds of false positives. The actual project may be buried dozens of pages deep.
 35: 
 36: Use this priority order:
 37: 
 38: ### 1. Direct lookup (if you know the number)
 39: ```bash
 40: gh api graphql -f query='{
 41:   organization(login: "ORG") {
 42:     projectV2(number: 42) { id title }
 43:   }
 44: }' --jq '.data.organization.projectV2'
 45: ```
 46: 
 47: ### 2. Reverse lookup from a known issue (most reliable)
 48: If the user mentions an issue, epic, or milestone that's in the project, query that issue's `projectItems` to discover the project:
 49: 
 50: ```bash
 51: gh api graphql -f query='{
 52:   repository(owner: "OWNER", name: "REPO") {
 53:     issue(number: 123) {
 54:       projectItems(first: 10) {
 55:         nodes {
 56:           id
 57:           project { number title id }
 58:         }
 59:       }
 60:     }
 61:   }
 62: }' --jq '.data.repository.issue.projectItems.nodes[] | {number: .project.number, title: .project.title, id: .project.id}'
 63: ```
 64: 
 65: This is the most reliable approach for large orgs where name search fails.
 66: 
 67: ### 3. GraphQL name search with client-side filtering (fallback)
 68: Query a large page and filter client-side for an exact title match:
 69: 
 70: ```bash
 71: gh api graphql -f query='{
 72:   organization(login: "ORG") {
 73:     projectsV2(first: 100, query: "search term") {
 74:       nodes { number title id }
 75:     }
 76:   }
 77: }' --jq '.data.organization.projectsV2.nodes[] | select(.title | test("(?i)^exact name$"))'
 78: ```
 79: 
 80: If this returns nothing, paginate with `after` cursor or broaden the regex. Results are sorted by recency so older projects require pagination.
 81: 
 82: ### 4. MCP tool (small orgs only)
 83: Call `mcp__github__projects_list` with `method: "list_projects"`. This works well for orgs with <50 projects but has no name filter, so you must scan all results.
 84: 
 85: ## Project discovery for progress reports
 86: 
 87: When a user asks for a progress update on a project (e.g., "Give me a progress update for Project X"), follow this workflow:
 88: 
 89: 1. **Find the project** — use the [finding a project](#finding-a-project-by-name) strategies above. Ask the user for a known issue number if name search fails.
 90: 
 91: 2. **Discover fields** - call `projects_list` with `list_project_fields` to find the Status field (its options tell you the workflow stages) and any Iteration field (to scope to the current sprint).
 92: 
 93: 3. **Get all items** - call `projects_list` with `list_project_items`. For large projects (100+ items), paginate through all pages. Each item includes its field values (status, iteration, assignees).
 94: 
 95: 4. **Build the report** - group items by Status field value and count them. For iteration-based projects, filter to the current iteration first. Present a breakdown like:
 96: 
 97:    ```
 98:    Project: Issue Fields (Iteration 42, Mar 2-8)
 99:    15 actionable items:
100:      🎉 Done:        4 (27%)
101:      In Review:      3
102:      In Progress:    3
103:      Ready:          2
104:      Blocked:        2
105:    ```
106: 
107: 5. **Add context** - if items have sub-issues, include `subIssuesSummary` counts. If items have dependencies, note blocked items and what blocks them.
108: 
109: ## OAuth Scope Requirements
110: 
111: | Operation | Required scope |
112: |-----------|---------------|
113: | Read projects, fields, items | `read:project` |
114: | Add/update/delete items, change field values | `project` |
115: 
116: **Common pitfall:** The default `gh auth` token often only has `read:project`. Mutations will fail with `INSUFFICIENT_SCOPES`. To add the write scope:
117: 
118: ```bash
119: gh auth refresh -h github.com -s project
120: ```
121: 
122: This triggers a browser-based OAuth flow. You must complete it before mutations will work.
123: 
124: ## Finding an Issue's Project Item ID
125: 
126: When you know the issue but need its project item ID (e.g., to update its Status), query from the issue side:
127: 
128: ```bash
129: gh api graphql -f query='
130: {
131:   repository(owner: "OWNER", name: "REPO") {
132:     issue(number: 123) {
133:       projectItems(first: 5) {
134:         nodes {
135:           id
136:           project { title number }
137:           fieldValues(first: 10) {
138:             nodes {
139:               ... on ProjectV2ItemFieldSingleSelectValue {
140:                 name
141:                 field { ... on ProjectV2SingleSelectField { name } }
142:               }
143:             }
144:           }
145:         }
146:       }
147:     }
148:   }
149: }' --jq '.data.repository.issue.projectItems.nodes'
150: ```
151: 
152: This returns the item ID, project info, and current field values in one query.
153: 
154: ## Using GraphQL via gh api (recommended)
155: 
156: Use `gh api graphql` to run GraphQL queries and mutations. This is more reliable than MCP tools for write operations.
157: 
158: **Find a project and its Status field options:**
159: ```bash
160: gh api graphql -f query='
161: {
162:   organization(login: "ORG") {
163:     projectV2(number: 5) {
164:       id
165:       title
166:       field(name: "Status") {
167:         ... on ProjectV2SingleSelectField {
168:           id
169:           options { id name }
170:         }
171:       }
172:     }
173:   }
174: }' --jq '.data.organization.projectV2'
175: ```
176: 
177: **List all fields (including iterations):**
178: ```bash
179: gh api graphql -f query='
180: {
181:   node(id: "PROJECT_ID") {
182:     ... on ProjectV2 {
183:       fields(first: 20) {
184:         nodes {
185:           ... on ProjectV2Field { id name }
186:           ... on ProjectV2SingleSelectField { id name options { id name } }
187:           ... on ProjectV2IterationField { id name configuration { iterations { id startDate } } }
188:         }
189:       }
190:     }
191:   }
192: }' --jq '.data.node.fields.nodes'
193: ```
194: 
195: **Update a field value (e.g., set Status to "In Progress"):**
196: ```bash
197: gh api graphql -f query='
198: mutation {
199:   updateProjectV2ItemFieldValue(input: {
200:     projectId: "PROJECT_ID"
201:     itemId: "ITEM_ID"
202:     fieldId: "FIELD_ID"
203:     value: { singleSelectOptionId: "OPTION_ID" }
204:   }) {
205:     projectV2Item { id }
206:   }
207: }'
208: ```
209: 
210: Value accepts one of: `text`, `number`, `date`, `singleSelectOptionId`, `iterationId`.
211: 
212: **Add an item:**
213: ```bash
214: gh api graphql -f query='
215: mutation {
216:   addProjectV2ItemById(input: {
217:     projectId: "PROJECT_ID"
218:     contentId: "ISSUE_OR_PR_NODE_ID"
219:   }) {
220:     item { id }
221:   }
222: }'
223: ```
224: 
225: **Delete an item:**
226: ```bash
227: gh api graphql -f query='
228: mutation {
229:   deleteProjectV2Item(input: {
230:     projectId: "PROJECT_ID"
231:     itemId: "ITEM_ID"
232:   }) {
233:     deletedItemId
234:   }
235: }'
236: ```
237: 
238: ## End-to-End Example: Set Issue Status to "In Progress"
239: 
240: ```bash
241: # 1. Get the issue's project item ID, project ID, and current status
242: gh api graphql -f query='{
243:   repository(owner: "github", name: "planning-tracking") {
244:     issue(number: 2574) {
245:       projectItems(first: 1) {
246:         nodes { id project { id title } }
247:       }
248:     }
249:   }
250: }' --jq '.data.repository.issue.projectItems.nodes[0]'
251: 
252: # 2. Get the Status field ID and "In Progress" option ID
253: gh api graphql -f query='{
254:   node(id: "PROJECT_ID") {
255:     ... on ProjectV2 {
256:       field(name: "Status") {
257:         ... on ProjectV2SingleSelectField { id options { id name } }
258:       }
259:     }
260:   }
261: }' --jq '.data.node.field'
262: 
263: # 3. Update the status
264: gh api graphql -f query='mutation {
265:   updateProjectV2ItemFieldValue(input: {
266:     projectId: "PROJECT_ID"
267:     itemId: "ITEM_ID"
268:     fieldId: "FIELD_ID"
269:     value: { singleSelectOptionId: "IN_PROGRESS_OPTION_ID" }
270:   }) { projectV2Item { id } }
271: }'
272: ```
273: ```
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-issues/references/search.md">
  1: # Advanced Issue Search
  2: 
  3: The `search_issues` MCP tool uses GitHub's issue search query format for cross-repo searches, supporting implicit-AND queries, date ranges, and metadata filters (but not explicit OR/NOT operators).
  4: 
  5: ## When to Use Search vs List vs Advanced Search
  6: 
  7: There are three ways to find issues, each with different capabilities:
  8: 
  9: | Capability | `list_issues` (MCP) | `search_issues` (MCP) | Advanced search (`gh api`) |
 10: |-----------|---------------------|----------------------|---------------------------|
 11: | **Scope** | Single repo only | Cross-repo, cross-org | Cross-repo, cross-org |
 12: | **Issue field filters** (`field.priority:P0`) | No | No | **Yes** (dot notation) |
 13: | **Issue type filter** (`type:Bug`) | No | Yes | Yes |
 14: | **Boolean logic** (AND/OR/NOT, nesting) | No | Yes (implicit AND only) | **Yes** (explicit AND/OR/NOT) |
 15: | **Label/state/date filters** | Yes | Yes | Yes |
 16: | **Assignee/author/mentions** | No | Yes | Yes |
 17: | **Negation** (`-label:x`, `no:label`) | No | Yes | Yes |
 18: | **Text search** (title/body/comments) | No | Yes | Yes |
 19: | **`since` filter** | Yes | No | No |
 20: | **Result limit** | No cap (paginate all) | 1,000 max | 1,000 max |
 21: | **How to call** | MCP tool directly | MCP tool directly | `gh api` with `advanced_search=true` |
 22: 
 23: **Decision guide:**
 24: - **Single repo, simple filters (state, labels, recent updates):** use `list_issues`
 25: - **Cross-repo, text search, author/assignee, issue types:** use `search_issues`
 26: - **Issue field values (Priority, dates, custom fields) or complex boolean logic:** use `gh api` with `advanced_search=true`
 27: 
 28: ## Query Syntax
 29: 
 30: The `query` parameter is a string of search terms and qualifiers. A space between terms is implicit AND.
 31: 
 32: ### Scoping
 33: 
 34: ```
 35: repo:owner/repo       # Single repo (auto-added if you pass owner+repo params)
 36: org:github            # All repos in an org
 37: user:octocat          # All repos owned by user
 38: in:title              # Search only in title
 39: in:body               # Search only in body
 40: in:comments           # Search only in comments
 41: ```
 42: 
 43: ### State & Close Reason
 44: 
 45: ```
 46: is:open               # Open issues (auto-added: is:issue)
 47: is:closed             # Closed issues
 48: reason:completed      # Closed as completed
 49: reason:"not planned"  # Closed as not planned
 50: ```
 51: 
 52: ### People
 53: 
 54: ```
 55: author:username       # Created by
 56: assignee:username     # Assigned to
 57: mentions:username     # Mentions user
 58: commenter:username    # Has comment from
 59: involves:username     # Author OR assignee OR mentioned OR commenter
 60: author:@me            # Current authenticated user
 61: team:org/team         # Team mentioned
 62: ```
 63: 
 64: ### Labels, Milestones, Projects, Types
 65: 
 66: ```
 67: label:"bug"                 # Has label (quote multi-word labels)
 68: label:bug label:priority    # Has BOTH labels (AND)
 69: label:bug,enhancement       # Has EITHER label (OR)
 70: -label:wontfix              # Does NOT have label
 71: milestone:"v2.0"            # In milestone
 72: project:github/57           # In project board
 73: type:"Bug"                  # Issue type
 74: ```
 75: 
 76: ### Missing Metadata
 77: 
 78: ```
 79: no:label              # No labels assigned
 80: no:milestone          # No milestone
 81: no:assignee           # Unassigned
 82: no:project            # Not in any project
 83: ```
 84: 
 85: ### Dates
 86: 
 87: All date qualifiers support `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`, and range (`..`) operators with ISO 8601 format:
 88: 
 89: ```
 90: created:>2026-01-01              # Created after Jan 1
 91: updated:>=2026-03-01             # Updated since Mar 1
 92: closed:2026-01-01..2026-02-01   # Closed in January
 93: created:<2026-01-01              # Created before Jan 1
 94: ```
 95: 
 96: ### Linked Content
 97: 
 98: ```
 99: linked:pr             # Issue has a linked PR
100: -linked:pr            # Issues not yet linked to any PR
101: linked:issue          # PR is linked to an issue
102: ```
103: 
104: ### Numeric Filters
105: 
106: ```
107: comments:>10          # More than 10 comments
108: comments:0            # No comments
109: interactions:>100     # Reactions + comments > 100
110: reactions:>50         # More than 50 reactions
111: ```
112: 
113: ### Boolean Logic & Nesting
114: 
115: Use `AND`, `OR`, and parentheses (up to 5 levels deep, max 5 operators):
116: 
117: ```
118: label:bug AND assignee:octocat
119: assignee:octocat OR assignee:hubot
120: (type:"Bug" AND label:P1) OR (type:"Feature" AND label:P1)
121: -author:app/dependabot          # Exclude bot issues
122: ```
123: 
124: A space between terms without an explicit operator is treated as AND.
125: 
126: ## Common Query Patterns
127: 
128: **Unassigned bugs:**
129: ```
130: repo:owner/repo type:"Bug" no:assignee is:open
131: ```
132: 
133: **Issues closed this week:**
134: ```
135: repo:owner/repo is:closed closed:>=2026-03-01
136: ```
137: 
138: **Stale open issues (no updates in 90 days):**
139: ```
140: repo:owner/repo is:open updated:<2026-01-01
141: ```
142: 
143: **Open issues without a linked PR (needs work):**
144: ```
145: repo:owner/repo is:open -linked:pr
146: ```
147: 
148: **Issues I'm involved in across an org:**
149: ```
150: org:github involves:@me is:open
151: ```
152: 
153: **High-activity issues:**
154: ```
155: repo:owner/repo is:open comments:>20
156: ```
157: 
158: **Issues by type and priority label:**
159: ```
160: repo:owner/repo type:"Epic" label:P1 is:open
161: ```
162: 
163: ## Issue Field Search
164: 
165: > **Reliability warning:** The `field.name:value` search qualifier syntax is experimental and may return 0 results even when matching issues exist. For reliable filtering by field values, use the GraphQL bulk query approach documented in [issue-fields.md](issue-fields.md#searching-by-field-values).
166: 
167: Issue fields can theoretically be searched via the `field.name:value` qualifier using **advanced search mode**. This works in the web UI but results from the API are inconsistent.
168: 
169: ### REST API
170: 
171: Add `advanced_search=true` as a query parameter:
172: 
173: ```bash
174: gh api "search/issues?q=org:github+field.priority:P0+type:Epic+is:open&advanced_search=true" \
175:   --jq '.items[] | "#\(.number): \(.title)"'
176: ```
177: 
178: ### GraphQL
179: 
180: Use `type: ISSUE_ADVANCED` instead of `type: ISSUE`:
181: 
182: ```graphql
183: {
184:   search(query: "org:github field.priority:P0 type:Epic is:open", type: ISSUE_ADVANCED, first: 10) {
185:     issueCount
186:     nodes {
187:       ... on Issue { number title }
188:     }
189:   }
190: }
191: ```
192: 
193: ### Issue Field Qualifiers
194: 
195: The syntax uses **dot notation** with the field's slug name (lowercase, hyphens for spaces):
196: 
197: ```
198: field.priority:P0                  # Single-select field equals value
199: field.priority:P1                  # Different option value
200: field.target-date:>=2026-04-01     # Date comparison
201: has:field.priority                 # Has any value set
202: no:field.priority                  # Has no value set
203: ```
204: 
205: **MCP limitation:** The `search_issues` MCP tool does not pass `advanced_search=true`. You must use `gh api` directly for issue field searches.
206: 
207: ### Common Field Search Patterns
208: 
209: **P0 epics across an org:**
210: ```
211: org:github field.priority:P0 type:Epic is:open
212: ```
213: 
214: **Issues with a target date this quarter:**
215: ```
216: org:github field.target-date:>=2026-04-01 field.target-date:<=2026-06-30 is:open
217: ```
218: 
219: **Open bugs missing priority:**
220: ```
221: org:github no:field.priority type:Bug is:open
222: ```
223: 
224: ## Limitations
225: 
226: - Query text: max **256 characters** (excluding operators/qualifiers)
227: - Boolean operators: max **5** AND/OR/NOT per query
228: - Results: max **1,000** total (use `list_issues` if you need all issues)
229: - Repo scan: searches up to **4,000** matching repositories
230: - Rate limit: **30 requests/minute** for authenticated search
231: - Issue field search requires `advanced_search=true` (REST) or `ISSUE_ADVANCED` (GraphQL); not available through MCP `search_issues`
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-issues/references/sub-issues.md">
  1: # Sub-Issues and Parent Issues
  2: 
  3: Sub-issues let you break down work into hierarchical tasks. Each parent issue can have up to 100 sub-issues, nested up to 8 levels deep. Sub-issues can span repositories within the same owner.
  4: 
  5: ## Recommended Workflow
  6: 
  7: The simplest way to create a sub-issue is **two steps**: create the issue, then link it.
  8: 
  9: ```bash
 10: # Step 1: Create the issue and capture its numeric ID
 11: ISSUE_ID=$(gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues \
 12:   -X POST \
 13:   -f title="Sub-task title" \
 14:   -f body="Description" \
 15:   --jq '.id')
 16: 
 17: # Step 2: Link it as a sub-issue of the parent
 18: # IMPORTANT: sub_issue_id must be an integer. Use --input (not -f) to send JSON.
 19: echo "{\"sub_issue_id\": $ISSUE_ID}" | gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{parent_number}/sub_issues -X POST --input -
 20: ```
 21: 
 22: **Why `--input` instead of `-f`?** The `gh api -f` flag sends all values as strings, but the API requires `sub_issue_id` as an integer. Using `-f sub_issue_id=12345` will return a 422 error.
 23: 
 24: Alternatively, use GraphQL `createIssue` with `parentIssueId` to do it in one step (see GraphQL section below).
 25: 
 26: ## Using MCP tools
 27: 
 28: **List sub-issues:**
 29: Call `mcp__github__issue_read` with `method: "get_sub_issues"`, `owner`, `repo`, and `issue_number`.
 30: 
 31: **Create an issue as a sub-issue:**
 32: There is no MCP tool for creating sub-issues directly. Use the workflow above or GraphQL.
 33: 
 34: ## Using REST API
 35: 
 36: **List sub-issues:**
 37: ```bash
 38: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{issue_number}/sub_issues
 39: ```
 40: 
 41: **Get parent issue:**
 42: ```bash
 43: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{issue_number}/parent
 44: ```
 45: 
 46: **Add an existing issue as a sub-issue:**
 47: ```bash
 48: # sub_issue_id is the numeric issue ID (not the issue number)
 49: # Get it from the .id field when creating or fetching an issue
 50: echo '{"sub_issue_id": 12345}' | gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{parent_number}/sub_issues -X POST --input -
 51: ```
 52: 
 53: To move a sub-issue that already has a parent, add `"replace_parent": true` to the JSON body.
 54: 
 55: **Remove a sub-issue:**
 56: ```bash
 57: echo '{"sub_issue_id": 12345}' | gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{parent_number}/sub_issue -X DELETE --input -
 58: ```
 59: 
 60: **Reprioritize a sub-issue:**
 61: ```bash
 62: echo '{"sub_issue_id": 6, "after_id": 5}' | gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{parent_number}/sub_issues/priority -X PATCH --input -
 63: ```
 64: 
 65: Use `after_id` or `before_id` to position the sub-issue relative to another.
 66: 
 67: ## Using GraphQL
 68: 
 69: **Read parent and sub-issues:**
 70: ```graphql
 71: {
 72:   repository(owner: "OWNER", name: "REPO") {
 73:     issue(number: 123) {
 74:       parent { number title }
 75:       subIssues(first: 50) {
 76:         nodes { number title state }
 77:       }
 78:       subIssuesSummary { total completed percentCompleted }
 79:     }
 80:   }
 81: }
 82: ```
 83: 
 84: **Add a sub-issue:**
 85: ```graphql
 86: mutation {
 87:   addSubIssue(input: {
 88:     issueId: "PARENT_NODE_ID"
 89:     subIssueId: "CHILD_NODE_ID"
 90:   }) {
 91:     issue { id }
 92:     subIssue { id number title }
 93:   }
 94: }
 95: ```
 96: 
 97: You can also use `subIssueUrl` instead of `subIssueId` (pass the issue's HTML URL). Add `replaceParent: true` to move a sub-issue from another parent.
 98: 
 99: **Create an issue directly as a sub-issue:**
100: ```graphql
101: mutation {
102:   createIssue(input: {
103:     repositoryId: "REPO_NODE_ID"
104:     title: "Implement login validation"
105:     parentIssueId: "PARENT_NODE_ID"
106:   }) {
107:     issue { id number }
108:   }
109: }
110: ```
111: 
112: **Remove a sub-issue:**
113: ```graphql
114: mutation {
115:   removeSubIssue(input: {
116:     issueId: "PARENT_NODE_ID"
117:     subIssueId: "CHILD_NODE_ID"
118:   }) {
119:     issue { id }
120:   }
121: }
122: ```
123: 
124: **Reprioritize a sub-issue:**
125: ```graphql
126: mutation {
127:   reprioritizeSubIssue(input: {
128:     issueId: "PARENT_NODE_ID"
129:     subIssueId: "CHILD_NODE_ID"
130:     afterId: "OTHER_CHILD_NODE_ID"
131:   }) {
132:     issue { id }
133:   }
134: }
135: ```
136: 
137: Use `afterId` or `beforeId` to position relative to another sub-issue.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-issues/references/templates.md">
 1: # Issue Templates
 2: 
 3: Copy and customize these templates for issue bodies.
 4: 
 5: ## Bug Report Template
 6: 
 7: ```markdown
 8: ## Description
 9: [Clear description of the bug]
10: 
11: ## Steps to Reproduce
12: 1. [First step]
13: 2. [Second step]
14: 3. [And so on...]
15: 
16: ## Expected Behavior
17: [What should happen]
18: 
19: ## Actual Behavior
20: [What actually happens]
21: 
22: ## Environment
23: - Browser: [e.g., Chrome 120]
24: - OS: [e.g., macOS 14.0]
25: - Version: [e.g., v1.2.3]
26: 
27: ## Screenshots/Logs
28: [If applicable]
29: 
30: ## Additional Context
31: [Any other relevant information]
32: ```
33: 
34: ## Feature Request Template
35: 
36: ```markdown
37: ## Summary
38: [One-line description of the feature]
39: 
40: ## Motivation
41: [Why is this feature needed? What problem does it solve?]
42: 
43: ## Proposed Solution
44: [How should this feature work?]
45: 
46: ## Acceptance Criteria
47: - [ ] [Criterion 1]
48: - [ ] [Criterion 2]
49: - [ ] [Criterion 3]
50: 
51: ## Alternatives Considered
52: [Other approaches considered and why they weren't chosen]
53: 
54: ## Additional Context
55: [Mockups, examples, or related issues]
56: ```
57: 
58: ## Task Template
59: 
60: ```markdown
61: ## Objective
62: [What needs to be accomplished]
63: 
64: ## Details
65: [Detailed description of the work]
66: 
67: ## Checklist
68: - [ ] [Subtask 1]
69: - [ ] [Subtask 2]
70: - [ ] [Subtask 3]
71: 
72: ## Dependencies
73: [Any blockers or related work]
74: 
75: ## Notes
76: [Additional context or considerations]
77: ```
78: 
79: ## Minimal Template
80: 
81: For simple issues:
82: 
83: ```markdown
84: ## Description
85: [What and why]
86: 
87: ## Tasks
88: - [ ] [Task 1]
89: - [ ] [Task 2]
90: ```
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-issues/SKILL.md">
  1: ---
  2: name: github-issues
  3: description: 'Create, update, and manage GitHub issues using MCP tools. Use this skill when users want to create bug reports, feature requests, or task issues, update existing issues, add labels/assignees/milestones, set issue fields (dates, priority, custom fields), set issue types, manage issue workflows, link issues, add dependencies, or track blocked-by/blocking relationships. Triggers on requests like "create an issue", "file a bug", "request a feature", "update issue X", "set the priority", "set the start date", "link issues", "add dependency", "blocked by", "blocking", or any GitHub issue management task.'
  4: ---
  5: 
  6: # GitHub Issues
  7: 
  8: Manage GitHub issues using the `@modelcontextprotocol/server-github` MCP server.
  9: 
 10: ## Available Tools
 11: 
 12: ### MCP Tools (read operations)
 13: 
 14: | Tool | Purpose |
 15: |------|---------|
 16: | `mcp__github__issue_read` | Read issue details, sub-issues, comments, labels (methods: get, get_comments, get_sub_issues, get_labels) |
 17: | `mcp__github__list_issues` | List and filter repository issues by state, labels, date |
 18: | `mcp__github__search_issues` | Search issues across repos using GitHub search syntax |
 19: | `mcp__github__projects_list` | List projects, project fields, project items, status updates |
 20: | `mcp__github__projects_get` | Get details of a project, field, item, or status update |
 21: | `mcp__github__projects_write` | Add/update/delete project items, create status updates |
 22: 
 23: ### CLI / REST API (write operations)
 24: 
 25: The MCP server does not currently support creating, updating, or commenting on issues. Use `gh api` for these operations.
 26: 
 27: | Operation | Command |
 28: |-----------|---------|
 29: | Create issue | `gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues -X POST -f title=... -f body=...` |
 30: | Update issue | `gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{number} -X PATCH -f title=... -f state=...` |
 31: | Add comment | `gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{number}/comments -X POST -f body=...` |
 32: | Close issue | `gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{number} -X PATCH -f state=closed` |
 33: | Set issue type | Include `-f type=Bug` in the create call (REST API only, not supported by `gh issue create` CLI) |
 34: 
 35: **Note:** `gh issue create` works for basic issue creation but does **not** support the `--type` flag. Use `gh api` when you need to set issue types.
 36: 
 37: ## Workflow
 38: 
 39: 1. **Determine action**: Create, update, or query?
 40: 2. **Gather context**: Get repo info, existing labels, milestones if needed
 41: 3. **Structure content**: Use appropriate template from [references/templates.md](references/templates.md)
 42: 4. **Execute**: Use MCP tools for reads, `gh api` for writes
 43: 5. **Confirm**: Report the issue URL to user
 44: 
 45: ## Creating Issues
 46: 
 47: Use `gh api` to create issues. This supports all parameters including issue types.
 48: 
 49: ```bash
 50: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues \
 51:   -X POST \
 52:   -f title="Issue title" \
 53:   -f body="Issue body in markdown" \
 54:   -f type="Bug" \
 55:   --jq '{number, html_url}'
 56: ```
 57: 
 58: ### Optional Parameters
 59: 
 60: Add any of these flags to the `gh api` call:
 61: 
 62: ```
 63: -f type="Bug"                    # Issue type (Bug, Feature, Task, Epic, etc.)
 64: -f labels[]="bug"                # Labels (repeat for multiple)
 65: -f assignees[]="username"        # Assignees (repeat for multiple)
 66: -f milestone=1                   # Milestone number
 67: ```
 68: 
 69: **Issue types** are organization-level metadata. To discover available types, use:
 70: ```bash
 71: gh api graphql -f query='{ organization(login: "ORG") { issueTypes(first: 10) { nodes { name } } } }' --jq '.data.organization.issueTypes.nodes[].name'
 72: ```
 73: 
 74: **Prefer issue types over labels for categorization.** When issue types are available (e.g., Bug, Feature, Task), use the `type` parameter instead of applying equivalent labels like `bug` or `enhancement`. Issue types are the canonical way to categorize issues on GitHub. Only fall back to labels when the org has no issue types configured.
 75: 
 76: ### Title Guidelines
 77: 
 78: - Be specific and actionable
 79: - Keep under 72 characters
 80: - When issue types are set, don't add redundant prefixes like `[Bug]`
 81: - Examples:
 82:   - `Login fails with SSO enabled` (with type=Bug)
 83:   - `Add dark mode support` (with type=Feature)
 84:   - `Add unit tests for auth module` (with type=Task)
 85: 
 86: ### Body Structure
 87: 
 88: Always use the templates in [references/templates.md](references/templates.md). Choose based on issue type:
 89: 
 90: | User Request | Template |
 91: |--------------|----------|
 92: | Bug, error, broken, not working | Bug Report |
 93: | Feature, enhancement, add, new | Feature Request |
 94: | Task, chore, refactor, update | Task |
 95: 
 96: ## Updating Issues
 97: 
 98: Use `gh api` with PATCH:
 99: 
100: ```bash
101: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{number} \
102:   -X PATCH \
103:   -f state=closed \
104:   -f title="Updated title" \
105:   --jq '{number, html_url}'
106: ```
107: 
108: Only include fields you want to change. Available fields: `title`, `body`, `state` (open/closed), `labels`, `assignees`, `milestone`.
109: 
110: ## Examples
111: 
112: ### Example 1: Bug Report
113: 
114: **User**: "Create a bug issue - the login page crashes when using SSO"
115: 
116: **Action**: 
117: ```bash
118: gh api repos/github/awesome-copilot/issues \
119:   -X POST \
120:   -f title="Login page crashes when using SSO" \
121:   -f type="Bug" \
122:   -f body="## Description
123: The login page crashes when users attempt to authenticate using SSO.
124: 
125: ## Steps to Reproduce
126: 1. Navigate to login page
127: 2. Click 'Sign in with SSO'
128: 3. Page crashes
129: 
130: ## Expected Behavior
131: SSO authentication should complete and redirect to dashboard.
132: 
133: ## Actual Behavior
134: Page becomes unresponsive and displays error." \
135:   --jq '{number, html_url}'
136: ```
137: 
138: ### Example 2: Feature Request
139: 
140: **User**: "Create a feature request for dark mode with high priority"
141: 
142: **Action**:
143: ```bash
144: gh api repos/github/awesome-copilot/issues \
145:   -X POST \
146:   -f title="Add dark mode support" \
147:   -f type="Feature" \
148:   -f labels[]="high-priority" \
149:   -f body="## Summary
150: Add dark mode theme option for improved user experience and accessibility.
151: 
152: ## Motivation
153: - Reduces eye strain in low-light environments
154: - Increasingly expected by users
155: 
156: ## Proposed Solution
157: Implement theme toggle with system preference detection.
158: 
159: ## Acceptance Criteria
160: - [ ] Toggle switch in settings
161: - [ ] Persists user preference
162: - [ ] Respects system preference by default" \
163:   --jq '{number, html_url}'
164: ```
165: 
166: ## Common Labels
167: 
168: Use these standard labels when applicable:
169: 
170: | Label | Use For |
171: |-------|---------|
172: | `bug` | Something isn't working |
173: | `enhancement` | New feature or improvement |
174: | `documentation` | Documentation updates |
175: | `good first issue` | Good for newcomers |
176: | `help wanted` | Extra attention needed |
177: | `question` | Further information requested |
178: | `wontfix` | Will not be addressed |
179: | `duplicate` | Already exists |
180: | `high-priority` | Urgent issues |
181: 
182: ## Tips
183: 
184: - Always confirm the repository context before creating issues
185: - Ask for missing critical information rather than guessing
186: - Link related issues when known: `Related to #123`
187: - For updates, fetch current issue first to preserve unchanged fields
188: 
189: ## Extended Capabilities
190: 
191: The following features require REST or GraphQL APIs beyond the basic MCP tools. Each is documented in its own reference file so the agent only loads the knowledge it needs.
192: 
193: | Capability | When to use | Reference |
194: |------------|-------------|-----------|
195: | Advanced search | Complex queries with boolean logic, date ranges, cross-repo search, issue field filters (`field.name:value`) | [references/search.md](references/search.md) |
196: | Sub-issues & parent issues | Breaking work into hierarchical tasks | [references/sub-issues.md](references/sub-issues.md) |
197: | Issue dependencies | Tracking blocked-by / blocking relationships | [references/dependencies.md](references/dependencies.md) |
198: | Issue types (advanced) | GraphQL operations beyond MCP `list_issue_types` / `type` param | [references/issue-types.md](references/issue-types.md) |
199: | Projects V2 | Project boards, progress reports, field management | [references/projects.md](references/projects.md) |
200: | Issue fields | Custom metadata: dates, priority, text, numbers (private preview) | [references/issue-fields.md](references/issue-fields.md) |
201: | Images in issues | Embedding images in issue bodies and comments via CLI | [references/images.md](references/images.md) |
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-release/references/commit-classification.md">
 1: # Commit Classification Heuristics
 2: 
 3: > **Role in the workflow:** Commit messages are a *secondary* signal. The code diff
 4: > is always read first and treated as ground truth. Use these heuristics to add
 5: > intent and context on top of what the diff already shows — not to replace it.
 6: > When a commit message contradicts the diff, trust the diff.
 7: 
 8: When reading `git log` output, map each commit to one of the categories below.
 9: Repos that follow Conventional Commits (https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) will
10: have explicit prefixes — use them directly. For freeform commit messages, use the
11: heuristics.
12: 
13: ---
14: 
15: ## Conventional Commit prefixes → category
16: 
17: | Prefix | Category |
18: |---|---|
19: | `feat:` / `feat(scope):` | feat |
20: | `fix:` / `fix(scope):` | fix |
21: | `perf:` | perf |
22: | `refactor:` | refactor |
23: | `docs:` | docs |
24: | `chore:` | chore |
25: | `test:` / `tests:` | test |
26: | `ci:` | chore |
27: | `build:` | chore |
28: | `style:` | chore |
29: | `revert:` | depends on what was reverted |
30: | `BREAKING CHANGE` in footer or `!` after type (e.g. `feat!:`) | breaking |
31: 
32: ---
33: 
34: ## Freeform commit message heuristics
35: 
36: **Breaking:**
37: - Contains words: *breaking*, *incompatible*, *remove*, *rename*, *drop support*
38: - Phrase patterns: *no longer*, *was removed*, *has been deleted*, *breaking change*
39: 
40: **Feat (new feature):**
41: - Starts with: *add*, *implement*, *introduce*, *support*, *new*
42: - Contains: *now supports*, *ability to*, *can now*
43: 
44: **Fix:**
45: - Starts with: *fix*, *patch*, *resolve*, *correct*, *handle*
46: - Contains: *bug*, *regression*, *crash*, *error*, *wrong*, *incorrect*, *broken*
47: 
48: **Perf:**
49: - Contains: *speed up*, *faster*, *reduce memory*, *optimize*, *performance*
50: 
51: **Refactor:**
52: - Contains: *refactor*, *clean up*, *reorganize*, *restructure*, *simplify*, *extract*
53: 
54: **Docs:**
55: - Contains: *docs*, *readme*, *comment*, *example*, *typo*
56: 
57: **Chore:**
58: - Contains: *bump*, *upgrade dependencies*, *update deps*, *version bump*, *ci*, *lint*
59: 
60: **Test:**
61: - Contains: *test*, *spec*, *coverage*, *fixture*
62: 
63: ---
64: 
65: ## Classifying merge commits
66: 
67: Merge commits (e.g., `Merge pull request #42`) are usually noise. Look at the PR title
68: or the commits inside the merge. If the PR title follows Conventional Commits, use that.
69: 
70: ---
71: 
72: ## When you can't tell
73: 
74: Default to **PATCH** if the commit looks like maintenance. Escalate to **MINOR** if
75: there's any mention of new functionality. Escalate to **MAJOR** only with explicit
76: evidence of a breaking change — don't guess at breaking.
77: 
78: ---
79: 
80: ## Mapping categories to Keep a Changelog sections
81: 
82: | Category | Changelog section |
83: |---|---|
84: | `breaking` + new behavior | Changed |
85: | `breaking` + removal | Removed |
86: | `feat` | Added |
87: | `fix`, `perf` | Fixed |
88: | `security` | Security |
89: | `refactor`, `docs`, `chore`, `test` | Omit (unless user-visible) |
90: 
91: **User-visible refactor example:** Extracting a previously internal helper into a
92: new public export → treat as Added, not Refactor.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-release/references/semver-rules.md">
 1: # SemVer Decision Rules
 2: 
 3: Reference: https://semver.org/
 4: 
 5: ---
 6: 
 7: ## Version format
 8: 
 9: ```
10: vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH
11: ```
12: 
13: - **MAJOR** — incompatible API changes
14: - **MINOR** — new backward-compatible functionality
15: - **PATCH** — backward-compatible bug fixes
16: 
17: Pre-1.0 note: if the current version is `0.x.y`, anything goes — MINOR bumps are
18: common for breaking changes. Once past `1.0.0`, the rules below apply strictly.
19: 
20: ---
21: 
22: ## What counts as a MAJOR bump (breaking change)
23: 
24: Breaking changes are any modifications that could cause a consumer of the library to
25: experience a compile error, runtime error, or behavior change **without changing their
26: own code**.
27: 
28: Examples:
29: - Removing a public function, class, method, or constant
30: - Renaming a public function, class, method, or constant
31: - Changing a function signature (adding required parameters, removing parameters,
32:   changing parameter types, changing return type)
33: - Changing observable behavior that callers depend on (e.g., error types thrown,
34:   event names emitted, return value shape)
35: - Changing a required configuration key or its accepted values
36: - Dropping support for a runtime/language version that was previously supported
37: - Removing or renaming a publicly exported module path
38: 
39: **When in doubt, prefer a MAJOR bump over a MINOR.** It's better to signal a breaking
40: change than to silently break consumers.
41: 
42: ---
43: 
44: ## What counts as a MINOR bump (new feature)
45: 
46: - Adding a new public function, class, method, or constant
47: - Adding optional parameters to an existing function (with backward-compatible defaults)
48: - Implementing a new protocol/interface that doesn't affect existing ones
49: - Adding new configuration keys with sensible defaults
50: - Deprecating (but not removing) a public API — removal comes in a future MAJOR
51: 
52: ---
53: 
54: ## What counts as a PATCH bump
55: 
56: - Fixing a bug where behavior was incorrect relative to documented intent
57: - Improving performance without changing the public API
58: - Internal refactoring with no external observable difference
59: - Documentation updates
60: - Dependency updates that don't change the library's own public surface
61: - CI/CD, test, tooling changes
62: - Security fixes that don't break the API
63: 
64: ---
65: 
66: ## Multiple changes — precedence
67: 
68: When a release contains a mix of change types, the **highest precedence** wins:
69: 
70: ```
71: MAJOR > MINOR > PATCH
72: ```
73: 
74: One breaking change + ten new features = MAJOR bump.
75: 
76: ---
77: 
78: ## First release (no prior tags)
79: 
80: Default to `1.0.0` regardless of what's in the diff. Inform the user.
81: 
82: ---
83: 
84: ## Edge cases
85: 
86: | Situation | Recommendation |
87: |---|---|
88: | Only internal/private symbols changed | PATCH |
89: | Type annotation added to previously untyped function | PATCH (non-breaking) |
90: | Changing default value of optional parameter | Treat as MAJOR if callers might rely on old default |
91: | Adding a new required config option to an optional block | MINOR if the block itself is optional, otherwise MAJOR |
92: | Reverting a previous commit entirely | Follow what the net diff shows, not the revert message |
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/github-release/SKILL.md">
  1: ---
  2: name: github-release
  3: description: >
  4:   Guides IA through releasing a new version of a GitHub library end-to-end.
  5:   Handles SemVer versioning and Keep a Changelog formatting automatically.
  6: compatibility: "requires: gh CLI and git"
  7: ---
  8: 
  9: # GitHub Release Skill
 10: 
 11: This skill automates the full release workflow for a single-package GitHub repository,
 12: from analysis through changelog authoring and PR creation. It relies exclusively on
 13: `gh` (GitHub CLI) and `git` no other tools needed.
 14: 
 15: Steps 1 - 4 are **read-only reconnaissance** nothing is written to the repo until
 16: Step 5, once the version number is confirmed.
 17: 
 18: ## When to Use This Skill
 19: 
 20: Use this skill whenever the user wants to cut a new release, publish a new version,
 21: bump a version, create a release branch, generate a changelog, or open a release PR
 22: on a GitHub repository. Trigger even if the user says something casual like "let's
 23: ship a new version" or "time to release".
 24: 
 25: ---
 26: 
 27: ## Prerequisites
 28: 
 29: Examples below include both Bash and PowerShell variants; Windows users should prefer
 30: the PowerShell blocks.
 31: 
 32: Before starting, verify the environment:
 33: 
 34: ```bash
 35: gh auth status                        # must be authenticated
 36: gh repo view --json nameWithOwner     # must be inside a GitHub repo
 37: git status                            # working tree should be clean
 38: ```
 39: 
 40: If any check fails, stop and tell the user what to fix before continuing.
 41: 
 42: Then ask the user one question:
 43: 
 44: > *"Which directory contains your library's public-facing source code?
 45: > (e.g. `src/`, `lib/`, `pkg/` - used to focus the diff on what consumers
 46: > actually see. Press Enter to scan the whole repo.)"*
 47: 
 48: Store the answer as `PUBLIC_PATH`. If empty, `PUBLIC_PATH` is `.` (repo root).
 49: Exclude these paths from all diffs regardless: `tests/`, `test/`, `spec/`,
 50: `__tests__/`, `docs/`, `*.lock`, `*-lock.json`, `*.sum`, generated files
 51: (files with a "do not edit" header comment), and build artefacts.
 52: 
 53: ---
 54: 
 55: ## The 9-Step Release Workflow
 56: 
 57: Work through every step in order. Show the user what command you're about to run and
 58: its output. Pause and ask for confirmation only when explicitly noted.
 59: 
 60: ---
 61: 
 62: ### Step 1 - Ensure main is up to date
 63: 
 64: ```bash
 65: git checkout main
 66: git pull origin main
 67: ```
 68: 
 69: Stay on `main` for now. The release branch is created in Step 5, after the version
 70: is confirmed.
 71: 
 72: ---
 73: 
 74: ### Step 2 - Grab the latest version tag
 75: 
 76: > **Why not `gh release list`?** GitHub Releases are an optional layer on top of Git
 77: > tags. Many repos tag releases with `git tag` without ever creating a GitHub Release,
 78: > so `gh release list` can return empty even when version tags exist. Reading tags
 79: > directly from git is the reliable source of truth.
 80: 
 81: ```bash
 82: # Fetch all tags from remote to ensure local view is current
 83: git fetch --tags
 84: 
 85: # Find the latest version tag, sorted semantically
 86: # --sort=-version:refname handles 1.10.0 > 1.9.0 correctly (unlike alphabetical)
 87: PREV_TAG=$(git tag --sort=-version:refname | grep -E '^v?[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+' | head -1)
 88: echo "Latest tag: $PREV_TAG"
 89: ```
 90: 
 91: ```PowerShell
 92: # Fetch all tags from remote to ensure local view is current
 93: git fetch --tags
 94: 
 95: # Find the latest version tag, sorted semantically
 96: # --sort=-version:refname handles 1.10.0 > 1.9.0 correctly (unlike alphabetical)
 97: $prevTag = git tag --sort='-version:refname' | `
 98:   Select-String '^[vV]?\d+\.\d+\.\d+' | `
 99:   Select-Object -First 1 -ExpandProperty Line
100: 
101: if ($prevTag) {
102:   $prevSha = git rev-list -n 1 $prevTag
103: } else {
104:   $prevSha = git rev-list --max-parents=0 HEAD
105: }
106: 
107: Write-Output "Latest tag: $prevTag"
108: ```
109: 
110: Then verify the tag exists on the remote (not just locally):
111: 
112: ```bash
113: git ls-remote --tags origin | grep "refs/tags/$PREV_TAG$"
114: ```
115: 
116: If the remote check returns nothing, warn the user that the tag appears to be local-only
117: and hasn't been pushed - they may want to push it before continuing.
118: 
119: - `PREV_TAG` is the tag name exactly as found (e.g. `v1.4.2`). Strip any leading `v`
120:   when doing arithmetic; preserve it when naming things.
121: - If **no tags exist at all**, treat `PREV_TAG` as `(none)`, set `PREV_SHA` to the
122:   first commit, and default the new version to `1.0.0` (skip Step 4 versioning logic;
123:   go straight to Step 5).
124: - If the tag does not point to a real commit (orphaned tag), fall back to
125:   `git rev-list --max-parents=0 HEAD` and warn the user.
126: 
127: ```bash
128: PREV_SHA=$(git rev-list -n 1 "$PREV_TAG" 2>/dev/null || git rev-list --max-parents=0 HEAD)
129: ```
130: 
131: ---
132: 
133: ### Step 3 - Analyse what changed since the last release
134: 
135: This step uses **two complementary signals**. The code diff is the primary source of
136: truth; commit messages provide supporting context about intent.
137: 
138: #### 3a - Code diff (primary signal)
139: 
140: ```bash
141: # Focused diff on the public source path, excluding noise
142: git diff "$PREV_SHA"..HEAD -- "$PUBLIC_PATH" \
143:   ':(exclude)tests/' ':(exclude)test/' ':(exclude)spec/' \
144:   ':(exclude)__tests__/' ':(exclude)docs/' \
145:   ':(exclude)*.lock' ':(exclude)*-lock.json' ':(exclude)*.sum'
146: ```
147: 
148: ```PowerShell
149: # Focused diff on the public source path, excluding noise
150: git diff "$($prevSha)..HEAD" -- $publicPath `
151:   ':(exclude)tests/' ':(exclude)test/' ':(exclude)spec/' `
152:   ':(exclude)__tests__/' ':(exclude)docs/' `
153:   ':(exclude)*.lock' ':(exclude)*-lock.json' ':(exclude)*.sum'
154: ```
155: 
156: Read the full diff output. For each changed file, identify:
157: 
158: 1. **Removed symbols** - functions, classes, methods, constants, exported names that
159:    existed before and are now gone. ? Strong signal for MAJOR.
160: 2. **Changed signatures** - functions that exist in both versions but with different
161:    parameters, return types, or thrown errors. ? Strong signal for MAJOR.
162: 3. **New exported symbols** - public functions, classes, constants that didn't exist
163:    before. ? Signal for MINOR.
164: 4. **Internal-only changes** - modifications that don't touch any public interface
165:    (private helpers, unexported functions, algorithm internals). ? PATCH.
166: 5. **Bug fixes** - corrections to logic that was provably wrong (e.g. off-by-one,
167:    null check, wrong condition), without changing the public API. ? PATCH.
168: 
169: If the diff is very large (thousands of lines), first run the stat summary to
170: prioritise which files to read in full:
171: 
172: ```bash
173: git diff "$PREV_SHA"..HEAD --stat -- "$PUBLIC_PATH"
174: ```
175: 
176: Focus your detailed reading on files with the most changes and files whose names
177: suggest they define public interfaces (e.g. `index.*`, `api.*`, `exports.*`,
178: `public.*`, `mod.*`, `__init__.*`).
179: 
180: #### 3b - Commit log (secondary signal)
181: 
182: ```bash
183: git log "$PREV_SHA"..HEAD --oneline --no-merges
184: ```
185: 
186: Use this to:
187: - Understand the **intent** behind code changes that aren't self-explanatory from
188:   the diff alone (e.g. a one-line security fix labelled as such).
189: - Catch changes that may be in paths outside `PUBLIC_PATH` but are still user-visible
190:   (e.g. a CLI flag change in a `cmd/` directory).
191: - Fill in context for changelog entries where the code alone doesn't tell the whole
192:   story.
193: 
194: See `references/commit-classification.md` for mapping message patterns to change types.
195: 
196: #### 3c - Reconcile the two signals
197: 
198: When signals agree ? use that classification with confidence.
199: 
200: When signals conflict ? **prefer the code diff**. Examples:
201: - Commit says `fix: typo` but the diff shows a removed public method ? treat as MAJOR.
202: - Commit says `feat: new API` but the diff only touches private internals ? treat as PATCH.
203: - Commit says `chore: refactor` but the diff adds new exported symbols ? treat as MINOR.
204: 
205: Document any conflicts you notice - flag them to the user during the changelog review
206: in Step 6.
207: 
208: ---
209: 
210: ### Step 4 - Determine the next SemVer version
211: 
212: Apply these rules to your analysis from Step 3 (full rules in `references/semver-rules.md`):
213: 
214: | Condition | Bump |
215: |---|---|
216: | Any breaking change to public API (removal, signature change, behaviour change) | MAJOR |
217: | New exported symbol or feature, no breaking changes | MINOR |
218: | Bug fix, perf improvement, security fix, docs, chore only | PATCH |
219: 
220: When a release contains a mix, the **highest precedence wins**:
221: `MAJOR > MINOR > PATCH`.
222: 
223: Compute `NEXT_VERSION`:
224: - Split `PREV_TAG` into `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH` integers.
225: - Apply the appropriate bump.
226: - Format as `vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`.
227: 
228: **Present the proposed version to the user** with a brief rationale that cites
229: specific code findings, not just commit messages. Example:
230: 
231: > *"I'm proposing v2.1.0. The diff shows two new exported functions (`NewClient` and
232: > `WithTimeout`) in `src/client.go`, and no existing public symbols were removed or
233: > changed. Commit messages corroborate this as feature additions."*
234: 
235: Ask: *"Does this version look right, or would you like to adjust it?"*
236: Wait for confirmation before proceeding.
237: 
238: ---
239: 
240: ### Step 5 - Create the release branch
241: 
242: Now that the version is confirmed, create the branch with the correct name from the start:
243: 
244: ```bash
245: git checkout -b release/vX.Y.Z
246: git push -u origin release/vX.Y.Z
247: ```
248: 
249: ---
250: 
251: ### Step 6 - Update CHANGELOG.md
252: 
253: Read the existing `CHANGELOG.md` (or create it if absent). Follow the
254: [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/) format strictly.
255: 
256: **Structure to insert** at the top (just below the `# Changelog` header):
257: 
258: ```markdown
259: ## [X.Y.Z] - YYYY-MM-DD
260: 
261: ### Added
262: - ...
263: 
264: ### Changed
265: - ...
266: 
267: ### Deprecated
268: - ...
269: 
270: ### Removed
271: - ...
272: 
273: ### Fixed
274: - ...
275: 
276: ### Security
277: - ...
278: ```
279: 
280: Rules:
281: - Use today's date in `YYYY-MM-DD` format.
282: - Omit sections that have no entries - don't leave empty headings.
283: - Write entries in **plain English from a user's perspective**, derived primarily
284:   from what the code diff shows, supplemented by commit message context.
285:   Good: *"Added `WithTimeout` option to HTTP client constructor."*
286:   Bad: *"feat: add timeout cfg param"*
287: - Map findings to sections:
288:   - New exported symbol ? Added
289:   - Breaking removal ? Removed
290:   - Breaking change to existing API ? Changed (flag it as breaking)
291:   - Bug/logic fix, perf ? Fixed
292:   - Security fix ? Security
293:   - Internal refactor, docs, chore, test ? omit unless user-visible
294: - If a commit message revealed intent that the code diff alone wouldn't convey
295:   (e.g. a security fix disguised as a one-line change), include that context in
296:   the changelog entry.
297: - Also update the diff link at the bottom of the file:
298:   ```markdown
299:   [X.Y.Z]: https://github.com/OWNER/REPO/compare/vPREV...vNEXT
300:   ```
301: 
302: **Show the user the proposed changelog section before writing it to disk.**
303: If any signal conflicts were found in Step 3c, flag them here so the user can verify.
304: Ask: *"Does this changelog look accurate? Any entries to add, remove, or reword?"*
305: Incorporate feedback, then write to disk.
306: 
307: ---
308: 
309: ### Step 7 - Commit and push
310: 
311: ```bash
312: git add CHANGELOG.md
313: git commit -m "chore: release vX.Y.Z"
314: git push origin release/vX.Y.Z
315: ```
316: 
317: Confirm the push succeeded before moving on.
318: 
319: ---
320: 
321: ### Step 8 - Open a Pull Request
322: 
323: **?? IMPORTANT:** Always use `--body-file` to pass PR body text, never `--body` with inline text.
324: Inline escape sequences like `\n` are not interpreted as newlines by PowerShell and will appear
325: as literal text in the PR. Using a file ensures proper markdown formatting.
326: 
327: ```bash
328: gh pr create \
329:   --base main \
330:   --head release/vX.Y.Z \
331:   --title "Release vX.Y.Z" \
332:   --body "$(cat <<'EOF'
333: ## Release vX.Y.Z
334: 
335: This PR prepares the **vX.Y.Z** release.
336: 
337: ### What's included
338: <!-- paste the changelog section here -->
339: 
340: ### Checklist
341: - [ ] Changelog reviewed
342: - [ ] Version bump verified
343: - [ ] CI passing
344: 
345: After merging, create the tag on the merge commit:
346: \`\`\`
347: git tag vX.Y.Z <merge-commit-sha>
348: git push origin vX.Y.Z
349: \`\`\`
350: EOF
351: )"
352: ```
353: 
354: ```PowerShell
355: # Create PR body using here-string (preserves actual newlines, not escape sequences)
356: $prBody = @"
357: ## Release vX.Y.Z
358: 
359: This PR prepares the **vX.Y.Z** release.
360: 
361: ### What's included
362: <paste changelog here>
363: 
364: ### Checklist
365: - [ ] Changelog reviewed
366: - [ ] Version bump verified
367: - [ ] CI passing
368: 
369: After merging, create the tag on the merge commit:
370: ``````
371: git tag vX.Y.Z <merge-commit-sha>
372: git push origin vX.Y.Z
373: ``````
374: "@
375: 
376: # Write to file and use --body-file (do NOT use inline --body with escape sequences)
377: $prBody | Out-File -FilePath release_pr_body.md -Encoding utf8 -NoNewline
378: gh pr create --base main --head release/vX.Y.Z --title "Release vX.Y.Z" --body-file release_pr_body.md
379: ```
380: 
381: Paste the changelog section into the PR body's "What's included" block (or leave placeholder for manual review).
382: 
383: 
384: ---
385: 
386: ### Step 9 - Hand off to the user
387: 
388: Tell the user:
389: 
390: > **Release PR is open! ??**
391: >
392: > New version: **vX.Y.Z**
393: >
394: > Once the PR is reviewed and merged, you'll need to **create the tag yourself** on
395: > the merge commit:
396: >
397: > ```bash
398: > git tag vX.Y.Z <merge-commit-sha>
399: > git push origin vX.Y.Z
400: > ```
401: >
402: > Then go to GitHub Releases and publish the release from that tag. You can copy the
403: > changelog section directly into the release notes.
404: 
405: ---
406: 
407: ## Error handling
408: 
409: | Situation | What to do |
410: |---|---|
411: | `gh auth status` fails | Stop; tell user to run `gh auth login` |
412: | Not inside a git repo | Stop; tell user to `cd` into their repo |
413: | Working tree is dirty | Warn; ask if they want to stash or abort |
414: | No commits since last tag | Tell user there's nothing to release |
415: | Tag exists but points to no commit | Use first commit as diff base; warn user |
416: | Latest tag exists locally but not on remote | Warn user; ask if they want to push the tag first or continue anyway |
417: | Diff is empty for `PUBLIC_PATH` but commits exist | Warn; all changes may be internal; ask if they still want to proceed |
418: | `git push` fails (e.g. protected branch rules) | Report the error verbatim; suggest they check branch protection settings |
419: 
420: ---
421: 
422: ## Troubleshooting in PowerShell
423: 
424: - If a command that works locally prints gh usage or treats a subcommand as separate token, ensure you're
425:   invoking the gh.exe on PATH (Get-Command gh) and avoid passing unexpanded nested substitutions; use the PowerShell
426:   patterns above.
427: - Recommend tests: gh --version; git fetch --tags; run the PowerShell snippet to set $prevTag and run git diff --name-only $prevSha..HEAD -- src/
428: 
429: ---
430: 
431: ## Limitations
432: 
433: - Requires the `gh` CLI to be installed and authenticated.
434: - Requires git tags to determine current version.
435: 
436: ---
437: 
438: ## Reference files
439: 
440: - `references/semver-rules.md` - Extended SemVer decision rules and edge cases
441: - `references/commit-classification.md` - Heuristics for classifying commit messages into change types
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/harness-engineering/SKILL.md">
  1: ---
  2: name: harness-engineering
  3: description: 'Adopt repository-level harness engineering for coding agents. Use when a user wants to prevent repeated AI coding-agent mistakes by turning failures into durable instructions, drift checks, regression tests, failure memory, and adoption reports tailored to the target repository.'
  4: ---
  5: 
  6: # Harness Engineering
  7: 
  8: Harness engineering turns repeated coding-agent mistakes into durable
  9: repository artifacts:
 10: 
 11: ```text
 12: Harness = Instructions + Constraints + Feedback + Memory + Evaluation + Governance
 13: ```
 14: 
 15: Use this skill when the user asks to:
 16: 
 17: - make a repository more reliable for GitHub Copilot or other coding agents
 18: - add durable agent instructions, repository rules, or guardrails
 19: - prevent repeated AI coding-agent mistakes
 20: - record known failure paths and the checks that prevent recurrence
 21: - add lightweight drift checks for project rules
 22: - review, refresh, or update an existing agent harness
 23: 
 24: Do not use this skill for ordinary feature implementation unless the user asks
 25: to improve the repository's agent operating environment.
 26: 
 27: ## Core Principles
 28: 
 29: - Treat the target repository as the source of truth.
 30: - Inspect before editing. Preserve the existing stack, package manager, CI,
 31:   docs, naming, and architecture.
 32: - Add the smallest useful harness. Prefer updating existing files over adding
 33:   duplicate guidance.
 34: - Make important rules enforceable where practical through tests, linters,
 35:   type checks, CI, pre-commit hooks, or drift scripts.
 36: - Use manual review points only when automation would be brittle or misleading.
 37: - Record high-risk failures that should not recur, and name the check or review
 38:   point that catches recurrence.
 39: - Do not copy generic templates blindly. Adapt every artifact to real evidence
 40:   in the target repository.
 41: 
 42: ## Discovery
 43: 
 44: Before proposing or making harness changes, inspect the repository for existing
 45: rules and evidence.
 46: 
 47: Read these files and folders when they exist:
 48: 
 49: - `README.md`
 50: - `AGENTS.md`
 51: - `.github/copilot-instructions.md`
 52: - `.github/instructions/`
 53: - `.github/workflows/`
 54: - `CONTRIBUTING.md`
 55: - package manifests such as `package.json`, `pyproject.toml`, `go.mod`,
 56:   `Cargo.toml`, `pom.xml`, or `build.gradle`
 57: - existing docs under `docs/`
 58: - existing scripts under `scripts/`
 59: - existing tests and CI checks
 60: 
 61: Then summarize:
 62: 
 63: - stack, package manager, and entry points
 64: - existing development and verification commands
 65: - current agent instructions or repository conventions
 66: - known failures, incidents, flaky paths, or repeated review comments
 67: - gaps where project rules are not enforced
 68: 
 69: ## Adoption Workflow
 70: 
 71: Follow this sequence:
 72: 
 73: 1. Choose the harness surface that fits the target repository.
 74: 2. Write target-specific agent instructions.
 75: 3. Add enforceable checks for high-value rules.
 76: 4. Record failure memory for high-risk or recurring failures.
 77: 5. Add drift checks for guidance that can silently become stale.
 78: 6. Report the adoption with evidence, assumptions, and follow-up.
 79: 
 80: ### 1. Choose the Harness Surface
 81: 
 82: Pick only the surfaces that fit the target repository:
 83: 
 84: | Need | Preferred artifact |
 85: | --- | --- |
 86: | Always-on agent behavior | `AGENTS.md` or `.github/copilot-instructions.md` |
 87: | File-scoped guidance | `.github/instructions/*.instructions.md` |
 88: | Recurring project checks | `scripts/check_*.py`, shell scripts, or package scripts |
 89: | CI enforcement | existing workflow files or a small new workflow |
 90: | Known failures | `docs/failures/*.md` |
 91: | Architecture or process decisions | `docs/decisions/*.md` |
 92: | Adoption evidence | `docs/harness/adoption-report.md` or similar |
 93: 
 94: If the repository already has an equivalent location, update it instead of
 95: creating a parallel system.
 96: 
 97: ### 2. Write Agent Instructions
 98: 
 99: Agent instructions should be concrete and operational. Include:
100: 
101: - project purpose and major ownership boundaries
102: - setup, test, lint, build, and verification commands
103: - package manager and dependency rules
104: - safe editing rules, generated file rules, and forbidden paths
105: - testing expectations for changed code
106: - PR and commit conventions if the repo has them
107: - how to record new failures or decisions
108: 
109: Avoid broad personality guidance, generic best practices, and rules that cannot
110: be checked or reviewed.
111: 
112: ### 3. Add Enforceable Checks
113: 
114: Convert high-value rules into checks. Good harness checks are:
115: 
116: - narrow enough to avoid false positives
117: - fast enough to run locally and in CI
118: - named clearly so agents can run them before finishing
119: - documented with the rule they protect
120: 
121: Examples:
122: 
123: ```text
124: Rule: Do not edit generated API clients.
125: Check: script scans diffs for generated paths and fails with a clear message.
126: 
127: Rule: Every failure memory note names a regression check.
128: Check: script validates docs/failures/*.md for a "Detection" section.
129: 
130: Rule: Profile docs and templates must stay aligned.
131: Check: test compares profile README files to expected template files.
132: ```
133: 
134: ### 4. Record Failure Memory
135: 
136: Record failures when they are user-visible, high-risk, or likely to recur.
137: Use a new file under `docs/failures/` unless an existing note already covers
138: the same root cause.
139: 
140: Recommended structure:
141: 
142: ```markdown
143: # Short Failure Title
144: 
145: ## Summary
146: 
147: What failed, who saw it, and why it matters.
148: 
149: ## Root Cause
150: 
151: The technical or process cause. Avoid blame.
152: 
153: ## Prevention
154: 
155: Instruction, test, drift check, CI gate, fixture, or manual review point that
156: prevents or detects recurrence.
157: 
158: ## Evidence
159: 
160: Links to issue, PR, test, log, command output, or file paths.
161: ```
162: 
163: If no automated check is practical, record the manual review point and why
164: automation would be unsafe or misleading.
165: 
166: ### 5. Add Drift Checks
167: 
168: Use drift checks for guidance that can silently become stale. Common examples:
169: 
170: - docs mention commands that no longer exist
171: - profile snippets and generated examples diverge
172: - failure notes omit regression checks
173: - decision records are missing for structural changes
174: - CI references stale scripts or package commands
175: 
176: Prefer small scripts using the repository's existing language. If the repo has
177: no scripting convention, Python with only the standard library is a portable
178: default.
179: 
180: ### 6. Report the Adoption
181: 
182: Finish substantial harness work with an adoption report that includes:
183: 
184: - files changed
185: - rules added or updated
186: - checks added or reused
187: - commands run and results
188: - assumptions and manual follow-up
189: - failure memory created or intentionally skipped
190: - how effectiveness will be measured
191: 
192: ## Review Workflow
193: 
194: When asked to review a harness change, take an opposing perspective. Look for:
195: 
196: - generic rules copied without evidence from the target repository
197: - duplicate or conflicting instruction files
198: - broad checks that are likely to fail on valid changes
199: - unenforced high-risk rules
200: - missing failure memory for repeated mistakes or runtime failures
201: - generated docs not refreshed after source changes
202: - CI gates that do not run the relevant checks
203: - target repository conventions being overwritten by harness defaults
204: 
205: Report findings first, ordered by severity, with file and line references when
206: available. Do not modify files during a review unless the user explicitly asks
207: for fixes.
208: 
209: ## Output Contract
210: 
211: Before finishing harness adoption work, verify:
212: 
213: - the target repository was inspected before edits
214: - new guidance is specific to the target repository
215: - changed checks can be run locally or have a documented manual substitute
216: - failure memory was recorded when required, or the final response explains why
217:   it was skipped
218: - generated docs or indexes are refreshed
219: - the final report names every command run and its result
220: 
221: ## Optional Reference
222: 
223: The prompt-first workflow in
224: `https://github.com/baskduf/harness-starter-kit` is a reference implementation
225: of these ideas. Use it as reference material only when the user asks for it or
226: when the repository already includes it. The target repository remains the
227: source of truth.
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/import-infrastructure-as-code/SKILL.md">
  1: ---
  2: name: import-infrastructure-as-code
  3: description: 'Import existing Azure resources into Terraform using Azure CLI discovery and Azure Verified Modules (AVM). Use when asked to reverse-engineer live Azure infrastructure, generate Infrastructure as Code from existing subscriptions/resource groups/resource IDs, map dependencies, derive exact import addresses from downloaded module source, prevent configuration drift, and produce AVM-based Terraform files ready for validation and planning across any Azure resource type.'
  4: ---
  5: 
  6: # Import Infrastructure as Code (Azure -> Terraform with AVM)
  7: 
  8: Convert existing Azure infrastructure into maintainable Terraform code using discovery data and Azure Verified Modules.
  9: 
 10: ## When to Use This Skill
 11: 
 12: Use this skill when the user asks to:
 13: 
 14: - Import existing Azure resources into Terraform
 15: - Generate IaC from live Azure environments
 16: - Handle any Azure resource type supported by AVM (and document justified non-AVM fallbacks)
 17: - Recreate infrastructure from a subscription or resource group
 18: - Map dependencies between discovered Azure resources
 19: - Use AVM modules instead of handwritten `azurerm_*` resources
 20: 
 21: ## Prerequisites
 22: 
 23: - Azure CLI installed and authenticated (`az login`)
 24: - Access to the target subscription or resource group
 25: - Terraform CLI installed
 26: - Network access to Terraform Registry and AVM index sources
 27: 
 28: ## Inputs
 29: 
 30: | Parameter | Required | Default | Description |
 31: |---|---|---|---|
 32: | `subscription-id` | No | Active CLI context | Azure subscription used for subscription-scope discovery and context setting |
 33: | `resource-group-name` | No | None | Azure resource group used for resource-group-scope discovery |
 34: | `resource-id` | No | None | One or more Azure ARM resource IDs used for specific-resource-scope discovery |
 35: 
 36: At least one of `subscription-id`, `resource-group-name`, or `resource-id` is required.
 37: 
 38: ## Step-by-Step Workflows
 39: 
 40: ### 1) Collect Required Scope (Mandatory)
 41: 
 42: Request one of these scopes before running discovery commands:
 43: 
 44: - Subscription scope: `<subscription-id>`
 45: - Resource group scope: `<resource-group-name>`
 46: - Specific resources scope: one or more `<resource-id>` values
 47: 
 48: Scope handling rules:
 49: 
 50: - Treat Azure ARM resource IDs (for example `/subscriptions/.../providers/...`) as cloud resource identifiers, not local file system paths.
 51: - Use resource IDs only with Azure CLI `--ids` arguments (for example `az resource show --ids <resource-id>`).
 52: - Never pass resource IDs to file-reading commands (`cat`, `ls`, `read_file`, glob searches) unless the user explicitly says they are local file paths.
 53: - If the user already provided one valid scope, do not ask for additional scope inputs unless required by a failing command.
 54: - Do not ask follow-up questions that can be answered from already-provided scope values.
 55: 
 56: If scope is missing, ask for it explicitly and stop.
 57: 
 58: ### 2) Authenticate and Set Context
 59: 
 60: Run only the commands required for the selected scope.
 61: 
 62: For subscription scope:
 63: 
 64: ```bash
 65: az login
 66: az account set --subscription <subscription-id>
 67: az account show --query "{subscriptionId:id, name:name, tenantId:tenantId}" -o json
 68: ```
 69: 
 70: Expected output: JSON object with `subscriptionId`, `name`, and `tenantId`.
 71: 
 72: For resource group or specific resource scope, `az login` is still required but `az account set` is optional if the active context is already correct.
 73: 
 74: When using specific resource scope, prefer direct `--ids`-based commands first and avoid extra discovery prompts for subscription or resource group unless needed for a concrete command.
 75: 
 76: ### 3) Run Discovery Commands
 77: 
 78: Discover resources using the selected scopes. Ensure to fetch all necessary information for accurate Terraform generation.
 79: 
 80: ```bash
 81: # Subscription scope
 82: az resource list --subscription <subscription-id> -o json
 83: 
 84: # Resource group scope
 85: az resource list --resource-group <resource-group-name> -o json
 86: 
 87: # Specific resource scope
 88: az resource show --ids <resource-id-1> <resource-id-2> ... -o json
 89: ```
 90: 
 91: Expected output: JSON object or array containing Azure resource metadata (`id`, `type`, `name`, `location`, `tags`, `properties`).
 92: 
 93: ### 4) Resolve Dependencies Before Code Generation
 94: 
 95: Parse exported JSON and map:
 96: 
 97: - Parent-child relationships (for example: NIC -> Subnet -> VNet)
 98: - Cross-resource references in `properties`
 99: - Ordering for Terraform creation
100: 
101: IMPORTANT: Generate the following documentation and save it to a docs folder in the root of the project.
102: - `exported-resources.json` with all discovered resources and their metadata, including dependencies and references.
103: - `EXPORTED-ARCHITECTURE.MD` file with a human-readable architecture overview based on the discovered resources and their relationships.
104: 
105: ### 5) Select Azure Verified Modules (Required)
106: 
107: Use the latest AVM version for each resource type.
108: 
109: ### Terraform Registry
110: 
111: - Search for "avm" + resource name
112: - Filter by "Partner" tag to find official AVM modules
113: - Example: Search "avm storage account" → filter by Partner
114: 
115: ### Official AVM Index
116: 
117: > **Note:** The following links always point to the latest version of the CSV files on the main branch. As intended, this means the files may change over time. If you require a point-in-time version, consider using a specific release tag in the URL.
118: 
119: - **Terraform Resource Modules**: `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Azure/Azure-Verified-Modules/refs/heads/main/docs/static/module-indexes/TerraformResourceModules.csv`
120: - **Terraform Pattern Modules**: `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Azure/Azure-Verified-Modules/refs/heads/main/docs/static/module-indexes/TerraformPatternModules.csv`
121: - **Terraform Utility Modules**: `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Azure/Azure-Verified-Modules/refs/heads/main/docs/static/module-indexes/TerraformUtilityModules.csv`
122: 
123: ### Individual Module information
124: 
125: Use the `web` tool or another suitable MCP method to get module information if not available locally in the `.terraform` folder.
126: 
127: Use AVM sources:
128: 
129: - Registry: `https://registry.terraform.io/modules/Azure/<module>/azurerm/latest`
130: - GitHub: `https://github.com/Azure/terraform-azurerm-avm-res-<service>-<resource>`
131: 
132: Prefer AVM modules over handwritten `azurerm_*` resources when an AVM module exists.
133: 
134: When fetching module information from GitHub repositories, the README.md file in the root of the repository typically contains all detailed information about the module, for example: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Azure/terraform-azurerm-avm-res-<service>-<resource>/refs/heads/main/README.md
135: 
136: ### 5a) Read the Module README Before Writing Any Code (Mandatory)
137: 
138: **This step is not optional.** Before writing a single line of HCL for a module, fetch and
139: read the full README for that module. Do not rely on knowledge of the raw `azurerm` provider
140: or prior experience with other AVM modules.
141: 
142: For each selected AVM module, fetch its README:
143: 
144: ```text
145: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Azure/terraform-azurerm-avm-res-<service>-<resource>/refs/heads/main/README.md
146: ```
147: 
148: Or if the module is already downloaded after `terraform init`:
149: 
150: ```bash
151: cat .terraform/modules/<module_key>/README.md
152: ```
153: 
154: From the README, extract and record **before writing code**:
155: 
156: 1. **Required Inputs** — every input the module requires. Any child resource listed here
157: 	 (NICs, extensions, subnets, public IPs) is managed **inside** the module. Do **not**
158: 	 create standalone module blocks for those resources.
159: 2. **Optional Inputs** — the exact Terraform variable names and their declared `type`.
160: 	 Do not assume they match the raw `azurerm` provider argument names or block shapes.
161: 3. **Usage examples** — check what resource group identifier is used (`parent_id` vs
162: 	 `resource_group_name`), how child resources are expressed (inline map vs separate module),
163: 	 and what syntax each input expects.
164: 
165: #### Apply module rules as patterns, not assumptions
166: 
167: Use the lessons below as examples of the *type* of mismatch that often causes imports to fail.
168: Do not assume these exact names apply to every AVM module. Always verify each selected module's
169: README and `variables.tf`.
170: 
171: **`avm-res-compute-virtualmachine` (any version)**
172: 
173: - `network_interfaces` is a **Required Input**. NICs are owned by the VM module. Never
174: 	create standalone `avm-res-network-networkinterface` modules alongside a VM module —
175: 	define every NIC inline under `network_interfaces`.
176: - TrustedLaunch is expressed through the top-level booleans `secure_boot_enabled = true`
177: 	and `vtpm_enabled = true`. The `security_type` argument exists only under `os_disk` for
178: 	Confidential VM disk encryption and must not be used for TrustedLaunch.
179: - `boot_diagnostics` is a `bool`, not an object. Use `boot_diagnostics = true`; use the
180: 	separate `boot_diagnostics_storage_account_uri` variable if a storage URI is needed.
181: - Extensions are managed inside the module via the `extensions` map. Do not create
182: 	standalone extension resources.
183: 
184: **`avm-res-network-virtualnetwork` (any version)**
185: 
186: - This module is backed by the AzAPI provider, not `azurerm`. Use `parent_id` (the full
187: 	resource group resource ID string) to specify the resource group, not `resource_group_name`.
188: - Every example in the README shows `parent_id`; none show `resource_group_name`.
189: 
190: Generalized takeaway for all AVM modules:
191: 
192: - Determine child resource ownership from **Required Inputs** before creating sibling modules.
193: - Determine accepted variable names and types from **Optional Inputs** and `variables.tf`.
194: - Determine identifier style and input shape from README usage examples.
195: - Do not infer argument names from raw `azurerm_*` resources.
196: 
197: ### 6) Generate Terraform Files
198: 
199: ### Before Writing Import Blocks — Inspect Module Source (Mandatory)
200: 
201: After `terraform init` downloads the modules, inspect each module's source files to determine
202: the exact Terraform resource addresses before writing any `import {}` blocks. Never write
203: import addresses from memory.
204: 
205: #### Step A — Identify the provider and resource label
206: 
207: ```bash
208: grep "^resource" .terraform/modules/<module_key>/main*.tf
209: ```
210: 
211: This reveals whether the module uses `azurerm_*` or `azapi_resource` labels. For example,
212: `avm-res-network-virtualnetwork` exposes `azapi_resource "vnet"`, not
213: `azurerm_virtual_network "this"`.
214: 
215: #### Step B — Identify child modules and nested paths
216: 
217: ```bash
218: grep "^module" .terraform/modules/<module_key>/main*.tf
219: ```
220: 
221: If child resources are managed in a sub-module (subnets, extensions, etc.), the import
222: address must include every intermediate module label:
223: 
224: ```text
225: module.<root_module_key>.module.<child_module_key>["<map_key>"].<resource_type>.<label>[<index>]
226: ```
227: 
228: #### Step C — Check for `count` vs `for_each`
229: 
230: ```bash
231: grep -n "count\|for_each" .terraform/modules/<module_key>/main*.tf
232: ```
233: 
234: Any resource using `count` requires an index in the import address. When `count = 1` (e.g.,
235: conditional Linux vs Windows selection), the address must end with `[0]`. Resources using
236: `for_each` use string keys, not numeric indexes.
237: 
238: #### Known import address patterns (examples from lessons learned)
239: 
240: These are examples only. Use them as templates for reasoning, then derive the exact addresses
241: from the downloaded source code for the modules in your current import.
242: 
243: | Resource | Correct import `to` address pattern |
244: |---|---|
245: | AzAPI-backed VNet | `module.<vnet_key>.azapi_resource.vnet` |
246: | Subnet (nested, count-based) | `module.<vnet_key>.module.subnet["<subnet_name>"].azapi_resource.subnet[0]` |
247: | Linux VM (count-based) | `module.<vm_key>.azurerm_linux_virtual_machine.this[0]` |
248: | VM NIC | `module.<vm_key>.azurerm_network_interface.virtualmachine_network_interfaces["<nic_key>"]` |
249: | VM extension (default deploy_sequence=5) | `module.<vm_key>.module.extension["<ext_name>"].azurerm_virtual_machine_extension.this` |
250: | VM extension (deploy_sequence=1–4) | `module.<vm_key>.module.extension_<n>["<ext_name>"].azurerm_virtual_machine_extension.this` |
251: | NSG-NIC association | `module.<vm_key>.azurerm_network_interface_security_group_association.this["<nic_key>-<nsg_key>"]` |
252: 
253: Produce:
254: 
255: - `providers.tf` with `azurerm` provider and required version constraints
256: - `main.tf` with AVM module blocks and explicit dependencies
257: - `variables.tf` for environment-specific values
258: - `outputs.tf` for key IDs and endpoints
259: - `terraform.tfvars.example` with placeholder values
260: 
261: ### Diff Live Properties Against Module Defaults (Mandatory)
262: 
263: After writing the initial configuration, compare every non-zero property of each discovered
264: live resource against the default value declared in the corresponding AVM module's
265: `variables.tf`. Any property where the live value differs from the module default must be
266: set explicitly in the Terraform configuration.
267: 
268: Pay particular attention to the following property categories, which are common sources
269: of silent configuration drift:
270: 
271: - **Timeout values** (e.g., Public IP `idle_timeout_in_minutes` defaults to `4`; live
272: 	deployments often use `30`)
273: - **Network policy flags** (e.g., subnet `private_endpoint_network_policies` defaults to
274: 	`"Enabled"`; existing subnets often have `"Disabled"`)
275: - **SKU and allocation** (e.g., Public IP `sku`, `allocation_method`)
276: - **Availability zones** (e.g., VM zone, Public IP zone)
277: - **Redundancy and replication** settings on storage and database resources
278: 
279: Retrieve full live properties with explicit `az` commands, for example:
280: 
281: ```bash
282: az network public-ip show --ids <resource_id> --query "{idleTimeout:idleTimeoutInMinutes, sku:sku.name, zones:zones}" -o json
283: az network vnet subnet show --ids <resource_id> --query "{privateEndpointPolicies:privateEndpointNetworkPolicies, delegation:delegations}" -o json
284: ```
285: 
286: Do not rely solely on `az resource list` output, which may omit nested or computed properties.
287: 
288: Pin module versions explicitly:
289: 
290: ```hcl
291: module "example" {
292: 	source  = "Azure/<module>/azurerm"
293: 	version = "<latest-compatible-version>"
294: }
295: ```
296: 
297: ### 7) Validate Generated Code
298: 
299: Run:
300: 
301: ```bash
302: terraform init
303: terraform fmt -recursive
304: terraform validate
305: terraform plan
306: ```
307: 
308: Expected output: no syntax errors, no validation errors, and a plan that matches discovered infrastructure intent.
309: 
310: ## Troubleshooting
311: 
312: | Problem | Likely Cause | Action |
313: |---|---|---|
314: | `az` command fails with authorization errors | Wrong tenant/subscription or missing RBAC role | Re-run `az login`, verify subscription context, confirm required permissions |
315: | Discovery output is empty | Incorrect scope or no resources in scope | Re-check scope input and run scoped list/show command again |
316: | No AVM module found for a resource type | Resource type not yet covered by AVM | Use native `azurerm_*` resource for that type and document the gap |
317: | `terraform validate` fails | Missing variables or unresolved dependencies | Add required variables and explicit dependencies, then re-run validation |
318: | Unknown argument or variable not found in module | AVM variable name differs from `azurerm` provider argument name | Read the module README `variables.tf` or Optional Inputs section for the correct name |
319: | Import block fails — resource not found at address | Wrong provider label (`azurerm_` vs `azapi_`), missing sub-module path, or missing `[0]` index | Run `grep "^resource" .terraform/modules/<key>/main*.tf` and `grep "^module"` to find exact address |
320: | `terraform plan` shows unexpected `~ update` on imported resource | Live value differs from AVM module default | Fetch live property with `az <resource> show`, compare to module default, add explicit value |
321: | Child-resource module gives "provider configuration not present" | Child resources declared as standalone modules even though parent module owns them | Check Required Inputs in README, remove incorrect standalone modules, and model child resources using the parent module's documented input structure |
322: | Nested child resource import fails with "resource not found" | Missing intermediate module path, wrong map key, or missing index | Inspect module blocks and `count`/`for_each` in source; build full nested import address including all module segments and required key/index |
323: | Tool tries to read ARM resource ID as file path or asks repeated scope questions | Resource ID not treated as `--ids` input, or agent did not trust already-provided scope | Treat ARM IDs strictly as cloud identifiers, use `az ... --ids ...`, and stop re-prompting once one valid scope is present |
324: 
325: ## Response Contract
326: 
327: When returning results, provide:
328: 
329: 1. Scope used (subscription, resource group, or resource IDs)
330: 2. Discovery files created
331: 3. Resource types detected
332: 4. AVM modules selected with versions
333: 5. Terraform files generated or updated
334: 6. Validation command results
335: 7. Open gaps requiring user input (if any)
336: 
337: ## Execution Rules for the Agent
338: 
339: - Do not continue if scope is missing.
340: - Do not claim successful import without listing discovered files and validation output.
341: - Do not skip dependency mapping before generating Terraform.
342: - Prefer AVM modules first; justify each non-AVM fallback explicitly.
343: - **Read the README for every AVM module before writing code.** Required Inputs identify
344: 	which child resources the module owns. Optional Inputs document exact variable names and
345: 	types. Usage examples show provider-specific conventions (`parent_id` vs
346: 	`resource_group_name`). Skipping the README is the single most common cause of
347: 	code errors in AVM-based imports.
348: - **Never assume NIC, extension, or public IP resources are standalone.** For
349: 	any AVM module, treat child resources as parent-owned unless the README explicitly indicates
350: 	a separate module is required. Check Required Inputs before creating sibling modules.
351: - **Never write import addresses from memory.** After `terraform init`, grep the downloaded
352: 	module source to discover the actual provider (`azurerm` vs `azapi`), resource labels,
353: 	sub-module nesting, and `count` vs `for_each` usage before writing any `import {}` block.
354: - **Never treat ARM resource IDs as file paths.** Resource IDs belong in Azure CLI `--ids`
355: 	arguments and API queries, not file IO tools. Only read local files when a real workspace
356: 	path is provided.
357: - **Minimize prompts when scope is already known.** If subscription, resource group, or
358: 	specific resource IDs are already provided, proceed with commands directly and only ask a
359: 	follow-up when a command fails due to missing required context.
360: - **Do not declare the import complete until `terraform plan` shows 0 destroys and 0
361: 	unwanted changes.** Telemetry `+ create` resources are acceptable. Any `~ update` or
362: 	`- destroy` on real infrastructure resources must be resolved.
363: 
364: ## References
365: 
366: - [Azure Verified Modules index (Terraform)](https://github.com/Azure/Azure-Verified-Modules/tree/main/docs/static/module-indexes)
367: - [Terraform AVM Registry namespace](https://registry.terraform.io/namespaces/Azure)
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/msgraph-sdk/references/dotnet.md">
  1: # Microsoft Graph SDK for .NET
  2: 
  3: Use this reference when the target project is written in C# or another .NET language.
  4: 
  5: ## Authoritative sources
  6: 
  7: - SDK repository: <https://github.com/microsoftgraph/msgraph-sdk-dotnet>
  8: - Samples: <https://github.com/microsoftgraph/msgraph-training-dotnet>
  9: - SDK changelog: <https://github.com/microsoftgraph/msgraph-sdk-dotnet/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md>
 10: 
 11: ## Packages
 12: 
 13: ```xml
 14: <!-- Microsoft Graph SDK v5 (current) -->
 15: <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Graph" Version="5.*" />
 16: 
 17: <!-- Azure Identity for credential providers -->
 18: <PackageReference Include="Azure.Identity" Version="1.*" />
 19: ```
 20: 
 21: Install via CLI:
 22: ```bash
 23: dotnet add package Microsoft.Graph
 24: dotnet add package Azure.Identity
 25: ```
 26: 
 27: ## Client setup
 28: 
 29: ### Managed Identity (Azure-hosted apps — preferred)
 30: 
 31: ```csharp
 32: using Azure.Identity;
 33: using Microsoft.Graph;
 34: 
 35: var credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();
 36: var graphClient = new GraphServiceClient(credential);
 37: ```
 38: 
 39: ### Client credentials (app-only / daemon)
 40: 
 41: ```csharp
 42: var credential = new ClientSecretCredential(
 43:     tenantId: Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("AZURE_TENANT_ID"),
 44:     clientId: Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("AZURE_CLIENT_ID"),
 45:     clientSecret: Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET")
 46: );
 47: var graphClient = new GraphServiceClient(credential);
 48: ```
 49: 
 50: Prefer `ClientCertificateCredential` over `ClientSecretCredential` in production.
 51: 
 52: ### On-Behalf-Of (OBO) — agent / API acting as the signed-in user
 53: 
 54: ```csharp
 55: // incomingToken is the bearer token received from the caller
 56: var credential = new OnBehalfOfCredential(
 57:     tenantId: Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("AZURE_TENANT_ID"),
 58:     clientId: Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("AZURE_CLIENT_ID"),
 59:     clientSecret: Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET"),
 60:     userAssertion: new UserAssertion(incomingToken)
 61: );
 62: var graphClient = new GraphServiceClient(credential);
 63: ```
 64: 
 65: ### Interactive (local dev / CLI)
 66: 
 67: ```csharp
 68: var credential = new InteractiveBrowserCredential();
 69: var graphClient = new GraphServiceClient(credential);
 70: ```
 71: 
 72: ## Common call patterns
 73: 
 74: ### Get a resource with field selection
 75: 
 76: ```csharp
 77: var user = await graphClient.Me.GetAsync(config =>
 78: {
 79:     config.QueryParameters.Select = ["displayName", "mail", "jobTitle"];
 80: });
 81: ```
 82: 
 83: ### List with filter and select
 84: 
 85: ```csharp
 86: var messages = await graphClient.Me.Messages.GetAsync(config =>
 87: {
 88:     config.QueryParameters.Filter = "isRead eq false";
 89:     config.QueryParameters.Select = ["subject", "from", "receivedDateTime"];
 90:     config.QueryParameters.Top = 25;
 91:     config.QueryParameters.Orderby = ["receivedDateTime desc"];
 92: });
 93: ```
 94: 
 95: ### Pagination with PageIterator
 96: 
 97: ```csharp
 98: var messages = await graphClient.Me.Messages.GetAsync();
 99: 
100: var allMessages = new List<Message>();
101: var pageIterator = PageIterator<Message, MessageCollectionResponse>
102:     .CreatePageIterator(graphClient, messages, (msg) =>
103:     {
104:         allMessages.Add(msg);
105:         return true; // return false to stop early
106:     });
107: 
108: await pageIterator.IterateAsync();
109: ```
110: 
111: ### Send an email
112: 
113: ```csharp
114: await graphClient.Me.SendMail.PostAsync(new SendMailPostRequestBody
115: {
116:     Message = new Message
117:     {
118:         Subject = "Hello from Graph",
119:         Body = new ItemBody { ContentType = BodyType.Text, Content = "Test message" },
120:         ToRecipients = [new Recipient { EmailAddress = new EmailAddress { Address = "user@contoso.com" } }]
121:     }
122: });
123: ```
124: 
125: ### Post a Teams channel message
126: 
127: ```csharp
128: await graphClient.Teams[teamId].Channels[channelId].Messages.PostAsync(new ChatMessage
129: {
130:     Body = new ItemBody { ContentType = BodyType.Html, Content = "<b>Hello from Graph!</b>" }
131: });
132: ```
133: 
134: ## Batch requests
135: 
136: ```csharp
137: using Microsoft.Graph.Models;
138: 
139: var batchRequestContent = new BatchRequestContentCollection(graphClient);
140: 
141: var meRequest = await batchRequestContent.AddBatchRequestStepAsync(
142:     graphClient.Me.ToGetRequestInformation());
143: var messagesRequest = await batchRequestContent.AddBatchRequestStepAsync(
144:     graphClient.Me.Messages.ToGetRequestInformation());
145: 
146: var batchResponse = await graphClient.Batch.PostAsync(batchRequestContent);
147: 
148: var me = await batchResponse.GetResponseByIdAsync<User>(meRequest);
149: var msgs = await batchResponse.GetResponseByIdAsync<MessageCollectionResponse>(messagesRequest);
150: ```
151: 
152: ## Delta queries
153: 
154: ```csharp
155: // First sync — get all + deltaLink
156: var deltaResponse = await graphClient.Users.Delta.GetAsDeltaGetResponseAsync();
157: string? deltaLink = null;
158: 
159: var pageIterator = PageIterator<User, Microsoft.Graph.Users.Delta.DeltaGetResponse>
160:     .CreatePageIterator(graphClient, deltaResponse, (user) => { /* process */ return true; },
161:         (req) => { deltaLink = /* extract from response */; return req; });
162: 
163: await pageIterator.IterateAsync();
164: // Store deltaLink for next run
165: 
166: // Subsequent sync — only changes
167: // Use the stored deltaLink directly as the next request URL
168: ```
169: 
170: ## Throttling / retry middleware
171: 
172: The SDK includes retry middleware enabled by default. For explicit control:
173: 
174: ```csharp
175: var handlers = GraphClientFactory.CreateDefaultHandlers();
176: // RetryHandler is included; configure max retries if needed
177: var httpClient = GraphClientFactory.Create(handlers);
178: var graphClient = new GraphServiceClient(httpClient, credential);
179: ```
180: 
181: Always check `Retry-After` if building custom retry logic — do not use fixed exponential backoff.
182: 
183: ## Dependency injection (ASP.NET Core / .NET Worker)
184: 
185: ```csharp
186: // Program.cs
187: builder.Services.AddSingleton<GraphServiceClient>(_ =>
188: {
189:     var credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();
190:     return new GraphServiceClient(credential);
191: });
192: ```
193: 
194: ## .NET-specific guidance
195: 
196: - Target .NET 8+ for new projects.
197: - Use `async`/`await` throughout — all Graph SDK calls are async.
198: - Register `GraphServiceClient` as a singleton (it caches tokens internally).
199: - Use `ILogger` to log Graph exceptions — catch `ODataError` for Graph-specific error details.
200: - For ASP.NET Core APIs using OBO, inject the incoming token from `IHttpContextAccessor` and construct the credential per-request (not as a singleton).
201: 
202: ```csharp
203: // Catching Graph errors
204: try
205: {
206:     var user = await graphClient.Me.GetAsync();
207: }
208: catch (ODataError odataError)
209: {
210:     Console.WriteLine($"Graph error: {odataError.Error?.Code} - {odataError.Error?.Message}");
211: }
212: ```
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/msgraph-sdk/references/python.md">
  1: # Microsoft Graph SDK for Python
  2: 
  3: Use this reference when the target project is written in Python.
  4: 
  5: ## Authoritative sources
  6: 
  7: - SDK repository: <https://github.com/microsoftgraph/msgraph-sdk-python>
  8: - Samples: <https://github.com/microsoftgraph/msgraph-training-python>
  9: - SDK changelog: <https://github.com/microsoftgraph/msgraph-sdk-python/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md>
 10: 
 11: ## Packages
 12: 
 13: ```bash
 14: pip install msgraph-sdk azure-identity
 15: ```
 16: 
 17: Or in `requirements.txt` / `pyproject.toml`:
 18: 
 19: ```
 20: msgraph-sdk>=1.0.0
 21: azure-identity>=1.15.0
 22: ```
 23: 
 24: ## Client setup
 25: 
 26: ### Managed Identity (Azure-hosted apps — preferred)
 27: 
 28: ```python
 29: from azure.identity.aio import DefaultAzureCredential
 30: from msgraph import GraphServiceClient
 31: 
 32: credential = DefaultAzureCredential()
 33: graph_client = GraphServiceClient(credential)
 34: ```
 35: 
 36: The Python SDK is async-first (`asyncio`). Use `azure.identity.aio` (async variants), not `azure.identity`.
 37: 
 38: ### Client credentials (app-only / daemon)
 39: 
 40: ```python
 41: import os
 42: from azure.identity.aio import ClientSecretCredential
 43: from msgraph import GraphServiceClient
 44: 
 45: credential = ClientSecretCredential(
 46:     tenant_id=os.environ["AZURE_TENANT_ID"],
 47:     client_id=os.environ["AZURE_CLIENT_ID"],
 48:     client_secret=os.environ["AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET"],
 49: )
 50: 
 51: graph_client = GraphServiceClient(credential)
 52: ```
 53: 
 54: Prefer `CertificateCredential` over `ClientSecretCredential` in production.
 55: 
 56: ### On-Behalf-Of (OBO) — agent / API acting as the signed-in user
 57: 
 58: ```python
 59: from azure.identity.aio import OnBehalfOfCredential
 60: 
 61: # incoming_token is the bearer token from the caller
 62: credential = OnBehalfOfCredential(
 63:     tenant_id=os.environ["AZURE_TENANT_ID"],
 64:     client_id=os.environ["AZURE_CLIENT_ID"],
 65:     client_secret=os.environ["AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET"],
 66:     user_assertion=incoming_token,
 67: )
 68: 
 69: graph_client = GraphServiceClient(credential)
 70: ```
 71: 
 72: Construct a new `GraphServiceClient` per request for OBO — the credential is user-scoped.
 73: 
 74: ### Device code (CLI / local dev)
 75: 
 76: ```python
 77: from azure.identity.aio import DeviceCodeCredential
 78: 
 79: credential = DeviceCodeCredential(
 80:     client_id=os.environ["AZURE_CLIENT_ID"],
 81:     tenant_id=os.environ["AZURE_TENANT_ID"],
 82: )
 83: graph_client = GraphServiceClient(credential, scopes=["User.Read", "Mail.Read"])
 84: ```
 85: 
 86: ## Common call patterns
 87: 
 88: All Graph SDK calls in Python are async. Always run inside an async context.
 89: 
 90: ### Get a resource with field selection
 91: 
 92: ```python
 93: import asyncio
 94: from msgraph.generated.me.me_request_builder import MeRequestBuilder
 95: from kiota_abstractions.base_request_configuration import RequestConfiguration
 96: 
 97: async def get_my_profile():
 98:     query_params = MeRequestBuilder.MeRequestBuilderGetQueryParameters(
 99:         select=["displayName", "mail", "jobTitle"]
100:     )
101:     config = RequestConfiguration(query_parameters=query_params)
102:     user = await graph_client.me.get(request_configuration=config)
103:     return user
104: 
105: asyncio.run(get_my_profile())
106: ```
107: 
108: ### List messages with filter and select
109: 
110: ```python
111: from msgraph.generated.me.messages.messages_request_builder import MessagesRequestBuilder
112: 
113: async def get_unread_messages():
114:     query_params = MessagesRequestBuilder.MessagesRequestBuilderGetQueryParameters(
115:         filter="isRead eq false",
116:         select=["subject", "from", "receivedDateTime"],
117:         top=25,
118:         orderby=["receivedDateTime desc"],
119:     )
120:     config = RequestConfiguration(query_parameters=query_params)
121:     result = await graph_client.me.messages.get(request_configuration=config)
122:     return result
123: ```
124: 
125: ### Pagination with PageIterator
126: 
127: ```python
128: from msgraph.generated.models.message import Message
129: from msgraph.core import PageIterator
130: 
131: async def get_all_messages():
132:     first_page = await graph_client.me.messages.get()
133:     all_messages: list[Message] = []
134: 
135:     async def process_message(message: Message) -> bool:
136:         all_messages.append(message)
137:         return True  # return False to stop early
138: 
139:     page_iterator = PageIterator(
140:         response=first_page,
141:         request_adapter=graph_client.request_adapter,
142:         constructor=Message,
143:     )
144:     await page_iterator.iterate(callback=process_message)
145:     return all_messages
146: ```
147: 
148: ### Send an email
149: 
150: ```python
151: from msgraph.generated.models.message import Message
152: from msgraph.generated.models.item_body import ItemBody
153: from msgraph.generated.models.body_type import BodyType
154: from msgraph.generated.models.recipient import Recipient
155: from msgraph.generated.models.email_address import EmailAddress
156: from msgraph.generated.me.send_mail.send_mail_post_request_body import SendMailPostRequestBody
157: 
158: async def send_email():
159:     body = SendMailPostRequestBody(
160:         message=Message(
161:             subject="Hello from Graph",
162:             body=ItemBody(content_type=BodyType.Text, content="Test message"),
163:             to_recipients=[
164:                 Recipient(email_address=EmailAddress(address="user@contoso.com"))
165:             ],
166:         )
167:     )
168:     await graph_client.me.send_mail.post(body)
169: ```
170: 
171: ### Post a Teams channel message
172: 
173: ```python
174: from msgraph.generated.models.chat_message import ChatMessage
175: from msgraph.generated.models.item_body import ItemBody
176: from msgraph.generated.models.body_type import BodyType
177: 
178: async def post_channel_message(team_id: str, channel_id: str):
179:     message = ChatMessage(
180:         body=ItemBody(content_type=BodyType.Html, content="<b>Hello from Graph!</b>")
181:     )
182:     await graph_client.teams.by_team_id(team_id).channels.by_channel_id(channel_id).messages.post(message)
183: ```
184: 
185: ## Batch requests
186: 
187: ```python
188: from kiota_http.middleware.options import ResponseHandlerOption
189: import json
190: 
191: async def batch_example():
192:     batch_body = {
193:         "requests": [
194:             {"id": "1", "method": "GET", "url": "/me"},
195:             {"id": "2", "method": "GET", "url": "/me/messages?$top=5&$select=subject"},
196:         ]
197:     }
198:     # Use the raw HTTP client for batch
199:     response = await graph_client.request_adapter.send_primitive_async(
200:         # Alternatively, use the requests library with a token from the credential
201:     )
202: ```
203: 
204: For batch in Python, it's often simpler to use `httpx` with an acquired token when the batch helper is not yet fully supported:
205: 
206: ```python
207: import httpx
208: from azure.identity.aio import ClientSecretCredential
209: 
210: async def batch_with_httpx(credential):
211:     token = await credential.get_token("https://graph.microsoft.com/.default")
212:     async with httpx.AsyncClient() as client:
213:         response = await client.post(
214:             "https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/$batch",
215:             headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {token.token}"},
216:             json={
217:                 "requests": [
218:                     {"id": "1", "method": "GET", "url": "/me"},
219:                     {"id": "2", "method": "GET", "url": "/me/messages?$top=5"},
220:                 ]
221:             },
222:         )
223:     return response.json()
224: ```
225: 
226: ## Delta queries
227: 
228: ```python
229: async def delta_sync(stored_delta_link: str | None = None):
230:     if stored_delta_link:
231:         # Use delta link directly
232:         response = await graph_client.request_adapter.send_async(...)
233:     else:
234:         response = await graph_client.users.delta.get()
235: 
236:     users = []
237:     async def collect(user):
238:         users.append(user)
239:         return True
240: 
241:     page_iterator = PageIterator(response=response, request_adapter=graph_client.request_adapter, constructor=...)
242:     await page_iterator.iterate(callback=collect)
243: 
244:     delta_link = page_iterator.delta_link  # store this for next run
245:     return users, delta_link
246: ```
247: 
248: ## Throttling / retry
249: 
250: The SDK's HTTP transport handles 429 retry automatically when using the default `GraphClientFactory`. For explicit control:
251: 
252: ```python
253: import asyncio
254: import httpx
255: 
256: async def call_with_retry(graph_client, call_fn, max_retries=5):
257:     for attempt in range(max_retries):
258:         try:
259:             return await call_fn()
260:         except Exception as e:
261:             if "429" in str(e):
262:                 retry_after = int(getattr(e, "retry_after", 10))
263:                 await asyncio.sleep(retry_after)
264:             else:
265:                 raise
266: ```
267: 
268: ## Python-specific guidance
269: 
270: - The Python Graph SDK is **async-first** — use `asyncio.run()` or an async framework (FastAPI, aiohttp).
271: - Always use `azure.identity.aio` (not `azure.identity`) for async contexts.
272: - Close credentials when done: `await credential.close()` or use as async context managers.
273: - Python SDK model classes use `snake_case` for properties (Graph JSON uses `camelCase` — the SDK maps automatically).
274: - Use `asyncio.gather()` for concurrent but independent Graph calls (mind throttling limits).
275: - For FastAPI: use lifespan events to init `GraphServiceClient` once and close the credential on shutdown.
276: 
277: ```python
278: # FastAPI integration example
279: from contextlib import asynccontextmanager
280: from fastapi import FastAPI
281: 
282: @asynccontextmanager
283: async def lifespan(app: FastAPI):
284:     credential = DefaultAzureCredential()
285:     app.state.graph_client = GraphServiceClient(credential)
286:     yield
287:     await credential.close()
288: 
289: app = FastAPI(lifespan=lifespan)
290: ```
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/msgraph-sdk/references/typescript.md">
  1: # Microsoft Graph SDK for TypeScript / JavaScript
  2: 
  3: Use this reference when the target project uses TypeScript or JavaScript (Node.js or browser).
  4: 
  5: ## Authoritative sources
  6: 
  7: - SDK repository: <https://github.com/microsoftgraph/msgraph-sdk-javascript>
  8: - Samples: <https://github.com/microsoftgraph/msgraph-training-typescript>
  9: - SDK changelog: <https://github.com/microsoftgraph/msgraph-sdk-javascript/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md>
 10: 
 11: ## Packages
 12: 
 13: ```bash
 14: npm install @microsoft/microsoft-graph-client @azure/identity
 15: npm install -D @microsoft/microsoft-graph-types   # TypeScript type definitions
 16: ```
 17: 
 18: For Node.js environments, also install the fetch polyfill:
 19: 
 20: ```bash
 21: npm install node-fetch
 22: ```
 23: 
 24: ## Client setup
 25: 
 26: ### Managed Identity (Azure-hosted apps — preferred)
 27: 
 28: ```typescript
 29: import { Client } from "@microsoft/microsoft-graph-client";
 30: import { TokenCredentialAuthenticationProvider } from "@microsoft/microsoft-graph-client/authProviders/azureTokenCredentials/index.js";
 31: import { DefaultAzureCredential } from "@azure/identity";
 32: 
 33: const credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();
 34: const authProvider = new TokenCredentialAuthenticationProvider(credential, {
 35:   scopes: ["https://graph.microsoft.com/.default"],
 36: });
 37: 
 38: const graphClient = Client.initWithMiddleware({ authProvider });
 39: ```
 40: 
 41: ### Client credentials (app-only / daemon)
 42: 
 43: ```typescript
 44: import { ClientSecretCredential } from "@azure/identity";
 45: 
 46: const credential = new ClientSecretCredential(
 47:   process.env.AZURE_TENANT_ID!,
 48:   process.env.AZURE_CLIENT_ID!,
 49:   process.env.AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET!
 50: );
 51: 
 52: const authProvider = new TokenCredentialAuthenticationProvider(credential, {
 53:   scopes: ["https://graph.microsoft.com/.default"],
 54: });
 55: 
 56: const graphClient = Client.initWithMiddleware({ authProvider });
 57: ```
 58: 
 59: ### On-Behalf-Of (OBO) — agent / API acting as the signed-in user
 60: 
 61: ```typescript
 62: import { OnBehalfOfCredential } from "@azure/identity";
 63: 
 64: // incomingToken is the bearer token received from the caller (e.g. from req.headers.authorization)
 65: const credential = new OnBehalfOfCredential({
 66:   tenantId: process.env.AZURE_TENANT_ID!,
 67:   clientId: process.env.AZURE_CLIENT_ID!,
 68:   clientSecret: process.env.AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET!,
 69:   userAssertionToken: incomingToken,
 70: });
 71: 
 72: const authProvider = new TokenCredentialAuthenticationProvider(credential, {
 73:   scopes: ["https://graph.microsoft.com/.default"],
 74: });
 75: 
 76: const graphClient = Client.initWithMiddleware({ authProvider });
 77: ```
 78: 
 79: For OBO, create a new client per request (credential is user-scoped, not singleton-safe).
 80: 
 81: ### Interactive (local dev / CLI — Node.js)
 82: 
 83: Use `InteractiveBrowserCredential` when a browser is available. Use `DeviceCodeCredential` for headless environments (SSH, CI-adjacent, WSL):
 84: 
 85: ```typescript
 86: import { InteractiveBrowserCredential, DeviceCodeCredential } from "@azure/identity";
 87: 
 88: // Opens a browser tab — requires redirect URI http://localhost in app registration
 89: const credential = new InteractiveBrowserCredential({
 90:   tenantId: process.env.AZURE_TENANT_ID!,
 91:   clientId: process.env.AZURE_CLIENT_ID!,
 92: });
 93: 
 94: // Prints a device code to the terminal — works in any environment
 95: const credential = new DeviceCodeCredential({
 96:   tenantId: process.env.AZURE_TENANT_ID!,
 97:   clientId: process.env.AZURE_CLIENT_ID!,
 98:   userPromptCallback: (info) => console.log(info.message),
 99: });
100: ```
101: 
102: Both require the app registration platform to be **"Mobile and desktop applications"**. Neither uses a client secret.
103: 
104: ## Common call patterns
105: 
106: ### Get a resource with field selection
107: 
108: ```typescript
109: import { User } from "@microsoft/microsoft-graph-types";
110: 
111: const user: User = await graphClient
112:   .api("/me")
113:   .select("displayName,mail,jobTitle")
114:   .get();
115: ```
116: 
117: ### List with filter, select, and ordering
118: 
119: ```typescript
120: const result = await graphClient
121:   .api("/me/messages")
122:   .filter("isRead eq false")
123:   .select("subject,from,receivedDateTime")
124:   .top(25)
125:   .orderby("receivedDateTime desc")
126:   .get();
127: ```
128: 
129: ### Pagination with PageIterator
130: 
131: ```typescript
132: import { PageIterator } from "@microsoft/microsoft-graph-client";
133: import { Message } from "@microsoft/microsoft-graph-types";
134: 
135: const firstPage = await graphClient.api("/me/messages").top(25).get();
136: 
137: const allMessages: Message[] = [];
138: 
139: const pageIterator = new PageIterator(
140:   graphClient,
141:   firstPage,
142:   (message: Message) => {
143:     allMessages.push(message);
144:     return true; // return false to stop early
145:   }
146: );
147: 
148: await pageIterator.iterate();
149: ```
150: 
151: ### Send an email
152: 
153: ```typescript
154: await graphClient.api("/me/sendMail").post({
155:   message: {
156:     subject: "Hello from Graph",
157:     body: { contentType: "Text", content: "Test message" },
158:     toRecipients: [{ emailAddress: { address: "user@contoso.com" } }],
159:   },
160: });
161: ```
162: 
163: ### Post a Teams channel message
164: 
165: ```typescript
166: await graphClient.api(`/teams/${teamId}/channels/${channelId}/messages`).post({
167:   body: { contentType: "html", content: "<b>Hello from Graph!</b>" },
168: });
169: ```
170: 
171: ### Upload a file to OneDrive (small files ≤ 4 MB)
172: 
173: ```typescript
174: const content = Buffer.from("file contents");
175: await graphClient
176:   .api(`/me/drive/root:/${fileName}:/content`)
177:   .putStream(content);
178: ```
179: 
180: For files > 4 MB, use an upload session (`createUploadSession`).
181: 
182: ## Batch requests
183: 
184: ```typescript
185: const batchRequestBody = {
186:   requests: [
187:     { id: "1", method: "GET", url: "/me" },
188:     { id: "2", method: "GET", url: "/me/messages?$top=5&$select=subject" },
189:   ],
190: };
191: 
192: const batchResponse = await graphClient.api("/$batch").post(batchRequestBody);
193: 
194: const meResponse = batchResponse.responses.find((r: any) => r.id === "1");
195: const messagesResponse = batchResponse.responses.find((r: any) => r.id === "2");
196: ```
197: 
198: ## Delta queries
199: 
200: ```typescript
201: // First sync
202: let response = await graphClient.api("/users/delta").get();
203: const users: any[] = [];
204: 
205: while (response["@odata.nextLink"]) {
206:   users.push(...response.value);
207:   response = await graphClient.api(response["@odata.nextLink"]).get();
208: }
209: users.push(...response.value);
210: 
211: const deltaLink: string = response["@odata.deltaLink"];
212: // Store deltaLink durably for next sync run
213: 
214: // Next sync — only changes
215: const changesResponse = await graphClient.api(deltaLink).get();
216: ```
217: 
218: ## Throttling / retry middleware
219: 
220: The SDK includes retry middleware by default. For explicit configuration:
221: 
222: ```typescript
223: import {
224:   Client,
225:   RetryHandlerOptions,
226:   RetryHandler,
227:   MiddlewareFactory,
228: } from "@microsoft/microsoft-graph-client";
229: 
230: const retryOptions = new RetryHandlerOptions({ maxRetries: 5 });
231: const middleware = MiddlewareFactory.getDefaultMiddlewareChain(authProvider);
232: 
233: const graphClient = Client.initWithMiddleware({ middleware });
234: ```
235: 
236: Always honour the `Retry-After` header value — do not use fixed backoff when Graph specifies a wait time.
237: 
238: ## TypeScript-specific guidance
239: 
240: - Import types from `@microsoft/microsoft-graph-types` for full IntelliSense on Graph resources.
241: - The `.api()` chain returns `any` — cast to the appropriate type from `@microsoft/microsoft-graph-types`.
242: - For ESM projects, use the `/index.js` path suffix on deep imports (e.g., `azureTokenCredentials/index.js`).
243: - Use `async`/`await` consistently — all Graph calls return Promises.
244: - Singleton the `graphClient` in application-level code (e.g., Express app init); for OBO flows, construct per-request.
245: - In Node.js 18+, `fetch` is available natively — no polyfill needed.
246: 
247: ```typescript
248: // Type-safe response example
249: import { MessageCollectionResponse } from "@microsoft/microsoft-graph-types";
250: 
251: const response: MessageCollectionResponse = await graphClient
252:   .api("/me/messages")
253:   .select("subject,from")
254:   .get();
255: 
256: const messages = response.value ?? [];
257: ```
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/msgraph-sdk/SKILL.md">
  1: ---
  2: name: msgraph-sdk
  3: description: 'Integrate Microsoft Graph SDK into any project — .NET, TypeScript/JavaScript, or Python. Covers auth patterns (client credentials, OBO, managed identity), SDK setup, calling Graph APIs, batching, delta queries, change notifications, throttling, and permission scopes. Use when accessing Microsoft 365 data (users, mail, calendar, Teams, files, SharePoint) from any application type.'
  4: ---
  5: 
  6: # Microsoft Graph SDK
  7: 
  8: Use this skill when integrating Microsoft Graph into an application to access Microsoft 365 data and services.
  9: 
 10: Always ground implementation in the current Microsoft Graph SDK documentation and SDK version for the target language rather than relying on memory alone.
 11: 
 12: ## Determine the target language first
 13: 
 14: 1. Use the **.NET** workflow when the project contains `.cs`, `.csproj`, or `.sln` files, or when the user asks for C# guidance. Follow [references/dotnet.md](references/dotnet.md).
 15: 2. Use the **TypeScript / JavaScript** workflow when the project contains `package.json`, `.ts`, or `.js` files, or when the user asks for Node.js / browser guidance. Follow [references/typescript.md](references/typescript.md).
 16: 3. Use the **Python** workflow when the project contains `.py`, `pyproject.toml`, or `requirements.txt`, or when the user asks for Python guidance. Follow [references/python.md](references/python.md).
 17: 4. If multiple languages are present, match the language of the files being edited or ask the user.
 18: 
 19: ## Always consult live documentation
 20: 
 21: - Microsoft Graph overview: <https://learn.microsoft.com/graph/overview>
 22: - Graph Explorer (try calls live): <https://developer.microsoft.com/graph/graph-explorer>
 23: - Graph permissions reference: <https://learn.microsoft.com/graph/permissions-reference>
 24: - Use Microsoft Docs MCP tooling when available to fetch current API shapes and SDK samples.
 25: 
 26: ## Authentication — choose the right pattern
 27: 
 28: Selecting the wrong auth flow is the most common Graph integration mistake. Apply this decision tree before writing any auth code:
 29: 
 30: | Scenario | Flow to use |
 31: |---|---|
 32: | Background service / daemon with no user | **Client credentials** (app-only) |
 33: | Agent or API acting on behalf of a signed-in user | **On-Behalf-Of (OBO)** |
 34: | App running in Azure (Function, Container App, VM) | **Managed Identity** (preferred over secrets) |
 35: | CLI tool or local dev script | **Device code** or **interactive browser** |
 36: | Single-page app (browser only) | **Authorization code + PKCE** |
 37: 
 38: - Never use client credentials when a user context is required — Graph enforces this at the permission level (application vs. delegated).
 39: - Prefer `DefaultAzureCredential` in Azure-hosted apps; it tries managed identity first and falls back gracefully for local dev.
 40: - Never hardcode secrets. Use environment variables, Azure Key Vault, or the Secret Manager.
 41: 
 42: ## Core SDK usage patterns
 43: 
 44: ### Building the client
 45: 
 46: Always construct `GraphServiceClient` once and reuse it (it manages token caching internally).
 47: 
 48: Pass a credential from the Azure Identity library — never build raw HTTP clients manually.
 49: 
 50: ### Making calls
 51: 
 52: - Use the fluent builder API: `client.Users[userId].Messages.GetAsync(...)`.
 53: - Always `await` async calls.
 54: - Specify `$select` to limit returned fields — Graph returns large default payloads.
 55: - Use `$filter` server-side rather than filtering returned collections in memory.
 56: - Use `$expand` to fetch related resources in a single call when relationships are small.
 57: 
 58: ### Pagination
 59: 
 60: Graph paginates collections. Never assume all items arrive in one response:
 61: - Check for an `@odata.nextLink` on the response.
 62: - Use the SDK's `PageIterator` helper (available in all three SDKs) to walk pages automatically.
 63: - Set `$top` to control page size (max varies by resource, typically 999).
 64: 
 65: ## Advanced patterns
 66: 
 67: ### Batch requests
 68: 
 69: Combine up to 20 independent Graph calls into a single HTTP request using the `$batch` endpoint. Use batching when:
 70: - Initializing data for a dashboard or agent that needs multiple resources upfront.
 71: - Reducing latency in high-call-count operations.
 72: 
 73: Batch responses arrive out of order — match them by the `id` field you assigned each request.
 74: 
 75: ### Delta queries
 76: 
 77: Use delta queries to sync changes incrementally instead of polling full collections:
 78: - First call: `GET /users/delta` returns all items + a `@odata.deltaLink`.
 79: - Subsequent calls: use the `deltaLink` to receive only what changed since the last sync.
 80: - Supported on: users, groups, messages, calendar events, Teams channels, and more.
 81: - Store the `deltaLink` durably (database, blob) between sync runs.
 82: 
 83: ### Change notifications (webhooks)
 84: 
 85: Subscribe to resource changes with `POST /subscriptions`:
 86: - Graph delivers change events to your HTTPS notification URL.
 87: - Subscriptions expire — renew them before `expirationDateTime` (max varies by resource; typically 1–3 days for mail/calendar, up to 4230 minutes for users/groups).
 88: - Validate the subscription handshake: Graph sends a `validationToken` query parameter on creation — echo it back as plain text with HTTP 200.
 89: - Use lifecycle notifications (`notificationUrl` + `lifecycleNotificationUrl`) to handle missed events and reauthorization.
 90: - For high-volume scenarios prefer **change notifications with resource data** (requires additional encryption setup).
 91: 
 92: ### Throttling
 93: 
 94: Graph throttles aggressively. Always handle HTTP 429:
 95: - Read the `Retry-After` header — it specifies exact seconds to wait, not a fixed backoff.
 96: - The SDK's built-in retry middleware handles 429 automatically when configured; enable it explicitly.
 97: - Avoid fan-out patterns that hit Graph with hundreds of parallel requests; use batching or queuing instead.
 98: 
 99: ## Permissions
100: 
101: Get permissions right before writing auth code — wrong scopes result in 403 errors that are hard to debug later.
102: 
103: - Application permissions run without a user (daemon / service). Require admin consent.
104: - Delegated permissions run in the context of a signed-in user. Some require admin consent.
105: - Request the **minimum permissions** needed. Graph's permission reference lists least-privilege options for every operation.
106: - Use the Graph Explorer to test which permissions a call actually requires before coding.
107: - In Azure app registrations: grant API permissions → Microsoft Graph → select type (Application or Delegated) → grant admin consent where required.
108: 
109: ## Common Graph resources — quick reference
110: 
111: | Goal | Resource path |
112: |---|---|
113: | Get signed-in user's profile | `GET /me` |
114: | List user's mailbox messages | `GET /me/messages` |
115: | Send an email | `POST /me/sendMail` |
116: | List calendar events | `GET /me/events` |
117: | Get user's OneDrive root | `GET /me/drive/root/children` |
118: | List Teams the user is in | `GET /me/joinedTeams` |
119: | Post a Teams channel message | `POST /teams/{id}/channels/{id}/messages` |
120: | List SharePoint site lists | `GET /sites/{siteId}/lists` |
121: | Search across M365 | `POST /search/query` |
122: | List all users in tenant (app-only) | `GET /users` |
123: | Get group members | `GET /groups/{id}/members` |
124: 
125: In similar fashion, use the SDK's fluent API to navigate to these resources in code.
126: 
127: ## Workflow
128: 
129: 1. Determine the target language and read the matching reference file.
130: 2. Identify the auth scenario and choose the correct flow from the table above.
131: 3. Fetch current SDK docs and Graph Explorer examples before making implementation choices.
132: 4. Apply least-privilege permissions — confirm in the Graph permissions reference.
133: 5. Implement pagination from the start — don't assume single-page responses.
134: 6. Enable retry middleware for throttling from day one.
135: 7. For syncing scenarios, prefer delta queries over polling.
136: 8. Use the language-specific package names, auth provider setup, and code patterns from the chosen reference file.
137: 
138: ## Completion criteria
139: 
140: - Auth flow matches the scenario (not defaulting to client credentials for user-context calls).
141: - `GraphServiceClient` is constructed once and reused.
142: - All collection reads handle pagination.
143: - Throttling (429) is handled via retry middleware or explicit `Retry-After` logic.
144: - Permissions are scoped to the minimum required.
145: - No secrets or credentials are hardcoded.
146: - Code matches current SDK version patterns for the selected language.
</file>

