<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/python-pypi-package-builder/references/architecture-patterns.md">
  1: # Architecture Patterns — Backend System, Config, Transport, CLI
  2: 
  3: ## Table of Contents
  4: 1. [Backend System (Plugin/Strategy Pattern)](#1-backend-system-pluginstrategy-pattern)
  5: 2. [Config Layer (Settings Dataclass)](#2-config-layer-settings-dataclass)
  6: 3. [Transport Layer (HTTP Client Abstraction)](#3-transport-layer-http-client-abstraction)
  7: 4. [CLI Support](#4-cli-support)
  8: 5. [Backend Injection in Core Client](#5-backend-injection-in-core-client)
  9: 6. [Decision Rules](#6-decision-rules)
 10: 
 11: ---
 12: 
 13: ## 1. Backend System (Plugin/Strategy Pattern)
 14: 
 15: Structure your `backends/` sub-package with a clear base protocol, a zero-dependency default
 16: implementation, and optional heavy implementations behind extras.
 17: 
 18: ### Directory Layout
 19: 
 20: ```
 21: your_package/
 22:   backends/
 23:     __init__.py    # Exports BaseBackend + factory; holds the Protocol/ABC
 24:     base.py        # Abstract base class (ABC) or Protocol definition
 25:     memory.py      # Default, zero-dependency in-memory implementation
 26:     redis.py       # Optional, heavier implementation (guarded by extras)
 27: ```
 28: 
 29: ### `backends/base.py` — Abstract Interface
 30: 
 31: ```python
 32: # your_package/backends/base.py
 33: from __future__ import annotations
 34: 
 35: from abc import ABC, abstractmethod
 36: 
 37: 
 38: class BaseBackend(ABC):
 39:     """Abstract storage/processing backend.
 40: 
 41:     All concrete backends must implement these methods.
 42:     Never import heavy dependencies at module level — guard them inside the class.
 43:     """
 44: 
 45:     @abstractmethod
 46:     def get(self, key: str) -> str | None:
 47:         """Retrieve a value by key. Return None when the key does not exist."""
 48:         ...
 49: 
 50:     @abstractmethod
 51:     def set(self, key: str, value: str, ttl: int | None = None) -> None:
 52:         """Store a value with an optional TTL (seconds)."""
 53:         ...
 54: 
 55:     @abstractmethod
 56:     def delete(self, key: str) -> None:
 57:         """Remove a key. No-op when the key does not exist."""
 58:         ...
 59: 
 60:     def close(self) -> None:  # noqa: B027  (intentionally non-abstract)
 61:         """Optional cleanup hook. Override in backends that hold connections."""
 62: ```
 63: 
 64: ### `backends/memory.py` — Default Zero-Dep Implementation
 65: 
 66: ```python
 67: # your_package/backends/memory.py
 68: from __future__ import annotations
 69: 
 70: import time
 71: from collections.abc import Iterator
 72: from contextlib import contextmanager
 73: from threading import Lock
 74: 
 75: from .base import BaseBackend
 76: 
 77: 
 78: class MemoryBackend(BaseBackend):
 79:     """Thread-safe in-memory backend. No external dependencies required."""
 80: 
 81:     def __init__(self) -> None:
 82:         self._store: dict[str, tuple[str, float | None]] = {}
 83:         self._lock = Lock()
 84: 
 85:     def get(self, key: str) -> str | None:
 86:         with self._lock:
 87:             entry = self._store.get(key)
 88:             if entry is None:
 89:                 return None
 90:             value, expires_at = entry
 91:             if expires_at is not None and time.monotonic() > expires_at:
 92:                 del self._store[key]
 93:                 return None
 94:             return value
 95: 
 96:     def set(self, key: str, value: str, ttl: int | None = None) -> None:
 97:         expires_at = time.monotonic() + ttl if ttl is not None else None
 98:         with self._lock:
 99:             self._store[key] = (value, expires_at)
100: 
101:     def delete(self, key: str) -> None:
102:         with self._lock:
103:             self._store.pop(key, None)
104: ```
105: 
106: ### `backends/redis.py` — Optional Heavy Implementation
107: 
108: ```python
109: # your_package/backends/redis.py
110: from __future__ import annotations
111: 
112: from .base import BaseBackend
113: 
114: 
115: class RedisBackend(BaseBackend):
116:     """Redis-backed implementation. Requires: pip install your-package[redis]"""
117: 
118:     def __init__(self, url: str = "redis://localhost:6379/0") -> None:
119:         try:
120:             import redis as _redis
121:         except ImportError as exc:
122:             raise ImportError(
123:                 "RedisBackend requires redis. "
124:                 "Install it with: pip install your-package[redis]"
125:             ) from exc
126:         self._client = _redis.from_url(url, decode_responses=True)
127: 
128:     def get(self, key: str) -> str | None:
129:         return self._client.get(key)  # type: ignore[return-value]
130: 
131:     def set(self, key: str, value: str, ttl: int | None = None) -> None:
132:         if ttl is not None:
133:             self._client.setex(key, ttl, value)
134:         else:
135:             self._client.set(key, value)
136: 
137:     def delete(self, key: str) -> None:
138:         self._client.delete(key)
139: 
140:     def close(self) -> None:
141:         self._client.close()
142: ```
143: 
144: ### `backends/__init__.py` — Public API + Factory
145: 
146: ```python
147: # your_package/backends/__init__.py
148: from __future__ import annotations
149: 
150: from .base import BaseBackend
151: from .memory import MemoryBackend
152: 
153: __all__ = ["BaseBackend", "MemoryBackend", "get_backend"]
154: 
155: 
156: def get_backend(backend_type: str = "memory", **kwargs: object) -> BaseBackend:
157:     """Factory: return the requested backend instance.
158: 
159:     Args:
160:         backend_type: "memory" (default) or "redis".
161:         **kwargs: Forwarded to the backend constructor.
162:     """
163:     if backend_type == "memory":
164:         return MemoryBackend()
165:     if backend_type == "redis":
166:         from .redis import RedisBackend  # Late import — redis is optional
167:         return RedisBackend(**kwargs)  # type: ignore[arg-type]
168:     raise ValueError(f"Unknown backend type: {backend_type!r}")
169: ```
170: 
171: ---
172: 
173: ## 2. Config Layer (Settings Dataclass)
174: 
175: Centralise all configuration in one `config.py` module. Avoid scattering magic values and
176: `os.environ` calls across the codebase.
177: 
178: ### `config.py`
179: 
180: ```python
181: # your_package/config.py
182: from __future__ import annotations
183: 
184: import os
185: from dataclasses import dataclass, field
186: 
187: 
188: @dataclass
189: class Settings:
190:     """All runtime configuration for your package.
191: 
192:     Attributes:
193:         api_key:  Authentication credential. Never log or expose this.
194:         timeout:  HTTP request timeout in seconds.
195:         retries:  Maximum number of retry attempts on transient failures.
196:         base_url: API base URL. Override in tests with a local server.
197:     """
198: 
199:     api_key: str
200:     timeout: int = 30
201:     retries: int = 3
202:     base_url: str = "https://api.example.com/v1"
203: 
204:     def __post_init__(self) -> None:
205:         if not self.api_key:
206:             raise ValueError("api_key must not be empty")
207:         if self.timeout < 1:
208:             raise ValueError("timeout must be >= 1")
209:         if self.retries < 0:
210:             raise ValueError("retries must be >= 0")
211: 
212:     @classmethod
213:     def from_env(cls) -> "Settings":
214:         """Construct Settings from environment variables.
215: 
216:         Required env var: YOUR_PACKAGE_API_KEY
217:         Optional env vars: YOUR_PACKAGE_TIMEOUT, YOUR_PACKAGE_RETRIES
218:         """
219:         api_key = os.environ.get("YOUR_PACKAGE_API_KEY", "")
220:         timeout = int(os.environ.get("YOUR_PACKAGE_TIMEOUT", "30"))
221:         retries = int(os.environ.get("YOUR_PACKAGE_RETRIES", "3"))
222:         return cls(api_key=api_key, timeout=timeout, retries=retries)
223: ```
224: 
225: ### Using Pydantic (optional, for larger projects)
226: 
227: ```python
228: # your_package/config.py  — Pydantic v2 variant
229: from __future__ import annotations
230: 
231: from pydantic import Field
232: from pydantic_settings import BaseSettings
233: 
234: 
235: class Settings(BaseSettings):
236:     api_key: str = Field(..., min_length=1)
237:     timeout: int = Field(30, ge=1)
238:     retries: int = Field(3, ge=0)
239:     base_url: str = "https://api.example.com/v1"
240: 
241:     model_config = {"env_prefix": "YOUR_PACKAGE_"}
242: ```
243: 
244: ---
245: 
246: ## 3. Transport Layer (HTTP Client Abstraction)
247: 
248: Isolate all HTTP concerns — headers, retries, timeouts, error parsing — in a dedicated
249: `transport/` sub-package. The core client depends on the transport abstraction, not on `httpx`
250: or `requests` directly.
251: 
252: ### Directory Layout
253: 
254: ```
255: your_package/
256:   transport/
257:     __init__.py    # Re-exports HttpTransport
258:     http.py        # Concrete httpx-based transport
259: ```
260: 
261: ### `transport/http.py`
262: 
263: ```python
264: # your_package/transport/http.py
265: from __future__ import annotations
266: 
267: from typing import Any
268: 
269: import httpx
270: 
271: from ..config import Settings
272: from ..exceptions import YourPackageError, RateLimitError, AuthenticationError
273: 
274: 
275: class HttpTransport:
276:     """Thin httpx wrapper that centralises auth, retries, and error mapping."""
277: 
278:     def __init__(self, settings: Settings) -> None:
279:         self._settings = settings
280:         self._client = httpx.Client(
281:             base_url=settings.base_url,
282:             timeout=settings.timeout,
283:             headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {settings.api_key}"},
284:         )
285: 
286:     def request(
287:         self,
288:         method: str,
289:         path: str,
290:         *,
291:         json: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
292:         params: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
293:     ) -> dict[str, Any]:
294:         """Send an HTTP request and return the parsed JSON body.
295: 
296:         Raises:
297:             AuthenticationError: on 401.
298:             RateLimitError: on 429.
299:             YourPackageError: on all other non-2xx responses.
300:         """
301:         response = self._client.request(method, path, json=json, params=params)
302:         self._raise_for_status(response)
303:         return response.json()
304: 
305:     def _raise_for_status(self, response: httpx.Response) -> None:
306:         if response.status_code == 401:
307:             raise AuthenticationError("Invalid or expired API key.")
308:         if response.status_code == 429:
309:             raise RateLimitError("Rate limit exceeded. Back off and retry.")
310:         if response.is_error:
311:             raise YourPackageError(
312:                 f"API error {response.status_code}: {response.text[:200]}"
313:             )
314: 
315:     def close(self) -> None:
316:         self._client.close()
317: 
318:     def __enter__(self) -> "HttpTransport":
319:         return self
320: 
321:     def __exit__(self, *args: object) -> None:
322:         self.close()
323: ```
324: 
325: ### Async variant
326: 
327: ```python
328: # your_package/transport/async_http.py
329: from __future__ import annotations
330: 
331: from typing import Any
332: 
333: import httpx
334: 
335: from ..config import Settings
336: from ..exceptions import YourPackageError, RateLimitError, AuthenticationError
337: 
338: 
339: class AsyncHttpTransport:
340:     """Async httpx wrapper. Use with `async with AsyncHttpTransport(...) as t:`."""
341: 
342:     def __init__(self, settings: Settings) -> None:
343:         self._settings = settings
344:         self._client = httpx.AsyncClient(
345:             base_url=settings.base_url,
346:             timeout=settings.timeout,
347:             headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {settings.api_key}"},
348:         )
349: 
350:     async def request(
351:         self,
352:         method: str,
353:         path: str,
354:         *,
355:         json: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
356:         params: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
357:     ) -> dict[str, Any]:
358:         response = await self._client.request(method, path, json=json, params=params)
359:         self._raise_for_status(response)
360:         return response.json()
361: 
362:     def _raise_for_status(self, response: httpx.Response) -> None:
363:         if response.status_code == 401:
364:             raise AuthenticationError("Invalid or expired API key.")
365:         if response.status_code == 429:
366:             raise RateLimitError("Rate limit exceeded. Back off and retry.")
367:         if response.is_error:
368:             raise YourPackageError(
369:                 f"API error {response.status_code}: {response.text[:200]}"
370:             )
371: 
372:     async def aclose(self) -> None:
373:         await self._client.aclose()
374: 
375:     async def __aenter__(self) -> "AsyncHttpTransport":
376:         return self
377: 
378:     async def __aexit__(self, *args: object) -> None:
379:         await self.aclose()
380: ```
381: 
382: ---
383: 
384: ## 4. CLI Support
385: 
386: Add a CLI entry point via `[project.scripts]` in `pyproject.toml`.
387: 
388: ### `pyproject.toml` entry
389: 
390: ```toml
391: [project.scripts]
392: your-cli = "your_package.cli:main"
393: ```
394: 
395: After installation, the user can run `your-cli --help` directly from the terminal.
396: 
397: ### `cli.py` — Using Click
398: 
399: ```python
400: # your_package/cli.py
401: from __future__ import annotations
402: 
403: import sys
404: 
405: import click
406: 
407: from .config import Settings
408: from .core import YourClient
409: 
410: 
411: @click.group()
412: @click.version_option()
413: def main() -> None:
414:     """your-package CLI — interact with the API from the command line."""
415: 
416: 
417: @main.command()
418: @click.option("--api-key", envvar="YOUR_PACKAGE_API_KEY", required=True, help="API key.")
419: @click.option("--timeout", default=30, show_default=True, help="Request timeout (s).")
420: @click.argument("query")
421: def search(api_key: str, timeout: int, query: str) -> None:
422:     """Search the API and print results."""
423:     settings = Settings(api_key=api_key, timeout=timeout)
424:     client = YourClient(settings=settings)
425:     try:
426:         results = client.search(query)
427:         for item in results:
428:             click.echo(item)
429:     except Exception as exc:
430:         click.echo(f"Error: {exc}", err=True)
431:         sys.exit(1)
432: ```
433: 
434: ### `cli.py` — Using Typer (modern alternative)
435: 
436: ```python
437: # your_package/cli.py
438: from __future__ import annotations
439: 
440: import typer
441: 
442: from .config import Settings
443: from .core import YourClient
444: 
445: app = typer.Typer(help="your-package CLI.")
446: 
447: 
448: @app.command()
449: def search(
450:     query: str = typer.Argument(..., help="Search query."),
451:     api_key: str = typer.Option(..., envvar="YOUR_PACKAGE_API_KEY"),
452:     timeout: int = typer.Option(30, help="Request timeout (s)."),
453: ) -> None:
454:     """Search the API and print results."""
455:     settings = Settings(api_key=api_key, timeout=timeout)
456:     client = YourClient(settings=settings)
457:     results = client.search(query)
458:     for item in results:
459:         typer.echo(item)
460: 
461: 
462: def main() -> None:
463:     app()
464: ```
465: 
466: ---
467: 
468: ## 5. Backend Injection in Core Client
469: 
470: **Critical:** always accept `backend` as a constructor argument. Never instantiate the backend
471: inside the constructor without a fallback parameter — that makes testing impossible.
472: 
473: ```python
474: # your_package/core.py
475: from __future__ import annotations
476: 
477: from .backends.base import BaseBackend
478: from .backends.memory import MemoryBackend
479: from .config import Settings
480: 
481: 
482: class YourClient:
483:     """Primary client. Accepts an injected backend for testability.
484: 
485:     Args:
486:         settings: Resolved configuration. Use Settings.from_env() for production.
487:         backend:  Storage/processing backend. Defaults to MemoryBackend when None.
488:         timeout:  Deprecated — pass a Settings object instead.
489:         retries:  Deprecated — pass a Settings object instead.
490:     """
491: 
492:     def __init__(
493:         self,
494:         api_key: str | None = None,
495:         *,
496:         settings: Settings | None = None,
497:         backend: BaseBackend | None = None,
498:         timeout: int = 30,
499:         retries: int = 3,
500:     ) -> None:
501:         if settings is None:
502:             if api_key is None:
503:                 raise ValueError("Provide either 'api_key' or 'settings'.")
504:             settings = Settings(api_key=api_key, timeout=timeout, retries=retries)
505:         self._settings = settings
506:         # CORRECT — default injected, not hardcoded
507:         self.backend: BaseBackend = backend if backend is not None else MemoryBackend()
508: 
509:     # ... methods
510: ```
511: 
512: ### Anti-Pattern — Never Do This
513: 
514: ```python
515: # BAD: hardcodes the backend; impossible to swap in tests
516: class YourClient:
517:     def __init__(self, api_key: str) -> None:
518:         self.backend = MemoryBackend()          # ← no injection possible
519: 
520: # BAD: hardcodes the package name literal in imports
521: from your_package.backends.memory import MemoryBackend   # only fine in your_package itself
522: # use relative imports inside the package:
523: from .backends.memory import MemoryBackend               # ← correct
524: ```
525: 
526: ---
527: 
528: ## 6. Decision Rules
529: 
530: ```
531: Does the package interact with external state (cache, DB, queue)?
532: ├── YES → Add backends/ with BaseBackend + MemoryBackend
533: │         Add optional heavy backends behind extras_require
534: │
535: └── NO → Skip backends/ entirely; keep core.py simple
536: 
537: Does the package call an external HTTP API?
538: ├── YES → Add transport/http.py; inject via Settings
539: │
540: └── NO → Skip transport/
541: 
542: Does the package need a command-line interface?
543: ├── YES, simple (1–3 commands) → Use argparse or click
544: │   Add [project.scripts] in pyproject.toml
545: │
546: ├── YES, complex (sub-commands, plugins) → Use click or typer
547: │
548: └── NO → Skip cli.py
549: 
550: Does runtime behaviour depend on user-supplied config?
551: ├── YES → Add config.py with Settings dataclass
552: │   Expose Settings.from_env() for production use
553: │
554: └── NO → Accept params directly in the constructor
555: ```
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/python-pypi-package-builder/references/ci-publishing.md">
  1: # CI/CD, Publishing, and Changelog
  2: 
  3: ## Table of Contents
  4: 1. [Changelog format](#1-changelog-format)
  5: 2. [ci.yml — lint, type-check, test matrix](#2-ciyml)
  6: 3. [publish.yml — triggered on version tags](#3-publishyml)
  7: 4. [PyPI Trusted Publishing (no API tokens)](#4-pypi-trusted-publishing)
  8: 5. [Manual publish fallback](#5-manual-publish-fallback)
  9: 6. [Release checklist](#6-release-checklist)
 10: 7. [Verify py.typed ships in the wheel](#7-verify-pytyped-ships-in-the-wheel)
 11: 8. [Semver change-type guide](#8-semver-change-type-guide)
 12: 
 13: ---
 14: 
 15: ## 1. Changelog Format
 16: 
 17: Keep a `CHANGELOG.md` following [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/) conventions.
 18: Every PR should update the `[Unreleased]` section. Before releasing, move those entries to a
 19: new version section with the date.
 20: 
 21: ```markdown
 22: # Changelog
 23: 
 24: All notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.
 25: 
 26: The format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/),
 27: and this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html).
 28: 
 29: ---
 30: 
 31: ## [Unreleased]
 32: 
 33: ### Added
 34: - (in-progress features go here)
 35: 
 36: ---
 37: 
 38: ## [1.0.0] - 2026-04-02
 39: 
 40: ### Added
 41: - Initial stable release
 42: - `YourMiddleware` with gradual, strict, and combined modes
 43: - In-memory backend (no extra deps)
 44: - Optional Redis backend (`pip install pkg[redis]`)
 45: - Per-route override via `Depends(RouteThrottle(...))`
 46: - `py.typed` marker — PEP 561 typed package
 47: - GitHub Actions CI: lint, mypy, test matrix, Trusted Publishing
 48: 
 49: ### Changed
 50: ### Fixed
 51: ### Removed
 52: 
 53: ---
 54: 
 55: ## [0.1.0] - 2026-03-01
 56: 
 57: ### Added
 58: - Initial project scaffold
 59: 
 60: [Unreleased]: https://github.com/you/your-package/compare/v1.0.0...HEAD
 61: [1.0.0]: https://github.com/you/your-package/compare/v0.1.0...v1.0.0
 62: [0.1.0]: https://github.com/you/your-package/releases/tag/v0.1.0
 63: ```
 64: 
 65: ### Semver — what bumps what
 66: 
 67: | Change type | Bump | Example |
 68: |---|---|---|
 69: | Breaking API change | MAJOR | `1.0.0 → 2.0.0` |
 70: | New feature, backward-compatible | MINOR | `1.0.0 → 1.1.0` |
 71: | Bug fix | PATCH | `1.0.0 → 1.0.1` |
 72: 
 73: ---
 74: 
 75: ## 2. `ci.yml`
 76: 
 77: Runs on every push and pull request. Tests across all supported Python versions.
 78: 
 79: ```yaml
 80: # .github/workflows/ci.yml
 81: name: CI
 82: 
 83: on:
 84:   push:
 85:     branches: [main, master]
 86:   pull_request:
 87:     branches: [main, master]
 88: 
 89: jobs:
 90:   lint:
 91:     name: Lint, Format & Type Check
 92:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
 93:     steps:
 94:       - uses: actions/checkout@v4
 95:       - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
 96:         with:
 97:           python-version: "3.11"
 98:       - name: Install dev dependencies
 99:         run: pip install -e ".[dev]"
100:       - name: ruff lint
101:         run: ruff check .
102:       - name: ruff format check
103:         run: ruff format --check .
104:       - name: mypy
105:         run: |
106:           if [ -d "src" ]; then
107:               mypy src/
108:           else
109:               mypy {mod}/
110:           fi
111: 
112:   test:
113:     name: Test (Python ${{ matrix.python-version }})
114:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
115:     strategy:
116:       matrix:
117:         python-version: ["3.10", "3.11", "3.12", "3.13"]
118: 
119:     steps:
120:       - uses: actions/checkout@v4
121:         with:
122:           fetch-depth: 0    # REQUIRED for setuptools_scm to read git tags
123: 
124:       - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
125:         with:
126:           python-version: ${{ matrix.python-version }}
127: 
128:       - name: Install dependencies
129:         run: pip install -e ".[dev]"
130: 
131:       - name: Run tests with coverage
132:         run: pytest --cov --cov-report=xml
133: 
134:       - name: Upload coverage
135:         uses: codecov/codecov-action@v4
136:         with:
137:           token: ${{ secrets.CODECOV_TOKEN }}
138:           fail_ci_if_error: false
139: 
140:   test-redis:
141:     name: Test Redis backend
142:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
143:     services:
144:       redis:
145:         image: redis:7-alpine
146:         ports: ["6379:6379"]
147:     steps:
148:       - uses: actions/checkout@v4
149:         with:
150:           fetch-depth: 0
151: 
152:       - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
153:         with:
154:           python-version: "3.11"
155: 
156:       - name: Install with Redis extra
157:         run: pip install -e ".[dev,redis]"
158: 
159:       - name: Run Redis tests
160:         run: pytest tests/test_redis_backend.py -v
161: ```
162: 
163: > **Always add `fetch-depth: 0`** to every checkout step when using `setuptools_scm`.
164: > Without full git history, `setuptools_scm` can't find tags and the build fails with a version
165: > detection error.
166: 
167: ---
168: 
169: ## 3. `publish.yml`
170: 
171: Triggered automatically when you push a tag matching `v*.*.*`. Uses Trusted Publishing (OIDC) —
172: no API tokens in repository secrets.
173: 
174: ```yaml
175: # .github/workflows/publish.yml
176: name: Publish to PyPI
177: 
178: on:
179:   push:
180:     tags:
181:       - "v*.*.*"
182: 
183: jobs:
184:   build:
185:     name: Build distribution
186:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
187:     steps:
188:       - uses: actions/checkout@v4
189:         with:
190:           fetch-depth: 0      # Critical for setuptools_scm
191: 
192:       - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
193:         with:
194:           python-version: "3.11"
195: 
196:       - name: Install build tools
197:         run: pip install build twine
198: 
199:       - name: Build package
200:         run: python -m build
201: 
202:       - name: Check distribution
203:         run: twine check dist/*
204: 
205:       - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
206:         with:
207:           name: dist
208:           path: dist/
209: 
210:   publish:
211:     name: Publish to PyPI
212:     needs: build
213:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
214:     environment: pypi
215:     permissions:
216:       id-token: write     # Required for Trusted Publishing (OIDC)
217: 
218:     steps:
219:       - uses: actions/download-artifact@v4
220:         with:
221:           name: dist
222:           path: dist/
223: 
224:       - name: Publish to PyPI
225:         uses: pypa/gh-action-pypi-publish@release/v1
226: ```
227: 
228: ---
229: 
230: ## 4. PyPI Trusted Publishing
231: 
232: Trusted Publishing uses OpenID Connect (OIDC) so PyPI can verify that a publish came from your
233: specific GitHub Actions workflow — no long-lived API tokens required, no rotation burden.
234: 
235: ### One-time setup
236: 
237: 1. Create an account at https://pypi.org
238: 2. Go to **Account → Publishing → Add a new pending publisher**
239: 3. Fill in:
240:    - GitHub owner (your username or org)
241:    - Repository name
242:    - Workflow filename: `publish.yml`
243:    - Environment name: `pypi`
244: 4. Create the `pypi` environment in GitHub:
245:    **repo → Settings → Environments → New environment → name it `pypi`**
246: 
247: That's it. The next time you push a `v*.*.*` tag, the workflow authenticates automatically.
248: 
249: ---
250: 
251: ## 5. Manual Publish Fallback
252: 
253: If CI isn't set up yet or you need to publish from your machine:
254: 
255: ```bash
256: pip install build twine
257: 
258: # Build wheel + sdist
259: python -m build
260: 
261: # Validate before uploading
262: twine check dist/*
263: 
264: # Upload to PyPI
265: twine upload dist/*
266: 
267: # OR test on TestPyPI first (recommended for first release)
268: twine upload --repository testpypi dist/*
269: pip install --index-url https://test.pypi.org/simple/ your-package
270: python -c "import your_package; print(your_package.__version__)"
271: ```
272: 
273: ---
274: 
275: ## 6. Release Checklist
276: 
277: ```
278: [ ] All tests pass on main/master
279: [ ] CHANGELOG.md updated — move [Unreleased] items to new version section with date
280: [ ] Update diff comparison links at bottom of CHANGELOG
281: [ ] git tag vX.Y.Z
282: [ ] git push origin master --tags
283: [ ] Monitor GitHub Actions publish.yml run
284: [ ] Verify on PyPI: pip install your-package==X.Y.Z
285: [ ] Test the installed version:
286:     python -c "import your_package; print(your_package.__version__)"
287: ```
288: 
289: ---
290: 
291: ## 7. Verify py.typed Ships in the Wheel
292: 
293: After every build, confirm the typed marker is included:
294: 
295: ```bash
296: python -m build
297: unzip -l dist/your_package-*.whl | grep py.typed
298: # Must print: your_package/py.typed
299: # If missing, check [tool.setuptools.package-data] in pyproject.toml
300: ```
301: 
302: If it's missing from the wheel, users won't get type information even though your code is
303: fully typed. This is a silent failure — always verify before releasing.
304: 
305: ---
306: 
307: ## 8. Semver Change-Type Guide
308: 
309: | Change | Version bump | Example |
310: |---|---|---|
311: | Breaking API change (remove/rename public symbol) | MAJOR | `1.2.3 → 2.0.0` |
312: | New feature, fully backward-compatible | MINOR | `1.2.3 → 1.3.0` |
313: | Bug fix, no API change | PATCH | `1.2.3 → 1.2.4` |
314: | Pre-release | suffix | `2.0.0a1 → 2.0.0rc1 → 2.0.0` |
315: | Packaging-only fix (no code change) | post-release | `1.2.3 → 1.2.3.post1` |
</file>

<file path=".github/skills/mcpee-boost-mcpee-cloud/skills/python-pypi-package-builder/references/community-docs.md">
  1: # Community Docs, PR Checklist, Anti-patterns, and Release Checklist
  2: 
  3: ## Table of Contents
  4: 1. [README.md required sections](#1-readmemd-required-sections)
  5: 2. [Docstrings — Google style](#2-docstrings--google-style)
  6: 3. [CONTRIBUTING.md template](#3-contributingmd)
  7: 4. [SECURITY.md template](#4-securitymd)
  8: 5. [GitHub Issue Templates](#5-github-issue-templates)
  9: 6. [PR Checklist](#6-pr-checklist)
 10: 7. [Anti-patterns to avoid](#7-anti-patterns-to-avoid)
 11: 8. [Master Release Checklist](#8-master-release-checklist)
 12: 
 13: ---
 14: 
 15: ## 1. `README.md` Required Sections
 16: 
 17: A good README is the single most important file for adoption. Users decide in 30 seconds whether
 18: to use your library based on the README.
 19: 
 20: ```markdown
 21: # your-package
 22: 
 23: > One-line description — what it does and why it's useful.
 24: 
 25: [![PyPI version](https://badge.fury.io/py/your-package.svg)](https://pypi.org/project/your-package/)
 26: [![Python Versions](https://img.shields.io/pypi/pyversions/your-package)](https://pypi.org/project/your-package/)
 27: [![CI](https://github.com/you/your-package/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/you/your-package/actions/workflows/ci.yml)
 28: [![Coverage](https://codecov.io/gh/you/your-package/branch/master/graph/badge.svg)](https://codecov.io/gh/you/your-package)
 29: [![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](LICENSE)
 30: 
 31: ## Installation
 32: 
 33: pip install your-package
 34: 
 35: # With Redis backend:
 36: pip install "your-package[redis]"
 37: 
 38: ## Quick Start
 39: 
 40: (A copy-paste working example — no setup required to run it)
 41: 
 42: from your_package import YourClient
 43: 
 44: client = YourClient(api_key="sk-...")
 45: result = client.process({"input": "value"})
 46: print(result)
 47: 
 48: ## Features
 49: 
 50: - Feature 1
 51: - Feature 2
 52: 
 53: ## Configuration
 54: 
 55: | Parameter | Type | Default | Description |
 56: |---|---|---|—--|
 57: | api_key | str | required | Authentication credential |
 58: | timeout | int | 30 | Request timeout in seconds |
 59: | retries | int | 3 | Number of retry attempts |
 60: 
 61: ## Backends
 62: 
 63: Brief comparison — in-memory vs Redis — and when to use each.
 64: 
 65: ## Contributing
 66: 
 67: See [CONTRIBUTING.md](./CONTRIBUTING.md)
 68: 
 69: ## Changelog
 70: 
 71: See [CHANGELOG.md](./CHANGELOG.md)
 72: 
 73: ## License
 74: 
 75: MIT — see [LICENSE](./LICENSE)
 76: ```
 77: 
 78: ---
 79: 
 80: ## 2. Docstrings — Google Style
 81: 
 82: Use Google-style docstrings for every public class, method, and function. IDEs display these
 83: as tooltips, mkdocs/sphinx can auto-generate documentation from them, and they convey intent
 84: clearly to contributors.
 85: 
 86: ```python
 87: class YourClient:
 88:     """
 89:     Main client for <purpose>.
 90: 
 91:     Args:
 92:         api_key: Authentication credential.
 93:         timeout: Request timeout in seconds. Defaults to 30.
 94:         retries: Number of retry attempts. Defaults to 3.
 95: 
 96:     Raises:
 97:         ValueError: If api_key is empty or timeout is non-positive.
 98: 
 99:     Example:
100:         >>> from your_package import YourClient
101:         >>> client = YourClient(api_key="sk-...")
102:         >>> result = client.process({"input": "value"})
103:     """
104: ```
105: 
106: ---
107: 
108: ## 3. `CONTRIBUTING.md`
109: 
110: ```markdown
111: # Contributing to your-package
112: 
113: ## Development Setup
114: 
115: git clone https://github.com/you/your-package
116: cd your-package
117: pip install -e ".[dev]"
118: pre-commit install
119: 
120: ## Running Tests
121: 
122: pytest
123: 
124: ## Running Linting
125: 
126: ruff check .
127: black . --check
128: mypy your_package/
129: 
130: ## Submitting a PR
131: 
132: 1. Fork the repository
133: 2. Create a feature branch: `git checkout -b feat/your-feature`
134: 3. Make changes with tests
135: 4. Ensure CI passes: `pre-commit run --all-files && pytest`
136: 5. Update `CHANGELOG.md` under `[Unreleased]`
137: 6. Open a PR — use the PR template
138: 
139: ## Commit Message Format (Conventional Commits)
140: 
141: - `feat: add Redis backend`
142: - `fix: correct retry behavior on timeout`
143: - `docs: update README quick start`
144: - `chore: bump ruff to 0.5`
145: - `test: add edge cases for memory backend`
146: 
147: ## Reporting Bugs
148: 
149: Use the GitHub issue template. Include Python version, package version,
150: and a minimal reproducible example.
151: ```
152: 
153: ---
154: 
155: ## 4. `SECURITY.md`
156: 
157: ```markdown
158: # Security Policy
159: 
160: ## Supported Versions
161: 
162: | Version | Supported |
163: |---|---|
164: | 1.x.x   | Yes       |
165: | < 1.0   | No        |
166: 
167: ## Reporting a Vulnerability
168: 
169: Do NOT open a public GitHub issue for security vulnerabilities.
170: 
171: Report via: GitHub private security reporting (preferred)
172: or email: security@yourdomain.com
173: 
174: Include:
175: - Description of the vulnerability
176: - Steps to reproduce
177: - Potential impact
178: - Suggested fix (if any)
179: 
180: We aim to acknowledge within 48 hours and resolve within 14 days.
181: ```
182: 
183: ---
184: 
185: ## 5. GitHub Issue Templates
186: 
187: ### `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/bug_report.md`
188: 
189: ```markdown
190: ---
191: name: Bug Report
192: about: Report a reproducible bug
193: labels: bug
194: ---
195: 
196: **Python version:**
197: **Package version:**
198: 
199: **Describe the bug:**
200: 
201: **Minimal reproducible example:**
202: ```python
203: # paste code here
204: ```
205: 
206: **Expected behavior:**
207: 
208: **Actual behavior:**
209: ```
210: 
211: ### `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/feature_request.md`
212: 
213: ```markdown
214: ---
215: name: Feature Request
216: about: Suggest a new feature or enhancement
217: labels: enhancement
218: ---
219: 
220: **Problem this would solve:**
221: 
222: **Proposed solution:**
223: 
224: **Alternatives considered:**
225: ```
226: 
227: ---
228: 
229: ## 6. PR Checklist
230: 
231: All items must be checked before requesting review. CI must be fully green.
232: 
233: ### Code Quality Gates
234: ```
235: [ ] ruff check . — zero errors
236: [ ] black . --check — zero formatting issues
237: [ ] isort . --check-only — imports sorted correctly
238: [ ] mypy your_package/ — zero type errors
239: [ ] pytest — all tests pass
240: [ ] Coverage >= 80% (enforced by fail_under in pyproject.toml)
241: [ ] All GitHub Actions workflows green
242: ```
243: 
244: ### Structure
245: ```
246: [ ] pyproject.toml: name, dynamic/version, description, requires-python, license, authors,
247:     keywords (10+), classifiers, dependencies, all [project.urls] filled in
248: [ ] dynamic = ["version"] if using setuptools_scm
249: [ ] [tool.setuptools_scm] with local_scheme = "no-local-version"
250: [ ] setup.py shim present (if using setuptools_scm)
251: [ ] py.typed marker file exists in the package directory (empty file)
252: [ ] py.typed listed in [tool.setuptools.package-data]
253: [ ] "Typing :: Typed" classifier in pyproject.toml
254: [ ] __init__.py has __all__ listing all public symbols
255: [ ] __version__ via importlib.metadata (not hardcoded string)
256: ```
257: 
258: ### Testing
259: ```
260: [ ] conftest.py has shared fixtures for client and backend
261: [ ] Core happy path tested
262: [ ] Error conditions and edge cases tested
263: [ ] Each backend tested independently in isolation
264: [ ] Redis backend tested in separate CI job with redis service (if applicable)
265: [ ] asyncio_mode = "auto" in pyproject.toml (for async tests)
266: [ ] fetch-depth: 0 in all CI checkout steps
267: ```
268: 
269: ### Optional Backend (if applicable)
270: ```
271: [ ] BaseBackend abstract class defines the interface
272: [ ] MemoryBackend works with zero extra deps
273: [ ] RedisBackend raises ImportError with clear pip install hint if redis not installed
274: [ ] Both backends unit-tested independently
275: [ ] redis extra declared in [project.optional-dependencies]
276: [ ] README shows both install paths (base and [redis])
277: ```
278: 
279: ### Changelog & Docs
280: ```
281: [ ] CHANGELOG.md updated under [Unreleased]
282: [ ] README has: description, install, quick start, config table, badges, license
283: [ ] All public symbols have Google-style docstrings
284: [ ] CONTRIBUTING.md: dev setup, test/lint commands, PR instructions
285: [ ] SECURITY.md: supported versions, reporting process
286: [ ] .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/bug_report.md
287: [ ] .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/feature_request.md
288: ```
289: 
290: ### CI/CD
291: ```
292: [ ] ci.yml: lint + mypy + test matrix (all supported Python versions)
293: [ ] ci.yml: separate job for Redis backend with redis service
294: [ ] publish.yml: triggered on v*.*.* tags, uses Trusted Publishing (OIDC)
295: [ ] fetch-depth: 0 in all workflow checkout steps
296: [ ] pypi environment created in GitHub repo Settings → Environments
297: [ ] No API tokens in repository secrets
298: ```
299: 
300: ---
301: 
302: ## 7. Anti-patterns to Avoid
303: 
304: | Anti-pattern | Why it's bad | Correct approach |
305: |---|---|---|
306: | `__version__ = "1.0.0"` hardcoded with setuptools_scm | Goes stale after first git tag | Use `importlib.metadata.version()` |
307: | Missing `fetch-depth: 0` in CI checkout | setuptools_scm can't find tags → version = `0.0.0+dev` | Add `fetch-depth: 0` to **every** checkout step |
308: | `local_scheme` not set | `+g<hash>` suffix breaks PyPI uploads (local versions rejected) | `local_scheme = "no-local-version"` |
309: | Missing `py.typed` file | IDEs and mypy don't see package as typed | Create empty `py.typed` in package root |
310: | `py.typed` not in `package-data` | File missing from installed wheel — useless | Add to `[tool.setuptools.package-data]` |
311: | Importing optional dep at module top | `ImportError` on `import your_package` for all users | Lazy import inside the function/class that needs it |
312: | Duplicating metadata in `setup.py` | Conflicts with `pyproject.toml`; drifts | Keep `setup.py` as 3-line shim only |
313: | No `fail_under` in coverage config | Coverage regressions go unnoticed | Set `fail_under = 80` |
314: | No mypy in CI | Type errors silently accumulate | Add mypy step to `ci.yml` |
315: | API tokens in GitHub Secrets for PyPI | Security risk, rotation burden | Use Trusted Publishing (OIDC) |
316: | Committing directly to `main`/`master` | Bypasses CI checks | Enforce via `no-commit-to-branch` pre-commit hook |
317: | Missing `[Unreleased]` section in CHANGELOG | Changes pile up and get forgotten at release time | Keep `[Unreleased]` updated every PR |
318: | Pinning exact dep versions in a library | Breaks dependency resolution for users | Use `>=` lower bounds only; avoid `==` |
319: | No `__all__` in `__init__.py` | Users can accidentally import internal helpers | Declare `__all__` with every public symbol |
320: | `from your_package import *` in tests | Tests pass even when imports are broken | Always use explicit imports |
321: | No `SECURITY.md` | No path for responsible vulnerability disclosure | Add file with response timeline |
322: | `Any` everywhere in type hints | Defeats mypy entirely | Use `object` for truly arbitrary values |
323: | `Union` return types | Forces every caller to write `isinstance()` checks | Return concrete types; use overloads |
324: | `setup.cfg` + `pyproject.toml` both active | Conflicts and confusing for contributors | Migrate everything to `pyproject.toml` |
325: | Releasing on untagged commits | Version number is meaningless | Always tag before release |
326: | Not testing on all supported Python versions | Breakage discovered by users, not you | Matrix test in CI |
327: | `license = {text = "MIT"}` (old form) | Deprecated; PEP 639 uses SPDX strings | `license = "MIT"` |
328: | No issue templates | Bug reports are inconsistent | Add `bug_report.md` + `feature_request.md` |
329: 
330: ---
331: 
332: ## 8. Master Release Checklist
333: 
334: Run through every item before pushing a release tag. CI must be fully green.
335: 
336: ### Code Quality
337: ```
338: [ ] ruff check . — zero errors
339: [ ] ruff format . --check — zero formatting issues
340: [ ] mypy src/your_package/ — zero type errors
341: [ ] pytest — all tests pass
342: [ ] Coverage >= 80% (fail_under enforced in pyproject.toml)
343: [ ] All GitHub Actions CI jobs green (lint + test matrix)
344: ```
345: 
346: ### Project Structure
347: ```
348: [ ] pyproject.toml — name, description, requires-python, license (SPDX string), authors,
349:     keywords (10+), classifiers (Python versions + Typing :: Typed), urls (all 5 fields)
350: [ ] dynamic = ["version"] set (if using setuptools_scm or hatch-vcs)
351: [ ] [tool.setuptools_scm] with local_scheme = "no-local-version"
352: [ ] setup.py shim present (if using setuptools_scm)
353: [ ] py.typed marker file exists (empty file in package root)
354: [ ] py.typed listed in [tool.setuptools.package-data]
355: [ ] "Typing :: Typed" classifier in pyproject.toml
356: [ ] __init__.py has __all__ listing all public symbols
357: [ ] __version__ reads from importlib.metadata (not hardcoded)
358: ```
359: 
360: ### Testing
361: ```
362: [ ] conftest.py has shared fixtures for client and backend
363: [ ] Core happy path tested
364: [ ] Error conditions and edge cases tested
365: [ ] Each backend tested independently in isolation
366: [ ] asyncio_mode = "auto" in pyproject.toml (for async tests)
367: [ ] fetch-depth: 0 in all CI checkout steps
368: ```
369: 
370: ### CHANGELOG and Docs
371: ```
372: [ ] CHANGELOG.md: [Unreleased] entries moved to [x.y.z] - YYYY-MM-DD
373: [ ] README has: description, install commands, quick start, config table, badges
374: [ ] All public symbols have Google-style docstrings
375: [ ] CONTRIBUTING.md: dev setup, test/lint commands, PR instructions
376: [ ] SECURITY.md: supported versions, reporting process with timeline
377: ```
378: 
379: ### Versioning
380: ```
381: [ ] All CI checks pass on the commit you plan to tag
382: [ ] CHANGELOG.md updated and committed
383: [ ] Git tag follows format v1.2.3 (semver, v prefix)
384: [ ] No stale local_scheme suffixes will appear in the built wheel name
385: ```
386: 
387: ### CI/CD
388: ```
389: [ ] ci.yml: lint + mypy + test matrix (all supported Python versions)
390: [ ] publish.yml: triggered on v*.*.* tags, uses Trusted Publishing (OIDC)
391: [ ] pypi environment created in GitHub repo Settings → Environments
392: [ ] No API tokens stored in repository secrets
393: ```
394: 
395: ### The Release Command Sequence
396: ```bash
397: # 1. Run full local validation
398: ruff check . ; ruff format . --check ; mypy src/your_package/ ; pytest
399: 
400: # 2. Update CHANGELOG.md — move [Unreleased] to [x.y.z]
401: # 3. Commit the changelog
402: git add CHANGELOG.md
403: git commit -m "chore: prepare release vX.Y.Z"
404: 
405: # 4. Tag and push — this triggers publish.yml automatically
406: git tag vX.Y.Z
407: git push origin main --tags
408: 
409: # 5. Monitor: https://github.com/<you>/<pkg>/actions
410: # 6. Verify: https://pypi.org/project/your-package/
411: ```
</file>

