  1: # Library Core Patterns, OOP/SOLID, and Type Hints
  2: 
  3: ## Table of Contents
  4: 1. [OOP & SOLID Principles](#1-oop--solid-principles)
  5: 2. [Type Hints Best Practices](#2-type-hints-best-practices)
  6: 3. [Core Class Design](#3-core-class-design)
  7: 4. [Factory / Builder Pattern](#4-factory--builder-pattern)
  8: 5. [Configuration Pattern](#5-configuration-pattern)
  9: 6. [`__init__.py` — explicit public API](#6-__init__py--explicit-public-api)
 10: 7. [Optional Backends (Plugin Pattern)](#7-optional-backends-plugin-pattern)
 11: 
 12: ---
 13: 
 14: ## 1. OOP & SOLID Principles
 15: 
 16: Apply these principles to produce maintainable, testable, extensible packages.
 17: **Do not over-engineer** — apply the principle that solves a real problem, not all of them
 18: at once.
 19: 
 20: ### S — Single Responsibility Principle
 21: 
 22: Each class/module should have **one reason to change**.
 23: 
 24: ```python
 25: # BAD: one class handles data, validation, AND persistence
 26: class UserManager:
 27:     def validate(self, user): ...
 28:     def save_to_db(self, user): ...
 29:     def send_email(self, user): ...
 30: 
 31: # GOOD: split responsibilities
 32: class UserValidator:
 33:     def validate(self, user: User) -> None: ...
 34: 
 35: class UserRepository:
 36:     def save(self, user: User) -> None: ...
 37: 
 38: class UserNotifier:
 39:     def notify(self, user: User) -> None: ...
 40: ```
 41: 
 42: ### O — Open/Closed Principle
 43: 
 44: Open for extension, closed for modification. Use **protocols or ABCs** as extension points.
 45: 
 46: ```python
 47: from abc import ABC, abstractmethod
 48: 
 49: class StorageBackend(ABC):
 50:     """Define the interface once; never modify it for new implementations."""
 51:     @abstractmethod
 52:     def get(self, key: str) -> str | None: ...
 53:     @abstractmethod
 54:     def set(self, key: str, value: str) -> None: ...
 55: 
 56: class MemoryBackend(StorageBackend):    # Extend by subclassing
 57:     ...
 58: 
 59: class RedisBackend(StorageBackend):     # Add new impl without touching StorageBackend
 60:     ...
 61: ```
 62: 
 63: ### L — Liskov Substitution Principle
 64: 
 65: Subclasses must be substitutable for their base. Never narrow a contract in a subclass.
 66: 
 67: ```python
 68: class BaseProcessor:
 69:     def process(self, data: dict) -> dict: ...
 70: 
 71: # BAD: raises TypeError for valid dicts — breaks substitutability
 72: class StrictProcessor(BaseProcessor):
 73:     def process(self, data: dict) -> dict:
 74:         if not data:
 75:             raise TypeError("Must have data")  # Base never raised this
 76: 
 77: # GOOD: accept what base accepts, fulfill the same contract
 78: class StrictProcessor(BaseProcessor):
 79:     def process(self, data: dict) -> dict:
 80:         if not data:
 81:             return {}   # Graceful — same return type, no new exceptions
 82: ```
 83: 
 84: ### I — Interface Segregation Principle
 85: 
 86: Prefer **small, focused protocols** over large monolithic ABCs.
 87: 
 88: ```python
 89: # BAD: forces all implementers to handle read+write+delete+list
 90: class BigStorage(ABC):
 91:     @abstractmethod
 92:     def read(self): ...
 93:     @abstractmethod
 94:     def write(self): ...
 95:     @abstractmethod
 96:     def delete(self): ...
 97:     @abstractmethod
 98:     def list_all(self): ...   # Not every backend needs this
 99: 
100: # GOOD: separate protocols — clients depend only on what they need
101: from typing import Protocol
102: 
103: class Readable(Protocol):
104:     def read(self, key: str) -> str | None: ...
105: 
106: class Writable(Protocol):
107:     def write(self, key: str, value: str) -> None: ...
108: 
109: class Deletable(Protocol):
110:     def delete(self, key: str) -> None: ...
111: ```
112: 
113: ### D — Dependency Inversion Principle
114: 
115: High-level modules depend on **abstractions** (protocols/ABCs), not concrete implementations.
116: Pass dependencies in via `__init__` (constructor injection).
117: 
118: ```python
119: # BAD: high-level class creates its own dependency
120: class ApiClient:
121:     def __init__(self) -> None:
122:         self._cache = RedisCache()   # Tightly coupled to Redis
123: 
124: # GOOD: depend on the abstraction; inject the concrete at call site
125: class ApiClient:
126:     def __init__(self, cache: CacheBackend) -> None:  # CacheBackend is a Protocol
127:         self._cache = cache
128: 
129: # User code (or tests):
130: client = ApiClient(cache=RedisCache())    # Real
131: client = ApiClient(cache=MemoryCache())  # Test
132: ```
133: 
134: ### Composition Over Inheritance
135: 
136: Prefer delegating to contained objects over deep inheritance chains.
137: 
138: ```python
139: # Prefer this (composition):
140: class YourClient:
141:     def __init__(self, backend: StorageBackend, http: HttpTransport) -> None:
142:         self._backend = backend
143:         self._http = http
144: 
145: # Avoid this (deep inheritance):
146: class YourClient(BaseClient, CacheMixin, RetryMixin, LoggingMixin):
147:     ...    # Fragile, hard to test, MRO confusion
148: ```
149: 
150: ### Exception Hierarchy
151: 
152: Always define a base exception for your package; layer specifics below it.
153: 
154: ```python
155: # your_package/exceptions.py
156: class YourPackageError(Exception):
157:     """Base exception — catch this to catch any package error."""
158: 
159: class ConfigurationError(YourPackageError):
160:     """Raised when package is misconfigured."""
161: 
162: class AuthenticationError(YourPackageError):
163:     """Raised on auth failure."""
164: 
165: class RateLimitError(YourPackageError):
166:     """Raised when rate limit is exceeded."""
167:     def __init__(self, retry_after: int) -> None:
168:         self.retry_after = retry_after
169:         super().__init__(f"Rate limited. Retry after {retry_after}s.")
170: ```
171: 
172: ---
173: 
174: ## 2. Type Hints Best Practices
175: 
176: Follow PEP 484 (type hints), PEP 526 (variable annotations), PEP 544 (protocols),
177: PEP 561 (typed packages). These are not optional for a quality library.
178: 
179: ```python
180: from __future__ import annotations    # Enables PEP 563 deferred evaluation — always add this
181: 
182: # For ARGUMENTS: prefer abstract / protocol types (more flexible for callers)
183: from collections.abc import Iterable, Mapping, Sequence, Callable
184: 
185: def process_items(items: Iterable[str]) -> list[int]: ...   # ✓ Accepts any iterable
186: def process_items(items: list[str]) -> list[int]: ...       # ✗ Too restrictive
187: 
188: # For RETURN TYPES: prefer concrete types (callers know exactly what they get)
189: def get_names() -> list[str]: ...                           # ✓ Concrete
190: def get_names() -> Iterable[str]: ...                       # ✗ Caller can't index it
191: 
192: # Use X | Y syntax (Python 3.10+), not Union[X, Y] or Optional[X]
193: def find(key: str) -> str | None: ...                       # ✓ Modern
194: def find(key: str) -> Optional[str]: ...                    # ✗ Old style
195: 
196: # None should be LAST in unions
197: def get(key: str) -> str | int | None: ...                  # ✓
198: 
199: # Avoid Any — it disables type checking entirely
200: def process(data: Any) -> Any: ...                          # ✗ Loses all safety
201: def process(data: dict[str, object]) -> dict[str, object]:  # ✓
202: 
203: # Use object instead of Any when a param accepts literally anything
204: def log(value: object) -> None: ...                         # ✓
205: 
206: # Avoid Union return types — they require isinstance() checks at every call site
207: def get_value() -> str | int: ...                           # ✗ Forces callers to branch
208: ```
209: 
210: ### Protocols vs ABCs
211: 
212: ```python
213: from typing import Protocol, runtime_checkable
214: from abc import ABC, abstractmethod
215: 
216: # Use Protocol when you don't control the implementer classes (duck typing)
217: @runtime_checkable    # Makes isinstance() checks work at runtime
218: class Serializable(Protocol):
219:     def to_dict(self) -> dict[str, object]: ...
220: 
221: # Use ABC when you control the class hierarchy and want default implementations
222: class BaseBackend(ABC):
223:     @abstractmethod
224:     async def get(self, key: str) -> str | None: ...
225: 
226:     def get_or_default(self, key: str, default: str) -> str:
227:         result = self.get(key)
228:         return result if result is not None else default
229: ```
230: 
231: ### TypeVar and Generics
232: 
233: ```python
234: from typing import TypeVar, Generic
235: 
236: T = TypeVar("T")
237: T_co = TypeVar("T_co", covariant=True)   # For read-only containers
238: 
239: class Repository(Generic[T]):
240:     """Type-safe generic repository."""
241:     def __init__(self, model_class: type[T]) -> None:
242:         self._store: list[T] = []
243: 
244:     def add(self, item: T) -> None:
245:         self._store.append(item)
246: 
247:     def get_all(self) -> list[T]:
248:         return list(self._store)
249: ```
250: 
251: ### dataclasses for data containers
252: 
253: ```python
254: from dataclasses import dataclass, field
255: 
256: @dataclass(frozen=True)   # frozen=True → immutable, hashable (good for configs/keys)
257: class Config:
258:     api_key: str
259:     timeout: int = 30
260:     headers: dict[str, str] = field(default_factory=dict)
261: 
262:     def __post_init__(self) -> None:
263:         if not self.api_key:
264:             raise ValueError("api_key must not be empty")
265: ```
266: 
267: ### TYPE_CHECKING guard (avoid circular imports)
268: 
269: ```python
270: from __future__ import annotations
271: from typing import TYPE_CHECKING
272: 
273: if TYPE_CHECKING:
274:     from your_package.models import HeavyModel    # Only imported during type checking
275: 
276: def process(model: "HeavyModel") -> None:
277:     ...
278: ```
279: 
280: ### Overload for multiple signatures
281: 
282: ```python
283: from typing import overload
284: 
285: @overload
286: def get(key: str, default: None = ...) -> str | None: ...
287: @overload
288: def get(key: str, default: str) -> str: ...
289: def get(key: str, default: str | None = None) -> str | None:
290:     ...    # Single implementation handles both
291: ```
292: 
293: ---
294: 
295: ## 3. Core Class Design
296: 
297: The main class of your library should have a clear, minimal `__init__`, sensible defaults for all
298: parameters, and raise `TypeError` / `ValueError` early for invalid inputs. This prevents confusing
299: errors at call time rather than at construction.
300: 
301: ```python
302: # your_package/core.py
303: from __future__ import annotations
304: 
305: from your_package.exceptions import YourPackageError
306: 
307: 
308: class YourClient:
309:     """
310:     Main entry point for <your purpose>.
311: 
312:     Args:
313:         api_key: Required authentication credential.
314:         timeout: Request timeout in seconds. Defaults to 30.
315:         retries: Number of retry attempts. Defaults to 3.
316: 
317:     Raises:
318:         ValueError: If api_key is empty or timeout is non-positive.
319: 
320:     Example:
321:         >>> from your_package import YourClient
322:         >>> client = YourClient(api_key="sk-...")
323:         >>> result = client.process(data)
324:     """
325: 
326:     def __init__(
327:         self,
328:         api_key: str,
329:         timeout: int = 30,
330:         retries: int = 3,
331:     ) -> None:
332:         if not api_key:
333:             raise ValueError("api_key must not be empty")
334:         if timeout <= 0:
335:             raise ValueError("timeout must be positive")
336:         self._api_key = api_key
337:         self.timeout = timeout
338:         self.retries = retries
339: 
340:     def process(self, data: dict) -> dict:
341:         """
342:         Process data and return results.
343: 
344:         Args:
345:             data: Input dictionary to process.
346: 
347:         Returns:
348:             Processed result as a dictionary.
349: 
350:         Raises:
351:             YourPackageError: If processing fails.
352:         """
353:         ...
354: ```
355: 
356: ### Design rules
357: 
358: - Accept all config in `__init__`, not scattered across method calls.
359: - Validate at construction time — fail fast with a clear message.
360: - Keep `__init__` signatures stable. Adding new **keyword-only** args with defaults is backwards
361:   compatible. Removing or reordering positional args is a breaking change.
362: 
363: ---
364: 
365: ## 4. Factory / Builder Pattern
366: 
367: Use a factory function when users need to create pre-configured instances. This avoids cluttering
368: `__init__` with a dozen keyword arguments and keeps the common case simple.
369: 
370: ```python
371: # your_package/factory.py
372: from __future__ import annotations
373: 
374: from your_package.core import YourClient
375: from your_package.backends.memory import MemoryBackend
376: 
377: 
378: def create_client(
379:     api_key: str,
380:     *,
381:     timeout: int = 30,
382:     retries: int = 3,
383:     backend: str = "memory",
384:     backend_url: str | None = None,
385: ) -> YourClient:
386:     """
387:     Factory that returns a configured YourClient.
388: 
389:     Args:
390:         api_key: Required API key.
391:         timeout: Request timeout in seconds.
392:         retries: Number of retry attempts.
393:         backend: Storage backend type. One of 'memory' or 'redis'.
394:         backend_url: Connection URL for the chosen backend.
395: 
396:     Example:
397:         >>> client = create_client(api_key="sk-...", backend="redis", backend_url="redis://localhost")
398:     """
399:     if backend == "redis":
400:         from your_package.backends.redis import RedisBackend
401:         _backend = RedisBackend(url=backend_url or "redis://localhost:6379")
402:     else:
403:         _backend = MemoryBackend()
404: 
405:     return YourClient(api_key=api_key, timeout=timeout, retries=retries, backend=_backend)
406: ```
407: 
408: **Why a factory, not a class method?** Both work. A standalone factory function is easier to
409: mock in tests and avoids coupling the factory logic into the class itself.
410: 
411: ---
412: 
413: ## 5. Configuration Pattern
414: 
415: Use a dataclass (or Pydantic `BaseModel`) to hold configuration. This gives you free validation,
416: helpful error messages, and a single place to document every option.
417: 
418: ```python
419: # your_package/config.py
420: from __future__ import annotations
421: from dataclasses import dataclass, field
422: 
423: 
424: @dataclass
425: class YourSettings:
426:     """
427:     Configuration for YourClient.
428: 
429:     Attributes:
430:         timeout: HTTP timeout in seconds.
431:         retries: Number of retry attempts on transient errors.
432:         base_url: Base API URL.
433:     """
434:     timeout: int = 30
435:     retries: int = 3
436:     base_url: str = "https://api.example.com"
437:     extra_headers: dict[str, str] = field(default_factory=dict)
438: 
439:     def __post_init__(self) -> None:
440:         if self.timeout <= 0:
441:             raise ValueError("timeout must be positive")
442:         if self.retries < 0:
443:             raise ValueError("retries must be non-negative")
444: ```
445: 
446: If you need environment variable loading, use `pydantic-settings` as an **optional** dependency —
447: declare it in `[project.optional-dependencies]`, not as a required dep.
448: 
449: ---
450: 
451: ## 6. `__init__.py` — Explicit Public API
452: 
453: A well-defined `__all__` is not just style — it tells users (and IDEs) exactly what's part of your
454: public API, and prevents accidental imports of internal helpers as part of your contract.
455: 
456: ```python
457: # your_package/__init__.py
458: """your-package: <one-line description>."""
459: 
460: from importlib.metadata import version, PackageNotFoundError
461: 
462: try:
463:     __version__ = version("your-package")
464: except PackageNotFoundError:
465:     __version__ = "0.0.0-dev"
466: 
467: from your_package.core import YourClient
468: from your_package.config import YourSettings
469: from your_package.exceptions import YourPackageError
470: 
471: __all__ = [
472:     "YourClient",
473:     "YourSettings",
474:     "YourPackageError",
475:     "__version__",
476: ]
477: ```
478: 
479: Rules:
480: - Only export what users are supposed to use. Internal helpers go in `_utils.py` or submodules.
481: - Keep imports at the top level of `__init__.py` shallow — avoid importing heavy optional deps
482:   (like `redis`) at module level. Import them lazily inside the class or function that needs them.
483: - `__version__` is always part of the public API — it enables `your_package.__version__` for
484:   debugging.
485: 
486: ---
487: 
488: ## 7. Optional Backends (Plugin Pattern)
489: 
490: This pattern lets your package work out-of-the-box (no extra deps) with an in-memory backend,
491: while letting advanced users plug in Redis, a database, or any custom storage.
492: 
493: ### 5.1 Abstract base class — defines the interface
494: 
495: ```python
496: # your_package/backends/__init__.py
497: from abc import ABC, abstractmethod
498: 
499: 
500: class BaseBackend(ABC):
501:     """Abstract storage backend interface.
502: 
503:     Implement this to add a custom backend (database, cache, etc.).
504:     """
505: 
506:     @abstractmethod
507:     async def get(self, key: str) -> str | None:
508:         """Retrieve a value by key. Returns None if not found."""
509:         ...
510: 
511:     @abstractmethod
512:     async def set(self, key: str, value: str, ttl: int | None = None) -> None:
513:         """Store a value. Optional TTL in seconds."""
514:         ...
515: 
516:     @abstractmethod
517:     async def delete(self, key: str) -> None:
518:         """Delete a key."""
519:         ...
520: ```
521: 
522: ### 5.2 Memory backend — zero extra deps
523: 
524: ```python
525: # your_package/backends/memory.py
526: from __future__ import annotations
527: 
528: import asyncio
529: import time
530: from your_package.backends import BaseBackend
531: 
532: 
533: class MemoryBackend(BaseBackend):
534:     """Thread-safe in-memory backend. Works out of the box — no extra dependencies."""
535: 
536:     def __init__(self) -> None:
537:         self._store: dict[str, tuple[str, float | None]] = {}
538:         self._lock = asyncio.Lock()
539: 
540:     async def get(self, key: str) -> str | None:
541:         async with self._lock:
542:             entry = self._store.get(key)
543:             if entry is None:
544:                 return None
545:             value, expires_at = entry
546:             if expires_at is not None and time.time() > expires_at:
547:                 del self._store[key]
548:                 return None
549:             return value
550: 
551:     async def set(self, key: str, value: str, ttl: int | None = None) -> None:
552:         async with self._lock:
553:             expires_at = time.time() + ttl if ttl is not None else None
554:             self._store[key] = (value, expires_at)
555: 
556:     async def delete(self, key: str) -> None:
557:         async with self._lock:
558:             self._store.pop(key, None)
559: ```
560: 
561: ### 5.3 Redis backend — raises clear ImportError if not installed
562: 
563: The key design: import `redis` lazily inside `__init__`, not at module level. This way,
564: `import your_package` never fails even if `redis` isn't installed.
565: 
566: ```python
567: # your_package/backends/redis.py
568: from __future__ import annotations
569: from your_package.backends import BaseBackend
570: 
571: try:
572:     import redis.asyncio as aioredis
573: except ImportError as exc:
574:     raise ImportError(
575:         "Redis backend requires the redis extra:\n"
576:         "  pip install your-package[redis]"
577:     ) from exc
578: 
579: 
580: class RedisBackend(BaseBackend):
581:     """Redis-backed storage for distributed/multi-process deployments."""
582: 
583:     def __init__(self, url: str = "redis://localhost:6379") -> None:
584:         self._client = aioredis.from_url(url, decode_responses=True)
585: 
586:     async def get(self, key: str) -> str | None:
587:         return await self._client.get(key)
588: 
589:     async def set(self, key: str, value: str, ttl: int | None = None) -> None:
590:         await self._client.set(key, value, ex=ttl)
591: 
592:     async def delete(self, key: str) -> None:
593:         await self._client.delete(key)
594: ```
595: 
596: ### 5.4 How users choose a backend
597: 
598: ```python
599: # Default: in-memory, no extra deps needed
600: from your_package import YourClient
601: client = YourClient(api_key="sk-...")
602: 
603: # Redis: pip install your-package[redis]
604: from your_package.backends.redis import RedisBackend
605: client = YourClient(api_key="sk-...", backend=RedisBackend(url="redis://localhost:6379"))
606: ```
